// ============================================================
// data/starter-activities.js — Corrective starter activities by misconception tag
// Loaded after data/forge-data.js and data/misconception-labels.js, before
// any page that offers a starter: teacher.html.
//
// Until this file existed, MC_STARTERS lived inline in teacher.html. It is
// split out for the same reason MC_LABELS was: the starter is content, it is
// edited far more often than the dashboard around it, and a second page will
// want it.
//
// A starter is the five-minute board activity a teacher runs when a
// misconception keeps firing. Every entry targets ONE specific error and
// makes the student commit to an answer before the reveal — that commitment
// is the point, so a starter that only restates the correct fact is not
// worth having.
//
// Scope: the tags a heatmap can actually aggregate — those used by two or
// more questions. There are 1,233 of them and 642 have a starter.
// Single-use tags (2,633 of them) are per-question identifiers rather than
// misconception categories; getStarterActivity() falls back to a generic
// drill built from the resolved label for those.
// Every active tag also resolves through a structured, question-aware
// activity builder rather than a subject-wide instruction shell.
//
// The 591 aggregatable tags still without a starter are almost all tags that
// happen to be used once in each of two different subjects, rather than a
// cluster a single teacher would ever see on one heatmap. The subjects with a
// genuine within-subject taxonomy — psych, gcse-geo, econ, soc and now
// gcse-econ — are fully covered.
//
// GCSE Economics was retagged in full: its 420 questions previously carried
// 420 tags unique to themselves, so nothing aggregated and every starter fell
// back to the generic drill. They now use 76 shared MC-GE-* tags, averaging
// 5.5 questions per tag with none used only once. That change alone removed
// 418 single-use tags and added 75 aggregatable ones, all with starters below.
// The remaining subjects listed in CLAUDE.md still need the same treatment.
//
// A-Level History was retagged the same way: 141 of its 208 questions moved
// from per-question ids onto 37 shared MC-HIST-* tags, all with starters
// below. The other 67 keep their per-question tag deliberately — see
// docs/history-misconception-mapping.md.
//
// Shapes in use: sort, classify, distinguish, correct, diagnose, diagram,
// chain, compare, evaluate, apply, match, trace, sequence, rewrite, graph,
// trigger, explain, scenario, calculate. `items` renders as a list to work
// through; `headers`/`rows` renders as a table to complete; `prompt` is
// board text; `answer` is the reveal, shown to the teacher only.
// ============================================================

var MC_STARTERS = {
  "MC-INF-01":{type:"sort",title:"Inflation or Disinflation?",instruction:"Sort these statements into Inflation, Disinflation, or Deflation. One fits two categories — be ready to justify.",items:["Price level rising at 4%","Price level rising at 2%, down from 4%","Price level falling","CPI at 103, up from 101","The rate of inflation is slowing","Prices are lower than last year"],answer:"Inflation: 'rising at 4%', 'CPI at 103, up from 101'. Disinflation: 'the rate of inflation is slowing'. Deflation: 'price level falling', 'prices are lower than last year'. The one fitting TWO is 'rising at 2%, down from 4%' — that is inflation (prices are still going up) AND disinflation (the rate has fallen). This is the whole point: disinflation is a fall in the RATE, deflation a fall in the LEVEL. Under disinflation prices are still rising, just more slowly, so nothing has got cheaper."},
  "MC-INF-03":{type:"diagnose",title:"Diagnose the Diagram",instruction:"A student drew AD/AS to show demand-pull inflation but made two errors. Identify both and correct them.",prompt:"Student labelled the shift 'AD moves right' and wrote 'result: higher prices AND higher output', showing the new equilibrium on a vertical LRAS.",answer:"Error 1: on a VERTICAL LRAS, output cannot rise — the economy is already at full capacity, so a rightward AD shift is purely inflationary. Higher prices AND higher output is impossible on the diagram drawn. Error 2: if the student wants both effects, the shift must be shown against an upward-sloping SRAS with spare capacity, not LRAS. The underlying point: what a rightward AD shift does to output depends entirely on WHICH curve it meets, which is why the AS curve has to be chosen deliberately rather than drawn from habit."},
  "MC-INF-07":{type:"correct",title:"Spot the Error",instruction:"Find and correct the error in this student sentence.",prompt:"'The Bank of England's MPC must keep inflation at 0%, as higher prices damage the economy.'",answer:"Target is 2% CPI (symmetric). The MPC writes to the Chancellor if inflation exceeds 3% or falls below 1%."},
  "MC-GRO-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Level vs Rate",instruction:"Label each GDP figure as 'level of GDP', 'rate of growth', or 'both'. Write one sentence explaining the difference.",items:["UK GDP: £2.2 trillion","GDP grew by 0.4% last quarter","GDP per capita rose from £32,000 to £33,500","The economy is 18% larger than in 2010"],answer:"Level: 'UK GDP: £2.2 trillion' — a size at a point in time. Rate: 'grew by 0.4% last quarter' and '18% larger than in 2010' — both express change, the second over a longer period. Both: 'GDP per capita rose from £32,000 to £33,500' — it states two levels and implies a rate (about 4.7%). The distinction: the LEVEL tells you how big the economy is, the RATE how fast it is changing. A country can have a very high level and near-zero growth, or a low level and rapid growth — which is why 'GDP is over £2 trillion' says nothing whatever about growth."},
  "MC-GRO-03":{type:"diagram",title:"Gap Check",instruction:"Draw an AD/AS diagram showing a positive output gap. Label actual GDP, potential GDP, and the price level. Explain in one sentence why it is called 'positive'.",prompt:"Common error: students draw actual GDP below potential and still call it positive.",answer:"A POSITIVE output gap means actual GDP is ABOVE potential GDP — the economy is producing beyond its sustainable capacity, with labour and capital worked unusually hard. On the diagram, AD intersects to the RIGHT of the LRAS/full-employment line, and the price level is above where it would otherwise sit: inflationary pressure. It is called positive because actual minus potential is a positive number. Drawing actual below potential is a NEGATIVE gap — spare capacity, downward pressure on prices, associated with recession."},
  "MC-EMP-01":{type:"classify",title:"Who Counts?",instruction:"Decide whether each person is ILO unemployed, claimant count unemployed, or economically inactive. One person may fit two categories.",items:["Jake, 19, not working but looking and available","Priya, 35, left work to care for children","Marcus, 24, working 3 hours/week (wants more)","Aisha, 28, claiming Universal Credit while job hunting","Lena, 67, retired"],answer:"Jake: ILO unemployed — out of work, actively seeking, available. Priya: economically inactive — not seeking work. Marcus: EMPLOYED — the ILO counts anyone doing at least one hour of paid work a week, so he is underemployed, not unemployed. Aisha: the one in TWO categories — ILO unemployed AND claimant count, because she is both job hunting and claiming. Lena: economically inactive. The rule that catches most students: the ILO measure asks what you are DOING (seeking and available), the claimant count asks what you are CLAIMING — so the claimant count misses those seeking work without an entitlement, and the ILO figure is usually the larger of the two."},
  "MC-EMP-03":{type:"evaluate",title:"Short Run or Long Run?",instruction:"Mark each claim T (true in short run), L (long run only), or F (false). Justify each.",items:["Lower unemployment always means higher inflation","The long-run Phillips curve is vertical","The MPC can reduce unemployment permanently by cutting rates","Worker expectations adjust — removing the permanent trade-off"],answer:"1 — FALSE. There is a short-run trade-off, but 'always' is wrong: cost-push inflation raises inflation AND unemployment together, shifting the curve rather than moving along it. 2 — LONG RUN. The LRPC is vertical at the natural rate. 3 — FALSE. Cutting rates lowers unemployment only until expectations adjust; the long-run effect is higher inflation at the same natural rate. 4 — LONG RUN. Expectation adjustment is precisely the mechanism that removes the permanent trade-off. The unifying idea: the short-run curve works because inflation surprises people. Once it stops being a surprise, it stops working."},
  "MC-BOP-02":{type:"correct",title:"Spot the Confusion",instruction:"Identify the conceptual error and rewrite correctly.",prompt:"'The UK's current account deficit is the same as the government's fiscal deficit — both measure the gap between government income and spending.'",answer:"Two entirely different balances. The CURRENT ACCOUNT records transactions with the REST OF THE WORLD — trade in goods and services, primary income, transfers. The FISCAL deficit records the GOVERNMENT's spending against its revenue, within the domestic economy. Correct version: 'The UK's current account deficit measures the gap between what the UK earns from and pays to the rest of the world; the fiscal deficit measures the gap between government spending and tax revenue.' They can move together — the 'twin deficits' argument runs that government borrowing raises demand and sucks in imports — but a correlation is not an identity, and the twin deficits hypothesis is exactly the point the confusion destroys."},
  "MC-BOP-03":{type:"diagram",title:"Draw the J-Curve",instruction:"Sketch a J-curve showing the effect of sterling depreciation on the current account over time. Label the initial worsening phase, turning point, and improvement phase. Add a 6-word explanation of why it worsens first.",prompt:"Common error: students draw immediate improvement after depreciation.",answer:"Shape: falls first, troughs, then rises above the starting level. Why it worsens: depreciation raises IMPORT PRICES immediately, but trade VOLUMES take time to respond — contracts are fixed, buyers are slow to switch suppliers, firms need time to expand export capacity. In the short run demand for imports and exports is inelastic, so the higher price of the same quantity of imports worsens the account. Over time volumes adjust, the Marshall-Lerner condition is met (PEDx + PEDm > 1), and the account improves. Six-word explanation: 'Prices change immediately, volumes adjust slowly.'"},
  "MC-AD-01":{type:"correct",title:"Fix the Formula",instruction:"A student wrote: AD = C + S + G + (X − M). Identify the error and correct it. Explain why savings belongs in a different model.",answer:"AD = C + I + G + (X − M). Savings is a withdrawal from the circular flow, not a component of AD."},
  "MC-AD-04":{type:"diagram",title:"What Happens to Prices?",instruction:"On an AD/AS diagram, show a rightward shift in AD. Mark the new equilibrium. Does the price level rise or fall? Why does the answer depend on where the economy is on the SRAS curve?",prompt:"Common error: students state AD shifting right always causes lower prices.",answer:"The price level RISES, or at worst stays flat — it never falls. How much depends on where the shift lands: on the horizontal (Keynesian) section, spare capacity means output rises with no price rise; on the upward-sloping section, both output and prices rise; on the vertical section, prices rise and output cannot. So the answer is a range, from 'all output' to 'all prices', governed by how much spare capacity exists. Students who say prices FALL are confusing a shift in AD with a shift in AS — a rightward AS shift is the one that raises output and lowers prices."},
  "MC-AS-01":{type:"sort",title:"SRAS or LRAS?",instruction:"Sort these factors into 'shifts SRAS only', 'shifts LRAS only', or 'shifts both'. Justify one controversial placement.",items:["Oil price rise","Worker productivity improvement","Infrastructure investment","Rise in nominal wages","Technological innovation","Consumer confidence falls"],answer:"SRAS only: oil price rise (a short-run cost shock), rise in nominal wages. BOTH: worker productivity improvement, infrastructure investment, technological innovation — each lowers costs now AND raises productive capacity. NEITHER: consumer confidence falls — that shifts AD, and it is the trap in the list. The controversial placement is the oil price rise: if the change is permanent it degrades productive capacity and arguably moves LRAS too. The test for LRAS is always the same — has the QUANTITY OR QUALITY of the factors of production changed?"},
  "MC-FIS-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Policy Match",instruction:"Match each action to 'fiscal' or 'monetary'. Three are ambiguous — annotate why.",items:["MPC cuts the base rate","Chancellor raises income tax","Government increases road spending","BoE buys government bonds (QE)","Government introduces Help to Buy","MPC raises rates to 5.25%"],answer:"Monetary (the Bank of England): cutting the base rate, QE, raising rates to 5.25%. Fiscal (the Treasury): income tax rise, road spending, Help to Buy. The three worth annotating: QE is monetary but operates by buying GOVERNMENT DEBT, which blurs the line with fiscal policy; Help to Buy is fiscal but works on credit conditions in a single market, like targeted monetary policy; road spending is fiscal demand-side AND supply-side, since it raises capacity as well as AD. The clean test is WHO ACTS — the independent central bank or the elected government — not what the policy is trying to achieve."},
  "MC-MON-04":{type:"chain",title:"QE Transmission Chain",instruction:"Complete the chain from start to finish. Each arrow must be a specific mechanism, not 'the economy improves'.",prompt:"BoE buys £60bn of government bonds → [step] → [step] → [step] → aggregate demand rises. Common error: students say QE 'prints money that goes straight to consumers'.",answer:"BoE buys £60bn of bonds from pension funds, insurers and banks → bond prices rise and yields FALL, dragging down long-term interest rates across the economy → sellers hold cash they must reinvest, pushing up other asset prices and making borrowing cheaper for firms and households → investment and consumption rise on cheaper credit and higher wealth → AD rises. The error in 'prints money that goes straight to consumers': the new money is central bank RESERVES, and it goes to the institutions selling the bonds, not to households. QE works INDIRECTLY, through interest rates and asset prices — which is also why its distributional effect is criticised, since asset holders gain first."},
  "MC-NI-01":{type:"classify",title:"Injection or Withdrawal?",instruction:"Classify each item as Injection (J) or Withdrawal (W). Then write the equilibrium condition using I, G, X, S, T, M.",items:["Consumer spending on imports","Government NHS spending","Household savings","Export revenue from UK services","Corporation tax receipts","Business investment"],answer:"Injections: government NHS spending (G), export revenue (X), business investment (I). Withdrawals: spending on imports (M), household savings (S), corporation tax receipts (T). Equilibrium: S + T + M = I + G + X — total withdrawals equal total injections. Note it is the TOTALS that must match, not each pair: savings need not equal investment individually for the circular flow to be in equilibrium. Imports are the one most often misplaced, because the spending feels domestic — but the money leaves the domestic circular flow, which is what makes it a withdrawal."},
  "MC-SI-01":{type:"correct",title:"Fix the Stat",instruction:"A student wrote: 'Asch found that 37% of participants conformed.' Identify the error and write the three correct Asch statistics.",answer:"37% = conformity on critical trials. 75% = participants who conformed at least once. ~32% = overall conformity rate across all trials."},
  "MC-SI-03":{type:"correct",title:"Milgram: Check the Figure",instruction:"A student wrote: 'Milgram showed that most people refused to obey.' Correct this and name one variation that significantly reduced obedience.",answer:"65% delivered maximum 450V in the baseline. Obedience fell to 40% when authority gave orders by phone; to 20% when the study moved from Yale."},
  "MC-MEM-01":{type:"diagram",title:"Reconstruct the MSM",instruction:"From memory, draw the Multi-Store Model. Label all three stores with capacity and duration. Mark the two processes between them. Identify one criticism.",prompt:"Common error: students reverse transfer direction or omit the rehearsal loop.",answer:"Sensory register: capacity very large, duration 0.25–2 seconds, modality-specific (iconic, echoic). ATTENTION transfers to short-term memory: 5–9 items, 18–30 seconds, coded acoustically. REHEARSAL maintains it there and, if prolonged, transfers to long-term memory: unlimited capacity, potentially a lifetime, coded semantically. Retrieval runs LTM back to STM. Criticism: the case of KF, whose digit span was impaired for auditory but not visual material, shows STM is not the single unitary store the model requires — the Working Memory Model was built to fix exactly this. Craik and Lockhart add a second: the DEPTH of processing predicts recall better than the amount of rehearsal."},
  "MC-MEM-08":{type:"scenario",title:"Which Type of Interference?",instruction:"State whether proactive or retroactive interference is operating in each scenario. Write one sentence explaining the direction of disruption.",items:["You learned French first — your Spanish keeps coming out as French","You learn a new phone PIN and can no longer recall the old one","New revision notes keep pushing out the original version","Old work password resurfaces instead of the current one"],answer:"Proactive: 1 (French learned first disrupts Spanish) and 4 (old password resurfaces). Retroactive: 2 (new PIN blocks the old) and 3 (new notes push out the original). The direction rule: PRO- means forwards — OLD learning interferes with NEW. RETRO- means backwards — NEW learning interferes with OLD. Ask which memory is the VICTIM, not which is the cause: if you cannot recall the newer thing, it is proactive; if you cannot recall the older thing, it is retroactive. Interference is strongest when the two sets of material are similar, which is McGeoch and McDonald's finding and the reason revision timetables separate similar subjects."},
  "MC-ATT-01":{type:"correct",title:"Bowlby: Spot the Error",instruction:"Find and correct the error.",prompt:"'Bowlby argued that infants form equal attachments to all caregivers simultaneously and that any of these can serve as the primary attachment figure.'",answer:"Bowlby argued for monotropy — one primary attachment that is qualitatively stronger. This forms the internal working model; other attachments are secondary."},
  "MC-MET-01":{type:"evaluate",title:"Ethical Matrix",instruction:"Complete this matrix for covert participant observation. Then decide: is it ever justifiable? Apply one BPS ethical guideline.",headers:["Ethical issue","Problem for covert obs","How researcher might respond"],rows:[["Informed consent","",""],["Right to withdraw","",""],["Deception","",""],["Protection from harm",""," "]],answer:"Informed consent — cannot be obtained without destroying the study; response: seek retrospective consent at debrief, or presumptive consent from a similar group beforehand. Right to withdraw — participants do not know they are in a study, so cannot exercise it; response: offer the right to withdraw DATA after the debrief. Deception — inherent and total; response: justify by cost-benefit judgement and independent ethics committee approval. Protection from harm — distress or embarrassment on learning they were observed, and risk to the researcher in some settings; response: anonymise fully, debrief, offer support. Is it justifiable? Defensible where the behaviour genuinely cannot be studied otherwise, because participants would change their behaviour if observed openly — Rosenhan's pseudopatient study is the standard example. The BPS guidance to apply is the RISK/cost-benefit principle: the harm must be outweighed by the scientific value, and the judgement is not the researcher's alone to make."},
  "MC-TEC-01":{type:"diagram",title:"Boundary Types",instruction:"Draw cross-sections of a destructive boundary and a constructive boundary. Label plate types, direction of movement, crust destroyed/created, and hazards at each. Explain why oceanic crust subducts rather than continental.",prompt:"Common error: students reverse which plate subducts or omit oceanic vs continental labelling.",answer:"Destructive: two plates CONVERGE, arrows pointing towards each other; the denser OCEANIC plate subducts beneath the continental one at an ocean trench; crust is DESTROYED in the Benioff zone; hazards are deep and powerful earthquakes, explosive composite volcanoes, tsunami, and fold mountains inland. Constructive: plates DIVERGE, arrows apart; magma rises to fill the gap and NEW basaltic crust is created at a mid-ocean ridge; hazards are shallow, low-magnitude earthquakes and effusive shield or fissure volcanoes. Oceanic crust subducts because it is DENSER (about 3.0 g/cm³ against 2.7) and thinner — density is the reason, not thickness or age on its own. Where two oceanic plates meet, the older and colder one subducts, for the same reason."},
  "MC-TEC-02":{type:"compare",title:"Why did Haiti lose more lives than Christchurch?",instruction:"Complete this comparison table for the two earthquakes, then write a two-sentence conclusion explaining why development level mattered more than magnitude.",headers:["Factor","Haiti 2010","Christchurch 2011"],rows:[["Magnitude","7.0","6.3"],["Deaths","~230,000","185"],["Building quality","",""],["Emergency services","",""],["GDP per capita","",""],["Conclusion: why the difference?","",""]],answer:"Building quality — Haiti: no enforced building code, unreinforced concrete, heavy floors that pancaked. Christchurch: strict seismic code, ductile steel frames, base isolation on key buildings. Emergency services — Haiti: overwhelmed, port and airport damaged, government buildings destroyed, response dependent on foreign aid arriving days later. Christchurch: professional urban search and rescue on site within hours. GDP per capita — Haiti roughly $670; New Zealand roughly $32,000. Conclusion: the Haiti quake was slightly stronger, but the deaths came from VULNERABILITY, not energy — poor construction with no code enforcement, extreme population density in Port-au-Prince, and no capacity to respond in the first 72 hours when most survivors are recovered. New Zealand's wealth had already been converted into resistant buildings and a rehearsed response, so the same shaking killed 185 rather than 230,000. Be fair to the physical factors in the comparison — Haiti's focus was shallower and directly beneath a capital city — but development level is what turned a hazard into a catastrophe."},
  "MC-EDU-02":{type:"apply",title:"Hidden Curriculum in Practice",instruction:"Commit to a mapping before discussion: link each feature to a specific element of the correspondence principle (Bowles & Gintis), then name the social class it benefits. Teacher move: probe one mapping that could plausibly be mistaken for ordinary classroom management.",prompt:"Students line up in silence; are rewarded for punctuality; and are streamed by assessed ability from Year 7.",answer:"Silence and obedience mirror the subordinate workplace role; punctuality mirrors the time discipline of paid work; and streaming mirrors the hierarchy of occupational roles. Bowles and Gintis argue that these practices prepare working-class pupils for subordinate labour while making unequal outcomes appear meritocratic."},
  "MC-TEC-03":{type:"correct",title:"Why do composite volcanoes explode?",instruction:"A student wrote: 'Composite volcanoes erupt explosively because they are very tall and the pressure builds up inside.' Find the two errors in this explanation and write the correct version.",prompt:"Correct explanation must include: silica-rich (acidic) magma from subducted oceanic crust, high viscosity preventing gas escape, pressure builds until explosive release. Tall = shield volcano (basaltic, effusive). Height is a consequence, not a cause.",answer:"Composite volcanoes erupt explosively because silica-rich magma from the subducted plate is highly viscous — it traps gases which build until a violent explosive eruption occurs. The height of the volcano is a result of previous eruptions, not the cause of explosivity."},
  "MC-TEC-04":{type:"classify",title:"Earthquakes — which boundary?",instruction:"Classify each earthquake example as destructive, constructive, or conservative boundary. One is a trap — be ready to justify.",items:["2010 Haiti — conservative plate boundary (Caribbean/North American plates sliding)",  "2004 Indian Ocean — destructive boundary (subduction triggered megathrust)","San Andreas Fault, California — conservative (transform fault)","Mid-Atlantic Ridge — constructive boundary (can produce earthquakes)","2011 Tohoku, Japan — destructive (Pacific plate subducting under Eurasian)"],answer:"Every classification as listed is CORRECT — that is the trap. Haiti is genuinely a CONSERVATIVE boundary (the Enriquillo–Plantain Garden strike-slip fault), and students reject it because the death toll makes them expect a destructive margin; damage reflects vulnerability, not boundary type. The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is the second catch: constructive boundaries DO produce earthquakes, just shallow and low-magnitude ones, so 'constructive means no earthquakes' is wrong. The pattern worth stating: destructive margins produce the deepest and largest earthquakes because crust is being forced down a Benioff zone; conservative margins produce shallow but sometimes devastating ones as locked plates slip; constructive margins produce frequent minor ones."},
  "MC-TEC-06":{type:"diagram",title:"Focus or epicentre?",instruction:"Draw a cross-section diagram of an earthquake. Label: the focus (hypocentre), the epicentre, seismic waves, and the fault line. Then write one sentence defining each term precisely.",prompt:"Common error: students say the epicentre is where the earthquake originates — it is the point on the SURFACE directly ABOVE the focus. The focus (hypocentre) is the underground origin point. Shallow-focus earthquakes (<70km) cause more surface damage than deep-focus events.",answer:"Focus: the point underground where the earthquake originates and energy is released. Epicentre: the point on the Earth's surface directly above the focus — where shaking is usually most intense."},
  "MC-TEC-07":{type:"explain",title:"Why is Iceland so volcanically active?",instruction:"Iceland sits on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge (constructive boundary). But that alone does not explain its exceptional volcanic activity. Write a two-part explanation: (1) what the constructive boundary contributes, (2) what the hot spot adds — and mark Iceland's position on a blank Atlantic diagram.",prompt:"Constructive boundary: plates diverge → magma rises to fill gap → effusive basaltic eruptions (e.g. Eyjafjallajökull fissure eruptions). Hot spot: a fixed mantle plume of unusually hot material beneath the crust — independently generates extra volcanic activity. Iceland sits above BOTH simultaneously, making it more active than any other constructive boundary location.",answer:"Two factors combine: (1) The Mid-Atlantic Ridge causes plates to diverge, allowing basaltic magma to rise — producing regular effusive eruptions. (2) A mantle hot spot beneath Iceland provides an additional source of heat independent of the boundary, further raising volcanic output. Neither factor alone explains Iceland's exceptional activity."},
  "MC-BUS-02":{type:"calculate",title:"Break-Even Check",instruction:"Selling price: £20, variable cost per unit: £12, fixed costs: £40,000. Show full workings using the contribution method. Explain why the formula uses contribution, not profit.",prompt:"Common error: students divide price by fixed costs rather than fixed costs by contribution per unit.",answer:"Contribution per unit = £20 − £12 = £8. Break-even = fixed costs ÷ contribution per unit = £40,000 ÷ £8 = 5,000 units. Break-even revenue = 5,000 × £20 = £100,000. Why contribution and not profit: each unit sold contributes £8 towards paying off the fixed costs, and only once all £40,000 is covered does the business start making profit at all. Using profit in the formula is circular — profit is what is LEFT after fixed costs, so it cannot be the thing you divide fixed costs by. Dividing price by fixed costs, the common error, is not merely the wrong figure but the wrong shape of calculation: it produces a number with no meaning."},
  "MC-CHEM-02":{type:"graph",title:"Ionisation Energy Jumps",instruction:"Sketch a graph of successive ionisation energies for sodium. Mark the large jump and explain why it occurs where it does.",prompt:"Common error: students describe all jumps as equal or fail to link the large jump to a new electron shell.",answer:"Sodium is 2,8,1. The graph rises overall, with the FIRST ionisation energy low and a very large jump between the 1st and 2nd. That jump happens because the first electron comes from the outer 3s shell, while the second must be removed from the full 2,8 shell — closer to the nucleus, less shielded, and breaking into a complete shell. A second, smaller jump appears between the 9th and 10th, as removal moves into the 1s shell. The jumps are NOT equal: within a shell each successive removal is only modestly harder, because the electron is pulled from the same distance against a rising positive charge. The pattern is direct evidence FOR shell structure — count electrons before the big jump and you have the group number."},
  "MC-CRIM-01":{type:"sort",title:"Crime, Deviance, or Both?",instruction:"Sort each behaviour and explain your reasoning. Identify one example where the classification has changed over time.",items:["Shoplifting","Wearing pyjamas to a funeral","Cheating on a partner","Cannabis possession","Tax avoidance (legal schemes)","Honour-based violence"],answer:"Crime AND deviance: shoplifting, cannabis possession, honour-based violence. Deviance only: pyjamas at a funeral, cheating on a partner, tax avoidance through legal schemes. The changed-over-time example is cannabis possession — decriminalised or legalised in many jurisdictions, criminal in others, and treated differently by police forces within one country. Homosexuality is the sharper historical case: criminal in England and Wales until 1967, and no longer widely regarded as deviant either. The definitions do not nest: CRIME is breaking the law, DEVIANCE is breaking social norms, and each contains cases the other does not. Honour-based violence is the case that shows deviance is relative to the group doing the judging — criminal everywhere in the UK, but treated as normative within the community that practises it, which is exactly why sociologists insist deviance has no fixed content."},
  "MC-BIO-03":{type:"sequence",title:"Replication Steps",instruction:"Put these events in the correct order, then annotate each with the enzyme responsible and its specific function.",items:["DNA polymerase adds complementary nucleotides","Helicase unwinds the double helix","Free nucleotides are activated","Two identical double helices produced","Hydrogen bonds between base pairs break","DNA ligase joins Okazaki fragments"],answer:"Correct order: (1) Helicase unwinds the double helix; (2) hydrogen bonds between base pairs break; (3) free nucleotides are activated; (4) DNA polymerase adds complementary nucleotides; (5) DNA ligase joins Okazaki fragments; (6) two identical double helices produced. Enzymes: HELICASE breaks the hydrogen bonds between complementary bases, unwinding and separating the strands. DNA POLYMERASE catalyses condensation reactions forming phosphodiester bonds between adjacent nucleotides, and can only work 5' to 3' — which is why the lagging strand is built in fragments at all. DNA LIGASE joins those Okazaki fragments into a continuous strand. The result is SEMI-CONSERVATIVE: each new helix keeps one original strand and one new one, which is what Meselson and Stahl's density experiment demonstrated."},
  "MC-HIST-04":{type:"chain",title:"Fire to Dictatorship",instruction:"Complete the causal chain. Each link must be a specific event or law.",prompt:"Reichstag Fire (Feb 1933) → [step] → [step] → [step] → Hitler has dictatorial powers (July 1933). Common error: jumping from fire directly to Enabling Act without naming the Decree of the Reich President.",answer:"Reichstag Fire, 27 February 1933 → the DECREE OF THE REICH PRESIDENT FOR THE PROTECTION OF PEOPLE AND STATE (28 February), suspending civil liberties and permitting detention without trial, under which around 4,000 communists were arrested → the March 1933 election, fought with the KPD leadership already in custody and the SA policing the campaign, followed by the exclusion of KPD deputies from the chamber → the ENABLING ACT of 23 March 1933, passed with the two-thirds majority this exclusion and Centre Party support made possible, transferring legislative power to the cabinet → the banning of trade unions (May) and the Law against the Establishment of Parties (14 July), leaving the NSDAP the only legal party. The step students skip is the February decree: without it the arrests were not lawful, the election was not winnable on those terms, and the two-thirds majority for the Enabling Act does not exist. It also mattered that the decree was never repealed — it remained the legal basis of the police state until 1945."},
  "MC-LAW-02":{type:"apply",title:"Intent or Recklessness?",instruction:"Classify each scenario as direct intent, oblique intent (Woollin), or Cunningham recklessness. Cite the defining case.",items:["D shoots V with aim of killing","D blows up plane for insurance — passengers die","D throws stone at window knowing it might break — it does","D drives at high speed in residential area — child killed"],answer:"1 — DIRECT intent: the consequence is D's aim or purpose (R v Mohan, 'a decision to bring about the prohibited consequence'). 2 — OBLIQUE intent: death was not the aim but was a virtual certainty and D appreciated it, so the jury may FIND intention (R v Woollin, refining Nedrick; Hyam is the older, wider test). 3 — Subjective RECKLESSNESS: D foresaw the risk and took it anyway (R v Cunningham, restored for criminal damage by R v G [2003] after Caldwell). 4 — depends on the facts: subjective recklessness if D actually foresaw the risk to others, gross negligence manslaughter if he simply fell far below the standard of a reasonable driver (R v Adomako), and in practice charged as causing death by dangerous driving. The dividing line throughout is FORESIGHT: intention asks what D aimed at or knew was certain, recklessness asks whether D saw a risk and ran it anyway. Woollin is a rule about what a jury MAY infer, not a definition of intention — a distinction examiners reward."},
  "MC-PHYS-01":{type:"correct",title:"Forces vs Motion",instruction:"A student wrote: 'When an object moves at constant velocity, a net forward force must be acting.' Correct this using Newton's First Law and draw a free body diagram for a car at constant speed.",prompt:"Common error: students assume motion requires ongoing force — confusing Aristotelian with Newtonian mechanics.",answer:"Newton's First Law: a body continues at rest or at constant velocity unless acted on by a RESULTANT force. Constant velocity therefore means the resultant force is ZERO — not that there is no force, but that the forces balance. Free body diagram for a car at constant speed: driving force forwards, equal and opposite drag and friction backwards; weight down, normal reaction up, equal. Correct version of the sentence: 'When an object moves at constant velocity, the resultant force acting on it is zero.' The underlying error is Aristotelian — the intuition that motion needs a cause to sustain it. Newton's insight is that force causes CHANGE in motion (acceleration), not motion itself, which is why an object in deep space keeps moving with nothing pushing it at all."},
  "MC-CS-01":{type:"trace",title:"Trace the Loop",instruction:"Trace through this loop for i = 1 to 5 and record the output. Then identify the off-by-one error and rewrite to print only values 2, 3, 4.",prompt:"FOR i ← 1 TO 5\n  IF i MOD 2 ≠ 0 THEN OUTPUT i\nENDFOR",answer:"Trace: i=1, 1 MOD 2 = 1 ≠ 0, output 1. i=2, 2 MOD 2 = 0, no output. i=3, output 3. i=4, no output. i=5, output 5. Output is 1, 3, 5 — the ODD values, because MOD 2 ≠ 0 is the test for odd. To print only 2, 3, 4 the range must change AND the condition must go: FOR i ← 2 TO 4 / OUTPUT i / ENDFOR. Two separate defects, and students usually fix only one: the loop bounds are off (1 TO 5 rather than 2 TO 4), and the condition filters by parity when no filter is wanted at all. Note that this FOR loop is inclusive of both bounds — the off-by-one that bites hardest in languages where the upper bound is exclusive."},
  "MC-MATH-01":{type:"correct",title:"Chain Rule Check",instruction:"A student differentiated y = sin(3x²) and wrote dy/dx = cos(3x²). Find the error and write the correct derivative showing all steps.",prompt:"Common error: differentiating the outer function only, not multiplying by the derivative of the inner function.",answer:"Chain rule: let u = 3x², so y = sin u. du/dx = 6x and dy/du = cos u. dy/dx = dy/du × du/dx = cos(3x²) × 6x = 6x cos(3x²). The student differentiated the OUTER function only and stopped. The check that catches it every time: if the bracket contains anything other than a bare x, its derivative must appear as a factor in the answer. Here the missing factor is 6x. Worth noting the special case that hides the error — for y = sin(x), the inner derivative is 1, so the shortcut appears to work, which is exactly how the habit forms."},
  "MC-DE-04":{type:"correct",title:"Word Order Fix",instruction:"Each sentence has a subordinate clause word-order error. Underline and rewrite correctly.",items:["Ich denke, dass er kommt morgen.","Sie sagt, obwohl sie ist müde.","Wir wissen, weil er hat das gemacht.","Er hofft, dass sie kommt nicht."],answer:"1: 'Ich denke, dass er morgen kommt.' 2: 'Sie sagt, obwohl sie müde ist.' 3: 'Wir wissen, weil er das gemacht hat.' 4: 'Er hofft, dass sie nicht kommt.' Rule: in a subordinate clause introduced by a subordinating conjunction (dass, weil, obwohl, wenn, ob), the FINITE verb goes to the END of the clause. In 3 the whole verb phrase reorders — the auxiliary 'hat' is the finite verb and goes last, after the participle 'gemacht'. In 4, 'nicht' sits immediately before the verb it negates, which is now at the end. The trap is that these are all correct as MAIN clauses; it is the conjunction that triggers the reordering, so the fix is always to spot the conjunction first."},
  "MC-RS-04":{type:"distinguish",title:"Which Problem of Evil?",instruction:"Label each argument as Logical PoE, Evidential PoE, or theodicy. Identify the philosopher and write one counter-argument for the theodicy.",items:["God and evil are logically incompatible (Mackie)","The quantity of suffering makes God's existence improbable (Rowe)","Evil is necessary for soul-making (Hick)","Evil entered through free will and original sin (Augustine)"],answer:"1 — LOGICAL problem of evil (Mackie): the inconsistent triad, holding that omnipotence, omnibenevolence and evil cannot all be true together. 2 — EVIDENTIAL (Rowe): evil does not disprove God but counts as strong evidence against, argued from the pointlessness of particular suffering such as the fawn dying in a forest fire. 3 — THEODICY (Hick, Irenaean soul-making): a world without suffering could not produce moral and spiritual growth, so evil serves a purpose. 4 — THEODICY (Augustine): evil is a privation of good, entering a perfect creation through the free choice of angels and humans; suffering is a just consequence of the Fall. Counter to Hick: the DISTRIBUTION and scale defeat the argument — suffering is not proportioned to spiritual benefit, those who die in infancy gain nothing, and animal suffering across evolutionary time serves no soul-making at all. Counter to Augustine: Schleiermacher's logical objection that a genuinely perfect world could not go wrong of itself, and the evolutionary evidence that there was never a perfect original state to fall from."},
  "MC-HSC-03":{type:"match",title:"Erikson Stage Match",instruction:"Match each behaviour to the correct Erikson stage and conflict. Write one consequence of failing to resolve each.",items:["A toddler insists on dressing themselves","A 6-year-old compares their reading to classmates","A teenager changes friendship group every term","A 40-year-old mentors junior colleagues","A 75-year-old writes their memoirs with pride"],answer:"Toddler dressing themselves — Autonomy vs Shame and Doubt (roughly 1–3); failure produces shame and doubt in one's own abilities. Six-year-old comparing reading to classmates — Industry vs Inferiority (roughly 6–11); failure produces a lasting sense of inferiority and incompetence. Teenager changing friendship groups each term — Identity vs Role Confusion (roughly 12–18); failure produces role confusion and an unstable sense of self. Forty-year-old mentoring juniors — Generativity vs Stagnation; failure produces stagnation and self-absorption. Seventy-five-year-old writing memoirs with pride — Integrity vs Despair; failure produces despair and regret at a life felt to be wasted. The structural point students miss: each stage is a CONFLICT resolved on a spectrum, not a pass or fail, and an unresolved stage is revisited rather than closed — which is why Erikson's model is lifespan and Freud's is not."},
  "MC-FR-03":{type:"trigger",title:"Subjunctive Triggers",instruction:"Highlight the trigger in each sentence and conjugate the verb correctly. Then write your own sentence using 'il faut que' with an irregular subjunctive.",items:["Je veux que tu (venir) avec moi.","Il est possible qu'elle (être) absente.","Nous partons avant qu'il (pleuvoir).","Bien qu'il (faire) froid, ils sont sortis."],answer:"Triggers and forms: 'Je veux que' (verb of will) → tu VIENNES. 'Il est possible que' (possibility) → elle SOIT. 'avant que' (conjunction) → il PLEUVE. 'Bien que' (concession) → il FASSE. Example with an irregular: 'Il faut que tu ailles à la banque' (aller → aille), or 'Il faut que je fasse mes devoirs' (faire → fasse). The four trigger families: will and desire (vouloir que, souhaiter que), doubt and possibility (il est possible que, je ne pense pas que), emotion (je suis content que, avoir peur que), and fixed conjunctions (avant que, bien que, pour que, à moins que, jusqu'à ce que). The two that catch students out are the ones that do NOT take it: 'après que' takes the indicative, since what has happened is not in doubt, and so does 'il est certain que' — certainty is the opposite of what the subjunctive is for."},
  "MC-RS-06":{type:"compare",title:"Bentham vs Mill",instruction:"Complete this comparison table. Then write two sentences evaluating where Mill's qualitative distinction creates a practical problem.",headers:["Feature","Bentham","Mill"],rows:[["Basis of judgement","",""],["How pleasures counted","",""],["Type of utilitarianism","",""],["Key criticism","",""]],answer:"Basis of judgement — Bentham: the QUANTITY of pleasure produced, measured by the hedonic calculus across its seven criteria (intensity, duration, certainty, propinquity, fecundity, purity, extent). Mill: quality as well as quantity, with higher pleasures counting for more. How pleasures counted — Bentham: all alike, 'the quantity of pleasure being equal, push-pin is as good as poetry'. Mill: higher (intellectual, moral, aesthetic) pleasures rank above lower (bodily) ones; 'better to be Socrates dissatisfied than a fool satisfied'. Type — Bentham: ACT utilitarian, calculating each situation afresh. Mill: RULE utilitarian, following rules that generally maximise utility. Key criticism — Bentham: permits atrocities against a minority whenever the majority gains, and reduces humans to what Mill's critics called a doctrine worthy of swine. Mill: the qualitative distinction is elitist and, worse, unmeasurable. The practical problem: Mill's test is the verdict of COMPETENT JUDGES who have experienced both kinds of pleasure — but competent judges disagree, which leaves no decision procedure at all, and ranking pleasures by quality appeals to a standard OTHER than utility, which concedes the utilitarian case."},
  "MC-HSC-04":{type:"rewrite",title:"Command Word Upgrade",instruction:"Rewrite this student answer at the 'assess' level. Question: 'Assess the effectiveness of person-centred care for an elderly patient with dementia.'",prompt:"Student: 'Person-centred care means putting the patient at the centre. It involves respecting their wishes and dignity. It is used in care homes across the UK.'",answer:"The student answer is pure description (AO1) — it defines the approach and says where it is used, but makes no judgement. 'Assess' requires a supported verdict weighing strengths against limitations. Model shape: STRENGTHS — preserves dignity and identity, which Kitwood argues is what dementia care most threatens; involving the person in decisions reduces distress and agitation, and is associated with lower antipsychotic prescribing; life-story work supports communication as verbal ability declines. LIMITATIONS — it is demanding of staff time and training, so is undermined by high ratios and turnover; fluctuating capacity makes 'the patient's wishes' hard to establish and inconsistent day to day; wishes can conflict directly with safety, as with a patient who wants to walk unaided and falls. JUDGEMENT — effective where staffing, training and continuity allow it to be delivered as designed, but in under-resourced settings it becomes a stated value rather than a practice, so effectiveness depends more on the conditions of delivery than on the model itself. That final move — a verdict with a condition attached — is what separates 'assess' from 'describe'."},
  "MC-FR-05":{type:"correct",title:"Si Clause Fix",instruction:"Find and correct the error in each conditional sentence. Write the rule in your own words below each.",items:["Si j'aurais de l'argent, j'achèterais une voiture.","Si tu viendrais, je serais content.","S'il pleuvrait, nous resterons à la maison.","Si j'aurais su, je n'aurais pas dit ça."],answer:"1: 'Si j'avais de l'argent, j'achèterais une voiture.' 2: 'Si tu venais, je serais content.' 3: 'S'il pleut, nous resterons à la maison.' 4: 'Si j'avais su, je n'aurais pas dit ça.' The rule in one line: the conditional NEVER goes in the si-clause. The three sequences: si + PRESENT → future (a real possibility); si + IMPERFECT → conditional (hypothetical); si + PLUPERFECT → conditional perfect (an unreal past). Sentence 4 is the one worth dwelling on, because 'si j'aurais su' is common in speech and always wrong in writing — the pluperfect 'j'avais su' carries the si-clause, and the conditional perfect 'je n'aurais pas dit' carries the consequence. English does the same thing and hides it: 'if I had known' takes the past, not 'would have'."},
  "MC-HIST-01":{type:"evaluate",title:"Doomed from the Start?",instruction:"A student claims the Weimar Republic was structurally doomed from 1919. Your job: write one paragraph SUPPORTING this view, then one paragraph CHALLENGING it. Each paragraph must cite at least two specific pieces of evidence.",prompt:"Support: Article 48 emergency powers, proportional representation producing unstable coalitions, stab-in-the-back myth undermining legitimacy from the outset, war guilt clause economic damage. Challenge: Republic survived hyperinflation (1923), Stresemann's golden years (1924–29) with GDP recovery, cultural flourishing, Dawes Plan stability. Common error: students list structural factors only and ignore the contingency of the Depression — Weimar was NOT doomed until 1929.",answer:"SUPPORT: the constitution carried Article 48, which allowed government by presidential decree and was used 60 times before 1930 and constantly after; proportional representation produced 21 coalitions in 14 years, none able to govern for long; the Republic was born of defeat and burdened by the stab-in-the-back myth and the war guilt clause, so it was blamed for a surrender it did not negotiate; and the army, judiciary and civil service were staffed by men loyal to the old order, visible in the derisory sentences after the Kapp and Munich putsches. CHALLENGE: the Republic SURVIVED its worst years — the 1923 hyperinflation, French occupation of the Ruhr, and risings from both left and right; Stresemann's period from 1924 to 1929 brought the Dawes Plan, the Locarno treaties, League membership, a stabilised currency and output recovering to pre-war levels; the extremist vote collapsed, with the NSDAP taking just 2.6% in May 1928; and Weimar culture flourished in the Bauhaus, cinema and Berlin's artistic life. JUDGEMENT: the strongest answers reject the word 'doomed'. Weimar had severe structural weaknesses, but structural weakness is a vulnerability, not a fate — it took the CONTINGENT shock of the Wall Street Crash, and the specific political choices of Papen, Schleicher and Hindenburg in 1932–33, to convert weakness into collapse. The mark is lost by listing structural factors and treating 1933 as inevitable from 1919."},
  "MC-HIST-02":{type:"chain",title:"Failure to Long-term Gain",instruction:"The Munich Putsch failed. Complete this chain showing how a military failure became a long-term political asset. Each arrow must name a specific consequence or action.",prompt:"16 November 1923: Putsch collapses, Hitler arrested → [step: trial and speech] → [step: Mein Kampf and national platform] → [step: strategic shift] → [outcome by 1933]. Common error: students say Hitler 'learned from his mistakes' without specifying the decision to pursue power through legal means — the key strategic conclusion from the trial.",answer:"16 November 1923: the putsch collapses and Hitler is arrested → the TRIAL (February–April 1924) before sympathetic Bavarian judges, who allow him to speak at length; the national press carries his defence, and a treason conviction brings five years of which he serves nine months in comfort at Landsberg → MEIN KAMPF, written in that imprisonment, sets out Lebensraum, antisemitism and the Führer principle, and the trial has already made him a national rather than Bavarian figure → the STRATEGIC SHIFT to legality: power is to be won through the ballot box, not the street, so the NSDAP is refounded in February 1925, Hitler's leadership is settled against the Strasser wing at Bamberg in 1926, and the party builds a national organisation of Gaue, youth and professional bodies → by 1930 the machine is in place to convert Depression discontent into votes: 18.3% in 1930, 37.3% in July 1932, and the appointment as Chancellor on 30 January 1933. The step students leave vague is the third. 'He learned from his mistakes' earns nothing; the specific conclusion was that the state could not be overthrown by force while the army stayed loyal, so it would have to be captured from within — as Hitler put it, holding one's nose and entering the Reichstag."},
  "MC-HIST-03":{type:"correct",title:"Did Germans Vote Hitler into Power?",instruction:"Find and correct the two factual errors in this student sentence. Then add one sentence that explains how Hitler actually became Chancellor.",prompt:"'Hitler became Chancellor in January 1933 because the Nazi Party won over 50% of the vote in the November 1932 election, giving them a democratic mandate.'",answer:"Error 1: Nazi vote share in November 1932 was 33.1% — their vote had FALLEN from 37.4% in July 1932. Error 2: Hitler was not elected Chancellor — he was appointed by President Hindenburg on 30 January 1933 after backroom deals involving von Papen, who believed he could control Hitler. Correct sentence: Hitler became Chancellor through elite miscalculation, not popular mandate."},
  "MC-HIST-05":{type:"compare",title:"Browning vs Goldhagen",instruction:"Complete this table comparing the two historians' arguments. Then write a two-sentence judgement: which interpretation has stronger evidential support and why?",headers:["Feature","Browning (Ordinary Men)","Goldhagen (Hitler's Willing Executioners)"],rows:[["Primary cause of killing","",""],["Evidence used","Battalion 101 testimonies",""],["Nature of perpetrators","Ordinary, pressured",""],["Role of ideology","Secondary",""],["Main criticism","","Monocausal, ignores peer pressure"]],answer:"Browning — primary cause: SITUATIONAL. Conformity to the group, deference to authority, careerism, and brutalisation by the task itself; ideology matters but is not the driver. Goldhagen — primary cause: ELIMINATIONIST ANTISEMITISM, a distinctively German culture of belief that made ordinary Germans willing, even eager, killers. Evidence — Browning: the post-war interrogations of Reserve Police Battalion 101, around 125 testimonies. Goldhagen: the SAME battalion records, plus other police battalions, the death marches and work camps, with weight placed on gratuitous cruelty and photographs taken by perpetrators. Nature of perpetrators — Goldhagen: willing, ideologically motivated, acting on beliefs they already held. Role of ideology — Goldhagen: primary and sufficient. Main criticism — Browning: underplays antisemitism, and relies on testimony given under legal jeopardy decades later, when minimising one's role was in the witness's interest. JUDGEMENT: Browning's case has the stronger evidential support. Both men read the same files and Browning's reading accounts for the VARIATION in behaviour — at Józefów, Major Trapp offered an opt-out and only 10 to 20 per cent took it, a spread that a uniform national antisemitism cannot explain. Goldhagen's thesis also struggles with the substantial non-German participation in the killing and with the swift postwar disappearance of the eliminationist culture he describes as deep-rooted. The concession worth making is Goldhagen's: Browning does leave the antisemitic saturation of these men's upbringing curiously light."},
  "MC-HIST-06":{type:"chain",title:"Truman Doctrine Transmission",instruction:"The Truman Doctrine (1947) marked a permanent shift in US foreign policy. Complete this chain — each link must be a specific claim, not a vague assertion.",prompt:"Soviet pressure on Greece and Turkey (1946–47) → [step: British withdrawal] → [step: Truman's speech to Congress, March 1947] → [step: the doctrine's key commitment] → [consequence for US foreign policy 1947–1989]. Common error: students describe the Doctrine as 'helping Greece' without stating the universal principle — that the US would support free peoples resisting communist subjugation anywhere in the world. This is what made it a doctrine, not just an aid package.",answer:"Soviet pressure on Greece and Turkey, 1946–47 → BRITISH WITHDRAWAL: on 21 February 1947 an effectively bankrupt Britain informs Washington it can no longer fund the Greek government or Turkish defence, leaving a vacuum the US must fill or concede → TRUMAN'S SPEECH to Congress, 12 March 1947, requesting $400 million and framing the request not as aid but as a choice between two ways of life → THE COMMITMENT: 'it must be the policy of the United States to support free peoples who are resisting attempted subjugation by armed minorities or by outside pressures' — universal, open-ended, and geographically unbounded → the framework of CONTAINMENT for the next four decades: the Marshall Plan in 1947, NATO in 1949, Korea in 1950, and ultimately Vietnam, each justified by the same principle. The distinction that earns the marks: an aid package helps one country, a DOCTRINE commits you everywhere. Kennan, whose Long Telegram supplied the analysis, spent years objecting that Truman had turned a case for selective resistance at points of industrial importance into a blank cheque — which is precisely why the speech, not the money, is the turning point."},
  "MC-HIST-07":{type:"evaluate",title:"Berlin Blockade — Who Won?",instruction:"Write two sentences: one arguing the Blockade was a Soviet victory, one arguing it was a Western victory. Then write a third sentence giving your overall judgement with one piece of supporting evidence.",prompt:"Soviet case: forced Allies to concede German partition was permanent; demonstrated Western vulnerability in Berlin. Western case: airlift succeeded (277,000 flights, 2.3m tonnes), Soviet capitulation in May 1949, NATO formed April 1949, FRG founded May 1949. Common error: students say Soviets 'gave up' without noting the political consequences — the crisis accelerated exactly the Western alliance the Soviets sought to prevent.",answer:"SOVIET CASE: the crisis confirmed that Germany's division was permanent and workable on Soviet terms in the east, and it exposed the Allies' physical vulnerability in a city 100 miles inside the Soviet zone, which remained a pressure point until 1961. WESTERN CASE: the airlift worked — some 277,000 flights delivering 2.3 million tonnes — and Stalin lifted the blockade on 12 May 1949 having gained nothing he sought; the West held Berlin without firing on anyone, and won the propaganda contest decisively across Europe. JUDGEMENT: a Western victory, and the strongest evidence is what the crisis CREATED rather than what it prevented. NATO was signed in April 1949 and the Federal Republic founded in May, both accelerated by the blockade — Stalin's attempt to stop a consolidated, Western-aligned German state produced exactly that state, bound into a permanent American military alliance. The nuance worth adding: the outcome the Soviets got, a divided Germany with a secure eastern half, was one they could live with, so 'defeat' overstates it. What they lost was the chance of influence over the whole."},
  "MC-HIST-08":{type:"correct",title:"Cuban Missile Crisis Resolution",instruction:"A student wrote: 'Kennedy stood firm and forced Khrushchev to back down.' Find three problems with this account and rewrite it accurately in three sentences.",prompt:"Problems: (1) 'Stood firm' ignores the secret deal — removal of Jupiter missiles from Turkey was a real US concession; (2) 'forced Khrushchev' implies Soviet defeat — both sides compromised; (3) ignores the public pledge not to invade Cuba. The crisis is better described as a mutual face-saving compromise than a US victory.",answer:"Three problems: (1) 'Stood firm' conceals a real concession — the US secretly agreed to withdraw the Jupiter missiles from Turkey, and the secrecy is what allowed the compromise to be presented as a Soviet climbdown. (2) 'Forced Khrushchev to back down' misreads a negotiated settlement as a defeat; both leaders were seeking a way out that avoided war, and the Ex-Comm record shows Kennedy resisting the Joint Chiefs' pressure for air strikes. (3) It omits the public American pledge not to invade Cuba — which secured Khrushchev's original stated objective, since Castro's regime survived. Accurate rewrite in three sentences: 'In October 1962 Kennedy imposed a naval quarantine on Cuba and demanded the removal of Soviet missiles, bringing the superpowers close to war. The crisis was settled by a compromise: Khrushchev withdrew the missiles in exchange for a public US pledge not to invade Cuba and a secret undertaking to remove American Jupiter missiles from Turkey. Because the Turkish element stayed secret, the outcome appeared at the time to be a one-sided American victory, and it was long taught as one.'"},
  "MC-HIST-09":{type:"sort",title:"Détente or Containment?",instruction:"Sort each policy/event as primarily 'détente' (reducing Cold War tensions) or 'containment' (resisting Soviet expansion). Two are genuinely ambiguous — be ready to argue both ways.",items:["SALT I (1972)","Nixon visits China (1972)","Helsinki Accords (1975)","Carter boycotts Moscow Olympics (1980)","US arms mujahideen in Afghanistan","Ford refuses to meet Solzhenitsyn","Jackson-Vanik Amendment (1974)","Reagan 'Evil Empire' speech (1983)"],answer:"DÉTENTE: SALT I (1972), the Helsinki Accords (1975), and Ford's refusal to meet Solzhenitsyn — a deliberate avoidance of provoking Moscow. CONTAINMENT: the Moscow Olympics boycott (1980), arming the mujahideen in Afghanistan, and Reagan's 'evil empire' speech (1983). The two genuinely ambiguous: NIXON IN CHINA is détente in form — the first presidential visit, reduced tension, a new diplomatic channel — but containment in purpose, since triangular diplomacy was designed to exploit the Sino-Soviet split and increase pressure on Moscow. The JACKSON-VANIK AMENDMENT is the sharper case: it used the machinery of détente, trade normalisation, as leverage on Soviet Jewish emigration, and in doing so helped kill the trade agreement it was attached to — détente's instruments turned to containment's ends. The lesson the sort is for: détente was never the ABANDONMENT of containment. It was containment pursued by negotiation rather than confrontation, which is why the two lists shade into each other and why the 1970s can be narrated either way."},
  "MC-HIST-12":{type:"chain",title:"Why did the USSR Collapse?",instruction:"Historians disagree about the relative weight of internal vs external causes. Complete both chains, then write one sentence judging which was more important.",prompt:"Internal chain: Command economy stagnation → [step] → [step] → [outcome: 1991]. External chain: Arms race cost → [step: Afghanistan] → [step] → [outcome: 1991]. Common error: students attribute collapse solely to Gorbachev's reforms or solely to Reagan's arms race pressure — examiners credit multi-causal analysis that identifies the interaction between economic weakness and political reform.",answer:"INTERNAL: command economy stagnation, with growth falling towards zero by the early 1980s and chronic consumer shortages → the 1986 collapse in oil prices removes the hard currency that had masked the problem, and Chernobyl exposes the cost of systemic secrecy → glasnost and perestroika, intended to save the system, instead licence criticism the Party cannot contain and half-reform an economy into shortage → nationalist movements in the Baltics and Caucasus, the failed August 1991 coup, and dissolution in December 1991. EXTERNAL: the arms race consuming an estimated 15–25% of Soviet GDP against roughly 6% of American → Afghanistan from 1979, nine years, some 15,000 Soviet dead, and a demonstration that Soviet power could be resisted → Reagan's build-up and SDI raising the cost of competition, while Gorbachev's decision not to intervene in Eastern Europe lets the outer empire go in 1989 → 1991. JUDGEMENT: the internal chain carries more weight. Economic stagnation was the reason reform was attempted at all, and it long predates Reagan; external pressure raised the price of the status quo and shortened the timetable, but did not create the weakness. The answer to avoid is the single-cause one in either direction — that Gorbachev destroyed it, or that Reagan spent it into the ground. What examiners credit is the INTERACTION: a stagnant economy made reform necessary, and reform in a system that permitted no criticism proved impossible to control."},
  "MC-HIST-13":{type:"evaluate",title:"Korea — Limited War or Global Crisis?",instruction:"Write one sentence arguing Korea showed Cold War restraint, and one sentence arguing it nearly became World War Three. Then add a judgement: which interpretation better fits the evidence?",prompt:"Restraint case: both superpowers avoided direct confrontation, MacArthur dismissed for wanting to escalate, armistice restored original boundary — war ended where it started. Escalation risk case: MacArthur pushed to Yalu River triggering Chinese intervention (300,000 troops), nuclear weapons considered, Truman declared national emergency. Key judgement: the war demonstrated that both sides feared escalation — limited war became the Cold War norm precisely because Korea showed the alternative.",answer:"RESTRAINT: neither superpower attacked the other directly — Soviet pilots flew in Korean markings and both sides pretended not to notice — Truman dismissed MacArthur in April 1951 precisely for demanding escalation, and the 1953 armistice restored a border close to the 38th parallel where the war began. ESCALATION RISK: MacArthur's advance to the Yalu brought roughly 300,000 Chinese troops into the war, turning a limited intervention into a major land conflict; the use of nuclear weapons was seriously considered and publicly hinted at by Truman in November 1950; and a national emergency was declared in December 1950. JUDGEMENT: both are true, and the sharper reading is that they are the same fact seen twice. Korea came close enough to general war to teach both sides what the alternative to limitation looked like — and it was that lesson, learned in real time, that made LIMITED war the Cold War norm. MacArthur's dismissal is the strongest single piece of evidence: an American president sacked his most popular general rather than widen the conflict, which shows restraint was a deliberate and costly choice, not an accident of circumstance."},

  // ---- GCSE Geography — coasts, rivers, hazards, ecosystems, energy ----
  "MC-COAST-01":{type:"classify",title:"Which Erosion Process?",instruction:"Name the erosion process in each description. Two are commonly swapped — be ready to say which and why.",items:["Waves compress air into a crack until the rock shatters","Pebbles carried by waves scrape and grind the cliff face","Rocks knock against each other and become smaller and rounder","Seawater dissolves the calcium carbonate in limestone"],answer:"1 Hydraulic action, 2 Abrasion, 3 Attrition, 4 Solution. Hydraulic action and abrasion are the pair students swap: hydraulic action needs no rock-on-rock contact, which is exactly why it works so well on Holderness's soft boulder clay."},
  "MC-COAST-02":{type:"correct",title:"Why Does the Headland Stick Out?",instruction:"Find the error in this student sentence and rewrite it in one accurate sentence.",prompt:"'Headlands form because the hard rock grows outwards faster than the soft rock around it.'",answer:"Nothing grows. Differential erosion: the clay is eroded quickly to form a bay, while the chalk resists and is left protruding. It is the difference in the RATE OF EROSION, not a difference in rate of formation."},
  "MC-COAST-03":{type:"sequence",title:"Fill the Missing Stage",instruction:"A student wrote the headland sequence as: crack → arch → stack → stump. Insert the missing stage in the right place, then write one sentence for each arrow explaining what the waves are actually doing.",answer:"crack → CAVE → arch → stack → stump. Waves widen a weakness into a cave; erosion cuts through the headland to form an arch; the arch roof collapses leaving an isolated stack; the stack is undercut and eroded down to a stump."},
  "MC-COAST-04":{type:"diagram",title:"Draw the Zigzag",instruction:"Draw a beach with waves approaching at an angle. Add arrows for swash and backwash, then mark the net direction of sediment movement. Finally, mark where a spit would form and explain why it forms there and not elsewhere.",prompt:"Common error: students draw backwash returning at the same angle as swash. Backwash returns straight down the beach under gravity — that is what makes the movement a zigzag rather than a straight line.",answer:"The diagram must show SWASH running up the beach at the ANGLE of wave approach, set by the prevailing wind, and BACKWASH returning STRAIGHT down the beach, perpendicular to the shoreline, under gravity. That asymmetry is the whole mechanism: sediment is carried diagonally up and dragged straight back, so each wave moves it a short distance along the coast, producing a zigzag net transport known as longshore drift. Drawing backwash returning at the same angle as the swash produces no net movement at all — the sediment would simply return to where it started, and there would be no spits, bars or need for groynes. A SPIT forms where the coastline changes direction abruptly or at a river mouth: drift carries sediment on into open water, wave energy drops, and deposition builds a ridge out from the headland. The distal end is usually recurved by wave refraction or a secondary wind direction, and the sheltered water behind it accumulates silt and develops salt marsh — which is why spits are drawn with a hook, not as a straight finger."},
  "MC-COAST-05":{type:"evaluate",title:"Solved for Whom?",instruction:"A student says the Mappleton groynes 'solved erosion for the Holderness coast'. Write one sentence on who benefits, one on who loses, and a judgement on whether 'solved' is the right word.",prompt:"Mappleton: rock groynes plus revetment protect the village and the B1242 coast road.",answer:"Groynes trap sediment moving by longshore drift, so the protected stretch keeps its beach — but settlements downdrift (e.g. Great Cowden) lose their sediment supply and erode faster. The problem is displaced along the coast, not removed. 'Managed' is defensible; 'solved' is not."},
  "MC-COAST-06":{type:"distinguish",title:"Storm Beach or Summer Beach?",instruction:"For each statement, decide whether it describes constructive or destructive waves, then explain in one sentence why a beach can be wide in summer and narrow after a winter storm.",items:["Strong backwash drags material offshore","Swash is stronger than backwash, building the beach up","Low frequency, long wavelength, spilling","High frequency, steep, plunging"],answer:"1 destructive, 2 constructive, 3 constructive, 4 destructive. Storm waves move sediment offshore into bars; calmer constructive waves return it, so a narrow post-storm beach is not necessarily permanent loss."},
  "MC-RIV-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Where Does the River Erode?",instruction:"For each part of the long profile, state whether vertical erosion, lateral erosion or deposition dominates — and give the landform that results.",items:["Upper course, steep gradient, high energy","Middle course, gentler gradient","Lower course, very low gradient, wide floodplain"],answer:"Upper: vertical erosion → V-shaped valley, waterfalls, interlocking spurs. Middle: lateral erosion → meanders. Lower: deposition → floodplain, levees, delta. The error to correct is 'erosion happens everywhere' — energy and gradient fall downstream, so deposition takes over."},
  "MC-RIV-02":{type:"diagram",title:"Cross-Section of a Meander",instruction:"Draw a cross-section through a meander. Label the river cliff, the slip-off slope, the deepest water and the fastest flow. Add a curved arrow showing helicoidal flow, then write one sentence linking the flow to the asymmetry.",prompt:"Common error: students say the outside bend erodes 'because the water hits it'. That describes the result, not the mechanism.",answer:"Helicoidal (corkscrew) flow carries the fastest current to the outside bend, undercutting it into a river cliff, then sweeps eroded material across the bed and deposits it on the slower inside bend as a slip-off slope."},
  "MC-RIV-03":{type:"chain",title:"Why Tewkesbury?",instruction:"Complete the chain. Each link must be a specific physical or locational factor, not 'it rained a lot'.",prompt:"Wettest summer on record (2007) → [step: antecedent conditions] → [step: what the confluence does] → thousands of homes flooded and the town cut off.",answer:"Prior rainfall had already saturated the ground, so infiltration was minimal and surface runoff was rapid → Tewkesbury sits at the confluence of the Severn and the Avon, so two flood peaks arrive and combine at the same place → discharge exceeded channel capacity. Rainfall alone does not explain why Tewkesbury specifically flooded."},
  "MC-HAZ-01":{type:"correct",title:"Pinatubo — Boundary and Effect",instruction:"A student wrote the sentence below. Find both errors and write the correct version, including one measurable global effect.",prompt:"'Pinatubo erupted on a constructive boundary in 1991, and its effects were limited to the Philippines.'",answer:"Error 1: Pinatubo sits on a DESTRUCTIVE (subduction) boundary. Error 2: the effects were global — sulfur dioxide injected into the stratosphere cooled global average temperatures by roughly 0.5°C for about two years."},
  "MC-HAZ-02":{type:"evaluate",title:"Big Impact, No Deaths?",instruction:"Eyjafjallajökull killed almost nobody but is studied as a major hazard event. Write two sentences justifying its inclusion, then state the general principle in one sentence.",prompt:"2010: over 100,000 flights cancelled across European airspace.",answer:"The eruption was small (far below Pinatubo's VEI 6) and Iceland is sparsely populated near the volcano, so direct deaths were minimal — but ash grounded European aviation for days, stranding passengers and disrupting freight and perishable exports worldwide. Principle: hazard impact is measured in economic and social disruption, not only in deaths."},
  "MC-HAZ-03":{type:"correct",title:"What Actually Flooded New Orleans?",instruction:"Find the error and rewrite accurately in two sentences.",prompt:"'New Orleans flooded because Katrina's Category 5 winds were strong enough to destroy the city.'",answer:"Katrina had weakened to a strong Category 3 by landfall. The flooding was caused by storm surge overwhelming and breaching the levees in a city much of which lies below sea level — a defence failure, not wind damage."},
  "MC-HAZ-04":{type:"distinguish",title:"Wind or Water?",instruction:"Haiyan had among the highest landfall wind speeds ever recorded, yet most of the roughly 6,300 deaths had a different cause. Name it, then write one sentence explaining why the same killer appears in both Haiyan and Katrina.",answer:"Storm surge. In both events the sea, driven ashore by low pressure and wind, caused most deaths — Tacloban's surge reached over 5m. Wind destroys buildings; water drowns people. The difference in death toll between Haiyan and Katrina reflects preparedness and income level, not the physics."},
  "MC-MGT-01":{type:"sort",title:"Monitor, Predict, Protect or Plan?",instruction:"Sort each action into one of the four hazard management strategies. Two could be argued into a second category — be ready to justify.",items:["Seismometers recording tremors in real time","Earthquake-resistant buildings with cross-bracing","Annual evacuation drills in schools","Forecasting where a hurricane will make landfall","Tsunami warning sirens along a coast","Zoning laws banning building on a floodplain"],answer:"Monitor: seismometers, warning sirens (arguably protect). Predict: hurricane landfall forecasting. Protect: earthquake-resistant buildings. Plan: drills, zoning laws. The distinction students miss is monitoring (tracking what is happening now) versus prediction (forecasting what will happen)."},
  "MC-GEO-BIO-01":{type:"chain",title:"Climate to Biodiversity",instruction:"Complete the chain, then use it to explain why tropical rainforest and desert sit at opposite ends of the biodiversity range.",prompt:"Temperature and precipitation → [step] → [step] → level of biodiversity.",answer:"Climate determines growing season length → growing season determines net primary productivity (plant growth) → plant growth determines the food and habitat available, and so biodiversity. Hot and wet all year = long growing season = high biodiversity; hot and dry = short or absent growing season = low biodiversity."},
  "MC-GEO-BIO-05":{type:"sort",title:"Four Kinds of Ecosystem Service",instruction:"Sort each into provisioning, regulating, cultural or supporting. One is regularly misfiled — find it.",items:["Timber for construction","Forests absorbing CO2 and moderating climate","A woodland used for recreation and wellbeing","Wetlands filtering pollutants from water","Nutrient cycling in soil","Medicinal plants"],answer:"Provisioning (goods): timber, medicinal plants. Regulating (processes): carbon absorption, water filtration. Cultural: recreation. Supporting: nutrient cycling. The commonly misfiled one is water filtration — students call it provisioning because it involves water, but the service is the PROCESS, not the resource."},
  "MC-GEO-BIO-08":{type:"correct",title:"Deforestation and the Carbon Cycle",instruction:"A student wrote the sentence below. It states a real effect but misses the direct one. Add the missing mechanism and explain which is larger.",prompt:"'Deforestation affects the carbon cycle because fewer trees means less oxygen is produced.'",answer:"The direct effect is on CARBON, not oxygen: felling removes a carbon sink (trees can no longer absorb CO2 by photosynthesis) AND, if the timber is burned or left to rot, releases stored carbon back to the atmosphere. So deforestation both removes future absorption and creates an immediate emission."},
  "MC-GEO-BIO-11":{type:"compare",title:"Malthus vs Boserup",instruction:"Complete the table, then write one sentence saying which theory the Green Revolution supports and why.",headers:["Feature","Malthus","Boserup"],rows:[["View of population growth","",""],["What happens when resources run short","",""],["Role of technology","",""],["Overall outlook","",""]],answer:"Malthus: population grows geometrically while food grows arithmetically; shortage causes checks (famine, disease, war); technology is not assumed; pessimistic. Boserup: population growth is the driver of innovation; shortage causes necessity-led invention; technology expands the ceiling; optimistic. The Green Revolution supports Boserup — rising demand produced higher-yielding crops rather than mass famine."},
  "MC-FOR-02":{type:"match",title:"Adaptation to Function",instruction:"Match each rainforest adaptation to the problem it solves. Then name one adaptation that solves a problem NOT on this list.",items:["Drip tips on leaves","Buttress roots","Lianas","Waxy leaf surface","Shallow root systems"],answer:"Drip tips — shed heavy rain fast, preventing mould and leaf damage. Buttress roots — provide support because nutrients (and so roots) stay shallow. Lianas — reach the canopy light without building a trunk. Waxy surface — repels water. Shallow roots — capture nutrients from the thin topsoil where they are concentrated. The key link students miss: shallow roots are WHY buttresses are needed."},
  "MC-FOR-04":{type:"chain",title:"Keystone Collapse",instruction:"Complete the chain showing how a dam far from the forest changes the forest itself. Each arrow must be a specific transfer.",prompt:"Dam blocks salmon migration → [step] → [step] → [step] → forest nutrient levels fall.",answer:"Salmon cannot reach upstream spawning grounds → bears and wolves lose a key seasonal food source, so populations fall or move → fewer salmon carcasses are carried into the forest and left to decompose → the marine-derived nitrogen they deliver to soils is lost. This is what 'disproportionate to their numbers' means — salmon are a nutrient pump between ocean and forest."},
  "MC-FOR-11":{type:"evaluate",title:"Does REDD Work?",instruction:"Write one sentence explaining what REDD actually pays for, then one strength and one weakness. Finish with a judgement on whether it reduces global emissions or relocates them.",answer:"Developed countries and TNCs fund forest protection in developing countries to offset their own emissions. Strength: channels money to conservation and can involve local communities. Weaknesses: no agreed definition of 'forest' (plantations can count), leakage (logging moves to an unprotected area), and it permits continued emissions at source. Judgement: it can reduce deforestation locally while leaving the emitter's behaviour unchanged."},
  "MC-FOR-12":{type:"distinguish",title:"Is This Sustainable Forestry?",instruction:"For each practice, decide sustainable or not, and give the reason in six words or fewer.",items:["Replanting two trees for every one felled","Clear-felling a block then moving on","Selective logging of mature trees only","Replacing natural taiga with a single-species plantation"],answer:"1 Sustainable — replacement exceeds removal. 2 Not — no replacement, habitat destroyed. 3 Sustainable — canopy and habitat retained. 4 Not — biodiversity lost even though tree cover returns. The test is whether the FOREST persists, not whether trees do."},
  "MC-ENE-01":{type:"sort",title:"Renewable, Non-Renewable or Recyclable?",instruction:"Sort each source. One category surprises most students — identify it and explain why it exists.",items:["Natural gas","Wind","Biofuel","Coal","Geothermal","Nuclear","Tidal","Oil"],answer:"Non-renewable: gas, coal, oil. Renewable: wind, geothermal, tidal. Recyclable: biofuel, nuclear. The surprise is 'recyclable' — biofuel can be regrown and nuclear fuel can be reprocessed, so neither is strictly finite nor strictly infinite. Calling nuclear 'renewable' is the error to correct."},
  "MC-ENE-03":{type:"explain",title:"Coal in the Ground, Unused",instruction:"The UK has large coal reserves that are barely touched. Give one physical, one economic and one political reason. Then say which is decisive and why.",answer:"Physical: remaining seams are deep, thin and expensive to reach. Economic: imported coal and gas have been cheaper than domestic deep-mined coal. Political: climate commitments and the phase-out of coal-fired generation removed the market. Decisive: political — the demand was legislated away, so no price would justify extraction."},
  "MC-ENE-07":{type:"distinguish",title:"What Makes It 'Unconventional'?",instruction:"Write a one-sentence definition of 'unconventional', then complete the table for tar sands and shale gas.",headers:["Feature","Tar sands","Shale gas"],rows:[["Where it is found","",""],["Extraction method","",""],["Main environmental cost","",""]],answer:"Unconventional = the fuel cannot be extracted by simply drilling and pumping; it needs additional processing or stimulation. Tar sands: bitumen-rich sands (Athabasca, Canada), surface mining and steam treatment, huge water use and tailings ponds — nearly 60% of Canada's oil output. Shale gas: gas trapped in shale rock, hydraulic fracturing, groundwater contamination and induced seismicity."},
  "MC-ENE-09":{type:"chain",title:"Energy Gap to Insecurity",instruction:"Define an energy gap in one sentence, then complete the chain to show why it is a security problem and not just an accounting one.",prompt:"Domestic demand exceeds domestic supply → [step] → [step] → energy security falls.",answer:"The shortfall must be imported → the country depends on other states, or on a single supplier or route, for a necessity → supply can be interrupted or repriced for reasons outside its control (conflict, sanctions, market shocks). Security means uninterrupted, affordable and accessible supply — an import-dependent country can fail on any of the three."},
  "MC-RVF-01":{type:"correct",title:"Write a Bradshaw Hypothesis",instruction:"Using Bradshaw's model, write a hypothesis testable across the three Darent sites (Westerham → Park Farm → Lullingstone). Then state which variables should DECREASE downstream.",prompt:"Weak version: 'The river changes downstream.' Why does this score nothing?",answer:"A usable hypothesis is directional and measurable, e.g. 'Discharge increases with distance downstream from Westerham.' Increasing downstream: width, depth, velocity, discharge, load quantity. Decreasing: gradient and load particle size. The weak version cannot be proved false, so it cannot be tested."},
  "MC-RVF-04":{type:"explain",title:"Why Three Velocity Readings?",instruction:"Students time a float at the left bank, centre and right bank. Explain why the readings differ, then explain why they repeat each three times.",answer:"Friction with the bed and banks slows the water at the edges, so velocity peaks in the centre (the thalweg). Three positions capture that variation instead of over-reporting one point; three repeats at each position allow a mean that reduces the effect of one-off errors — an eddy, a snagged float, or slow reaction time on the stopwatch."},
  "MC-RVF-05":{type:"calculate",title:"Discharge — Show the Method",instruction:"Cross-sectional area 1.8 m², mean velocity 0.4 m/s. Calculate discharge, writing both formulas and the substitution. Then state the units and explain why method marks are lost more often than the answer.",answer:"Cross-sectional area = width × mean depth. Discharge = area × velocity = 1.8 × 0.4 = 0.72 m³/s. Students who write only '0.72' lose the method marks; those who omit or invent units lose more. The unit is cubic metres per second — volume passing a point per second."},

  // ---- GCSE Geography — development, India, UK human, urbanisation, enquiry ----
  "MC-DEV-01":{type:"compare",title:"Why Not Just Use GDP?",instruction:"Complete the table, then name one thing HDI still fails to capture.",headers:["Measure","What it includes","What it misses"],rows:[["GDP per capita","",""],["HDI","",""]],answer:"GDP per capita: average economic output per person; misses health, education, distribution, informal economy, environmental cost. HDI: life expectancy (health), mean/expected years of schooling (education) and GNI per capita (economic), scored 0–1; still misses inequality, political freedom, gender disparity and environmental quality. Development is multi-strand, so a single economic indicator gives a partial picture."},
  "MC-DEV-03":{type:"classify",title:"Developing, Emerging or Developed?",instruction:"Classify each country description using Edexcel B's three categories, then state the single feature that most clearly separates emerging from developing.",items:["Low human development, inadequate services, subsistence agriculture dominant","Rapid industrial-led growth, rising incomes, widening internal inequality","High human development, service-dominated economy, ageing population"],answer:"1 Developing, 2 Emerging, 3 Developed. The separating feature is the RATE and SOURCE of change: emerging economies are growing rapidly on the back of industrialisation, which is why their inequality often widens as growth concentrates in urban cores."},
  "MC-DEV-04":{type:"sort",title:"Sort the Causes of Inequality",instruction:"Sort each cause into environmental, historical or political/economic. One belongs in two categories — argue for it.",items:["Colonial extraction of raw materials","Landlocked position with no port access","Trade rules favouring processed over raw exports","Endemic malaria reducing workforce productivity","Corruption and weak governance","Neo-colonial debt repayment"],answer:"Environmental: landlocked position, malaria. Historical: colonialism, neo-colonial debt. Political/economic: trade rules, governance. Trade rules are the arguable one — they are a present-day economic structure with an explicitly historical origin."},
  "MC-DEV-06":{type:"sequence",title:"Rostow's Five Stages",instruction:"Put the stages in order and give one feature of each. Then write the single strongest criticism of the model.",items:["Age of mass consumption","Traditional society","Drive to maturity","Pre-conditions for take-off","Take-off"],answer:"1 Traditional society (subsistence, barter) → 2 Pre-conditions (surpluses traded, infrastructure) → 3 Take-off (industrial and regional growth) → 4 Drive to maturity (technology spreads across the economy) → 5 Mass consumption. Criticism: it is Eurocentric and assumes every country can follow the path the West took, ignoring that colonised economies started from extraction, not a blank slate."},
  "MC-DEV-07":{type:"diagram",title:"Draw the Core and Periphery",instruction:"Draw two boxes labelled core and periphery. Add arrows for what flows each way, labelling each arrow with a specific good or payment. Then write one sentence on why Frank says the relationship keeps the periphery poor.",answer:"Periphery → core: coffee, cocoa, sugar, minerals — low-value raw materials. Core → periphery: manufactured goods, loans, and the profits repatriated by TNCs. Frank's claim is that the periphery is not 'undeveloped' but ACTIVELY UNDERDEVELOPED — the terms of trade transfer value to the core, so participation in the system entrenches the gap rather than closing it."},
  "MC-DEV-08":{type:"sort",title:"Top-Down or Bottom-Up?",instruction:"Sort each scheme, then write one sentence on the trade-off between the two approaches.",items:["A World Bank-funded hydroelectric dam","An NGO distributing low-cost water filters","A new international airport","Village-level microfinance loans","A national motorway programme"],answer:"Top-down: dam, airport, motorway. Bottom-up: water filters, microfinance. Trade-off: top-down delivers scale and national infrastructure but is expensive, imposed, and often displaces people; bottom-up is cheap, appropriate and locally owned but rarely transforms a national economy."},
  "MC-DEV-14":{type:"distinguish",title:"Which DTM Stage?",instruction:"For each description, give the DTM stage. Then state exactly what is happening to the GAP between birth and death rates in stage 3.",items:["High birth rate, high death rate, low total population","Death rate falling fast, birth rate still high","Birth rate falling rapidly, death rate low and falling slowly","Both rates low, population stable","Birth rate below death rate, population declining"],answer:"1, 2, 3, 4, 5. In stage 3 the gap is NARROWING — population still grows, but at a decelerating rate. Students routinely say population falls in stage 3; it does not, it grows more slowly."},
  "MC-DEV-15":{type:"diagnose",title:"Read the Pyramid",instruction:"A pyramid has a very narrow base and a wide top. State the DTM stage, name a country that fits, and list two consequences the government must plan for. Then say what a very WIDE base would have indicated instead.",answer:"Narrow base, wide top = stage 5 (e.g. Japan) — ageing population, low fertility. Consequences: rising dependency ratio, pension and healthcare costs, shrinking workforce. A very wide concave base = stage 2 (e.g. Niger) — youthful, high birth and death rates, pressure on schools rather than pensions."},
  "MC-DEV-16":{type:"calculate",title:"Dependency Ratio — Show the Working",instruction:"30 million young dependents, 10 million old dependents, 80 million of working age. Write the formula, substitute, and state what the answer MEANS in a sentence. Then say whether a ratio of 50 is high or low, and for whom that matters.",answer:"(young + old) ÷ working population × 100 = (30 + 10) ÷ 80 × 100 = 50. Fifty dependents for every hundred workers. That is comparatively low — a demographic dividend. High ratios (over 100) mean each worker supports more than one dependent, straining tax revenue, pensions and services."},
  "MC-IND-01":{type:"correct",title:"India by the Numbers",instruction:"Fix the three errors in this student sentence, then add the one statistic that best captures the gap between India's total size and its individual living standards.",prompt:"'India is the largest country in the world by area, has the largest population, and its high GDP per capita makes it a developed country.'",answer:"Seventh largest by AREA; second largest by POPULATION (1.3+ billion); fifth largest ECONOMY (overtook the UK in Sept 2022) with GDP around US$3.18 trillion. The decisive statistic is GDP PER CAPITA — around US$2,250, far below developed-country levels. Large total, small average."},
  "MC-IND-02":{type:"compare",title:"GDP Share vs Employment Share",instruction:"Complete the table for India, then write one sentence explaining why the two columns tell different stories.",headers:["Sector","Share of GDP","Share of employment"],rows:[["Agriculture","",""],["Industry","",""],["Services","",""]],answer:"Agriculture: small and falling share of GDP but still the largest single EMPLOYER (from ~75% of workers to just over 50% by 2011). Services: dominate GDP. The gap is the exam trap — it shows low agricultural productivity: half the workforce producing a small fraction of output, which is precisely why rural incomes lag."},
  "MC-IND-05":{type:"compare",title:"Delhi vs Bihar",instruction:"Complete the table from memory, then write one sentence stating which figure you would use in an exam and why one well-learned pair beats five half-remembered ones.",headers:["Indicator","Delhi (urban core)","Bihar (rural periphery)"],rows:[["Life expectancy","",""],["Literacy","",""],["GDP per capita","",""],["Fertility rate","",""]],answer:"Life expectancy 72.9 vs 68.7 years; literacy 86.21% vs 69.83%; GDP per capita US$7,500 vs US$630; fertility 1.6 vs 3.0. The GDP per capita pair is the strongest single citation — a twelvefold gap inside one country makes the regional inequality point instantly."},
  "MC-IND-06":{type:"explain",title:"34% Urban — Why Does That Matter?",instruction:"State India's urban proportion and annual rural-urban migration figure, then explain in two sentences why being only a third urban is significant for the FUTURE rather than the present.",answer:"34% urban in 2021 (475 million people), growing about 2% a year, with roughly 20 million migrating from rural to urban areas annually. Significance: India is still majority rural, so it has decades of urbanisation ahead — the infrastructure, housing and service pressures already visible in Mumbai will intensify, and the largest absolute urban growth in world history is still to come."},
  "MC-IND-07":{type:"chain",title:"Development to Demography",instruction:"Complete both chains with specific mechanisms, then state which DTM stage India is now entering.",prompt:"Chain A: Economic growth → [step] → life expectancy rises from 45 (1960) to 70 (2021). Chain B: Economic growth → [step] → [step] → fertility falls.",answer:"Chain A: better diets, wider healthcare availability and cleaner water reduce infant and infectious-disease mortality. Chain B: more girls educated and more women in paid work → later marriage, contraception available and desired family size falls. India is moving into stage 3 — birth rate falling rapidly while death rate is already low."},
  "MC-IND-11":{type:"chain",title:"Untreated Sewage — Follow It Downstream",instruction:"Only about 30% of India's sewage is treated. Complete the chain to a named consequence, then add the two industrial pollutants that compound it.",prompt:"70% of sewage untreated → [step] → [step] → named river consequence.",answer:"Untreated sewage discharges directly into watercourses → bacterial and nutrient loading raises disease risk and deoxygenates water → the Ganges and Indus rank among the world's ten most polluted rivers, with cholera and typhoid risk for those using them. Compounding it: industrial waste water carrying mercury, lead and arsenic, plus heavy pesticide use including DDT, banned elsewhere."},
  "MC-IND-13":{type:"evaluate",title:"Evidence of Influence",instruction:"Rank these four pieces of evidence from strongest to weakest as proof of India's growing GEOPOLITICAL influence, and justify your top choice in one sentence.",items:["Membership of BRICS, G20 and the Asian Development Bank","The IMF attributing about 15% of global growth to India","A growing role in ASEAN negotiations","Second largest population in the world"],answer:"Strongest is usually the IMF's 15% of global growth — it is quantified, independent and economic weight is what converts into diplomatic leverage. Population is weakest: size is a precondition for influence, not evidence of it. Membership of institutions shows recognition; a role in negotiations shows influence being exercised."},
  "MC-URB-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Regeneration or Just Building?",instruction:"Write a one-sentence definition of urban regeneration, then decide which of these count as regeneration and which are simply construction.",items:["Converting derelict docks into offices and flats","Building a housing estate on greenfield land","Extending the Jubilee Line to Canary Wharf","Demolishing a tower block and leaving the site empty"],answer:"Regeneration is a planned, long-term attempt to reverse decline in a RUN-DOWN area, improving economy, environment and social conditions together. 1 and 3 qualify — Docklands reversed decline after the Port of London's container trade moved downstream. 2 is greenfield development, not regeneration. 4 is demolition without renewal."},
  "MC-URB-02":{type:"chain",title:"Why the Docks Closed",instruction:"Complete the chain. Each link must name a specific cause, not 'things changed'.",prompt:"1960s–80s: London's docks and East End manufacturing shed jobs → [step: shipping technology] → [step: geography of ports] → [step: overseas production] → deindustrialisation.",answer:"Containerisation required far larger ships and mechanised handling → those ships needed deep water, so trade moved downstream to Tilbury and Felixstowe, stranding the upstream docks → cheaper overseas labour and automation removed the remaining manufacturing base. Deindustrialisation is the long-term decline of manufacturing, not a single closure."},
  "MC-URB-03":{type:"correct",title:"Migration Adds More Than Numbers",instruction:"A student says international migration 'just increases London's population'. Give two ways this understates it, each with a specific London example.",answer:"It reshapes ethnic and cultural composition, producing distinctive neighbourhoods (Brick Lane's Bangladeshi community, Southall's Punjabi community) — a change in character, not just count. It also alters the age structure and labour supply: migrants are disproportionately of working age, which lowers the dependency ratio and fills roles in health, construction and hospitality."},
  "MC-URB-04":{type:"chain",title:"Counter-Urbanisation in Cornwall",instruction:"Complete the chain from national trend to local consequence. Then name the group that loses out and explain why.",prompt:"Retirees and second-home buyers move to coastal Cornwall → [step] → [step] → some villages feel 'empty' for much of the year.",answer:"Demand for a fixed housing stock rises sharply → prices rise faster than local wages, and homes shift to second-home or holiday-let use → properties stand empty outside the season, so shops, buses and schools lose year-round custom. The losers are young local people, priced out of the area they grew up in — an affordability gap, not a housing shortage in the raw sense."},
  "MC-URB-05":{type:"evaluate",title:"Eden — What Was Reclaimed?",instruction:"State what the Eden Project site was before, then give one economic and one environmental benefit. Finish with one limitation of relying on a single attraction.",answer:"A disused china clay pit near St Austell — derelict and environmentally scarred. Economic: thousands of jobs and over a million visitors a year, diversifying away from declining primary industry. Environmental: reclamation of degraded land plus an education and conservation role. Limitation: visitor numbers are seasonal and vulnerable to recession or fashion, so it concentrates rather than spreads Cornwall's economic risk."},
  "MC-URB-06":{type:"evaluate",title:"Has Tourism Replaced Mining?",instruction:"A student says the shift from tin mining and fishing to tourism means Cornwall's economy has simply swapped one industry for another. Write one sentence agreeing and two challenging.",answer:"Agree: tourism is now the dominant sector, as mining once was. Challenge 1: tourism is strongly seasonal, so many jobs are part-time, low-paid and insecure — annual income is not comparable to year-round industrial work. Challenge 2: tourism drives the second-home demand that prices out the same local population it employs, so the replacement carries a cost mining did not."},
  "MC-MUM-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Two Sources of Megacity Growth",instruction:"Mumbai grew from about 3 million in 1950 to over 20 million today. Name the two components of that growth, then explain which dominates in a rapidly urbanising lower-middle-income country and why.",answer:"Natural increase (births minus deaths) and rural-urban migration. In rapidly urbanising cities migration usually dominates initially — but migrants are overwhelmingly of childbearing age, so they RAISE natural increase too. The two are compounding, not alternative, which is why 'purely births' and 'purely migration' are both wrong."},
  "MC-MUM-02":{type:"sort",title:"Push, Pull — and Reality",instruction:"Sort each factor as push or pull. Then write one sentence on why the pull factors often fail to materialise.",items:["Drought and failed harvests","Perceived availability of jobs","Lack of land ownership in the village","Better schools and hospitals","Rural poverty and debt"],answer:"Push: drought, landlessness, rural poverty. Pull: jobs, schools and hospitals. The reality gap: Mumbai's FORMAL job market and housing supply cannot absorb arrivals at the rate they come, so many migrants enter informal work and informal settlements — the pull is a perception of opportunity, not a guarantee of it."},
  "MC-MUM-03":{type:"correct",title:"Dharavi Is Not Only Deprivation",instruction:"A student calls Dharavi 'just an area of poverty'. Give three specific pieces of evidence that complicate this, then write a balanced one-sentence description.",prompt:"Roughly 1 million people in about 2.1 km², in the middle of Mumbai.",answer:"Evidence: a major informal industrial centre (recycling, leather, pottery, garments) turning over hundreds of millions of dollars a year; extremely high employment participation; strong community networks and social organisation. Balanced description: Dharavi combines severe deficits in sanitation, density and tenure security with a highly productive informal economy — describing only one half misrepresents it."},
  "MC-MUM-04":{type:"classify",title:"Whose Problem Is It?",instruction:"For each problem, decide whether it affects mainly informal settlements or the whole city. Then state the general principle in one sentence.",items:["Traffic congestion on arterial roads","Lack of household sanitation","Air pollution","Overcrowding on suburban rail","Water supply shortfalls"],answer:"Whole city: congestion, air pollution, rail overcrowding, water shortfalls. Mainly informal areas: household sanitation. Principle: rapid unplanned growth strains CITY-WIDE systems — the mistake is assuming the problems of rapid urbanisation are confined to slums, when the middle-class commuter and the Dharavi resident share the same failing infrastructure."},
  "MC-MUM-05":{type:"evaluate",title:"Judge the Redevelopment",instruction:"The Dharavi Redevelopment Project proposes demolition and rehousing in high-rise flats, part-funded by selling reclaimed land. Write one sentence for it, two against, and a judgement.",answer:"For: replaces insecure housing with formal flats and legal sanitation. Against 1: high-rise flats cannot house the ground-floor workshops the informal economy depends on, so rehousing destroys livelihoods along with housing. Against 2: eligibility rules exclude many residents who cannot prove long tenure. Judgement: it improves the housing metric while damaging the economy that makes the location valuable to residents — a classic top-down scheme measuring the wrong outcome."},
  "MC-MUM-06":{type:"compare",title:"Sites and Services vs Demolition",instruction:"Complete the table, then say which approach you would recommend and on what evidence.",headers:["Feature","Sites and services","Dharavi-style redevelopment"],rows:[["Who builds the housing","",""],["Cost to government","",""],["Speed","",""],["Effect on livelihoods","",""]],answer:"Sites and services: residents build incrementally on a legal plot with water, power and sanitation provided; low public cost; slow but continuous; livelihoods preserved because people stay in place and control their own layout. Redevelopment: contractor-built; high cost, part-funded by land sales; fast but disruptive; livelihoods often destroyed. Bottom-up trades speed for ownership and appropriateness."},
  "MC-ENQ-01":{type:"rewrite",title:"Fix the Hypothesis",instruction:"Rewrite this hypothesis so it is directional and measurable, then write the rule for what makes a hypothesis testable in your own words.",prompt:"'The river is different at different points.'",answer:"Usable version: 'River discharge increases with distance downstream from the source.' The rule: a hypothesis must state a DIRECTION of change in a MEASURABLE variable, so that data can show it false. 'Is different' cannot be disproved, so it cannot be tested."},
  "MC-ENQ-03":{type:"sort",title:"Primary or Secondary?",instruction:"Sort each source. Two are arguable — say why.",items:["Your own river depth measurements","Census population data","A photograph you took at the site","An old photograph from the local archive","A questionnaire you administered","An Environment Agency flood map"],answer:"Primary (collected first-hand): your measurements, your photograph, your questionnaire. Secondary (collected by others, reused): census, archive photograph, flood map. The arguable pair are the photographs — the distinction is not the medium but WHO collected it and when."},
  "MC-ENQ-04":{type:"classify",title:"Name the Sampling Method",instruction:"Identify the sampling method in each case, then state the main weakness of the one used for sites every 200m along a transect.",items:["Sites every 200 metres along a transect","Grid squares selected using random numbers","Sampling 10 people from each age group in proportion to the population","Interviewing whoever is willing to stop"],answer:"1 Systematic, 2 Random, 3 Stratified, 4 Opportunistic (convenience). Systematic's weakness: not every site has an equal chance of selection, so a regular interval can coincide with a regular feature (every 200m falling on a road junction) and build bias into what looks like objective coverage."},
  "MC-ENQ-09":{type:"calculate",title:"Mode, Mean, Median — All Three",instruction:"For the data set 2, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5, 5, 8: calculate the mode, mean and median, showing working for each. Then say which best represents the set and why.",answer:"Mode = 5 (occurs three times). Mean = 35 ÷ 8 = 4.375. Median = mean of the 4th and 5th ranked values = (4+5) ÷ 2 = 4.5. The mode is the only one that must be an actual value in the set; the mean is pulled upward by the 8. For a small skewed set like this the median is usually the fairest summary."},
  "MC-ENQ-11":{type:"distinguish",title:"Conclusion or Evaluation?",instruction:"Label each statement C (conclusion) or E (evaluation). Then write one sentence stating the difference.",items:["The hypothesis was supported: discharge increased downstream","Only three sites were sampled, which is too few to generalise","Velocity rose from 0.2 to 0.6 m/s between sites 1 and 3","The float method was affected by wind on the day","Site 2 was chosen for access, not representativeness"],answer:"C: 1, 3. E: 2, 4, 5. A conclusion returns to the hypothesis and answers it using the data. An evaluation turns on the ENQUIRY ITSELF — sample size, site selection, method reliability, conditions — and asks how much confidence the conclusion deserves."},
  "MC-ENQ-12":{type:"explain",title:"Float vs Flow Meter",instruction:"Explain why a flow meter would improve on a float, naming the specific source of error the float introduces. Then give one reason a class might still use a float.",answer:"The float depends on a stopwatch started and stopped by hand, so human reaction time adds an error of a few tenths of a second to every reading — significant over a 10m stretch where the total time may be under 30 seconds. It also measures only surface velocity, which is faster than the mean. A flow meter reads directly at a set depth. Floats are still used because they are cheap, robust and need no calibration."},
  "MC-URF-02":{type:"explain",title:"Why Stratford?",instruction:"Explain what makes Stratford unusually good for a regeneration enquiry, naming three contrasting sites within walking distance and what each represents.",answer:"Regeneration is investment to reverse decline in a run-down area, and Stratford compresses the whole sequence into a small area: Carpenters Estate (1960s social housing, partly vacant pending redevelopment) represents pre-regeneration decline; the Olympic Park and East Village represent large-scale planned investment; Old Stratford represents the pre-existing town centre absorbing the change. A student can survey all three in one afternoon and compare directly."},
  "MC-URF-03":{type:"classify",title:"Does It Belong on an EQS?",instruction:"Decide which features belong on an Environmental Quality Survey and which do not. Then state the scale an EQS normally uses.",items:["Litter","River discharge","Building condition","Graffiti","Green space","Traffic volume"],answer:"All belong except river discharge — that is a river fieldwork measurement, and it is a standard exam distractor. An EQS scores features on a BIPOLAR scale, typically −2 to +2, so each factor runs from a clearly negative to a clearly positive description rather than being simply present or absent."},
  "MC-URF-05":{type:"distinguish",title:"Closed, Scale or Open?",instruction:"Classify each question type, then state one advantage and one disadvantage of the statement/scale format used near Carpenters Estate.",items:["'How long have you lived here?'","'The regeneration has improved this neighbourhood' — strongly agree to strongly disagree","'What are your views on the regeneration?'"],answer:"1 Closed, 2 Statement/scale, 3 Open. Advantage of the scale: responses are directly comparable and quickly quantified into a chart. Disadvantage: it forces a complex opinion onto a fixed line and cannot capture WHY — which is why a good questionnaire pairs it with an open follow-up."},
  "MC-DEC-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Is It a Geographical Issue?",instruction:"Decide which of these is a genuine geographical issue suitable for Paper 3, and say what disqualifies the others.",items:["Whether to build a road through a rainforest","The height of Mount Everest","Where to site a new reservoir","How a stretch of coastline should be managed","The population of Mumbai in 2021"],answer:"Issues: the road, the reservoir, the coastline. The others are FACTS, not issues — they have a single correct answer. A geographical issue is a genuine dilemma that combines physical and human geography, affects different groups differently, and has more than one defensible answer."},
  "MC-DEC-02":{type:"diagnose",title:"Why Did This Score Level 1?",instruction:"A student's decision-making answer lists only the advantages of their chosen option and ignores every source suggesting problems. Explain what the mark scheme rewards, then rewrite the opening sentence of a Level 3 answer.",answer:"Levels-based marking rewards a BALANCED, well-evidenced argument that uses multiple sources across the booklet — including those favouring a rival option — and then justifies the choice despite them. A one-sided answer cannot reach the top level however accurate it is. Level 3 opening: 'Option B is the strongest choice, although Figure 4 shows a real environmental cost that must be weighed against it.'"},
  "MC-DEC-03":{type:"sort",title:"Environmental, Economic or Social?",instruction:"Sort each piece of evidence, then explain why the same fact can sometimes sit in two categories.",items:["350 construction jobs created","Loss of 40 hectares of woodland","120 households displaced","£45m investment in the local economy","Increased air pollution near the new road","Improved hospital access for a rural village"],answer:"Economic: jobs, investment. Environmental: woodland loss, air pollution. Social: displacement, hospital access. Overlap is real — air pollution is environmental in cause and social in effect (health). Saying so explicitly is what separates a strong answer from a mechanical one."},
  "MC-DEC-08":{type:"distinguish",title:"Describe, Explain, Justify",instruction:"Write the same content three ways about a proposed reservoir — once for each command word, one sentence each. Then state which assessment objective each serves.",answer:"Describe: 'Figure 3 shows the reservoir would flood 40 hectares of farmland in the valley floor.' (AO1/AO3 — features and patterns from evidence.) Explain: 'The valley floor floods because the dam raises water levels behind it.' (AO1/AO2 — reasons and causes.) Justify: 'The reservoir should still proceed, because Figure 5 shows the water shortfall affects 200,000 people, which outweighs the loss of 40 hectares.' (AO3 — a defended choice weighing evidence.)"},
  "MC-DEC-12":{type:"evaluate",title:"Why Include Contradictory Sources?",instruction:"A booklet contains one report predicting job creation and another predicting job losses from the same scheme. Explain why both are included, then say what a strong answer does with the contradiction.",answer:"Real geographical issues have contested evidence, and the sources have different authors with different interests — a mining company's own forecast is not as impartial as an independent economic study. A strong answer does not pick one and ignore the other, nor split the difference: it evaluates the SOURCE (who produced it, what they gain, what method they used) and weighs the evidence accordingly, saying so explicitly."},

  // ---- A-Level Psychology — Approaches, Biopsychology, Attachment, Memory, Social Influence ----
  "MC-APP-ORIGINS":{type:"correct",title:"What Did Wundt Actually Ask For?",instruction:"Find the error in this student sentence and write the correct version, including the year and place.",prompt:"'Wundt's introspection asked participants to describe their feelings and childhood memories in their own words.'",answer:"Introspection was STRUCTURED and standardised: trained participants broke down their conscious experience of a controlled stimulus into its component sensations, images and feelings, under identical conditions each time. Leipzig, 1879 — the first psychology laboratory. The point was replicability, which is why free description of memories is the opposite of the method."},
  "MC-APP-OPERANT":{type:"sort",title:"The Reinforcement Grid",instruction:"Draw a 2×2 grid: rows 'behaviour increases / decreases', columns 'something added / removed'. Place each example, then state the rule for the word 'negative'.",items:["A child is praised for tidying up","A rat presses a lever to switch off a loud noise","A driver is fined for speeding","Painkillers remove a headache, so you take them again","A student loses phone privileges for missing homework"],answer:"Positive reinforcement: praise. Negative reinforcement: switching off the noise, taking painkillers. Positive punishment: the fine. Negative punishment: losing phone privileges. The rule: 'reinforcement' ALWAYS means the behaviour increases; 'negative' means something is REMOVED, not that the outcome is unpleasant. Negative reinforcement and punishment are opposites, not synonyms."},
  "MC-APP-SLT":{type:"sequence",title:"ARMM in Order",instruction:"Name Bandura's four mediational processes in order and say what happens at each. Then explain what they add that behaviourism lacks.",answer:"Attention (you must notice the model) → Retention (you must remember what you saw) → Motor reproduction (you must be physically able to do it) → Motivation (you must want to, shaped by vicarious reinforcement). They sit between observing a model and imitating it. They are cognitive — internal processes standing between stimulus and response, which is exactly what traditional behaviourism refuses to include."},
  "MC-APP-COG":{type:"apply",title:"Schema in Action",instruction:"Write a one-sentence definition of a schema, then explain what your 'restaurant' schema predicts. Finish by giving one advantage and one cost of relying on schemas.",answer:"A schema is a package of knowledge built from experience that tells you what to expect and how to behave. The restaurant schema predicts being seated, given a menu, ordering, eating, then paying — so you can act without deliberating. Advantage: it lets you process huge amounts of information quickly. Cost: it distorts what you notice and recall, filling gaps with what SHOULD have happened rather than what did."},
  "MC-APP-BIO":{type:"distinguish",title:"Genotype or Phenotype?",instruction:"Label each as genotype, phenotype or both. Then explain why identical twins are the standard illustration.",items:["The DNA sequence a person inherits","Height at age 18","Eye colour","Being a carrier of a recessive allele with no visible sign","A twin who smokes and develops a cough"],answer:"Genotype: DNA sequence, carrier status. Phenotype: height, the cough. Eye colour is both — it is a visible characteristic produced by the code. Identical twins share a genotype but differ in phenotype (height, weight, health), proving the phenotype is the genotype INTERACTING with environment, not a readout of it."},
  "MC-APP-PSYDYN":{type:"compare",title:"Id, Ego, Superego",instruction:"Complete the table, then answer: what does the ego actually DO?",headers:["Structure","Present from","Principle","Conscious?"],rows:[["Id","","",""],["Ego","","",""],["Superego","","",""]],answer:"Id: from birth, pleasure principle, entirely unconscious. Ego: from about age 2, reality principle, partly conscious. Superego: from about age 5 (end of the phallic stage), morality principle, partly conscious. The ego is not a 'middle amount of personality' — it is the MEDIATOR, using defence mechanisms to manage the conflict between the id's demands and the superego's prohibitions."},
  "MC-APP-HUM":{type:"scenario",title:"Spot the Condition of Worth",instruction:"For each parental statement, decide whether it imposes a condition of worth or offers unconditional positive regard, and rewrite the conditional ones.",items:["'I love you.'","'I'll be proud of you if you get an A.'","'Good girls don't get angry.'","'I'm disappointed in what you did, but I love you.'"],answer:"Unconditional: 1 and 4 — 4 separates the behaviour from the person, which is the key move. Conditions of worth: 2 and 3. Rogers argued children internalise these, so self-acceptance becomes conditional and the gap between self-concept and ideal self widens — producing incongruence. Rewrites should praise or criticise the ACT, never the child's worth."},
  "MC-APP-COMPARE":{type:"classify",title:"Rank the Approaches on Free Will",instruction:"Place each approach on a line from hard determinism to free will, then justify the two ends.",items:["Behaviourist","Biological","Psychodynamic","Cognitive","Humanistic"],answer:"Hard determinism: behaviourist (environmental) and biological (genetic/neural) — behaviour is fully caused by forces outside conscious control. Psychodynamic: psychic determinism, unconscious conflict. Cognitive: soft determinism — choice within limits. Humanistic: free will, standing alone. That position is why the humanistic approach rejects the scientific reductionism of the others and treats people as active agents."},
  "MC-BIO-NS":{type:"diagram",title:"Branch the Nervous System",instruction:"Draw the nervous system as a branching diagram, dividing it twice. Label every branch, then name what each division does in four words or fewer.",answer:"Nervous system → central (brain, spinal cord) and peripheral. PNS → somatic (voluntary movement, carries sensory and motor information) and autonomic. Autonomic → sympathetic (arousal, fight-or-flight) and parasympathetic (rest and digest). The common error is putting the spinal cord in the PNS — it is central; the nerves LEAVING it are peripheral."},
  "MC-BIO-SYNAPSE":{type:"chain",title:"Across the Gap",instruction:"Complete the chain, then answer in one sentence: which parts are electrical and which chemical?",prompt:"Action potential arrives at presynaptic terminal → [step] → [step] → [step] → postsynaptic neuron is excited or inhibited.",answer:"Vesicles fuse with the membrane and release neurotransmitter into the synaptic cleft → neurotransmitter diffuses across and binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane → the signal is converted back into an electrical impulse (excitatory or inhibitory depending on the neurotransmitter and receptor). Transmission WITHIN a neuron is electrical; BETWEEN neurons it is chemical. Synaptic transmission is also unidirectional — a frequent exam point."},
  "MC-BIO-FIGHT":{type:"sequence",title:"Order the Acute Stress Response",instruction:"Put the stages in order, then name the hormone released and two of its physical effects.",items:["Adrenal medulla releases adrenaline","Amygdala appraises the threat","Sympathetic branch of the ANS is activated","Hypothalamus is signalled"],answer:"Amygdala appraises → hypothalamus signalled → sympathetic branch activated → adrenal medulla releases adrenaline. Effects: increased heart rate and blood pressure, dilated pupils, diverted blood from digestion to skeletal muscle, glucose released. This is the SAM pathway and it is acute — do not confuse it with the slower HPA axis, which handles sustained stress via cortisol."},
  "MC-BIO-LOCAL":{type:"distinguish",title:"Localisation or Holism?",instruction:"Label each claim L (localisation) or H (holistic), then name the region responsible for each function listed.",items:["Damage to a specific area removes a specific function","Every part of the brain contributes to every process","Broca's area","Wernicke's area","The motor cortex"],answer:"L: 1, 3, 4, 5. H: 2. Broca's area — speech PRODUCTION, left frontal lobe. Wernicke's area — language COMPREHENSION, left temporal lobe. Motor cortex — voluntary movement, frontal lobe (contralateral control). Localisation theory replaced the earlier holistic view; plasticity research is what partially rehabilitates holism."},
  "MC-BIO-SPLIT":{type:"chain",title:"Follow the Route",instruction:"Trace the pathway for an object shown to the LEFT visual field of a split-brain patient. Then explain why the patient can pick the object up with their left hand but cannot name it.",answer:"Left visual field → right hemisphere. In an intact brain the corpus callosum would carry this to the language centres in the LEFT hemisphere, so the person could name it. With the callosum cut, the information cannot cross. The right hemisphere controls the left hand, so the patient can select the object by touch — demonstrating the information IS there, just not available to the language centres. That dissociation is Sperry's whole point."},
  "MC-BIO-PLAST":{type:"compare",title:"Maguire vs Draganski",instruction:"Define plasticity in one sentence, then complete the table. Finish by stating what the two studies together show that neither shows alone.",headers:["Study","Participants","Finding"],rows:[["Maguire (2000)","",""],["Draganski (2006)","",""]],answer:"Plasticity is the brain's capacity to reorganise by forming new synaptic connections in response to learning and experience. Maguire: London taxi drivers, significantly more grey matter in the posterior hippocampus, correlated with years of service. Draganski: medical students, changes in the parietal cortex and hippocampus measured before and after final exams. Together: Maguire shows long-term structural change, Draganski shows it over MONTHS — so plasticity is not confined to childhood or to extreme cases."},
  "MC-BIO-SCAN":{type:"compare",title:"Pick the Right Scan",instruction:"Complete the table, then state which technique you would choose to study the timing of a response, and why.",headers:["Technique","What it measures","Main strength","Main weakness"],rows:[["fMRI","","",""],["EEG","","",""],["Post-mortem","","",""]],answer:"fMRI: blood oxygenation; excellent spatial resolution (1–2mm) and non-invasive; poor temporal resolution (~5s delay). EEG: electrical activity; excellent temporal resolution (milliseconds); poor spatial resolution. Post-mortem: physical brain structure after death; allows deep-brain examination; no causal link to living behaviour and no consent from the deceased. For timing, choose EEG — fMRI is too slow."},
  "MC-BIO-RHYTHM":{type:"sort",title:"Circadian, Infradian or Ultradian?",instruction:"Sort each rhythm by its duration, then state the rule that decides each category.",items:["Sleep-wake cycle","Menstrual cycle","Stages of sleep within one night","Core body temperature variation","Seasonal affective disorder","The 90-minute BRAC"],answer:"Circadian (about 24 hours): sleep-wake cycle, core body temperature (lowest around 4am). Infradian (LONGER than 24 hours): menstrual cycle (~28 days), SAD (annual). Ultradian (SHORTER than 24 hours): sleep stages, the 90-minute basic rest-activity cycle. The rule is duration relative to a day — 'infra' means below the FREQUENCY of daily, which is why it means a longer cycle. That inversion is what students get wrong."},
  "MC-ATT-02":{type:"sequence",title:"Eight Episodes, Five Behaviours",instruction:"Name the five behaviours the Strange Situation measures, then explain why reunion behaviour matters more than separation behaviour for classification.",answer:"Proximity seeking, exploration and secure-base behaviour, stranger anxiety, separation anxiety, and response to reunion. Reunion is decisive because it distinguishes types that look similar on separation: a Type A infant shows little distress AND avoids the caregiver on return, while a Type B infant shows distress but is easily soothed. It is a CONTROLLED OBSERVATION, not an experiment or interview — a frequent exam slip."},
  "MC-ATT-03":{type:"correct",title:"Is a Calm Baby Securely Attached?",instruction:"A student describes a Type B infant as 'one who doesn't cry at all when the caregiver leaves'. Say which type they have actually described, then give the correct description of all three.",answer:"They have described Type A (insecure-avoidant): little distress on separation, ignores or avoids the caregiver on reunion. Type B (secure): MODERATE distress, easily soothed on reunion, uses the caregiver as a safe base to explore. Type C (insecure-resistant): intense distress, but resists comfort on reunion — seeks and rejects simultaneously. Secure means well-REGULATED distress, not absent distress."},
  "MC-ATT-04":{type:"evaluate",title:"Judge the Strange Situation",instruction:"Sort each statement into strength or limitation, then write one sentence on the culture-bias criticism.",items:["Standardised and replicable with clear behavioural categories","May cause distress to the infant","High inter-rater reliability","Categories may not transfer across cultures","Lab-based and therefore artificial"],answer:"Strengths: standardisation, inter-rater reliability. Limitations: ethical distress, artificiality, cultural transferability. On culture bias: the procedure assumes separation anxiety indicates attachment quality, but in cultures where infants are rarely separated from mothers (Japan) distress may be extreme for reasons of unfamiliarity, and in cultures encouraging independence (Germany) low distress may be adaptive — so an imposed etic can misclassify normal behaviour as insecure."},
  "MC-ATT-05":{type:"chain",title:"From Infancy to Adult Relationships",instruction:"Complete the chain, then name the study that provides supporting evidence.",prompt:"Early attachment experience → [step] → [step] → adult relationship style.",answer:"The infant forms an internal working model — a mental representation of self, caregiver and what relationships are like → this acts as a TEMPLATE for expectations of others (trustworthy and responsive, or unreliable) → those expectations shape how the person behaves in later relationships, confirming the model. Hazan and Shaver's 'love quiz' found adult attachment styles correlated with recalled childhood attachment. The model is a set of expectations, not a memory."},
  "MC-ATT-06":{type:"distinguish",title:"Deprivation, Separation, Privation",instruction:"Define each of the three terms, then classify each case. Say which one Bowlby's hypothesis is actually about.",items:["A child in hospital for two weeks, visited daily","A child whose mother dies when they are 18 months old","A child raised in an institution who has never formed an attachment"],answer:"Separation: brief, attachment intact. Deprivation: prolonged loss of an existing attachment. Privation: an attachment never formed. Cases: 1 separation, 2 deprivation, 3 privation. Bowlby's maternal deprivation hypothesis concerns DEPRIVATION during the critical period (0–2.5 years), claiming irreversible damage — low IQ, delinquency, affectionless psychopathy. Rutter's key criticism is precisely that Bowlby conflated deprivation with privation."},
  "MC-ATT-07":{type:"evaluate",title:"44 Thieves — What Does It Prove?",instruction:"State Bowlby's findings with the figures, then give the two strongest methodological criticisms.",answer:"Of 44 juvenile thieves, 14 showed affectionless psychopathy, and 12 of those 14 had experienced prolonged early separation from their mothers, against just 2 of the remaining 30. Criticisms: (1) Bowlby conducted the assessments and the interviews himself knowing his hypothesis — investigator bias; (2) it is correlational and retrospective, relying on parental recall of events years earlier, so it cannot establish that separation CAUSED the psychopathy rather than accompanying poverty or family disruption."},
  "MC-ATT-08":{type:"compare",title:"Harlow vs Bowlby",instruction:"Complete the table, then state what Harlow's work disproves and what it does NOT establish.",headers:["Feature","Harlow (1958)","Bowlby"],rows:[["Species studied","",""],["Core claim","",""],["Method","",""]],answer:"Harlow: rhesus monkeys; contact comfort matters more than food; lab experiment with wire and cloth surrogates. Bowlby: humans; monotropy and the internal working model within a critical period; observation and interview. Harlow DISPROVES cupboard love — frightened infants ran to the cloth mother, not the feeding wire mother. He does NOT establish monotropy in humans; the surrogate-reared monkeys were later socially and sexually abnormal, which supports the critical-period idea but by extrapolation across species."},
  "MC-MEM-02":{type:"correct",title:"7±2, and What the ± Means",instruction:"A student says STM capacity is 'about 5 items'. Correct it, explain what chunking does to that figure, and give the later revision.",prompt:"Is BBCITVFBI nine items or three?",answer:"Miller's magic number is 7±2 — the range is part of the finding, not decoration. Chunking increases EFFECTIVE capacity by recoding items into meaningful units: BBCITVFBI is nine letters or three chunks, and capacity is measured in chunks. Cowan (2001) later argued four chunks is more accurate. The error is quoting a single number and dropping both the range and the chunking qualification."},
  "MC-MEM-03":{type:"diagnose",title:"What Could the MSM Not Explain?",instruction:"Name the MSM limitation that motivated the working memory model, then draw the WMM with all four components labelled.",prompt:"Evidence: people perform two VERBAL tasks together much worse than one verbal and one visual task.",answer:"The MSM treats STM as UNITARY — one store. If that were true, any two concurrent tasks would compete equally. They do not, so there must be separate subsystems. The WMM: central executive (attention controller) directing the phonological loop (verbal, subdivided into the phonological store and articulatory process) and the visuo-spatial sketchpad (visual and spatial), with the episodic buffer added in 2000 to integrate information and link to LTM."},
  "MC-MEM-04":{type:"correct",title:"The Central Executive Is Not a Store",instruction:"A student calls the central executive 'a big memory store with lots of capacity'. Give the two errors, then state what it actually does.",answer:"Error 1: it is not a store — it holds no information itself. Error 2: its capacity is LIMITED, not large — you cannot attend to everything at once. The central executive is the attentional controller: it decides what to focus on and allocates work to the slave systems. It is also the least well specified part of the model, which is the standard evaluation point — 'attention' is not a precise mechanism."},
  "MC-MEM-05":{type:"distinguish",title:"Response Bias or Memory Change?",instruction:"Loftus and Palmer found 'smashed' produced higher speed estimates than 'contacted' (about 41mph vs 32mph). State the two competing explanations, then say which finding from Experiment 2 decides between them.",answer:"Response bias: the verb influences the ANSWER GIVEN, leaving the memory intact. Substitution/memory change: the verb alters the stored memory itself. Experiment 2 decides it — a week later, the 'smashed' group were significantly more likely to report broken glass that was never in the film. Reporting a detail that did not exist cannot be explained by biased estimation of a real one, so post-event information had altered the memory."},
  "MC-MEM-06":{type:"classify",title:"Is It a Cognitive Interview Technique?",instruction:"Mark each Y or N. For each N, name what it actually is. Then state which technique the ENHANCED version adds.",items:["Report everything, including trivial detail","Mental reinstatement of context","Asking leading questions to prompt recall","Changing the temporal order of recall","Recalling from another person's perspective","Repeating the question until the answer changes"],answer:"Y: report everything, mental reinstatement, change of order, change of perspective — the four techniques. N: leading questions (the standard police interview practice the CI was designed to REPLACE) and repeating until the answer changes (pressure, which reduces accuracy). The enhanced CI adds social dynamics — building rapport, controlling eye contact and minimising anxiety."},
  "MC-SI-02":{type:"distinguish",title:"Kelman's Three Types",instruction:"Label each scenario compliance, identification or internalisation, then state which involves genuine private change and which is most durable.",items:["Agreeing with friends about a band in public, disagreeing privately","Adopting a new team's values while working there, dropping them on leaving","Becoming genuinely vegetarian after living with vegetarian housemates"],answer:"1 Compliance, 2 Identification, 3 Internalisation. Internalisation involves genuine private acceptance and is the most durable — it persists in the group's absence. Compliance is shallowest: behaviour changes, belief does not. The student error is defining compliance as 'genuinely changing your mind', which describes internalisation."},
  "MC-SI-04":{type:"correct",title:"Define the Agentic State",instruction:"Write a precise definition of the agentic state, name its opposite, and explain what triggers the shift between them.",answer:"The agentic state is acting as an AGENT of an authority figure, handing responsibility for one's actions to them — the person still knows the act is wrong but no longer feels answerable for it. Its opposite is the autonomous state, acting on one's own free will and accepting responsibility. The agentic SHIFT occurs when someone perceives another as a legitimate authority within an accepted social hierarchy. It explains why Milgram's participants showed visible distress yet continued."},
  "MC-SI-05":{type:"explain",title:"Why Consistency Works",instruction:"State Moscovici's key finding, then explain the mechanism by which a consistent minority changes a majority. Add the two other factors that strengthen minority influence.",answer:"In the blue-green slide study a consistent minority calling blue slides green produced agreement on about 8% of trials, far above an inconsistent minority. Mechanism: consistency forces the majority to engage in DEEPER PROCESSING — 'they keep saying it, maybe they have a point' — whereas an inconsistent minority is dismissed as mistaken. The other factors are commitment (the minority appears principled, e.g. through the augmentation principle of personal cost) and flexibility (rigid repetition is counterproductive)."},
  "MC-SI-06":{type:"scenario",title:"NSI or ISI?",instruction:"Label each case NSI or ISI, then state which type of conformity each typically produces and which is stronger under ambiguity.",items:["Laughing at a joke you didn't find funny so the group likes you","Copying the person next to you in a test because you don't know the answer","Following the crowd out of a building during an unclear alarm","Wearing what everyone else wears to avoid standing out"],answer:"NSI: 1, 4 — driven by the desire to be liked, produces compliance, strongest where the group can see you. ISI: 2, 3 — driven by uncertainty and the desire to be right, more likely to produce internalisation, strongest where the situation is AMBIGUOUS or the others appear expert. Asch's line task is mainly NSI (the answer is obvious); Sherif's autokinetic effect is ISI (genuinely ambiguous)."},

  // ---- A-Level Psychology — Research Methods, Issues & Debates, Cognitive Development, Forensic, Psychopathology, Stress ----
  "MC-RM-VAR":{type:"rewrite",title:"Operationalise It",instruction:"Rewrite each vague statement so both variables are operationalised precisely enough for another researcher to replicate exactly.",items:["Sleep affects memory","Noise affects concentration","Age affects reaction time"],answer:"E.g. 'Participants given 4 versus 8 hours of sleep recall fewer words from a 20-word list after 24 hours.' The test is replication: another researcher must be able to run the identical procedure from your description alone. 'Sleep' and 'memory' are constructs; '4 versus 8 hours' and 'words recalled from a 20-item list' are measurable operations."},
  "MC-RM-DESIGN":{type:"compare",title:"Three Designs, Three Problems",instruction:"Complete the table, then name the technique that controls order effects and explain how it works.",headers:["Design","Main weakness","Main strength"],rows:[["Repeated measures","",""],["Independent groups","",""],["Matched pairs","",""]],answer:"Repeated measures: order effects (practice or fatigue) and demand characteristics; but no participant variables and fewer participants needed. Independent groups: participant variables confound results; but no order effects. Matched pairs: time-consuming and never a perfect match; but controls both. Counterbalancing controls order effects — half do condition A then B, half B then A, so any practice or fatigue effect is spread evenly across both rather than favouring one."},
  "MC-RM-STATS":{type:"calculate",title:"One Outlier, Three Averages",instruction:"For the scores 2, 3, 3, 4, 88: calculate mean, median and mode. Then say which describes the data best and what that shows about the mean.",answer:"Mean = 100 ÷ 5 = 20. Median = 3. Mode = 3. The mean of 20 describes NOBODY in the set — every score is either far below it or far above it. That is the mean's weakness: it uses every value, so a single extreme score drags it. It is also its strength, and it is the most powerful measure, which is why it is preferred when data are interval and roughly symmetrical."},
  "MC-RM-TEST":{type:"match",title:"Choose the Test",instruction:"For each study, name the test. Say aloud the three decisions you make each time.",items:["Difference, independent groups, ordinal data","Difference, repeated measures, ordinal data","Correlation, ordinal data","Difference, independent groups, nominal data"],answer:"1 Mann-Whitney, 2 Wilcoxon, 3 Spearman's rho, 4 Chi-squared. The three decisions: (1) difference or correlation? (2) related design (repeated measures, matched pairs) or unrelated (independent groups)? (3) level of measurement — nominal, ordinal or interval? Students who memorise the table without the three questions cannot handle an unfamiliar scenario."},
  "MC-RM-VALID":{type:"distinguish",title:"Internal or External?",instruction:"Label each threat internal or external validity, then define both in one sentence each.",items:["A confounding variable the researcher did not control","Findings that only apply to psychology undergraduates","Participants guessing the aim and changing behaviour","Results from a 1970s study that may not hold today","An unreliable measure of the dependent variable"],answer:"Internal: confounding variable, demand characteristics, unreliable measure. External: undergraduate sample (population validity), historical change (temporal validity). Internal validity asks whether the study measured what it intended, free of confounds, so the effect can be attributed to the IV. External validity asks how far findings generalise — to other people, settings and times."},
  "MC-RM-SCIENCE":{type:"evaluate",title:"Three Jobs of Peer Review",instruction:"Name the three functions of peer review, then give one criticism of the process for each of two of them.",answer:"Functions: (1) allocating research funding; (2) validating the quality and accuracy of submitted work; (3) suggesting improvements before publication. Criticisms: anonymity can permit reviewers to bury a rival's work or protect an established position; there is publication bias towards positive, novel findings, so replications and null results go unpublished — which distorts the literature the review process is supposed to protect."},
  "MC-RM-EXPT":{type:"classify",title:"Which Kind of Experiment?",instruction:"Classify each as laboratory, field, natural or quasi-experiment. Then say what natural and quasi have in common and how they differ.",items:["Romanian orphan studies of institutionalisation","Memory tested under controlled conditions in a university lab","Helping behaviour measured on a real train","Comparing reaction times of men and women"],answer:"1 Natural, 2 Laboratory, 3 Field, 4 Quasi. Both natural and quasi lack random allocation, so causal claims are weaker. The difference: in a natural experiment the IV varies naturally through an event and the researcher measures the effect; in a quasi-experiment the IV is a pre-existing characteristic of the participant (sex, age) that cannot be manipulated at all. Natural experiments allow research that would be unethical to arrange — no committee would assign children to institutions."},
  "MC-ID-GENDER":{type:"distinguish",title:"Alpha or Beta Bias?",instruction:"Label each example, then state which type each is a form of, and why 'beta bias sounds harmless' is wrong.",items:["Freud's claim that women develop a weaker superego","Assuming fight-or-flight research on males applies to everyone","Claiming women are naturally better at nurturing","Drug trials conducted on men and dosed for all"],answer:"Alpha bias (EXAGGERATES difference, usually devaluing one sex): 1, 3. Beta bias (MINIMISES or ignores difference): 2, 4. Both are forms of androcentrism when the male is the standard. Beta bias is not harmless: ignoring difference means female physiology is never studied, so 'universal' findings and dosages are built on male data — Taylor's tend-and-befriend research exists precisely because a beta-biased literature had missed it."},
  "MC-ID-CULTURE":{type:"apply",title:"Spot the Imposed Etic",instruction:"For each case, decide whether the approach is emic, etic or an imposed etic. Then explain what makes the imposed etic an error rather than a choice.",items:["Studying one culture's child-rearing on its own terms","Seeking universal principles of memory across many cultures","Using the Strange Situation, developed in the USA, to classify Japanese infants"],answer:"1 Emic, 2 Etic, 3 Imposed etic. The error is not studying across cultures — it is taking a measure BUILT IN one culture, applying it elsewhere, and treating the results as if the measure were culture-free. Japanese infants show extreme distress on separation because it is rare in that culture, so the American scoring system misreads normal behaviour as insecure attachment. The tool imports its origin culture's assumptions."},
  "MC-ID-FREEWILL":{type:"classify",title:"Place It on the Determinism Line",instruction:"Label each position hard determinism, soft determinism or free will, then explain why soft determinism is the position most compatible with the legal system.",items:["Behaviour is entirely caused by prior events","Behaviour has causes, but people choose within those constraints","People are wholly self-determining agents","The cognitive approach's usual position","William James's position"],answer:"1 Hard determinism, 2 and 4 and 5 soft determinism, 3 free will (humanistic). Soft determinism, associated with James and adopted by the cognitive approach, holds that behaviour is caused but conscious choice operates within those constraints. It fits the legal system because law requires that people could have acted otherwise — hard determinism would make moral and criminal responsibility incoherent."},
  "MC-ID-NATURE":{type:"chain",title:"Diathesis Plus Stress",instruction:"Complete the chain, then explain why the model is INTERACTIONIST rather than a compromise between nature and nurture.",prompt:"Inherited vulnerability (diathesis) → [step] → disorder develops.",answer:"The predisposition alone does not produce the disorder — an environmental trigger (the stressor) must occur, and only then does the vulnerability express itself. Tienari's Finnish adoption study is the standard evidence: adoptees with a genetic risk of schizophrenia developed it far more often when raised in dysfunctional adoptive families. It is interactionist, not a midpoint: neither factor has an effect INDEPENDENT of the other, so the causes multiply rather than add."},
  "MC-ID-REDUCT":{type:"evaluate",title:"The Cost of Holism",instruction:"State the main criticism of holism, then give one approach that is holistic and one that is reductionist, and say what each gains and loses.",answer:"The criticism is that holism does not lend itself to rigorous scientific testing: if behaviour must be studied as an indivisible whole, no single variable can be isolated and manipulated, so predictions are hard to test and competing explanations hard to separate. Holistic: humanistic, and Jahoda's account of ideal mental health — gains validity and completeness, loses testability. Reductionist: biological (behaviour to neurotransmitters) — gains scientific credibility and effective drug treatments, loses the meaning of behaviour in context."},
  "MC-ID-IDIO":{type:"compare",title:"Idiographic vs Nomothetic",instruction:"Complete the table, then name one approach that uses each and explain why the distinction is a spectrum rather than a binary.",headers:["Feature","Idiographic","Nomothetic"],rows:[["Aim","",""],["Typical methods","",""],["Data type","",""]],answer:"Idiographic: understand the individual as unique; case studies, unstructured interviews, thematic analysis; qualitative. Used by humanistic and psychodynamic approaches. Nomothetic: establish general laws across people; experiments, correlations, psychometric testing; quantitative. Used by behaviourist, biological and cognitive approaches. It is a spectrum because case studies generate hypotheses that are then tested nomothetically — HM's case produced general claims about memory stores."},
  "MC-ID-ETHICS":{type:"evaluate",title:"Socially Sensitive Research",instruction:"Define social sensitivity as Sieber and Stanley did, then for each research area name the group at risk and the potential misuse.",items:["Heritability of intelligence","Genetic basis of sexual orientation","Reliability of eyewitness testimony"],answer:"Socially sensitive research has potential consequences for the participants or for the wider GROUP they represent. Intelligence heritability: ethnic and class groups — used historically to justify segregation and selective education policy. Sexual orientation: LGBT people — risk of framing orientation as a defect to be prevented or 'treated'. Eyewitness testimony: defendants and victims — findings change what courts accept as evidence, affecting real convictions. The point is that the ethics extend beyond the people in the room."},
  "MC-COG-PIAGET":{type:"scenario",title:"Assimilate or Accommodate?",instruction:"Label each case, then state the term for the discomfort that drives the change and complete the cycle.",items:["A child who knows 'dog' calls a cat a dog","The child learns cats and dogs are different and creates a new schema","A child who knows 'car' sees a lorry and calls it a big car","The child learns lorries carry goods and forms a separate schema"],answer:"Assimilate: 1, 3 — new information is forced into an EXISTING schema. Accommodate: 2, 4 — the schema itself is changed or a new one created. The driver is DISEQUILIBRIUM, the discomfort of a schema that no longer fits, resolved by accommodation to reach equilibration. The cycle: equilibrium → new experience → disequilibrium → accommodation → new equilibrium."},
  "MC-COG-VYGOTSKY":{type:"diagram",title:"Draw the ZPD",instruction:"Draw three nested regions: what the child can do alone, what they can do with help, and what remains out of reach. Label the ZPD, then state what Vygotsky reverses about Piaget.",answer:"The ZPD is the middle band — between current independent ability and potential ability with guidance from a more knowledgeable other. Learning happens INSIDE this zone. The reversal: for Piaget, development enables learning (a child cannot learn what their stage does not permit); for Vygotsky, LEARNING DRIVES DEVELOPMENT — instruction pitched into the ZPD pulls the child forward. That is why scaffolding is a Vygotskian idea and readiness is a Piagetian one."},
  "MC-COG-BAILLARGEON":{type:"diagnose",title:"What Did Piaget's Task Confound?",instruction:"Explain the flaw Baillargeon identified in Piaget's object permanence task, then describe how the violation of expectation method avoids it.",answer:"Piaget's search task requires the infant to REACH for a hidden object, so it confounds knowledge with motor ability — an infant may know the object still exists but lack the coordination or the memory-plus-planning to retrieve it. Failure to search is therefore not proof of failure to understand. Baillargeon measures LOOKING TIME instead: infants are familiarised with a possible event, then shown an impossible one, and look significantly longer at the impossible event — implying object permanence far earlier than Piaget claimed."},
  "MC-COG-SELMAN":{type:"explain",title:"Selman's Method and What It Found",instruction:"Name Selman's method, summarise the Holly dilemma in one sentence, and say what he was measuring — then give one limitation of the method.",answer:"Method: the clinical interview, using hypothetical scenarios. Holly has promised her father she will not climb trees but finds a friend's kitten stuck up one; children are asked how each person would feel. Selman was measuring social perspective-taking — the ability to represent another person's viewpoint as distinct from your own — and identified five levels from egocentric to societal. Limitation: it measures what children SAY about a hypothetical situation, which may not match how they act in a real one."},
  "MC-COG-TOM":{type:"explain",title:"What Makes Sally-Anne Hard?",instruction:"Describe the task in two sentences, state the correct answer, and explain precisely what cognitive step a child must take to give it.",answer:"Sally hides a marble in a basket and leaves the room; Anne moves it to a box; the child is asked where Sally will look for it. The correct answer is 'the basket'. To give it the child must represent Sally's BELIEF as separate from reality — holding in mind something they know to be false, and attributing it to another person. That is a false belief task, and Baron-Cohen used it to argue that children with autism have a specific difficulty with theory of mind rather than a general cognitive deficit."},
  "MC-COG-MIRROR":{type:"correct",title:"When Do Mirror Neurons Fire?",instruction:"Complete the definition precisely, then explain what makes the discovery significant and one reason for caution.",prompt:"'Mirror neurons are cells that fire when ___ and also when ___.'",answer:"They fire when an individual PERFORMS an action and also when they OBSERVE the same action performed by another. Rizzolatti's team found this accidentally — motor neurons in a monkey's premotor cortex fired as the monkey watched a researcher pick up food. Significance: the same cells code doing and seeing, offering a possible neural basis for imitation, intention-reading and empathy. Caution: most direct single-cell evidence is from monkeys, and the leap to human empathy and autism is inference, not demonstration."},
  "MC-COG-COMPARE":{type:"compare",title:"The Engine of Development",instruction:"Complete the table, then answer in one sentence: for each theorist, does learning cause development or does development permit learning?",headers:["Feature","Piaget","Vygotsky"],rows:[["Child is best described as","",""],["Main driver of development","",""],["Role of other people","",""],["Universal stages?","",""]],answer:"Piaget: a lone scientist constructing understanding through interaction with the physical world; driver is individual discovery through disequilibrium; others are peers who create cognitive conflict; yes, universal invariant stages. Vygotsky: an apprentice; driver is social interaction and cultural tools, especially language; the more knowledgeable other is essential; no, development is culturally variable. Piaget — development permits learning. Vygotsky — learning drives development."},
  "MC-FOR-PROFILE":{type:"compare",title:"Organised vs Disorganised",instruction:"Complete the table, then state where the typology came from and give the strongest criticism of it.",headers:["Feature","Organised offender","Disorganised offender"],rows:[["Planning","",""],["Crime scene","",""],["Victim","",""],["Likely social/work profile","",""]],answer:"Organised: planned in advance; controlled scene with little evidence left; targeted victim; likely socially and sexually competent, in skilled work, may be married. Disorganised: little planning, impulsive; chaotic scene with evidence left behind; random victim; likely socially immature, unskilled or unemployed, living alone. Source: the FBI's Behavioral Science Unit interviewed 36 sexually motivated murderers, including Bundy and Berkowitz. Criticism: a self-selecting sample of 36 manipulative offenders is a weak basis for a typology, and Canter's analysis found the two types are not statistically distinct — organised features appear across most cases."},
  "MC-FOR-GEO":{type:"distinguish",title:"Marauder or Commuter?",instruction:"Define both, then say what the circle theory assumes about offenders and what it needs in order to work.",answer:"A marauder operates OUT FROM a home base that lies within the circle enclosing their offences — so the centre of the circle approximates their base. A commuter travels AWAY from their home area to offend, so the circle contains no useful information about where they live. The assumption is that offenders reveal themselves through spatial behaviour, offending within a comfort zone they know. It needs a series of linked offences — a single crime yields no pattern — which is why geographical profiling is only useful for serial offending."},
  "MC-FOR-BIO":{type:"evaluate",title:"Lombroso — What Survives?",instruction:"State Lombroso's claim and two of his supposed atavistic features, then give the two strongest criticisms and say what, if anything, the theory contributed.",answer:"Claim (1876): criminals are a biologically primitive separate species, unable to adjust to civilised society, so offending is innate rather than chosen. Features included a strong prominent jaw, high cheekbones, extra nipples, toes or fingers. Criticisms: (1) no control group of non-criminals, so the features were never shown to distinguish anything; (2) the theory is scientifically racist, describing features common in people of African descent as primitive. Contribution: it shifted criminology towards seeking causes of offending rather than assigning moral blame — the beginning of a scientific approach, on entirely unsound evidence."},
  "MC-FOR-PSYCH":{type:"chain",title:"Eysenck's Three Dimensions",instruction:"Name the three dimensions and the biological basis of each, then complete the chain for extraversion.",prompt:"Underactive reticular activating system → [step] → [step] → offending more likely.",answer:"Extraversion (underactive RAS), neuroticism (reactive sympathetic nervous system), psychoticism (linked to high testosterone), measured by the EPQ. Chain: chronically under-aroused, the extravert seeks stimulation → they take risks and are harder to condition, since conditioning depends on responding to punishment → socialisation fails to instil the anxiety that normally inhibits offending. The criminal personality scores high on all three. Key criticism: Mischel's argument that personality is not consistent across situations undermines the idea of a stable criminal type."},
  "MC-FOR-COG":{type:"sequence",title:"Kohlberg's Six Stages",instruction:"Name the three levels and their two stages each, then state which level offenders are more likely to reason at and what characterises that reasoning.",answer:"Pre-conventional: (1) avoid punishment, (2) gain personal reward. Conventional: (3) gain approval, (4) maintain law and order. Post-conventional: (5) social contract, (6) universal ethical principles. Offenders are more likely to reason at the PRE-CONVENTIONAL level — a crime is wrong because you get caught, not because it harms someone, so if the punishment can be evaded the reason not to offend disappears. Note the qualification: this is more true of crimes involving personal gain than impulsive violence."},
  "MC-FOR-DIFF":{type:"explain",title:"Learned Like Anything Else",instruction:"State what Sutherland claims is learned, from whom, and what determines whether someone offends. Then give one strength and one limitation.",answer:"Two things are learned: attitudes towards the law, and the specific TECHNIQUES for committing offences — both acquired through interaction within intimate personal groups, not from the media or strangers. Offending follows when pro-criminal attitudes outweigh anti-criminal ones. Strength: it explains crime across all social classes, including white-collar crime, without appealing to biology or poverty. Limitation: the ratio of attitudes is not measurable, so the theory cannot be tested precisely or used to predict who will offend."},
  "MC-FOR-TREAT":{type:"apply",title:"Design a Token Economy",instruction:"Name the principle it applies, then specify: two target behaviours, what the tokens are technically called, and two exchangeable rewards. Finish with the main limitation.",answer:"Principle: operant conditioning. Target behaviours must be OPERATIONALISED IN ADVANCE — e.g. keeping a cell tidy, attending education sessions. Tokens are SECONDARY reinforcers: valueless in themselves, valuable through exchange. Rewards (primary reinforcers): phone calls, extra exercise time, canteen items. Limitation: behaviour learned for tokens rarely generalises beyond custody — once the reinforcement schedule stops on release, the behaviour extinguishes, so it manages the institution more effectively than it rehabilitates the offender."},
  "MC-PATH-DEF":{type:"evaluate",title:"Failure to Function — Where It Breaks",instruction:"Name Rosenhan and Seligman's signs, give the definition's main strength, then explain the two situations where it misclassifies.",answer:"Signs: not conforming to interpersonal rules, severe personal distress, irrational or dangerous behaviour. Strength: it takes the INDIVIDUAL'S OWN EXPERIENCE seriously and is a practical threshold for when someone needs help. It misclassifies in two directions: (1) behaviour that deviates from social norms but harms nobody — an unconventional lifestyle — can be read as dysfunction, so it is culturally relative; (2) someone with a serious disorder may continue functioning normally at work and be missed entirely. Functioning and disorder are not the same variable."},
  "MC-PATH-PHOB":{type:"chain",title:"Acquired vs Maintained",instruction:"Complete both halves of Mowrer's model with the correct conditioning type, then explain why avoidance is the reason phobias persist.",prompt:"Acquisition: neutral stimulus + frightening event → [type of conditioning]. Maintenance: avoid the stimulus → [what happens] → [type of conditioning].",answer:"Acquisition is CLASSICAL: a neutral stimulus paired with something frightening becomes a conditioned stimulus — Watson and Rayner's Little Albert. Maintenance is OPERANT: avoiding the stimulus removes the anxiety, which is NEGATIVE REINFORCEMENT, so the avoidance behaviour is strengthened. Avoidance is why phobias persist — it prevents the person from ever experiencing the feared stimulus without harm, so the association is never extinguished. That is also exactly why treatment requires exposure."},
  "MC-PATH-DEP":{type:"apply",title:"Run the ABC Model",instruction:"State what A, B and C stand for, then complete the model for the same event with a rational and an irrational belief. Say what this shows about where the emotion comes from.",prompt:"A: you fail an exam.",answer:"A = Activating event, B = Belief, C = Consequence (emotional and behavioural). Rational B: 'That went badly, I need a better revision plan' → C: disappointment, then action. Irrational B: 'I must succeed at everything or I am worthless' → C: depression, withdrawal. Same event, different consequence — so the EVENT does not cause the emotion, the BELIEF does. That is the whole basis of REBT: dispute the belief, not the event, since the event cannot be changed."},
  "MC-PATH-OCD":{type:"explain",title:"COMT and SERT",instruction:"State what each gene does, what the OCD-associated variant does to the neurotransmitter, then explain what 'candidate gene' and 'polygenic' mean here.",answer:"COMT regulates dopamine; the variant more common in OCD patients produces HIGHER dopamine levels. SERT affects serotonin transport; the associated variant produces LOWER serotonin. 'Candidate gene' means a gene suspected of contributing, not one that causes the disorder. 'Polygenic' means many genes each contribute a small amount — Taylor identified up to 230 implicated genes — so no single gene test is possible. The standard evaluation: concordance in identical twins is well short of 100%, so genes create vulnerability, not certainty."},
  "MC-STR-GAS":{type:"sequence",title:"Alarm, Resistance, Exhaustion",instruction:"Name Selye's three stages in order with what happens physiologically in each. Then explain why he called it GENERAL.",answer:"Alarm: the stressor is perceived and the sympathomedullary pathway activates — adrenaline, raised heart rate, fight-or-flight. Resistance: the body adapts and appears to cope, with cortisol sustaining the response, but resources are being depleted. Exhaustion: resources are spent, the adrenal glands can no longer maintain output, and stress-related illness follows — ulcers, immunosuppression, cardiovascular damage. It is GENERAL because Selye exposed rats to widely different stressors and found the SAME physiological response regardless of the cause."},
  "MC-STR-HPA":{type:"chain",title:"The HPA Chain in Order",instruction:"Complete the chain naming each structure and hormone. Then state what distinguishes this pathway from the SAM pathway.",prompt:"Sustained stressor → hypothalamus → [hormone] → [structure] → [hormone] → [structure] → [hormone].",answer:"Hypothalamus secretes corticotrophin releasing hormone (CRH) → anterior pituitary secretes adrenocorticotrophic hormone (ACTH) → adrenal CORTEX secretes cortisol. Distinction: SAM is the ACUTE response — fast, adrenaline, adrenal MEDULLA, seconds. HPA is the CHRONIC response — slower, cortisol, adrenal CORTEX, minutes to hours, and sustained. Cortisol maintains glucose supply but suppresses immune function, which is the link to stress-related illness."},
  "MC-STR-LIFE":{type:"evaluate",title:"What the SRRS Measures",instruction:"State what the SRRS actually counts and give the top and bottom of its scale. Then give the two strongest criticisms.",answer:"It lists 43 life events ranked by the amount of READJUSTMENT each demands, from death of a spouse at 100 life change units down to minor violations of the law at 11; participants total the units for events in the past year. Criticisms: (1) it counts positive events (marriage, promotion) alongside negative ones as equally stressful, when the evidence suggests they differ; (2) it ignores individual differences in appraisal and ignores DAILY HASSLES, which several studies find predict health outcomes better than major life events do."},
  "MC-STR-DRUGS":{type:"chain",title:"How Benzodiazepines Calm",instruction:"Complete the chain, then contrast the mechanism with beta blockers and say which symptom each targets.",prompt:"BZ binds to GABA receptor → [step] → [step] → anxiety reduced.",answer:"The BZ enhances GABA's inhibitory effect at the receptor → more chloride ions enter the neuron → the neuron is hyperpolarised and less likely to fire, producing generalised reduced arousal. Beta blockers work entirely differently: they act on the heart and blood vessels, blocking adrenaline at beta-adrenergic receptors to lower heart rate and blood pressure. BZs target the PSYCHOLOGICAL experience of anxiety via the CNS; beta blockers target the PHYSICAL symptoms and leave the felt anxiety largely intact."},

  // ---- A-Level Economics — Themes 1 and 2 ----
  "MC-GRO-02":{type:"distinguish",title:"Actual or Potential Growth?",instruction:"For each case, state which type of growth and which diagram you would draw. Then say why examiners treat the confusion as a major error.",items:["Income tax cut raises consumer spending and short-run real GDP","Investment in education raises workforce skills","Discovery of a new oil field","Recovery from recession as spare capacity is used"],answer:"Actual growth (AD shifts right, movement along or towards LRAS): 1 and 4. Potential growth (LRAS shifts right): 2 and 3. They require DIFFERENT diagrams and different policies — actual growth uses existing capacity, potential growth expands the ceiling. An answer that treats a tax cut as raising productive potential has misunderstood which curve moves, so no amount of accurate detail afterwards can rescue it."},
  "MC-GRO-04":{type:"diagram",title:"Label the Trade Cycle",instruction:"Draw a trade cycle with a trend line. Label boom, downturn, recession, trough and recovery in the right places. Then define recession precisely and mark where it sits relative to the trend.",answer:"Sequence: boom (above trend) → downturn → recession → trough (lowest point) → recovery (GDP rising back towards trend) → boom. Recession is two consecutive quarters of NEGATIVE growth — a fall in real GDP, not merely growth below trend. Recovery is the upswing: GDP is rising, but still below trend, which is why students who draw it flat lose the mark. Output can be growing while still below potential."},
  "MC-GRO-05":{type:"explain",title:"Why PPP, Not Market Rates?",instruction:"Explain in two sentences why PPP exchange rates are used for cross-country GDP comparison, using a specific example of a non-traded good.",answer:"Market exchange rates are driven by traded goods and by speculative capital flows, so they reveal little about what money actually buys at home. A haircut, a bus fare or a bowl of rice costs far less in a low-income country, so converting at market rates understates real living standards there — PPP adjusts for the domestic cost of a comparable basket, which is why PPP-adjusted GDP per capita gaps are consistently narrower than market-rate ones."},
  "MC-GRO-06":{type:"evaluate",title:"The Easterlin Paradox",instruction:"State the paradox precisely — what holds within a country and what fails across time. Then give the two standard explanations and one policy implication.",answer:"Within a country at a point in time, richer people report greater happiness; but as a whole country grows richer over decades, average reported happiness barely moves. Explanations: (1) RELATIVE income — what matters is your position against others, and growth raises everyone; (2) the HEDONIC TREADMILL — people adapt to a higher standard and reset their expectations. Implication: raising GDP is not a sufficient objective, which is why governments have begun measuring wellbeing directly alongside output."},
  "MC-INF-04":{type:"sort",title:"What Else Pushes Costs Up?",instruction:"Wages are one cause of cost-push inflation. Sort these into cost-push causes and things that are NOT, then draw what happens on AD/AS.",items:["Oil price shock","A rise in consumer confidence","Sterling depreciation raising import prices","An increase in VAT","Government spending increase","A fall in labour productivity"],answer:"Cost-push: oil shock, depreciation, VAT rise, falling productivity (raises unit labour costs). NOT cost-push: consumer confidence and government spending — those are DEMAND-pull, shifting AD right. Cost-push shifts SRAS LEFT, raising the price level while REDUCING output — the distinguishing signature. Demand-pull raises price level and output together."},
  "MC-INF-05":{type:"evaluate",title:"Is All Deflation Bad?",instruction:"Distinguish malign from benign deflation, giving the cause and one real example of each. Then say which one Japan experienced and why it was so hard to escape.",answer:"Malign: falling AD → lower prices alongside falling output, rising unemployment, and a rising REAL debt burden → consumers delay purchases expecting further falls → a deflationary spiral. Benign: productivity gains or technological progress shift SRAS right → lower prices WITH rising output, as in consumer electronics. Japan in the 1990s–2000s experienced malign deflation; it was hard to escape because nominal interest rates hit zero, so conventional monetary policy could not lower real rates further."},
  "MC-INF-06":{type:"compare",title:"CPI vs RPI",instruction:"Complete the table, then state which is the official measure and why the other was downgraded.",headers:["Feature","CPI","RPI"],rows:[["Averaging method","",""],["Mortgage interest included?","",""],["Council tax included?","",""],["Usually higher or lower?","",""]],answer:"CPI: geometric mean; excludes mortgage interest payments and council tax; usually LOWER. RPI: arithmetic mean; includes mortgage interest; usually HIGHER. CPI is the official target measure (2%). RPI lost its National Statistic status because of a formula effect — the arithmetic mean introduces an upward bias — though it is still used for some index-linked contracts, which is why the gap between them has real fiscal consequences."},
  "MC-EMP-02":{type:"classify",title:"Which Unemployment, Which Policy?",instruction:"Classify each case and give the policy that would actually address it. The policy is the point — the wrong classification produces the wrong policy.",items:["Coal mines close permanently; workers' skills don't match tech vacancies","A graduate spends two months searching for their first job","Demand collapses in a recession and firms lay off across all sectors","Hotel staff laid off at the end of the summer season"],answer:"1 Structural → retraining, relocation support, education. 2 Frictional → better job-matching information, employment services. 3 Cyclical (demand-deficient) → expansionary fiscal or monetary policy to raise AD. 4 Seasonal → diversification of the local economy. Using demand-side policy against structural unemployment is the classic error: raising AD cannot help if the vacancies require skills the unemployed do not have."},
  "MC-EMP-04":{type:"compare",title:"Claimant Count vs ILO",instruction:"UK claimant count 1.6m, ILO unemployment 1.4m. Complete the table, then give two reasons the figures differ.",headers:["Feature","Claimant count","ILO/LFS measure"],rows:[["Data source","",""],["Who is counted","",""],["Cost and speed","",""]],answer:"Claimant count: administrative data on those claiming Universal Credit or JSA for unemployment reasons; cheap and fast but changes whenever eligibility rules change. ILO: Labour Force Survey — those out of work, actively seeking and available to start within two weeks; internationally comparable but slower and sample-based. Differences: some claimants are not actively seeking (so excluded from ILO) while some genuine job seekers do not qualify for or do not claim benefits (so excluded from the claimant count)."},
  "MC-EMP-05":{type:"diagram",title:"Label the Gap",instruction:"Draw a labour market diagram with a minimum wage set above equilibrium. Label W_min, Qd, Qs and the gap. Name the gap precisely, then say what the gap is NOT.",answer:"At W_min, quantity of labour supplied exceeds quantity demanded. The gap (Qs − Qd) is REAL-WAGE (classical) unemployment. It is NOT the total number of unemployed people in the economy, and it is NOT the number who lost jobs — part of the gap is workers newly ATTRACTED into the market by the higher wage who cannot find work. Losing that distinction is what costs the mark."},
  "MC-EMP-08":{type:"chain",title:"Why Temporary Becomes Permanent",instruction:"Complete the chain, then explain what hysteresis implies about how quickly a government should respond to a recession.",prompt:"Recession causes job losses → [step] → [step] → [step] → the unemployment persists after recovery.",answer:"Long spells out of work erode skills and up-to-date experience → workers lose contact with workplace routines and networks → employers use long unemployment as a screening signal and stop interviewing them → they become effectively unemployable, so the natural rate itself rises. Implication: acting fast matters more than acting perfectly — a temporary demand shock left untreated inflicts PERMANENT supply-side damage, which is the core argument for early intervention."},
  "MC-BOP-01":{type:"evaluate",title:"Is a 3.1% Deficit Bad?",instruction:"A student writes that the UK's current account deficit is 'very bad'. Give two conditions under which it matters and two under which it does not. Finish with the decisive question.",answer:"It matters if: financed by short-term 'hot money' that can reverse suddenly (a sudden stop → currency crisis); or if it reflects declining competitiveness and a hollowed-out export base. It does not matter much if: financed by long-term FDI, which brings productive capacity; or if it reflects strong domestic growth pulling in capital imports and investment goods. The decisive question is HOW IT IS FINANCED and WHAT THE IMPORTS ARE — deficits funding investment differ fundamentally from deficits funding consumption."},
  "MC-BOP-04":{type:"correct",title:"The Missing Condition",instruction:"A student claims a depreciation will 'definitely' improve the current account. Name the condition they have ignored, state it precisely, and explain what happens if it fails.",answer:"The Marshall-Lerner condition: a depreciation improves the current account ONLY IF PEDx + PEDm > 1. If the sum is below 1, demand is too inelastic for volume effects to outweigh the worsened price effect — imports simply cost more for roughly the same quantity — so the current account WORSENS. This also explains the J-curve: elasticities are low in the short run as contracts and habits adjust, so the balance deteriorates before it improves."},
  "MC-BOP-05":{type:"sort",title:"Four Components, One Account",instruction:"Name the four components of the current account, then classify each transaction.",items:["UK exports of cars to Germany","A UK bank selling insurance to a US firm","Dividends paid to a UK resident holding foreign shares","UK government foreign aid payment","A German tourist's hotel spending in London"],answer:"Components: trade in goods (visible balance), trade in services (invisible), primary income (investment income and wages from abroad), secondary income (transfers — aid, remittances). Classification: 1 goods, 2 services, 3 primary income, 4 secondary income, 5 SERVICES (tourism is an export of services — the standard trap, since no physical good crosses a border)."},
  "MC-BOP-06":{type:"sort",title:"Switching or Reducing?",instruction:"Sort each policy, then explain the mechanism that distinguishes the two families and give one drawback of each.",items:["Devaluation of the currency","Raising income tax","Imposing tariffs on imports","Cutting government spending","Import quotas","Raising interest rates"],answer:"Expenditure-SWITCHING (redirect spending from foreign to domestic goods without cutting total spending): devaluation, tariffs, quotas. Expenditure-REDUCING (cut total demand, so imports fall with everything else): income tax rise, spending cuts, higher rates. Drawbacks: switching invites retaliation and breaches trade agreements; reducing lowers growth and raises unemployment — it treats the deficit by shrinking the economy."},
  "MC-BOP-08":{type:"explain",title:"Why the Accounts Must Balance",instruction:"Explain in two sentences why a current account deficit must be matched elsewhere in the balance of payments, then name what the country is doing in real terms.",answer:"The balance of payments balances by construction: a current account deficit means the country buys more from abroad than it sells, and those purchases must be paid for — by selling assets to foreigners or borrowing from them, which is recorded as a financial account SURPLUS. In real terms the country is running down its net foreign asset position: consuming more than it produces and financing the gap by transferring ownership of assets or incurring future obligations."},
  "MC-AD-03":{type:"chain",title:"Confidence to AD",instruction:"Complete the chain, then state which curve moves and which definitely does NOT.",prompt:"Consumer confidence falls → [step] → [step] → AD shifts ___.",answer:"Households become uncertain about future income and employment → precautionary saving rises and big-ticket purchases are deferred → consumption falls → AD shifts LEFT. Confidence is a DEMAND-side factor: it does not change firms' production costs, so SRAS does not move, and it does not change productive capacity, so LRAS does not move. Students who shift SRAS for a confidence shock have confused a willingness-to-spend change with a cost change."},
  "MC-AD-05":{type:"chain",title:"Depreciation to Net Exports",instruction:"Trace the effect of a sterling depreciation through to AD in two branches, then name the assumption the whole chain rests on.",prompt:"Sterling depreciates → [branch X] → [branch M] → net exports → AD.",answer:"Exports branch: UK goods are cheaper in foreign currency → more competitive abroad → X rises. Imports branch: foreign goods cost more in sterling → consumers switch to domestic substitutes → M falls. Both raise (X − M), a component of AD, so AD shifts right. The assumption: the Marshall-Lerner condition holds (PEDx + PEDm > 1). Without it the volume responses are too weak and the chain fails."},
  "MC-AD-06":{type:"distinguish",title:"Move Along or Shift?",instruction:"For each change, state movement along AD or shift of AD, and give the reason. Then name the three effects that make AD slope downwards.",items:["The general price level rises","Government increases infrastructure spending","Interest rates are cut","CPI inflation falls to 1%"],answer:"Movement ALONG: 1 and 4 — only a change in the PRICE LEVEL moves you along the curve. Shift: 2 and 3 — changes to C, I, G or (X−M) from any other cause. The three effects behind the downward slope: the real balance effect (savings buy less), the interest rate effect (higher prices raise money demand and rates), and the trade effect (domestic goods become less competitive)."},
  "MC-AD-08":{type:"calculate",title:"MPC or APC?",instruction:"Income rises from £20,000 to £25,000; consumption rises from £18,000 to £21,500. Calculate both the MPC and the new APC, then state which one drives the multiplier.",answer:"MPC = change in consumption ÷ change in income = £3,500 ÷ £5,000 = 0.7. APC = total consumption ÷ total income = £21,500 ÷ £25,000 = 0.86. They are different questions: MPC is about the EXTRA pound, APC about the whole income. The MULTIPLIER depends on the MPC (or on marginal withdrawals), not the APC — using the average in the multiplier formula is the error this tests."},
  "MC-AS-02":{type:"sort",title:"Which Way Does SRAS Move?",instruction:"Sort each into 'shifts SRAS left' or 'shifts SRAS right', then state the single test that decides every case.",items:["Wage rises above productivity growth","Fall in world oil prices","Increase in VAT","Sterling appreciation lowering import costs","New energy price cap raising business tariffs"],answer:"Left (costs rise): wage rises above productivity, VAT increase, higher energy tariffs. Right (costs fall): falling oil prices, appreciation cutting import costs. The single test: does it change the COST OF PRODUCTION at each price level? If yes, SRAS moves — up-and-left for higher costs. If it changes willingness to buy rather than cost to produce, it is AD."},
  "MC-AS-04":{type:"sort",title:"What Actually Shifts LRAS?",instruction:"Sort into 'shifts LRAS right' and 'does not shift LRAS', then state the test.",items:["Investment in new capital equipment","A cut in interest rates","Improved education and training","A rise in consumer spending","Deregulation of a protected industry","Net inward migration of working-age people"],answer:"Shifts LRAS right: capital investment, education and training, deregulation, inward migration. Does not: interest rate cut and consumer spending — both raise AD only. The test: does it change the QUANTITY OR QUALITY of the factors of production, or remove a barrier to their efficient use? Anything that merely raises willingness to spend leaves productive capacity untouched."},
  "MC-AS-06":{type:"calculate",title:"Unit Labour Costs",instruction:"Wages rise 6%; productivity rises 2%. State what happens to unit labour costs, which curve moves and in which direction, and name the type of inflation produced.",answer:"Unit labour cost = wage cost per unit of output, so it rises by roughly 4% — wages are outpacing what each worker produces. Each unit costs more, so SRAS shifts LEFT, raising the price level while reducing output: COST-PUSH inflation. The reverse is the key policy point — if productivity growth matches or exceeds wage growth, wages can rise with no inflationary pressure at all, which is why productivity is the central supply-side objective."},
  "MC-AS-07":{type:"diagram",title:"Three Sections, Three Policies",instruction:"Draw the Keynesian AS curve. Label the horizontal, upward-sloping and vertical sections, and write the effect of an AD increase in each.",answer:"Horizontal section — deep recession with mass unemployment: idle workers and machines can be brought into use, so raising AD increases OUTPUT with no price rise. Fiscal stimulus is effective here. Upward-sloping section — spare capacity shrinking: AD raises both output and prices. Vertical section — full capacity: raising AD is PURELY INFLATIONARY, output cannot rise. The whole point of the shape is that the right policy depends on where the economy currently sits."},
  "MC-NI-03":{type:"distinguish",title:"GDP or GNI?",instruction:"Define both, then decide which is larger for each country and say what that reveals.",items:["Ireland, with large foreign-owned multinationals repatriating profits","The Philippines, with many citizens working abroad and sending money home"],answer:"GDP = output produced WITHIN borders regardless of ownership. GNI = GDP + net primary income from abroad. Ireland: GDP much LARGER than GNI, because profits earned in Ireland flow out to foreign owners — GDP overstates what Irish residents actually receive. Philippines: GNI larger than GDP, because remittances flow in. The gap reveals who OWNS the income, which is why GNI is the better living-standards measure."},
  "MC-NI-04":{type:"calculate",title:"Strip Out the Inflation",instruction:"Nominal GDP rises 6%; inflation is 4%. State approximate real growth and explain what real GDP holds constant. Then say which one you must use to compare 2015 with 2025.",answer:"Real growth ≈ 6% − 4% = 2%. Real GDP values output at BASE-YEAR prices, holding the price level constant so that only changes in the quantity of goods and services show up. Nominal GDP can rise purely because prices rose, with output unchanged. Any comparison across time — or any growth rate — must use REAL GDP, otherwise inflation is counted as if it were extra output."},
  "MC-NI-06":{type:"evaluate",title:"Two Marks, One Limitation",instruction:"Write a full 2-mark answer giving ONE limitation of GDP for comparing living standards: state it, then develop it. Then list three other limitations you did not use.",answer:"Model answer: 'GDP per capita ignores the DISTRIBUTION of income (1), so a country with high average income but extreme inequality may have most of its population living in poverty despite a high GDP figure (1).' The structure is point plus development — a list of five undeveloped limitations scores 1. Others available: no PPP adjustment for cost of living, excludes the informal economy, ignores environmental degradation, omits unpaid household and voluntary work, says nothing about leisure or working hours."},
  "MC-NI-08":{type:"distinguish",title:"Three Methods, One Number",instruction:"Name the three methods of measuring GDP and what each sums. Then explain why the output method uses VALUE ADDED rather than total sales.",answer:"Output/production: sum of value added by each industry. Income: sum of factor incomes — wages, profits, rent, interest. Expenditure: C + I + G + (X − M). All three yield the same figure, because one person's spending is another's income is another's output. Value added is used to avoid DOUBLE COUNTING: if you summed total sales, the steel in a car would be counted when sold to the manufacturer and again inside the finished car."},
  "MC-TECH-01":{type:"diagnose",title:"Answer the Question Set",instruction:"A student asked about METHODS OF RAISING tax revenue writes entirely about what the government could spend it on. Name the error, then list what the answer should have covered.",answer:"The error is answering an adjacent question rather than the one set — flagged explicitly in the 2022 examiner report. Methods of RAISING revenue means: which taxes (income, VAT, corporation, capital gains), at what rates, on what base; direct versus indirect; the incidence and elasticity implications; the Laffer curve; and administrative feasibility and avoidance. Spending is a different topic. No amount of accurate economics scores if it is not the economics asked for."},
  "MC-TECH-02":{type:"rewrite",title:"Add the Evaluation",instruction:"A 12-mark answer (8 KAA + 4 EV) on expansionary fiscal policy and unemployment contains only analysis. Write three evaluation sentences that would earn the EV marks.",prompt:"Question: Evaluate whether expansionary fiscal policy is the most effective way to reduce unemployment.",answer:"1. 'This depends on the TYPE of unemployment: fiscal stimulus raises AD and so addresses cyclical unemployment, but cannot help structural unemployment where the skills mismatch remains.' 2. 'The size of the effect depends on the multiplier, which is smaller where the MPM is high, so much of the stimulus leaks abroad.' 3. 'In the long run, supply-side policies such as retraining may be more effective, though they act far more slowly and cost more up front.' EV means weighing magnitude, conditions and alternatives — not adding 'however, it could be argued'."},
  "MC-MON-03":{type:"chain",title:"The Cost of Raising Rates",instruction:"Complete the chain for a rate rise, then name three trade-offs the student's claim ignores.",prompt:"Base rate rises → [step] → [step] → inflation falls.",answer:"Borrowing costs rise and saving becomes more attractive → consumption and investment fall, and the exchange rate appreciates, reducing net exports → AD falls, easing demand-pull inflation. Trade-offs ignored: (1) lower growth and higher unemployment — the Phillips curve trade-off; (2) mortgage holders and indebted firms are hit hardest, so the burden is distributionally uneven; (3) it is ineffective against COST-PUSH inflation, where the cause is supply-side, and may deepen the downturn without addressing the source."},
  "MC-SSP-01":{type:"diagram",title:"Which Curve Do Supply-Side Policies Move?",instruction:"Draw AD/LRAS and show the effect of a successful supply-side policy. State the two things it must do to count, then give the twin benefit it uniquely delivers.",answer:"LRAS shifts RIGHT. To count, a policy must either improve the QUANTITY OR QUALITY of the factors of production (labour, capital, enterprise, land) or remove a barrier to efficient use. The unique twin benefit: output rises AND the price level falls — supply-side policy is the only route to growth without inflation, which is why it is the standard evaluation counterweight to demand-side stimulus. Its weakness is the long time lag."},
  "MC-SSP-02":{type:"sort",title:"Market-Based or Interventionist?",instruction:"Sort each policy, then state the ideological difference in one sentence and give one criticism of each family.",items:["Deregulation of a protected industry","Government spending on adult skills training","Cutting corporation tax","Infrastructure investment in rail","Trade union reform","Subsidies for business R&D"],answer:"Market-based: deregulation, corporation tax cut, trade union reform. Interventionist: skills training, rail infrastructure, R&D subsidies. Difference: market-based policies remove government from markets to sharpen incentives; interventionist policies use government spending to correct market failures in provision. Criticisms: market-based policies can worsen inequality and job security; interventionist policies carry high opportunity cost and risk government failure in picking winners."},
  "MC-INQ-04":{type:"rewrite",title:"Two Marks on Inequality",instruction:"Write a 2-mark answer giving ONE cause of UK income inequality — state it, then develop it with a mechanism. Then name three other causes.",answer:"Model answer: 'Differences in education and skills cause income inequality (1), because higher-skilled workers command higher wages in a labour market where demand for graduate-level skills has grown faster than supply (1).' Others available: regional economic imbalances, discrimination, globalisation (skilled workers gain while unskilled face import competition), technological change automating routine jobs, and the design of the tax and benefit system. The mark is for the MECHANISM, not the label."},
  "MC-INQ-05":{type:"distinguish",title:"Stock or Flow?",instruction:"Classify each as income or wealth, then give the case that proves they are independent.",items:["Annual salary of £30,000","A house worth £400,000","State pension payments","A pension pot of £250,000","Dividends received this year"],answer:"Income (a FLOW, £ per year): salary, state pension payments, dividends. Wealth (a STOCK, £ at a point in time): the house, the pension pot. The proof they are independent: an asset-rich retiree with a paid-off house and a large pension pot may have a very low annual income, while a high-earning graduate with student debt has high income and NEGATIVE wealth. The 2019 examiner report flagged this confusion specifically."},
  "MC-INQ-07":{type:"calculate",title:"Progressive, Proportional or Regressive?",instruction:"For each tax, calculate the share of income paid by someone on £15,000 and someone on £60,000, then classify the tax.",items:["A flat £1,000 charge on everyone","20% of all income","UK income tax: 20% basic, 40% higher"],answer:"1: 6.7% vs 1.7% — REGRESSIVE (falls more heavily on lower incomes as a proportion). 2: 20% vs 20% — PROPORTIONAL. 3: rising marginal rate means a rising average rate — PROGRESSIVE. The definition turns on the PROPORTION OF INCOME paid, not the cash amount: VAT is regressive even though everyone pays the same rate, because lower earners spend a larger share of their income."},
  "MC-POV-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Absolute or Relative?",instruction:"Define both with their standard thresholds, then explain how a country can eliminate one while the other rises.",answer:"Absolute poverty: a FIXED standard — can you afford the basic necessities of survival? World Bank line $2.15 a day. Relative poverty: a MOVING standard — how far below typical income are you? UK measure: below 60% of median income. A country can grow so that everyone clears the absolute threshold while incomes at the top pull away from the median, raising relative poverty. Absolute poverty measures deprivation; relative poverty measures inequality — which is why they can move in opposite directions."},
  "MC-POV-04":{type:"evaluate",title:"Which Policy Hits Absolute Poverty?",instruction:"Rank these by how DIRECTLY each reduces absolute poverty, and justify your top and bottom choices.",items:["Raising the National Living Wage","Free school meals and social housing","Cutting corporation tax to stimulate growth","Increasing Universal Credit payments"],answer:"Most direct: increasing Universal Credit and providing essentials in kind (free school meals, social housing) — they raise the floor immediately for those below it. The National Living Wage is direct but reaches only those IN WORK, missing the poorest who are not. Least direct: corporation tax cuts, which rely on a long and uncertain trickle-down chain through investment and employment. Directness matters because absolute poverty is urgent — a policy that works in five years does not feed anyone this winter."},
  "MC-FMK-01":{type:"classify",title:"Is That a Role of Financial Markets?",instruction:"Mark each Y or N. For each Y, name which of the six roles it is.",items:["Offering deposit accounts and bonds","Providing mortgages and business loans","Setting the government's fiscal policy","Operating payment and settlement systems","Forward markets in commodities and currencies","Enabling equity trading in a secondary market"],answer:"Y: 1 (facilitating saving), 2 (lending to individuals and firms), 4 (facilitating exchange of goods and services), 5 (providing forward markets to hedge risk), 6 (providing a market for equities). N: 3 — fiscal policy is set by government, not markets. The six roles are examined directly, and the equity role has two halves: raising new capital in the primary market and providing liquidity in the secondary market."},
  "MC-FMK-03":{type:"distinguish",title:"Adverse Selection or Moral Hazard?",instruction:"Label each case, then state the timing rule that separates them.",items:["Riskier borrowers are the most likely to apply for a loan","A bank takes excessive risk knowing it will be bailed out","Someone buys insurance then drives less carefully","Only people expecting to claim buy the extended warranty"],answer:"Adverse selection: 1, 4 — the problem exists BEFORE the contract, in who chooses to enter it. Moral hazard: 2, 3 — the problem arises AFTER the contract, in changed behaviour once protected. The timing rule: adverse selection is about SELECTION into the deal, moral hazard about BEHAVIOUR once inside it. Both stem from asymmetric information — the borrower knows their own risk and the lender does not — and both were central to 2008's 'too big to fail'."},
  "MC-BNK-03":{type:"evaluate",title:"Was the 2008 Bailout Right?",instruction:"Write a 4-mark evaluation: one sentence for, one against, and a judgement with a condition. Use at least two of the four concepts the examiner report identified.",answer:"For: systemic risk meant a major bank's failure would have cascaded through the payments system and the wider economy, as the run on Northern Rock demonstrated in miniature. Against: the bailout created moral hazard — banks rescued once have an incentive to take excessive risk again — and carried a large opportunity cost in public funds. Judgement: intervention was justified given the systemic threat, but only if paired with structural reform (ring-fencing, higher capital requirements) that reduces the chance of repetition — otherwise the rescue purchases stability now at the cost of a larger crisis later."},
  "MC-SD-02":{type:"diagram",title:"Which Curve Moves for a Cost Rise?",instruction:"Barista wages rise, raising the cost of producing coffee. Draw the diagram, state which curve moves and in which direction, and say what happens to price and quantity. Then state which curve definitely does NOT move.",answer:"SUPPLY shifts LEFT (up), because at every price producers will now offer less. Equilibrium price rises and quantity falls. The DEMAND curve does not move — a change in production cost has no effect on consumers' willingness to buy at a given price; the new equilibrium is reached by a MOVEMENT ALONG demand. Shifting demand for a cost change is the single most common diagram error at this level."},
  "MC-TECH-04":{type:"correct",title:"Label the Axes Properly",instruction:"A student labels the vertical axis of an AD/AS diagram 'price'. Explain why that loses marks, give the correct label for both axes, and say what 'price' would be correct for.",answer:"AD/AS is a MACROeconomic model: the vertical axis is the GENERAL PRICE LEVEL — an index of all prices, like CPI — and the horizontal axis is REAL GDP or real output. 'Price' means the price of one good, which belongs on a microeconomic supply and demand diagram. Examiners flag this consistently because the wrong label signals the student is reasoning about a single market when the question is about the whole economy."},
  "MC-TECH-05":{type:"calculate",title:"Work the Multiplier",instruction:"MPC = 0.8. Calculate the multiplier, then the final change in income from a £2bn injection. Write both formulas. Then say what happens to the multiplier if the MPM rises.",answer:"Multiplier = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5, or equivalently 1 ÷ MPW where MPW = MPS + MPT + MPM. Final ΔY = £2bn × 5 = £10bn. The mechanism: the injection becomes income, recipients spend a fraction (MPC), that spending becomes someone else's income, and so on. If the MPM rises, more of each round leaks abroad, so MPW rises, the multiplier FALLS, and the same injection produces less domestic income — which is why open economies have smaller multipliers."},

  // ---- A-Level Sociology, and single-bank tags across Business, Maths, German, Computer Science ----
  "MC-EDU-01":{type:"explain",title:"Role Allocation — and Who Disagrees",instruction:"State what Davis and Moore mean by role allocation and the mechanism they propose. Then give the Marxist objection in one sentence.",answer:"Education sifts and grades people through exams and qualifications, allocating the most talented to the most functionally important and highest-rewarded roles — so inequality of reward is functional, because it motivates the able to undertake long training. Marxist objection: the system does not measure talent neutrally, it reproduces class advantage, so what looks like meritocratic sorting is the legitimation of an inequality that already existed. Bowles and Gintis call the belief in fair sorting 'the myth of meritocracy'."},
  "MC-EDU-03":{type:"correct",title:"Answer the 'Less Intelligent' Claim",instruction:"A student writes that working-class pupils underachieve 'because they are less intelligent'. Give one external and one internal sociological factor that challenge this, each with a named sociologist.",answer:"External — material deprivation: Flaherty shows low income means no books, no tutor, no quiet study space and often part-time work, so the same ability produces lower attainment. Internal — labelling and the self-fulfilling prophecy: Becker's 'ideal pupil' research shows teachers judge working-class pupils against a middle-class standard, and the resulting streaming and low expectations depress attainment. Both explain the OUTCOME without any claim about innate ability, which is exactly why the student's claim is not a sociological explanation at all."},
  "MC-EDU-CLASS":{type:"apply",title:"Cultural Capital in the Classroom",instruction:"Read the scenario, then identify which features reward cultural capital rather than ability. Say what Bourdieu concludes from that.",prompt:"A school rewards confident class discussion, expects familiarity with museums and classical references, and marks essays for 'sophisticated expression'.",answer:"All three reward dispositions acquired at home rather than knowledge taught in school: confidence in talk, familiarity with high culture, and a valued register of language. Bourdieu's conclusion is that the education system is not neutral — it rewards the tastes, language and confidence of the dominant class, so middle-class children arrive already fluent in what schools value and APPEAR more able. Their advantage is converted into qualifications (cultural capital into educational capital) and looks like merit."},
  "MC-EDU-INSCHOOL":{type:"chain",title:"League Tables to Educational Triage",instruction:"Complete the chain, then name the group that loses out and explain why the school's behaviour is rational.",prompt:"League tables measure the % reaching a threshold grade → [step] → [step] → educational triage.",answer:"Schools are judged on the proportion crossing a single boundary → effort is concentrated on borderline pupils, where marginal input changes the published figure → pupils judged certain to pass and those judged hopeless are both deprioritised. The 'hopeless' group loses most. It is rational because the school is optimising the measure it is held to, not the education it provides — Gillborn and Youdell's A-to-C economy is a case of a performance indicator reshaping the behaviour it was meant to monitor."},
  "MC-EDU-GENDER":{type:"sort",title:"External or Internal?",instruction:"Sort each explanation for girls' improved achievement, then state which type most sociologists regard as the primary driver and why.",items:["The impact of feminism on expectations","GCSE coursework suiting girls' work patterns","More female-headed households creating breadwinner role models","Removal of gender stereotyping from textbooks","Changes in the labour market opening careers to women","Teachers' higher expectations of girls' behaviour"],answer:"External (outside school): feminism, female-headed households, labour market change. Internal (inside school): coursework, textbook destereotyping, teacher expectations. Most sociologists treat the EXTERNAL changes as primary — girls' ambitions changed first, as McRobbie's comparison of girls' magazines across decades shows, and the internal reforms then removed obstacles to acting on them. Note the standard evaluation: the gender gap is now smaller than the class gap, so gender alone is a partial explanation."},
  "MC-EDU-PERSPECTIVE":{type:"evaluate",title:"The New Right Argument",instruction:"State the New Right's diagnosis and their proposed remedy with two specific mechanisms. Then give the strongest criticism.",answer:"Diagnosis: a state monopoly has no competitive pressure, so schools have no incentive to improve and become unresponsive to parents. Remedy: apply market logic through parental choice, league tables, open enrolment and formula funding, so good schools expand and poor ones must improve or close. Criticism: the market advantages parents with the cultural capital and mobility to navigate it — Gewirtz's 'privileged-skilled choosers' — so competition redistributes existing advantage rather than raising standards evenly. Note the tension in the position: it demands a free market AND a compulsory national curriculum."},
  "MC-MET-THEORY":{type:"match",title:"Theory to Method",instruction:"Match each theoretical position to its typical methods and priority. Then explain why the pairing follows from what each believes society IS.",headers:["Position","Preferred methods","Priority"],rows:[["Positivism","",""],["Interpretivism","",""]],answer:"Positivism: questionnaires, structured interviews, official statistics; prioritises reliability, representativeness and generalisability. Interpretivism: participant observation, unstructured interviews, personal documents; prioritises validity and meaning. The pairing follows from ontology: positivists model sociology on natural science and believe society has observable external patterns that can be measured, so quantitative methods fit. Interpretivists hold that social reality is constructed through the MEANINGS actors give their actions, which cannot be captured by a tick-box — you have to ask, and listen."},
  "MC-BUS-03":{type:"correct",title:"Skimming Is the Opposite",instruction:"A student writes that price skimming means setting the lowest possible price. Correct it, then complete the table.",headers:["Strategy","Initial price","Target customer","Price over time"],rows:[["Skimming","","",""],["Penetration","","",""]],answer:"Skimming sets a HIGH initial price — the student has described penetration. Skimming: high initial price, early adopters willing to pay a premium (new smartphone launches), price falls as competition arrives and the segment is exhausted. Penetration: low initial price, mass market, price rises once market share and customer habit are established. Both are launch strategies; the choice depends on whether the product's appeal is novelty (skim) or value (penetrate)."},
  "MC-MATH-SPEC":{type:"calculate",title:"Gradient Between Two Points",instruction:"Find the gradient of the line through (2, 5) and (6, 13), writing the formula before substituting. Then do the same for (6, 13) and (2, 5) and explain why the answer is unchanged.",answer:"Gradient = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁) = (13 − 5) ÷ (6 − 2) = 8 ÷ 4 = 2. Reversing the points gives (5 − 13) ÷ (2 − 6) = −8 ÷ −4 = 2 — unchanged, because both differences change sign and the negatives cancel. The error this catches is subtracting in a DIFFERENT ORDER on top and bottom, e.g. (13 − 5) ÷ (2 − 6) = −2, which flips the sign of the gradient."},
  "MC-DE-07":{type:"correct",title:"Grammatical, Not Natural, Gender",instruction:"Find and correct the pronoun error in each sentence, then state the rule in one line.",items:["Ich habe das Buch gelesen, weil ich ihn interessant finde.","Der Tisch ist neu — ich habe sie gestern gekauft.","Die Schule ist groß, aber es ist alt.","Das Mädchen kommt später, weil sie noch arbeitet."],answer:"1: ihn → es (das Buch, neuter). 2: sie → ihn (der Tisch, masculine, accusative). 3: es → sie (die Schule, feminine). 4: CORRECT as written in everyday usage, though strictly das Mädchen is neuter (es) — the classic case where natural and grammatical gender pull apart. Rule: the pronoun matches the GRAMMATICAL gender of the noun it replaces, not the natural gender of what the noun refers to."},
  "MC-CS-ETHICS":{type:"match",title:"Which Data Protection Principle?",instruction:"Match each scenario to the principle it breaches, then state which principle is most often forgotten in exam answers.",items:["A shop keeps customer records for fifteen years with no reason","Data is stored unencrypted on an unlocked laptop","A charity uses donor emails to sell insurance","Records still list a customer's old address after they notified a change"],answer:"1 Storage limitation (kept no longer than necessary). 2 Integrity and confidentiality (security). 3 Purpose limitation (used only for the stated purpose). 4 Accuracy (kept up to date). The one most often forgotten is STORAGE LIMITATION — students recall security and consent but rarely mention that holding data indefinitely is itself a breach, even if it is held securely and was collected lawfully."},
  "MC-RS-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Aquinas: Locate the Logical Step",instruction:"Choose the step that makes the Unmoved Mover necessary before the reveal. Put Aquinas’s reasoning in order, then distinguish it from Anselm’s a priori argument.",items:["motion requires a mover","an infinite regress cannot explain the chain","there must be a first mover","the first mover is itself unmoved"],answer:"Motion requires a mover → an infinite regress cannot explain the chain → there must be a first mover → that mover is unmoved. This is a cosmological, a posteriori argument, unlike Anselm’s ontological argument."},
  "MC-RS-02":{type:"correct",title:"Hume Attacks the Analogy",instruction:"Write Hume’s criticism in one sentence before the reveal, then add the specific disanalogy between a watch and the universe.",prompt:"Paley compares the universe to a watch. What is Hume’s most direct criticism?",answer:"An analogy is only persuasive when the two things are sufficiently similar. We have experience of watches being designed, but not of universes being made; the universe is not sufficiently like a watch. Hume also notes that the conclusion could allow multiple or imperfect designers."},
  "MC-RS-03":{type:"correct",title:"Kant: Existence Is Not a Predicate",instruction:"Repair the Ontological Argument before the reveal. State the exact premise Kant rejects and use the 100 thalers example to show why.",prompt:"What is Kant’s objection to Anselm’s Ontological Argument?",answer:"Kant rejects the claim that existence is a real predicate or perfection. Saying that 100 thalers exist does not add a property to the concept of 100 thalers; it only says the concept is instantiated. The argument therefore cannot define God into existence."},
  "MC-RS-05":{type:"classify",title:"Which Formulation of the Categorical Imperative?",instruction:"Classify each maxim as passing or failing the universalisability test, then commit to the rule before the reveal.",items:["I will lie whenever it benefits me.","I will keep a promise even when it is inconvenient.","I will steal whenever I want something.","I will help others when I can."],answer:"A maxim passes only if it can be consistently willed as a universal law. Lying and stealing fail because universalising them destroys the practices they rely on; keeping promises and helping others can be universalised. This is Kant’s first formulation, not the humanity formulation."},
  "MC-RS-07":{type:"evaluate",title:"What Makes Religious Experience Evidence?",instruction:"Commit to the principle that does the evidential work before the reveal. Explain how Swinburne uses testimony and credulity, then give one defeater.",prompt:"What does religious experience as proof of God’s existence most fundamentally rely on?",answer:"Swinburne’s Principle of Credulity says it is rational to trust what experience seems to show unless there is a reason not to; his Principle of Testimony extends this to sincere reports by others. Hallucination, psychological explanation or cultural conditioning can act as defeaters. James’s four marks describe experiences but do not by themselves verify them."},
  "MC-CHEM-01":{type:"calculate",title:"Relative Atomic Mass Is a Mixture",instruction:"Commit to the correction before the reveal. Calculate the weighted mean for chlorine, then state why 35.5 cannot be the proton count of one atom.",prompt:"Chlorine is 75% ³⁵Cl and 25% ³⁷Cl. A student says Ar = 35.5 means each chlorine atom has 35.5 protons.",answer:"Ar = (0.75×35) + (0.25×37) = 35.5. Each chlorine atom has exactly 17 protons; 35.5 is the weighted mean for the isotope mixture."},
  "MC-CHEM-03":{type:"diagram",title:"Water Needs Lone Pairs",instruction:"Draw the dot-and-cross diagram before the reveal. Add the missing electrons, then predict the shape and bond angle using electron-pair repulsion.",prompt:"A student draws H₂O with no lone pairs on oxygen. What must be corrected?",answer:"Oxygen has two O–H bonding pairs and two lone pairs. Four electron pairs give tetrahedral electron geometry; the molecule is bent with an angle of about 104.5°, not linear."},
  "MC-CHEM-04":{type:"distinguish",title:"Giant Covalent or Simple Molecular?",instruction:"Choose the bonding model before the reveal. Compare diamond with carbon dioxide and identify which bonds must be broken when each substance melts.",prompt:"Which type of bonding explains diamond’s very high melting point?",answer:"Diamond is giant covalent: many strong covalent bonds extend through the lattice and must be broken. In simple molecular CO₂, only weak intermolecular forces are overcome; the covalent bonds inside each molecule remain intact."},
  "MC-CHEM-05":{type:"compare",title:"Empirical Versus Molecular Formula",instruction:"Complete the comparison before the reveal, then use ethene as a test case. State the calculation that converts an empirical formula into a molecular formula.",prompt:"Distinguish empirical formula from molecular formula.",answer:"Empirical formula = simplest whole-number ratio; molecular formula = actual number of atoms in a molecule. Ethene is C₂H₄ molecular and CH₂ empirical. Divide molar mass by empirical formula mass, then multiply every subscript by that integer."},
  "MC-CHEM-06":{type:"correct",title:"Electronegativity: Shielding Beats Proton Count",instruction:"Correct the claim before the reveal. Draw the outer electron and nucleus relationship for fluorine and chlorine, then name the two factors that decide the trend.",prompt:"Fluorine is more electronegative than chlorine even though chlorine has more protons. Why?",answer:"Fluorine is smaller and has less shielding, so its bonding electrons feel a stronger effective nuclear attraction. Chlorine’s extra shell and shielding outweigh its additional protons."},
  "MC-BIO-01":{type:"sequence",title:"Respiration Is Controlled, Not Burning",instruction:"Build the mechanism before the reveal. Put the stages and locations in order, then replace the word ‘burning’ with the precise biological term.",prompt:"A student says mitochondria produce ATP by ‘burning glucose’. Which term is more accurate?",answer:"Aerobic respiration is an enzyme-controlled series of redox reactions: glycolysis in the cytoplasm, link reaction/Krebs cycle in the matrix, and oxidative phosphorylation at the inner mitochondrial membrane. Overall: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + ATP."},
  "MC-BIO-02":{type:"compare",title:"Prokaryote or Eukaryote?",instruction:"Commit to a classification before the reveal. Complete a two-column comparison using nucleus, organelles, ribosomes and DNA location.",prompt:"Which features correctly distinguish prokaryotic from eukaryotic cells?",answer:"Prokaryotes have no nucleus or membrane-bound organelles, usually have circular naked DNA and 70S ribosomes. Eukaryotes have a nucleus, membrane-bound organelles, linear nuclear DNA and 80S ribosomes."},
  "MC-BIO-04":{type:"distinguish",title:"Facilitated Diffusion Is Still Passive",instruction:"Classify each transport route before the reveal. For each, state direction, protein requirement and ATP requirement; then identify the feature that separates facilitated diffusion from active transport.",items:["O₂ through the phospholipid bilayer","glucose through a carrier protein down its gradient","ions through a channel protein down their gradient","mineral ions moved against their gradient using ATP"],answer:"Simple diffusion: O₂, directly through the bilayer, down gradient, no ATP. Facilitated diffusion: glucose/ions through proteins, down gradient, no ATP. Active transport: mineral ions through carrier proteins, against gradient, ATP required."},
  "MC-BIO-05":{type:"calculate",title:"Magnification: Divide for Actual Size",instruction:"Commit to the rearranged formula before the reveal. Convert units, substitute, and explain why actual size is image size divided by magnification.",prompt:"A student uses actual size = image size × magnification. Correct the formula and calculate the actual size when image = 50 mm and magnification = ×10,000.",answer:"Magnification = image size ÷ actual size, so actual size = image size ÷ magnification. 50 mm ÷ 10,000 = 0.005 mm. Units must match before calculating."},
  "MC-BIO-06":{type:"diagram",title:"Draw the Fluid Mosaic Model",instruction:"Draw and label the membrane before the reveal. Include the bilayer, integral and peripheral proteins, cholesterol and a glycoprotein; annotate what ‘fluid’ and ‘mosaic’ mean.",prompt:"Which statement correctly describes the fluid mosaic model?",answer:"The phospholipid bilayer is fluid because lipids and proteins move laterally; it is mosaic because different proteins are scattered through it. Cholesterol regulates fluidity, while glycoproteins/glycolipids contribute to recognition."},
  "MC-PHYS-02":{type:"diagram",title:"Separate the Projectile Components",instruction:"Draw velocity arrows before the reveal. Resolve the motion into horizontal and vertical components, then state which component gravity changes.",prompt:"A ball is thrown horizontally from a cliff, ignoring air resistance. How does it move?",answer:"Horizontal velocity remains constant because there is no horizontal force. Vertical velocity accelerates downward at g = 9.81 m/s². The combined path is parabolic; gravity does not reduce horizontal velocity."},
  "MC-PHYS-03":{type:"distinguish",title:"Work Changes Kinetic Energy",instruction:"Commit to the theorem before the reveal. Match each expression to work, kinetic-energy change or impulse, then state what positive and negative net work do.",items:["W_net = ΔKE","W = Fd cosθ","Impulse = Ft","positive net work"],answer:"Net work equals change in kinetic energy: W_net = ΔKE. Work is Fd cosθ; Ft is impulse. Positive net work increases kinetic energy; negative net work decreases it."},
  "MC-PHYS-04":{type:"correct",title:"SUVAT: Choose the Sign Convention",instruction:"Repair the calculation before the reveal. Choose upward as positive, assign signs to velocity, acceleration and displacement, and identify the error caused by changing convention mid-solution.",prompt:"A student uses s = ut + ½at² for a ball thrown upwards but gets the wrong answer. What is the likely error?",answer:"SUVAT requires one consistent sign convention. If upward is positive, initial upward velocity is +u, gravitational acceleration is −9.81 m/s², and a later position below the start has negative displacement. Mixing signs produces the wrong result."},
  "MC-PHYS-05":{type:"compare",title:"Hooke’s Law Depends on What Is Fixed",instruction:"Complete the comparison before the reveal: same extension versus same force. Then decide whether the claim ‘a stiffer spring always stores more energy’ is true.",prompt:"A student says a stiffer spring always stores more energy. Is this correct?",answer:"EPE = ½kx² = F²/(2k). For the same extension, larger k means more energy. For the same force, a stiffer spring extends less and stores less energy. The claim is incomplete because the fixed quantity matters."},
  "MC-PHYS-06":{type:"distinguish",title:"Momentum Is Not Kinetic Energy",instruction:"Classify the conservation claim before the reveal. Compare an elastic and inelastic collision, then state which quantity is conserved in every closed-system collision.",prompt:"What does conservation of momentum mean?",answer:"Momentum is conserved in all collisions in a closed system with no external net force. Kinetic energy is conserved only in perfectly elastic collisions; in inelastic collisions it becomes heat, sound or deformation."},
  "MC-CS-02":{type:"compare",title:"Compiler or Interpreter?",instruction:"Commit to a distinction before the reveal. Complete a two-column comparison covering when translation happens, execution speed, debugging and portability; then place Python and C in the table.",prompt:"Which correctly distinguishes a compiler from an interpreter?",answer:"A compiler translates the whole program before execution; an interpreter translates and runs line by line. Compiled code usually runs faster but may be platform-specific; interpreted code is easier to debug and more portable. Python is commonly interpreted; C/C++ are compiled."},
  "MC-CS-03":{type:"calculate",title:"Binary Search Halves the Work",instruction:"Commit to the maximum number of comparisons before the reveal. Write the power of two that represents the list size, then explain why sorted data is required.",prompt:"What is the maximum number of binary-search comparisons for a sorted list of 1024 items?",answer:"1024 = 2¹⁰, so the maximum is 10 comparisons. Each comparison halves the remaining search space; binary search depends on the list being sorted."},
  "MC-CS-04":{type:"distinguish",title:"What Does O(n²) Tell You?",instruction:"Classify the complexity examples before the reveal. Match O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n) and O(n²) to their growth patterns, then explain why nested loops often produce O(n²).",items:["binary search","linear search","merge sort","bubble sort with nested passes","constant-time lookup"],answer:"O(log n): binary search; O(n): linear search; O(n log n): merge sort; O(n²): bubble sort with nested passes; O(1): constant-time lookup. Big O describes how runtime scales, not an exact number of seconds."},
  "MC-CS-05":{type:"distinguish",title:"Stack or Queue?",instruction:"Commit to the data structure before the reveal. Trace the order in which items leave a stack and a queue, then attach one real computing use to each.",prompt:"A student says a stack and a queue are the same because both store data. What is the key difference?",answer:"A stack is LIFO: the last item pushed is the first popped. A queue is FIFO: the first item enqueued is the first dequeued. Stacks support undo/function calls; queues support print scheduling and breadth-first search."},
  "MC-CS-06":{type:"classify",title:"Function, Procedure, or Whole Program?",instruction:"Classify each statement before the reveal, then write one reason subroutines improve maintainability.",items:["a named block called with parameters","a routine that returns a value","a routine that performs an action but returns no value","the complete application controlling the workflow"],answer:"A subroutine is a named reusable block; a function returns a value; a procedure performs an action without returning a value; the program is the complete application. Subroutines reduce duplication and make testing/debugging easier."},
  "MC-BUS-01":{type:"compare",title:"Product-Led or Market-Led?",instruction:"Commit to the orientation before the reveal. Complete a comparison using who starts the decision, the role of research and the main risk of each approach.",prompt:"What is the difference between product orientation and market orientation?",answer:"Product orientation starts with what the business can make; market orientation starts with researched customer needs and then develops the product. Market orientation is more responsive but costs more to maintain; confusing the two reverses the decision process."},
  "MC-BUS-04":{type:"distinguish",title:"Sole Trader or Private Limited Company?",instruction:"Choose the business form before the reveal. Compare liability, control, administration and access to finance, then circle the distinction most important to personal financial risk.",prompt:"What is a key difference between a sole trader and a private limited company?",answer:"A sole trader has unlimited liability and personal assets are at risk. An Ltd has limited liability for shareholders, but must file accounts and has more administration; its shares cannot be sold publicly on a stock exchange."},
  "MC-BUS-05":{type:"classify",title:"Herzberg: Hygiene or Motivator?",instruction:"Classify each workplace factor before the reveal, then explain why increasing salary may remove dissatisfaction without creating motivation.",items:["salary","job security","recognition","responsibility","working conditions","achievement"],answer:"Hygiene factors: salary, job security and working conditions; poor provision causes dissatisfaction but good provision does not itself motivate. Motivators: recognition, responsibility and achievement; their presence creates satisfaction and motivation."},
  "MC-BUS-06":{type:"classify",title:"Read Ansoff’s Matrix",instruction:"Commit to the strategy before the reveal. Place each example in the matrix, keeping product novelty and market novelty as separate decisions.",items:["existing product, existing market","existing product, new market","new product, existing market","new product, new market"],answer:"Existing/existing = market penetration; existing/new = market development; new/existing = product development; new/new = diversification. The common error is swapping product development with market development."},
  "MC-BUS-07":{type:"calculate",title:"Net Profit Margin Uses Revenue",instruction:"Calculate the margin before the reveal. Show net profit first, then divide by revenue—not total costs—and state the percentage.",prompt:"Revenue is £500,000 and total costs are £420,000. What is net profit margin?",answer:"Net profit = £500,000 − £420,000 = £80,000. Net profit margin = (£80,000 ÷ £500,000) × 100 = 16%."},
  "MC-MED-01":{type:"apply",title:"Decode the Preferred Reading",instruction:"Commit to the audience position before the reveal. Apply Hall’s three readings to a new audience response and explain how cultural position changes the meaning.",prompt:"A producer encodes a preferred reading that a charity campaign is empowering. One viewer accepts it, another partly accepts it but criticises the imagery, and a third rejects it as manipulative. Classify the three readings.",answer:"Accepts fully = dominant/hegemonic; partly accepts and modifies = negotiated; rejects and substitutes a different meaning = oppositional. Meaning is produced through the interaction between text and audience, not fixed in the text alone."},
  "MC-MED-02":{type:"evaluate",title:"Does Ownership Limit Diversity?",instruction:"Commit to a judgement before the reveal. Use one mechanism supporting Curran and Seaton, one counterpoint from digital media, and a condition that determines whether concentration reduces diversity.",prompt:"Evaluate the claim that concentrated media ownership reduces diversity of voices.",answer:"Concentration can reduce diversity because owners control editorial priorities and commercial pressure favours profitable, familiar content. Digital platforms can widen participation through user-generated content, but algorithms and global platform power may recreate concentration. The effect depends on who controls distribution as well as production."},
  "MC-MED-03":{type:"apply",title:"Cultivation or Choice?",instruction:"Commit to the theory before the reveal. Apply Gerbner’s first-order and second-order effects to a heavy viewer, then state one reason correlation does not prove cultivation.",prompt:"A heavy television viewer overestimates crime rates and becomes more fearful of the world. Which theory explains this, and what is the limitation?",answer:"Gerbner’s cultivation theory predicts first-order effects such as overestimating crime and second-order attitudes such as fear or the mean-world syndrome. The relationship may be correlation: people already fearful may choose more crime programmes, so choice and reinforcement also matter."},
  "MC-MED-04":{type:"correct",title:"Representation Is Construction",instruction:"Repair the claim before the reveal. Identify what a media text selects, omits and frames, then explain how that differs from simply reflecting reality.",prompt:"A student says representation means media mirror reality exactly. Correct the claim.",answer:"Representation is a re-presentation: media select and construct reality through framing, language, images and omission. The same event can be represented differently by a tabloid and a broadsheet; analysis must explain how the text constructs meaning and whose perspective is centred."},
  "MC-MED-05":{type:"evaluate",title:"Can Regulation Keep Up?",instruction:"Commit to a judgement before the reveal. Weigh one limitation of traditional broadcast regulation against one digital challenge, then name the condition a workable regulator would need.",prompt:"Evaluate Livingstone and Lunt’s criticism that traditional regulation struggles with digital media.",answer:"Traditional regulation assumes a small number of national producers, but convergence creates global platforms, user-generated content and algorithmic curation that cross jurisdictions and change rapidly. A workable model needs international cooperation, platform accountability and enough flexibility to respond without suppressing legitimate expression."},
  "MC-HSC-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"What Counts as Intellectual Development?",instruction:"Commit to the meaning before the reveal. Sort the examples into physical, intellectual, emotional and social development, then explain why IQ alone is too narrow.",items:["learning new vocabulary","developing balance and coordination","forming an attachment","taking turns in conversation"],answer:"Intellectual: learning vocabulary and other cognitive changes such as memory, language and problem-solving. Physical: balance and coordination. Emotional: attachment and managing feelings. Social: communication and relationships."},
  "MC-HSC-02":{type:"sequence",title:"Bowlby’s Critical Period",instruction:"Build the developmental chain before the reveal. Place critical period, primary attachment, internal working model and later relationship effects in order, then state the care implication.",prompt:"What does Bowlby mean by a critical period in attachment?",answer:"A primary attachment normally forms in the early sensitive period, roughly the first 2–2.5 years; that relationship contributes to an internal working model, which can influence later relationships. The care implication is to reduce prolonged separation and provide a consistent attachment figure."},
  "MC-HSC-05":{type:"sequence",title:"Maslow: Meet the Need First",instruction:"Commit to the level before the reveal. Put Maslow’s hierarchy in order, then apply it to a service user whose basic needs are unmet.",prompt:"Which level must be satisfied before a person can focus on esteem needs?",answer:"Physiological → safety → love/belonging → esteem → self-actualisation. A person without food, warmth or security needs those foundations addressed before achievement and recognition can become the main focus."},
  "MC-HSC-06":{type:"apply",title:"Person-Centred Care in Practice",instruction:"Choose the principle before the reveal. Turn the label into two observable care-worker actions and link one action to the service user’s wellbeing.",prompt:"A care worker follows an individual’s preferences, values and choices rather than organisational convenience. Which principle is illustrated?",answer:"Person-centred care. In practice, the worker respects choice and privacy, adapts the care plan to the individual’s preferences and protects dignity/independence. This can improve emotional wellbeing because the person retains control and feels respected."},
  "MC-HSC-07":{type:"scenario",title:"Falls Risk: Link Change to Care",instruction:"Commit to the physical change before the reveal. Explain the mechanism linking it to falls, then propose one care-plan response and one impact on independence.",prompt:"Which late-adulthood physical change most directly increases falls risk?",answer:"Reduced muscle mass and strength can impair balance and movement; reduced bone density increases fracture risk if a fall occurs. A care plan could include a falls assessment, safer footwear, environmental adaptations or physiotherapy. The loss of confidence may also reduce independence."},
  "MC-GCSE-P1-FND-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Scarcity or Opportunity Cost?",instruction:"Commit to the economic term before the reveal. Identify the scarce resource, the choice made and the next-best alternative forgone.",prompt:"A town builds a health centre instead of 120 homes because it cannot build both. Which idea is illustrated?",answer:"Scarcity forces a choice because resources are limited relative to wants. The opportunity cost is the next best alternative forgone: the 120 homes, not every possible alternative."},
  "MC-GCSE-P1-FND-02":{type:"classify",title:"Which Factor of Production?",instruction:"Classify each item before the reveal, then explain why capital means a productive resource rather than money in the bank.",items:["a delivery van","a trained employee","a forest","an entrepreneur organising a start-up","a factory machine","a bank balance"],answer:"Capital: delivery van and factory machine—man-made resources used to produce goods/services. Labour: trained employee. Land: forest. Enterprise: entrepreneur. A bank balance is finance, not the factor of production called capital."},
  "MC-GCSE-P1-FND-03":{type:"distinguish",title:"Scarcity Is Not Poverty",instruction:"Commit to the distinction before the reveal. Complete one sentence about scarcity and one about poverty, then apply both to a high-income country.",prompt:"Why can a high-income country still experience scarcity?",answer:"Scarcity is universal because resources such as time, land, skilled labour and raw materials are finite while wants are unlimited. Poverty is a low level of income or resources. A wealthy country can have high average income but still face choices because resources remain limited."},
  "MC-GCSE-P1-FND-04":{type:"compare",title:"The Trade-Off in Resource Allocation",instruction:"Compare what is gained and what is sacrificed before the reveal. Name the opportunity cost precisely rather than saying simply ‘something else’.",prompt:"A country switches workers and machines from food production to medical equipment. What is the main trade-off?",answer:"The country gains more medical equipment but sacrifices the food those workers and machines would have produced. That sacrificed food output is the opportunity cost—the next best alternative forgone."},
  "MC-GCSE-P1-FND-05":{type:"evaluate",title:"Does Division of Labour Raise Productivity?",instruction:"Commit to one benefit and one drawback before the reveal. Explain the mechanism that raises output and the condition that could weaken the benefit.",prompt:"A firm divides production into small repeated tasks. What is a likely benefit?",answer:"Productivity can rise because repetition improves skill, reduces time moving between tasks and allows specialised machinery. The benefit may be limited by boredom, low motivation or dependence on a production line, so the context matters."},
  "MC-EDU-ETHNIC":{type:"correct",title:"Repair the Ethnocentric Curriculum Definition",instruction:"Write a precise definition before the reveal. Then identify one curriculum example and link it to the possible experience of minority pupils.",prompt:"What is meant by an ethnocentric curriculum?",answer:"It treats one culture as the norm—in Britain, often a white British and European perspective. A history curriculum focused almost entirely on British imperial experience or literature dominated by white authors can devalue minority identities and contribute to feelings of exclusion or underachievement."},
  "MC-EDU-POLICY":{type:"evaluate",title:"Privatisation: Efficiency or Commodity?",instruction:"Commit to a judgement before the reveal. Give one example of privatisation in education, one benefit claimed by supporters and one criticism from Ball’s perspective.",prompt:"A student is revising the effect of privatisation in education policy. Which explanation is correct?",answer:"Privatisation can involve exam boards, inspection, IT, catering, PFI projects or academy chains being run by private organisations. Supporters argue competition and expertise may raise standards; critics argue public money can flow to shareholders and education becomes a commodity with less democratic control."},
  "MC-EDU-06":{type:"sequence",title:"How Marketisation Changes Schools",instruction:"Complete the policy chain before the reveal. Link the policy, parental choice, competition, funding and the intended or unintended outcome.",prompt:"Which policy introduced marketisation of schools in England?",answer:"The 1988 Education Reform Act introduced market-style mechanisms: parental choice and competition, funding following pupils and league tables ranking schools. Supporters expected standards to rise; Ball’s criticism is that middle-class families use cultural and economic capital to exploit choice, widening inequality."},
  "MC-EDU-09":{type:"apply",title:"Make the Hidden Curriculum Visible",instruction:"Commit to one example before the reveal. Apply the concept to a school practice and explain the workplace behaviour or social message it may transmit.",prompt:"What is meant by the hidden curriculum?",answer:"The hidden curriculum is the unofficial learning transmitted through routines, relationships and expectations rather than the formal syllabus. For example, punctuality and obedience can prepare pupils for workplace discipline; streaming can normalise hierarchy and unequal roles."},
  "MC-EDU-10":{type:"apply",title:"Educational Triage in a League-Table School",instruction:"Commit to the group most likely to lose out before the reveal. Trace the school’s incentive from the performance measure to the allocation of teaching time.",prompt:"What is meant by educational triage?",answer:"Educational triage is the prioritising of pupils near a target grade because they can most efficiently improve the school’s published results. Pupils already certain to pass and those judged unlikely to reach the threshold may receive less attention, even though the policy is rational for the school’s league-table incentive."},
  "MC-DE-01":{type:"correct",title:"Accusative After Kaufen",instruction:"Correct the sentence before the reveal. Underline the direct object, then change the article and adjective ending that mark masculine accusative.",prompt:"Which sentence correctly uses the accusative case after kaufen (to buy)?",answer:"A direct object takes the accusative: Ich kaufe den neuen Mantel. Masculine der becomes den, and the adjective takes -en. The common error is leaving der after a verb that governs the accusative."},
  "MC-DE-03":{type:"rewrite",title:"Build the Imperfect Passive",instruction:"Rewrite the active idea as an imperfect passive before the reveal. Mark the auxiliary, past participle and von + dative phrase.",prompt:"Translate: ‘The book was written by the author.’",answer:"Das Buch wurde von dem Autor geschrieben. Imperfect passive = wurde/wurden + past participle; von takes the dative: dem Autor."},
  "MC-DE-05":{type:"correct",title:"Sein or Werden?",instruction:"Repair the sentence before the reveal. State whether the meaning is ‘to be’ or ‘to become’, then remove the unnecessary profession article.",prompt:"Correct: Ich will ein Arzt sein.",answer:"If the intended meaning is ‘I want to be a doctor’: Ich will Arzt sein. Sein means to be; werden means to become: Ich will Arzt werden. German normally drops the article before a profession after sein/werden."},
  "MC-DE-06":{type:"rewrite",title:"Put the Modal Verb Last",instruction:"Rewrite the sentence before the reveal. Circle the conjugated modal and place the infinitive at the end of the clause.",prompt:"Correct: Er kann nicht kommen heute Abend.",answer:"Er kann heute Abend nicht kommen. The modal is conjugated in second position; the infinitive comes at the end, with nicht before it."},
  "MC-DE-08":{type:"distinguish",title:"Perfekt or Imperfekt Register?",instruction:"Classify each sentence as more natural in spoken conversation or a formal written narrative, then explain why a formal essay needs the appropriate register.",items:["Ich bin gestern ins Kino gegangen.","Er ging nach Hause und schrieb einen Brief.","Wir haben viel gelernt.","Sie hatte keine Zeit und blieb zu Hause."],answer:"Perfekt is generally conversational: bin gegangen, haben gelernt. Imperfekt is the expected written narrative register: ging, schrieb, hatte, blieb. Sein and haben commonly use the imperfect even in speech."},
  "MC-FR-01":{type:"correct",title:"Être Verb and Agreement",instruction:"Commit to the auxiliary before the reveal. Correct the sentence for a female speaker and explain why the past participle changes.",prompt:"What does ‘Je suis allé à Paris’ demonstrate, and how would a female speaker write it?",answer:"Aller takes être in the passé composé, so a female speaker writes Je suis allée à Paris. With être, the past participle agrees with the subject in gender and number."},
  "MC-FR-02":{type:"distinguish",title:"Imparfait or Passé Composé?",instruction:"Choose the tense before the reveal. Sort each time phrase by habitual/background action or completed event, then repair the translation.",items:["used to go to school by bus","yesterday","was raining","suddenly"],answer:"Used to and was raining signal imparfait; yesterday and suddenly usually signal a completed passé composé event. ‘I used to go to school by bus’ = J’allais à l’école en bus, not J’ai allé."},
  "MC-FR-04":{type:"correct",title:"Qui or Que?",instruction:"Repair the relative pronoun before the reveal. Identify whether the pronoun is subject or object, then check past-participle agreement.",prompt:"Is ‘Le livre qu’elle m’a donné était intéressant’ correct? Explain.",answer:"Yes. Que/qu’ is the direct object of donné: she gave me the book. The masculine singular direct object precedes the verb but does not create a visible change: donné. Qui would be the subject of the relative clause."},
  "MC-FR-06":{type:"correct",title:"Quantity Expressions Drop the Article",instruction:"Correct the phrase before the reveal. Write the rule after beaucoup de, then contrast it with the exception la plupart des.",prompt:"Correct: beaucoup de les étudiants.",answer:"Beaucoup d’étudiants. Expressions of quantity take de/d’ + noun and normally drop the article. Exception: la plupart des étudiants retains des."},
  "MC-FR-07":{type:"distinguish",title:"Malgré or Bien Que?",instruction:"Choose the connector before the reveal. Match each connector to noun phrase or subjunctive clause, then repair the sentence.",prompt:"Correct: Malgré il pleuvait, nous sommes sortis.",answer:"Malgré takes a noun: Malgré la pluie, nous sommes sortis. Bien que takes a subjunctive clause: Bien qu’il pleuve, nous sommes sortis. Do not mix the preposition and conjunction patterns."},
  "MC-SP-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Ser or Estar?",instruction:"Commit to the verb before the reveal. Classify origin/identity, temporary state, location and profession, then handle the exception estar muerto.",items:["origin: Es de España","temporary tiredness: María ___ cansada","location: El libro ___ en la mesa","profession: Es médico","dead: Está muerto"],answer:"Ser: origin and profession. Estar: temporary tiredness, location and the fixed exception estar muerto. Meaning changes: ser aburrido = boring; estar aburrido = bored."},
  "MC-SP-02":{type:"rewrite",title:"Duration Uses Llevar",instruction:"Rewrite the sentence before the reveal. Underline the duration and choose llevar + gerund rather than haber + past participle.",prompt:"Translate: ‘I had been studying for three hours when he called.’",answer:"Llevaba tres horas estudiando cuando llamó. Llevaba + time + gerund expresses an ongoing duration before another past event; había estudiado describes a completed prior action."},
  "MC-SP-03":{type:"classify",title:"Spot the Subjunctive Trigger",instruction:"Classify each trigger as requiring subjunctive or indicative, then commit to the verb form in one example.",items:["Espero que él (venir)","Creo que él (venir)","No creo que él (venir)","Es importante que él (estudiar)"],answer:"Espero que venga; creo que viene; no creo que venga; es importante que estudie. Wishes, doubt/denial and impersonal recommendations trigger subjunctive; belief normally takes indicative."},
  "MC-SP-04":{type:"correct",title:"Preterite or Imperfect Time Frame",instruction:"Repair the sentence before the reveal. Circle the completed-event marker and the habitual-background marker, then choose compatible tenses.",prompt:"Correct: Ayer, cuando era joven, fui al colegio.",answer:"The time frames conflict. Ayer fui al colegio describes a completed event; Cuando era joven, iba al colegio describes a habitual past action. Use the preterite for a specific completed event and imperfect for habit/background."},
  "MC-SP-05":{type:"distinguish",title:"Change of State: Ponerse or Hacerse?",instruction:"Choose the reflexive verb before the reveal. Match each verb to temporary change, gradual identity change, profound change or resulting state.",items:["se puso nervioso","se hizo médico","se volvió loco","se quedó embarazada"],answer:"Ponerse = temporary emotional/physical change; hacerse = gradual or deliberate change; volverse = profound change; quedarse = resulting state. Reflexive form changes the meaning from causing a state in someone else."},

  // ---- GCSE Economics (OCR J205): one starter per MC-GE-* tag.
  // Tags defined by GCSE_ECON_MC_TAGS in data/forge-data.js; labels in
  // data/misconception-labels.js. Every entry targets the single error that
  // tag represents and makes students commit before the reveal.
  "MC-GE-SCARCITY":{type:"classify",title:"What Was Actually Given Up?",instruction:"For each choice, write the opportunity cost as ONE thing. Reject any answer that lists everything forgone — the opportunity cost is the next best alternative only.",items:["A council builds a health centre instead of 120 homes or a car park","You revise instead of working a paid shift or seeing friends","A bakery uses its only oven for cakes instead of bread or pastries","A government funds a hospital instead of a sports centre or road repairs"],answer:"Each answer must name ONE forgone option — the next best. The error to catch is listing every alternative, or naming the thing chosen rather than the thing sacrificed."},
  "MC-GE-FACTORS":{type:"sort",title:"Land, Labour, Capital or Enterprise?",instruction:"Sort each item into one of the four factors. Two are commonly misplaced — be ready to justify.",items:["A delivery van owned by a bakery","£10,000 in the firm's bank account","A baker's time and skill","The founder who hires staff and risks savings","Coal in the ground","A science laboratory in a school"],answer:"Van = capital. £10,000 = NOT capital — money is finance, not a factor of production; capital means man-made things used to produce. Baker's time = labour. Founder = enterprise. Coal = land. Laboratory = capital. The two traps are money-as-capital and treating the entrepreneur as ordinary labour."},
  "MC-GE-PPF":{type:"diagram",title:"Inside It, Or Moving It?",instruction:"Draw one PPF. Mark point X inside the curve and point Y on the curve. Then draw a second curve showing an outward shift. Label which change causes which movement, and commit to an answer before the reveal.",prompt:"Which of these moves you from X to Y, and which shifts the whole curve outward? (a) unemployed workers find jobs (b) investment in new technology (c) better worker training (d) factories reopen after a strike",answer:"X to Y (using existing resources more fully): (a) and (d). Outward shift (more or better resources): (b) and (c). The error is calling any improvement 'a shift' — using idle resources you already have does not move the frontier."},
  "MC-GE-SECTORS":{type:"sort",title:"Which Sector?",instruction:"Sort each activity into primary, secondary or tertiary. One is argued over every year — find it.",items:["Extracting timber from a managed forest","Providing car insurance","Baking bread in a factory","Running a hotel","Catching fish","Providing accountancy advice"],answer:"Primary: timber, fish. Secondary: baking. Tertiary: insurance, hotel, accountancy. The recurring argument is baking — students call it primary because flour is 'natural'; the sector follows what THIS firm does (processing), not where the input came from."},
  "MC-GE-SPECIALISATION":{type:"compare",title:"Both Columns Must Be Filled",instruction:"Complete both columns for a hotel that gives each worker one narrow role. An answer with an empty right-hand column scores half marks in the exam.",headers:["Benefit of division of labour","Drawback of division of labour"],rows:[["Workers get faster through repetition",""],["",""],["",""]],answer:"Benefits: speed through repetition, focused training, specialist equipment worthwhile, less time moving between tasks. Drawbacks: boredom and low motivation, higher staff turnover, workers not interchangeable when someone is absent, over-dependence on one stage of the process."},
  "MC-GE-MARKET-DEF":{type:"classify",title:"Product Market or Factor Market?",instruction:"Label each transaction P (product market) or F (factor market), then write the one-line rule that separates them.",items:["A family buys a meal in a restaurant","A company pays wages to its employees","Consumers buy train tickets","A firm hires software engineers","A business pays rent for a warehouse"],answer:"P: meal, train tickets. F: wages, hiring engineers, warehouse rent. Rule: product markets trade finished goods and services; factor markets trade the RESOURCES used to produce them (land, labour, capital, enterprise). The direction of the flow reverses — in factor markets households sell and firms buy."},
  "MC-GE-PRICE-FUNC":{type:"distinguish",title:"Signal, Incentive or Ration?",instruction:"Label each situation with the function of the price mechanism it shows MOST directly. Several look like more than one — commit to the strongest before the reveal.",items:["Concert ticket prices rise so only some fans can buy","A price rise tells producers demand has increased","Higher strawberry prices lead growers to plant more next season","A 10p bag charge changes shopper behaviour","A congestion charge reduces driving at peak times"],answer:"Ration: concert tickets. Signal: the price rise conveying information. Incentive: strawberry planting. Signal AND incentive: bag charge, congestion charge. The error is treating all three functions as the same idea — signalling INFORMS, incentive REWARDS a response, rationing ALLOCATES what is scarce."},
  "MC-GE-DISEQ":{type:"diagram",title:"Above or Below Equilibrium?",instruction:"Draw one demand and supply diagram. Add a maximum price BELOW equilibrium, then on a second diagram a minimum price ABOVE it. Shade and label the gap in each. Predict shortage or surplus before you draw.",prompt:"Case 1: rent control set below the market rent. Case 2: a farm price floor set above the market price.",answer:"Max price below equilibrium → SHORTAGE (Qd > Qs) — rent control causes a shortage of homes. Min price above equilibrium → SURPLUS (Qs > Qd) — the farm price floor creates unsold output. The error is assuming a maximum price helps everyone; it helps those who get the good and leaves others queuing. A maximum price set ABOVE equilibrium does nothing at all."},
  "MC-GE-SHIFT-VS-MOVE":{type:"classify",title:"Shift It Or Slide Along It?",instruction:"For each change write SHIFT or MOVEMENT, and name the curve. The test is one question: did the good's OWN price change?",items:["The price of coffee rises and less coffee is bought","Household incomes fall and fewer meals are wanted at every price","The price of train travel rises, so more people want buses","A subsidy lowers the cost of making solar panels","The product's own price falls and more is supplied"],answer:"Movement (own price changed): coffee, the last one. Shift (something else changed): incomes → demand shifts left; train price → demand for buses shifts right; subsidy → supply shifts right. Rule: a change in the good's OWN price moves you ALONG the curve; anything else shifts the whole curve."},
  "MC-GE-DS-DETERMINANTS":{type:"sort",title:"Which Curve, Which Direction?",instruction:"For each change state (i) demand or supply and (ii) left or right. Write both parts — half an answer scores nothing.",items:["A rise in the price of train travel, a substitute for buses","A sharp rise in car prices, where petrol is a complement","Baker wages and energy costs rise","A government subsidy on solar panels","Crude oil supply falls, affecting petrol"],answer:"1 Demand for buses, RIGHT. 2 Demand for petrol, LEFT (fewer cars bought and used). 3 Supply of bread, LEFT. 4 Supply of panels, RIGHT. 5 Supply of petrol, LEFT. The classic error is moving demand when a COST changes — costs shift supply."},
  "MC-GE-EQUILIBRIUM":{type:"correct",title:"Fix the Equilibrium Statement",instruction:"Find and correct the error, then state the equilibrium condition precisely.",prompt:"'At equilibrium the market has sold everything it produced, so demand equals supply and the price can never change again.'",answer:"Two errors. (1) The condition is quantity DEMANDED equals quantity SUPPLIED at that price — not 'demand equals supply', which confuses the whole curve with a quantity at one price. (2) Equilibrium is not permanent: any shift in demand or supply produces a new equilibrium."},
  "MC-GE-ELASTICITY":{type:"calculate",title:"Elastic, Inelastic — and Which Elasticity?",instruction:"Calculate the elasticity, classify it, and state whether it is PED or PES. Show the formula before substituting.",items:["Price rises 10%, quantity demanded falls 2%","Price rises 10%, quantity demanded falls 30%","Tonight's concert has 500 seats; price doubles","New housing: planning takes years, price rises 20%"],answer:"1 PED = −2/10 = −0.2, price INELASTIC. 2 PED = −30/10 = −3, price ELASTIC. 3 PES = 0, perfectly inelastic — quantity cannot respond tonight. 4 PES low, inelastic in the short run. The error to catch is labelling 3 and 4 as PED: they concern how SUPPLY responds, not demand."},
  "MC-GE-REV-COST-PROFIT":{type:"calculate",title:"Three Formulas, No Shortcuts",instruction:"Work each out showing the formula first. One asks for average cost and one for profit — read carefully before calculating.",items:["2,000 notebooks sold at £4 each. Total revenue?","Fixed costs £6,000, variable costs £4,000, output 2,000. Average cost?","Revenue £40,000, total costs £31,000. Profit?","Revenue £24,000, total cost £27,000. State the result."],answer:"1 TR = price × quantity = £8,000. 2 AC = total cost ÷ output = £10,000 ÷ 2,000 = £5 per unit. 3 Profit = TR − TC = £9,000. 4 A £3,000 LOSS. The two errors: forgetting to ADD fixed and variable costs before dividing, and reporting a loss as a negative 'profit' without naming it."},
  "MC-GE-PRODUCTIVITY":{type:"calculate",title:"Production or Productivity?",instruction:"For each, state whether it measures PRODUCTION or PRODUCTIVITY, then calculate where asked. Commit before the reveal.",items:["Six workers produce 900 units. Output per worker?","A factory made 1,200 units this week","Output rises 900 → 1,200 with 6 workers. By how much does output per worker RISE?","A firm's total output doubled after hiring twice as many workers"],answer:"1 Productivity: 900 ÷ 6 = 150 units. 2 Production — a total, not a rate. 3 Productivity rises from 150 to 200, so the RISE is 50 units (not 200 — read the question). 4 Production doubled but productivity is UNCHANGED. The core error is treating 'more output' as 'more productive'."},
  "MC-GE-EOS":{type:"sort",title:"Economy or Diseconomy — and Which Type?",instruction:"Sort each into economy or diseconomy of scale, then name the type (purchasing, technical, managerial, financial).",items:["A large firm buys raw materials in bulk more cheaply","Communication slows as the organisation grows","Specialist machinery spreads fixed costs over more output","A large company borrows at a lower interest rate","A big firm employs specialist managers","Managers struggle to coordinate distant sites"],answer:"Economies: bulk buying (purchasing), specialist machinery (technical), cheaper borrowing (financial), specialist managers (managerial). Diseconomies: slow communication, coordination problems. Both change AVERAGE cost, not total cost — that is the distinction students miss."},
  "MC-GE-MARKET-STRUCT":{type:"compare",title:"How Many Firms?",instruction:"Complete the table, then place each example in the right row: a water network; four supermarkets holding 80% of sales; a town with many independent cafes.",headers:["Structure","Number of firms","Barriers to entry","Example"],rows:[["Monopoly","","",""],["Oligopoly","","",""],["Competitive market","","",""]],answer:"Monopoly: one dominant seller, very high barriers — water network. Oligopoly: a few firms with most market share, high barriers, firms watch each other's pricing — the supermarkets. Competitive: many firms, low barriers — the cafes. The error is calling any large firm a monopoly; market SHARE and barriers decide, not size."},
  "MC-GE-BARRIERS":{type:"classify",title:"Is That Actually a Barrier?",instruction:"Mark each B (barrier to entry) or N (not a barrier). Then explain why a barrier matters for consumers.",items:["A patent protecting a new medicine","An established chain's lower average costs from economies of scale","Existing firms advertise heavily","Customers prefer the incumbent's product","The industry is profitable","High set-up costs for a national network"],answer:"B: patent, economies of scale, heavy advertising, network set-up costs. N: customer preference alone, and profitability — high profits ATTRACT entry, they do not block it. Barriers matter because without the threat of entry an incumbent faces less pressure to keep prices low or quality high."},
  "MC-GE-NONPRICE-COMP":{type:"classify",title:"Price or Non-Price Competition?",instruction:"Label each P or NP. Then answer: why would a firm compete on anything other than price?",items:["A supermarket improves its loyalty scheme, shelf prices unchanged","A cinema sells cheap tickets and expensive drinks","A hotel adds a better breakfast","A firm spends heavily on branding, price unchanged","A shop cuts prices by 10%"],answer:"NP: loyalty scheme, better breakfast, branding. P: the 10% cut. The cinema is a LOSS LEADER — a price tactic used to sell profitable extras. Firms use non-price competition because it can make demand less price elastic: loyal customers see the product as less replaceable, so a rival's price cut takes fewer of them."},
  "MC-GE-DERIVED-DEMAND":{type:"chain",title:"Where Does Labour Demand Come From?",instruction:"Complete the chain. Each arrow must be a specific step, not 'the economy grows'.",prompt:"Online shopping becomes more popular → [step] → [step] → firms hire more delivery drivers. Then answer: if demand for the SERVICE collapsed, what happens to demand for these workers, and why?",answer:"Chain: more online orders → more parcels needing delivery → firms need more driver-hours to meet it → labour demand rises. If service demand collapses, labour demand falls WITH it — that is what 'derived' means. Demand for labour is not wanted for its own sake; it is derived from demand for the output labour helps produce."},
  "MC-GE-LABOUR-SUPPLY":{type:"classify",title:"Who Is In the Supply of Labour?",instruction:"Decide whether each person is part of the supply of labour. Commit before the reveal — two are contested.",items:["A worker actively offering their labour","Someone unemployed and actively seeking work","A retired person not seeking paid work","A full-time student not seeking a job","Someone unavailable for work and not looking"],answer:"IN: the worker, and the unemployed person actively seeking — the unemployed ARE part of labour supply, which is the point students miss. OUT: the retired person, the student, the unavailable person. Supply of labour = all those WILLING AND ABLE to offer labour, employed or not."},
  "MC-GE-WAGE-DIFFERENTIALS":{type:"explain",title:"Why Does a Surgeon Earn More?",instruction:"Write a two-part explanation using demand AND supply. An answer using only one side is incomplete.",prompt:"A specialist surgeon earns far more than a worker in a job needing fewer qualifications. Explain using both blades of the scissors. Then explain the night-shift wage premium the same way.",answer:"Surgeon: long training and qualification requirements RESTRICT supply, while demand for the skill is high — high wage. Night shift: unsocial hours REDUCE willingness to supply labour at any given wage, so employers pay a premium to attract workers. The error is explaining wages by effort or importance alone — nurses work hard and matter greatly, but supply is less restricted."},
  "MC-GE-MIN-WAGE":{type:"diagram",title:"Does the Minimum Wage Bite?",instruction:"Draw a labour market diagram twice: once with a minimum wage ABOVE equilibrium, once BELOW. Predict the effect of each before drawing.",prompt:"Case 1: minimum wage set above the market wage. Case 2: set below the market wage.",answer:"Above equilibrium: BINDING — quantity of labour supplied exceeds quantity demanded, creating excess supply (unemployment) in that market, though workers who keep jobs earn more. Below equilibrium: NON-BINDING — the market wage is already higher, so little or no effect. The error is assuming any minimum wage must cause unemployment; it only bites if it is above the market wage."},
  "MC-GE-IMMOBILITY":{type:"distinguish",title:"Geographical or Occupational?",instruction:"Label each G or O, then name one policy that would address each type.",items:["A qualified worker cannot afford to move to a region with vacancies","A retail worker lacks the qualifications to become an engineer","A miner's skills do not transfer to the new software jobs in town","A worker will not relocate because of family and school ties"],answer:"G: unaffordable move, family ties. O: lacks engineering qualifications, non-transferable mining skills. Policies — geographical: relocation support, affordable housing, better transport. Occupational: retraining and vocational education. The error is calling every mismatch 'geographical' when the barrier is actually skills."},
  "MC-GE-PAY":{type:"calculate",title:"Gross, Deductions, Net",instruction:"Calculate net pay, then state which of the deductions is a direct tax and which is a contribution towards state benefits.",items:["Gross £2,400, deductions £500. Net?","Gross £2,800, deductions £650. Net?"],answer:"1 £1,900. 2 £2,150. Net pay = gross pay − deductions. Income tax is a DIRECT tax on income; National Insurance is a contribution by workers and employers towards state benefits and pensions. The error is describing gross pay as 'what you take home'."},
  "MC-GE-MONEY-FUNC":{type:"match",title:"Which Function of Money?",instruction:"Match each situation to a function of money, then answer the final question.",items:["A shopper compares the prices of two products","Pounds are handed over to pay for a meal","Money is kept in a jar for next year","A price is agreed today for delivery in six months"],answer:"Unit of account (comparing), medium of exchange (paying), store of value (kept for later), standard of deferred payment (future settlement). Final question — why is barter inefficient? It requires a DOUBLE COINCIDENCE OF WANTS: each party must want exactly what the other offers. Rice fails as money because it spoils, is hard to standardise and is too plentiful."},
  "MC-GE-INTEREST":{type:"calculate",title:"Interest — Two Sides of One Rate",instruction:"Calculate each, then state whether the person gains or loses when the rate RISES.",items:["£3,000 saved at 4% simple interest for one year. Interest earned?","£5,000 saved at 3% for one year. Final balance?","A household with a variable-rate mortgage","A saver with a large deposit account"],answer:"1 £120. 2 £5,150 (£5,000 + £150). 3 LOSES — repayments rise, disposable income falls. 4 GAINS — higher return on savings. The interest rate is simultaneously the COST of borrowing and the REWARD for saving; a rise helps savers and hurts borrowers, which is why rate changes create winners and losers at once."},
  "MC-GE-FIN-INST":{type:"match",title:"Who Does What?",instruction:"Match each institution or product to what it actually does. Then answer: why does a bank charge a higher rate to a borrower with a poor credit history?",items:["Commercial bank","Insurance company","Building society","Insurance premium","Insurance excess"],answer:"Commercial bank: takes deposits and lends funds. Insurance company: compensates specified losses in return for a premium, pooling risk. Building society: a MUTUAL owned by its members. Premium: the payment FOR cover. Excess: the amount the policyholder pays TOWARDS a claim. Higher rate for poor credit = compensation for a greater risk of default."},
  "MC-GE-OBJECTIVES":{type:"sort",title:"Is That an Objective?",instruction:"Mark each YES (a UK macroeconomic objective) or NO. Then state the four main ones from memory.",items:["Low and stable inflation","Maximising the exchange rate at all times","Keeping every firm profitable","Low unemployment","Fixing every wage at the same level","A sustainable balance of payments"],answer:"YES: low and stable inflation, low unemployment, sustainable balance of payments — plus economic growth, the fourth. NO: maximising the exchange rate, keeping every firm profitable, fixing wages. Objectives concern the WHOLE economy, and they can conflict — growth may pull against price stability, which is why governments face trade-offs."},
  "MC-GE-GDP":{type:"calculate",title:"Level, Rate, or Per Head?",instruction:"Calculate each and state which of the three the question asked for. Show the formula first.",items:["Real GDP £500bn → £515bn. Growth rate?","Real GDP £800bn → £824bn. Growth rate?","GDP £2.1tn, population 70m. GDP per capita?","Real GDP rises 4%, population rises 3%. GDP per head?"],answer:"1 (15 ÷ 500) × 100 = 3%. 2 (24 ÷ 800) × 100 = 3%. 3 GDP ÷ population = £30,000. 4 Roughly +1%. The error this catches is quoting the LEVEL ('GDP is over £2 trillion') as evidence of high GROWTH — growth is a rate of change, and per capita divides by population."},
  "MC-GE-REAL-VS-NOMINAL":{type:"calculate",title:"Strip Out the Prices",instruction:"Work out what happened to REAL output in each case, then state why economists prefer real GDP.",items:["Nominal GDP rises 6%, prices rise 8%","Nominal GDP rises 3%, prices rise 5%","Nominal GDP rises 5%, prices are flat"],answer:"1 Real GDP FELL by about 2%. 2 Real output FELL by about 2%. 3 Real GDP rose about 5%. Approximate real change ≈ nominal change − inflation. Real GDP is preferred because it removes the effect of price changes, so a rise reflects more OUTPUT rather than higher prices. The error is reading any rise in nominal GDP as growth."},
  "MC-GE-CYCLE":{type:"sequence",title:"Where Are We in the Cycle?",instruction:"Place each description at the right stage of the economic cycle, then give the common definition of a recession.",items:["High activity, high employment, capacity strained","Output falling for two consecutive quarters","Factories below normal capacity, unemployment rising","Output recovering, confidence returning"],answer:"Boom, recession, slump/negative output gap, recovery. A recession is commonly defined as a fall in real GDP for two or more CONSECUTIVE QUARTERS. The error is calling any slowdown a recession — growth falling from 3% to 1% is still growth."},
  "MC-GE-GROWTH-CAUSES":{type:"sort",title:"Actual Growth or Potential Growth?",instruction:"Sort each into 'raises actual output now' or 'raises potential capacity'. One does both — find it.",items:["A tax cut increases household spending, capacity unchanged","Investment in productive machinery","Better worker training","Unemployed workers return to work","Fewer available workers from overseas"],answer:"Actual: the tax cut, unemployed returning to work. Potential: new machinery, training. Fewer overseas workers REDUCES potential growth. Infrastructure spending does both — it raises demand now and capacity later. The error is treating any rise in spending as 'economic growth' in the long-run sense."},
  "MC-GE-GDP-WELLBEING":{type:"evaluate",title:"Same GDP, Different Lives",instruction:"Two countries have identical GDP per capita. One has far greater inequality and pollution. Write two sentences on what GDP per capita misses, then name three indicators that fill the gap.",prompt:"Common error: writing 'GDP per capita is wrong' — it is not wrong, it is incomplete.",answer:"GDP per capita is an AVERAGE, so it hides how income is distributed, and it excludes non-market activity, environmental damage, health and leisure. Fuller indicators: life expectancy, adult literacy or school enrolment, access to clean water, HDI. The mark is earned by saying what is missing, not by dismissing the measure."},
  "MC-GE-SUSTAINABILITY":{type:"distinguish",title:"Growth vs Sustainable Growth",instruction:"Mark each policy S (supports sustainability) or N (not), then define sustainable development in one line.",items:["Investing in reliable low-emission public transport","Subsidising energy efficiency and renewable generation","Raising output as fast as possible regardless of emissions","A transport project improving long-term access for isolated communities"],answer:"S: public transport, energy efficiency, the access project (social sustainability). N: growth at any cost. Sustainable development meets present needs WITHOUT compromising the ability of future generations to meet theirs. The error is treating sustainability as purely environmental — it has social and economic dimensions too."},
  "MC-GE-UNEMP-TYPES":{type:"classify",title:"Name the Type",instruction:"Classify each as structural, cyclical, frictional or seasonal. Then state which type retraining helps most, and why.",items:["A car factory closes as demand shifts permanently to imported electric vehicles","A restaurant cuts staff in a recession because customers spend less","A graduate spends three weeks applying before accepting an offer","A ski instructor is out of work each summer","Automation permanently replaces routine jobs"],answer:"Structural: car factory, automation. Cyclical: restaurant. Frictional: graduate. Seasonal: ski instructor. Retraining helps STRUCTURAL unemployment most, because the jobs are not coming back — the mismatch is skills, not the state of the cycle. Cyclical unemployment needs demand to recover, not retraining."},
  "MC-GE-UNEMP-MEASURE":{type:"calculate",title:"Rate First, Then Who Counts",instruction:"Calculate each rate, then decide whether the last three people are unemployed, economically inactive, or employed.",items:["1.4 million unemployed in a labour force of 28 million","900,000 unemployed in a labour force of 30 million","A retired person not seeking work","A student not seeking or available for a job","Someone actively seeking work and available to start"],answer:"Rates: 1.4 ÷ 28 × 100 = 5%; 0.9 ÷ 30 × 100 = 3%. Watch the decimal — 0.5% and 0.3% are the classic slips. Retired person: INACTIVE. Student: INACTIVE. Actively seeking and available: UNEMPLOYED. To be unemployed you must be out of work AND available AND actively seeking; fail any of those and you are inactive, not unemployed. Full employment does NOT mean zero unemployment — some frictional unemployment always remains."},
  "MC-GE-UNEMP-EFFECTS":{type:"chain",title:"One Closure, Three Losers",instruction:"A factory closes. Complete a chain of consequences for each group. Each step must be specific.",prompt:"(a) the worker → … (b) nearby shops → … (c) the government budget → …",answer:"(a) Lost income → lower spending → possible loss of skills the longer unemployment lasts. (b) Lower household incomes → less spending locally → shops lose customers and may cut staff themselves (a local multiplier). (c) Income tax and NI receipts FALL while benefit spending RISES — the budget worsens from both sides at once. The error is treating unemployment as costly only to the person out of work."},
  "MC-GE-INCOME-VS-WEALTH":{type:"sort",title:"Flow or Stock?",instruction:"Sort each into income (a flow over time) or wealth (a stock of assets owned). Then explain why wealth is usually distributed less equally than income.",items:["The salary paid this month","A house owned by a household","Interest received this year","A weekly benefit payment","Shares held in a pension fund"],answer:"Income (flow): salary, interest received, benefit payment. Wealth (stock): the house, the shares. Wealth is typically MORE unequally distributed because assets accumulate and generate further returns — house price rises benefit existing owners, and returns compound over time, so gaps widen across generations."},
  "MC-GE-INEQUALITY":{type:"calculate",title:"Mean, Median, and the Share",instruction:"Answer each, committing before the reveal.",items:["Incomes: £18k, £20k, £22k, £24k, £300k. Find the mean and the median.","Which better describes a TYPICAL household here?","The richest fifth's share of income rises. More equal or less equal?"],answer:"Mean = £76,800; median = £22,000. The MEDIAN better describes a typical household, because a few very high incomes drag the mean upward and no actual household earns near £76,800. A rising top-fifth share means income is LESS equally distributed. The error is quoting an average income as if it described a typical person."},
  "MC-GE-REDISTRIBUTION":{type:"chain",title:"How Does Redistribution Actually Work?",instruction:"Complete the chain, then write one drawback of the policy.",prompt:"Progressive income tax → [step] → [step] → lower-income households better off. Drawback: ?",answer:"Chain: higher earners pay a higher PERCENTAGE of income → revenue funds benefits and public services (education, healthcare) → lower-income households gain both cash and access. Drawback: high marginal rates may weaken incentives to work extra hours, earn or invest; there may also be administrative cost. A full answer names the mechanism AND the trade-off."},
  "MC-GE-TAX-TYPES":{type:"sort",title:"Direct or Indirect? Progressive or Regressive?",instruction:"Give each tax TWO labels: direct/indirect, and progressive/regressive/proportional where it can be judged.",items:["Income tax","VAT","Corporation tax","A household on a low income pays 8% of income in a tax; a high earner pays 2%"],answer:"Income tax: direct, progressive. VAT: indirect, broadly regressive (it takes a larger share of a low income). Corporation tax: direct. The last case is REGRESSIVE — the rate FALLS as income rises. That one is the trap: students see 'a percentage of income' and answer progressive without checking which direction it moves. Direct = levied on income or wealth; indirect = levied on spending."},
  "MC-GE-INFLATION-DEF":{type:"correct",title:"Falling Inflation Is Not Falling Prices",instruction:"Correct each student statement in one sentence.",items:["'Inflation fell from 6% to 3%, so prices are now falling.'","'Inflation is 0%, so prices have gone back down.'","'The inflation rate is −0.5%, so inflation has stopped.'"],answer:"1 Prices are still RISING, just more slowly (disinflation). 2 Prices are stable at their current level — nothing has been reversed. 3 The price level is FALLING on average — that is DEFLATION, not the absence of inflation. Inflation is a sustained rise in the GENERAL PRICE LEVEL; the rate measures how fast that level is changing, not whether prices are high."},
  "MC-GE-CPI":{type:"calculate",title:"Build the Index",instruction:"Calculate each CPI, then answer the weighting question.",items:["Basket costs £50 in the base year, £60 now. CPI now?","Basket costs £80 in the base year, £88 later. CPI later?","Why does food get a larger weight than a rare leisure purchase?"],answer:"1 (60 ÷ 50) × 100 = 120 — prices are 20% higher than the base year. 2 (88 ÷ 80) × 100 = 110. The common slips are inverting the fraction (giving 83) or forgetting the ×100. Weighting: goods are weighted by the SHARE OF SPENDING they take in a typical household budget, so food moves the index more than an occasional purchase does."},
  "MC-GE-INFLATION-CAUSES":{type:"sort",title:"Demand-Pull or Cost-Push?",instruction:"Label each DP or CP, then state which policy tool best addresses each.",items:["Households spend rapidly while factories are near full capacity","Energy and raw-material costs rise sharply","A 10% wage rise follows a 6% productivity rise","Rapid growth in spending beyond productive capacity"],answer:"DP: rapid spending near capacity, spending beyond capacity. CP: energy costs, and the wage rise outstripping productivity (unit labour costs rise). Demand-pull responds to demand restraint — higher interest rates or tighter fiscal policy. Cost-push is harder: demand restraint worsens output, so supply-side measures that raise productivity work better over time."},
  "MC-GE-INFLATION-EFFECTS":{type:"classify",title:"Who Gains From Unexpected Inflation?",instruction:"Mark each GAINS or LOSES from unanticipated inflation, then calculate the real return.",items:["A borrower with a fixed-rate loan","A saver whose interest is below inflation","A household with a fixed-rate mortgage","Savings earn 2% while inflation is 5%. Real return?"],answer:"Borrower with fixed-rate loan: GAINS — the real value of the debt falls as prices and incomes rise. Saver below inflation: LOSES purchasing power. Fixed-rate mortgage holder: GAINS, same reason. Real return ≈ 2% − 5% = −3%. The error is assuming inflation harms everyone equally; it redistributes from lenders and savers to borrowers when it is unanticipated."},
  "MC-GE-GOVT-SPENDING":{type:"sort",title:"Is That Government Spending?",instruction:"Mark each IN or OUT of government expenditure, then answer the ageing-population question.",items:["Public healthcare","A consumer's private saving","A firm's retained profit","Household pocket money","State pensions"],answer:"IN: public healthcare, state pensions. OUT: private saving, retained profit, pocket money — these are private, not government, spending. Ageing population: spending pressure RISES (pensions, healthcare, social care) while a smaller share of working-age people pays income tax, so the budget is squeezed from both directions. Responses include encouraging later retirement or higher participation."},
  "MC-GE-BUDGET":{type:"calculate",title:"Deficit, Surplus, or Debt?",instruction:"State the answer for each, then write the one-line difference between a deficit and the national debt.",items:["Government collects £720bn, spends £760bn","Government collects £600bn, spends £570bn","Tax revenue equals government spending","'The budget deficit means the UK imports more than it exports.'"],answer:"1 A £40bn DEFICIT (spending exceeds revenue). 2 A £30bn SURPLUS. 3 A BALANCED budget. 4 FALSE — that describes a current account deficit; the two are different measures. Deficit vs debt: a deficit is a YEARLY shortfall; debt is the ACCUMULATED stock of past borrowing. Heavy borrowing can also push up interest rates and crowd out private investment."},
  "MC-GE-FISCAL":{type:"sort",title:"Which Direction Is This Policy Pushing?",instruction:"Label each EXPANSIONARY or CONTRACTIONARY, then state the aim of each.",items:["Cutting income tax and increasing government spending","Raising income tax and cutting public spending","Increasing infrastructure spending during a recession","Cutting spending when inflation is persistently high"],answer:"Expansionary: 1 and 3 — raise aggregate demand to support growth and employment. Contractionary: 2 and 4 — reduce demand to ease inflationary pressure. The error is matching 'higher taxes and lower spending' to expansionary because both words sound active. Ask instead: does this ADD to demand or TAKE FROM it? Expansionary policy near full capacity risks inflation rather than extra output."},
  "MC-GE-AUTO-STABILISERS":{type:"distinguish",title:"Automatic, or a New Decision?",instruction:"Mark each A (automatic stabiliser) or D (a discretionary decision). Then say what makes a stabiliser 'automatic'.",items:["Income tax receipts fall when incomes fall in a recession","Benefits rise as more people lose work","The Chancellor announces a new school-building programme","A temporary VAT cut is legislated in a Budget"],answer:"A: falling tax receipts, rising benefit spending. D: the building programme, the legislated VAT cut. A stabiliser is automatic because it responds to the cycle WITHOUT any new decision — it cushions demand immediately, with no announcement, vote or time lag."},
  "MC-GE-POLICY-LAGS":{type:"sequence",title:"Why Nothing Happens Immediately",instruction:"Put the stages in order for a school-building programme announced today, then estimate where the delay is longest.",items:["Construction begins and workers are hired","The problem is recognised in the data","Budgets are designed and approved","Output and employment respond","The policy is announced"],answer:"Recognition → announcement → design and approval → construction begins → output responds. The longest delays are usually approval and implementation. This is why fiscal policy can arrive after the problem has changed, and why supply-side policies (education, infrastructure) take years before productivity rises — a point worth stating explicitly in evaluation answers."},
  "MC-GE-BOE":{type:"correct",title:"Who Does What at the Bank?",instruction:"Correct each statement in one line.",items:["'The Chancellor sets the UK base interest rate.'","'The Bank of England's target is 0% inflation.'","'Quantitative easing means printing money that goes straight to consumers.'"],answer:"1 The Bank of England's MONETARY POLICY COMMITTEE sets Bank Rate, independently of the Chancellor. 2 The target is 2% CPI — low and stable, not zero; deflation causes its own problems. 3 QE is an EXPANSION OF THE MONEY SUPPLY through the central bank buying assets such as government bonds, which lowers yields and encourages lending and spending — it is not cash handed to households. A credible target helps because it anchors expectations for planning prices and wages."},
  "MC-GE-MONETARY":{type:"chain",title:"From Bank Rate to Spending",instruction:"Complete both chains. Each arrow must be a mechanism, not 'the economy changes'.",prompt:"Rate RISE → [step] → [step] → aggregate demand falls. Rate CUT → [step] → [step] → aggregate demand rises. Then: who loses from a rise, and what is the trade-off?",answer:"Rise: borrowing becomes dearer and saving more attractive → credit-financed purchases and investment fall → demand falls, easing price pressure. Cut: borrowing cheaper, saving less rewarding → spending and investment rise → demand rises. Losers from a rise: households with variable-rate mortgages, borrowers generally. Trade-off: it lowers inflation but weakens growth and employment — sharpest when inflation is high AND growth is already weak. The error is claiming higher rates 'encourage people to spend more'."},
  "MC-GE-RATES-AND-CURRENCY":{type:"chain",title:"Rates, Then the Pound",instruction:"Complete the chain and then predict the opposite case before the reveal.",prompt:"UK interest rates RISE → [step] → [step] → the pound appreciates. Now: UK rates are CUT — what happens to sterling, and why?",answer:"Rise: UK financial assets offer higher returns → overseas investors buy sterling to hold them → demand for pounds increases → the pound appreciates. Cut: some investors seek better returns elsewhere → demand for sterling falls (and supply rises as funds leave) → downward pressure on the pound. The step students skip is that a currency's value comes from DEMAND FOR AND SUPPLY OF that currency."},
  "MC-GE-SUPPLYSIDE":{type:"sort",title:"Capacity or Demand?",instruction:"Mark each SS (supply-side: raises productive capacity) or AD (works mainly through demand). Then say why supply-side policy is slow.",items:["Funding vocational training and skills development","A temporary VAT cut","Building a new rail network","Reducing unnecessary barriers to entry","Privatising a state industry","A one-off payment to households"],answer:"SS: training, rail network, removing barriers, privatisation. AD: VAT cut, one-off payment. Supply-side policy aims to let the economy produce MORE AT A GIVEN PRICE LEVEL — growth without inflation. It is slow because education, infrastructure and investment take years to raise productivity, and some reforms carry high upfront costs with uncertain results. Privatisation can sharpen cost incentives but risks abuse of market power in a natural monopoly without regulation."},
  "MC-GE-EXTERNALITIES":{type:"classify",title:"Who Is the Third Party?",instruction:"For each, name the THIRD PARTY and say whether the externality is positive or negative. If you cannot name a third party, it is not an externality.",items:["A factory's noise affects nearby households that do not buy its product","Training makes workers more productive for future employers too","Second-hand smoke affecting people near a smoker","A firm pays higher wages to its own staff","Pollution from a factory harms residents not involved in the sale"],answer:"1 Neighbours, negative. 2 Future employers and the wider economy, positive. 3 Bystanders, negative. 4 NOT an externality — staff are party to the transaction; this is a private cost. 5 Residents, negative. An externality is a cost or benefit falling on someone OUTSIDE the transaction, which is why the market price fails to reflect it."},
  "MC-GE-MERIT-GOODS":{type:"sort",title:"Merit, Demerit, or Public Good?",instruction:"Sort each and give the defining feature of the group you place it in.",items:["Education","A product with health costs from overconsumption","Street lighting","Vaccination","National defence"],answer:"Merit: education, vaccination — under-consumed because individuals underestimate the private and social benefits. Demerit: the harmful product — over-consumed because costs are underestimated. Public: street lighting, defence — NON-RIVAL and NON-EXCLUDABLE, so private firms under-provide them because of free-riding. The error is calling anything the government provides a public good; the test is the two characteristics, not who supplies it."},
  "MC-GE-MARKET-FAILURE":{type:"classify",title:"Which Kind of Failure?",instruction:"Name the type of market failure in each case, then define market failure in one line.",items:["A buyer knows less about a used car's condition than the seller","Nobody will pay for street lighting they can use for free","A market produces too much pollution at the unregulated equilibrium"],answer:"1 ASYMMETRIC INFORMATION. 2 The FREE-RIDER problem with a public good. 3 A negative externality — social costs are not reflected in the price. Market failure means the market allocation of resources is inefficient and does not maximise social welfare. The error is treating 'market failure' as 'the market collapsed' — it means the outcome is inefficient, not that trade stopped."},
  "MC-GE-INTERVENTION":{type:"match",title:"Match the Tool to the Failure",instruction:"Choose the intervention that fits each problem and state which way it shifts the curve.",items:["A good with negative externalities is over-consumed","A merit good is under-consumed","Pollution of a shared river","Consumers underestimate the benefits of healthy eating"],answer:"1 Indirect tax — raises firms' costs, shifts supply LEFT, raises price, reduces quantity. 2 Subsidy or public provision — lowers the effective price, increases uptake towards the socially efficient level. 3 Property rights or regulation — an owner has an incentive to protect the resource, though monitoring a large shared area is difficult. 4 Information provision. A tax makes producers and consumers face more of the SOCIAL cost — that phrase earns the mark."},
  "MC-GE-GOVT-FAILURE":{type:"evaluate",title:"When the Cure Causes Harm",instruction:"For each intervention, write one way it could make the outcome worse. Then define government failure.",items:["A pollution tax set too high","An information campaign about healthy eating","A subsidy for a good the government has misjudged"],answer:"1 Large fall in employment with little environmental gain — the cost exceeds the benefit. 2 Consumers may ignore or misunderstand the information, so behaviour does not change. 3 Public money spent with no welfare gain, plus the opportunity cost of the next best use of those funds. Government failure = intervention that produces a worse allocation of resources than before, through poor information, high cost or unintended effects. Every intervention answer should acknowledge this possibility."},
  "MC-GE-EXPORTS-IMPORTS":{type:"classify",title:"Which Way Is It Flowing?",instruction:"For the UK, label each an EXPORT or an IMPORT. Then write the two definitions.",items:["A UK firm buys computer equipment made in South Korea","A UK firm sells software to a customer abroad","A foreign visitor pays for a night in a UK hotel","A UK household pays for a hotel abroad","A UK accountancy firm advises an overseas client"],answer:"Imports: the Korean equipment, the UK household's hotel abroad. Exports: software sold abroad, the foreign visitor's UK hotel stay, advice to an overseas client. Exports are goods and services SOLD abroad; imports are BOUGHT from abroad. Services count — the two hotel cases are the ones students reverse, because the money and the customer move in opposite directions."},
  "MC-GE-TRADE-BENEFITS":{type:"explain",title:"Why Import What You Could Make?",instruction:"Answer in two sentences using opportunity cost, then define comparative advantage precisely.",prompt:"A country CAN produce food itself, but imports it and specialises in renewable-energy equipment instead. Why is that rational?",answer:"Because another country produces food at a LOWER OPPORTUNITY COST, so both gain by specialising where they are relatively efficient and exchanging. Comparative advantage means producing a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country — NOT producing it with fewer resources, which is absolute advantage. The gains are wider markets, economies of scale, lower prices and more choice. The error is assuming a country should make anything it is capable of making."},
  "MC-GE-PROTECTIONISM":{type:"evaluate",title:"Tariff on Steel — Who Wins, Who Loses?",instruction:"Complete the table for a tariff on imported steel, then decide whether 'this benefits everyone in the UK' is true.",headers:["Group","Effect","Winner or loser?"],rows:[["UK steel producers","",""],["UK firms that BUY steel","",""],["UK consumers","",""],["Government","",""]],answer:"Producers: protected from cheaper imports, output and jobs supported — winners. Firms buying steel: input costs rise — losers. Consumers: higher prices for steel-containing goods, less choice — losers. Government: gains tariff revenue. So 'benefits everyone' is FALSE: protection redistributes towards the protected industry at others' expense. A quota limits QUANTITY rather than raising price directly, but also raises prices and cuts choice. The infant-industry argument is the strongest case for temporary protection."},
  "MC-GE-TRADING-BLOCS":{type:"explain",title:"Why Join a Bloc?",instruction:"Give one benefit and one cost of joining a trading bloc with a common currency, then define a free trade agreement.",prompt:"Common error: describing a trading bloc as simply 'countries that are friendly'.",answer:"Benefit: trade barriers between members fall and, with a common currency, firms avoid currency-conversion costs and can compare prices easily — trade and competition increase. Cost: loss of independent monetary policy and of freedom to set external trade policy; trade may be diverted from cheaper non-members. A free trade agreement allows goods and services to move between member countries without restrictions such as tariffs and quotas."},
  "MC-GE-TRADE-RISKS":{type:"chain",title:"One Port, Many Factories",instruction:"Complete the chain, then name one way a firm can reduce this risk.",prompt:"A port closure delays components → [step] → [step] → prices rise in several countries. Risk reduction: ?",answer:"Chain: factories cannot obtain the components → production halts or slows in multiple countries → shortages push prices up well beyond the country where the disruption began. Mitigation: diversify suppliers across countries, hold larger buffer stocks, or shorten the supply chain. This is the flip side of interdependence — the same links that lower costs also transmit shocks."},
  "MC-GE-BOP":{type:"sort",title:"Is It In the Current Account?",instruction:"Mark each IN or OUT of the current account. For those in, name the component.",items:["A UK firm exports software to a French customer","An overseas company buys UK industrial land","A UK bank receives interest from a foreign borrower","Foreign aid sent from the UK","An overseas company buys a UK factory"],answer:"IN: software export (trade in services), interest received (primary income), foreign aid sent (secondary income). OUT: buying land, buying a factory — these are investment flows recorded in the financial account, not current trade. The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income. The error is putting any transaction with a foreign party into the current account."},
  "MC-GE-BOP-CALC":{type:"calculate",title:"Add the Components",instruction:"Calculate each balance, showing the components before the total. State deficit or surplus explicitly.",items:["Goods exports £200bn, goods imports £250bn, services surplus £30bn","Exports £80bn, imports £95bn, other current items balanced","Goods trade −£70bn, services trade +£50bn","Exports £120bn, imports £100bn"],answer:"1 −50 + 30 = £20bn DEFICIT. 2 £15bn deficit. 3 −70 + 50 = £20bn deficit. 4 £20bn SURPLUS. The two slips are ignoring the services figure entirely and losing the sign — always write the goods balance first as a negative or positive number, then add services."},
  "MC-GE-BOP-EVAL":{type:"evaluate",title:"Is This Deficit a Problem?",instruction:"For each, decide whether the current account deficit is more or less concerning, and give the reason.",items:["Imports are mostly advanced machinery to expand factories","The deficit is persistent and financed by short-term borrowing","The deficit reflects a temporary consumer boom","The deficit is financed by sustainable long-term inward investment"],answer:"Less concerning: machinery imports (they raise future productive capacity and export potential), sustainable inward investment, a temporary boom. More concerning: persistent and hard to finance. The question to ask is: is it TEMPORARY and financing productive investment, or PERSISTENT and difficult to finance? The error is treating any deficit as automatically a crisis."},
  "MC-GE-BOP-POLICY":{type:"sort",title:"What Actually Improves the Balance?",instruction:"Mark each policy LONG-RUN, SHORT-RUN, or UNRELIABLE for improving the current account, and give the mechanism.",items:["Improving productivity so domestic firms become more competitive","Investing in research and development","Allowing the currency to depreciate","Improving export quality"],answer:"Long-run: productivity, R&D, export quality — they make exports more attractive on non-price terms as well as price, which is durable. Depreciation is short-run and UNRELIABLE on its own: it only helps if demand for exports and imports responds sufficiently to the price change. The error is treating a weaker currency as a guaranteed fix."},
  "MC-GE-APPREC-DEPREC":{type:"correct",title:"Which Way Did It Move?",instruction:"State appreciation or depreciation for each, then define an exchange rate in one line.",items:["£1 buys €1.20 instead of €1.10","£1 falls from $1.40 to $1.25","The pound reaches its highest value against the dollar in a decade"],answer:"1 APPRECIATION — each pound buys more euros. 2 DEPRECIATION. 3 Appreciation. An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in terms of another. The check that prevents errors: if the number of foreign currency units per pound RISES, sterling has appreciated. Note that a strong pound is not automatically good news — it makes UK exports dearer abroad."},
  "MC-GE-CURRENCY-DEMAND":{type:"classify",title:"Demand for Pounds, or Supply of Pounds?",instruction:"Mark each as increasing DEMAND for sterling or increasing SUPPLY of sterling, then state the effect on its value.",items:["Overseas buyers purchase more UK exports","UK tourists buy goods abroad","A rise in foreign demand for UK financial assets","A country persistently imports much more than it exports"],answer:"Demand for sterling (value up): buying UK exports, buying UK financial assets — foreigners must obtain pounds first. Supply of sterling (value down): UK tourists spending abroad, persistent excess imports — UK residents sell pounds to buy foreign currency. A floating rate is set by demand for and supply of the currency; the error is thinking only governments move it."},
  "MC-GE-EXCHANGE-RATE":{type:"compare",title:"Weak Pound: Good or Bad?",instruction:"Complete the table for a DEPRECIATION of sterling, then write a one-sentence judgement.",headers:["Group","Effect of a weaker pound","Better or worse off?"],rows:[["UK exporters","",""],["UK consumers buying imported food and fuel","",""],["UK manufacturers using imported components","",""],["Inflation","",""]],answer:"Exporters: goods cheaper in foreign currency, more price competitive — better off. Consumers of imports: imported goods dearer — worse off. Manufacturers using imported inputs: input costs rise — worse off. Inflation: imported goods and inputs cost more, adding to cost-push pressure. Judgement: a depreciation REDISTRIBUTES between exporters and importers rather than helping or harming 'everyone'. Reverse every row for an appreciation."},
  "MC-GE-EXR-CALC":{type:"calculate",title:"Multiply or Divide?",instruction:"Work each out and write the rule you used BEFORE calculating. Getting the direction right matters more than the arithmetic.",items:["£1 = $1.25. How many dollars would £400 buy?","A UK company must pay €1,000 when £1 = €1.25. How many pounds?","A UK buyer must pay €18,000 when £1 = €1.20. How many pounds?","£1 falls to $1.25. What does a $28,000 payment cost in pounds?"],answer:"1 £400 × 1.25 = $500. 2 €1,000 ÷ 1.25 = £800. 3 €18,000 ÷ 1.20 = £15,000. 4 $28,000 ÷ 1.25 = £22,400. Rule: pounds → foreign currency, MULTIPLY by the rate; foreign currency → pounds, DIVIDE. The sense-check is whether the answer moved in a plausible direction."},
  "MC-GE-J-CURVE":{type:"diagram",title:"Why Doesn't It Improve Straight Away?",instruction:"Sketch the current account balance against time after a depreciation. Label the initial worsening, the turning point and the improvement. Then state the condition for improvement.",prompt:"Common error: drawing immediate improvement after a depreciation.",answer:"The balance WORSENS first: import prices rise immediately while contracts are already fixed and quantities take time to adjust, so the import bill rises before export volumes grow. It improves later as buyers respond. The condition is that demand for exports and imports must be sufficiently PRICE ELASTIC — if quantities barely respond, a depreciation can leave the balance worse. Price elasticity of demand for exports is the factor to name."},
  "MC-GE-GLOBALISATION":{type:"classify",title:"Is That Evidence of Globalisation?",instruction:"Mark each YES or NO as evidence of greater globalisation. Two are the reverse — find them.",items:["A firm designs in one country, sources components globally and sells worldwide","Every good being produced locally","A factory relying on components from several countries","A country ending all international trade","Online platforms connecting businesses across borders"],answer:"YES: the global firm, the multi-country supply chain, the online platforms. NO: local-only production and ending trade are DE-globalisation. Globalisation is growing economic integration and interdependence between countries; economic interdependence means countries rely on one another through trade and finance."},
  "MC-GE-MNC":{type:"evaluate",title:"Multinational Arrives — Two Columns",instruction:"A multinational builds a factory in a developing country. Complete both columns, then name what this investment is called.",headers:["Benefit to the host country","Concern for the host country"],rows:[["",""],["",""],["",""]],answer:"This is FOREIGN DIRECT INVESTMENT. Benefits: capital and jobs, skills and training, access to technology, tax revenue, export earnings. Concerns: profits shifted abroad rather than reinvested, weak regulation exploited, environmental damage, production moved elsewhere later to cut costs. This is why host governments regulate while trying to retain the investment — an answer with only one column is incomplete."},
  "MC-GE-GLOB-COSTS":{type:"chain",title:"Who Pays for Cheaper Goods?",instruction:"Complete the chain, then name the type of unemployment created and the policy that addresses it.",prompt:"A factory moves production overseas to lower costs → [step] → [step] → workers in the original country are affected.",answer:"Chain: domestic plant closes → workers' skills no longer match locally available vacancies → they face STRUCTURAL unemployment and need retraining. The policy is retraining and skills support, not demand stimulus, because those specific jobs are not returning. Freer movement of labour can ease skills shortages in some sectors, but the costs and benefits of globalisation fall on DIFFERENT groups — consumers gain from lower prices while displaced workers bear the loss."},

  // ---- GCSE Separate Sciences (Edexcel): one starter per topic tag across
  // gcse-sep-bio, gcse-sep-chem and gcse-sep-phys. Each targets the single
  // error that tag represents and makes students commit before the reveal.
  // ---- GCSE Separate Biology
  "MC-SEP-BIO-CELL":{type:"sort",title:"Which Structure Does What?",instruction:"Match each job to ONE structure. Two structures are confused every year — be ready to say which.",items:["Controls what enters and leaves the cell","Gives a plant cell its rigid shape","Site of aerobic respiration","Contains the genetic material","Site of protein synthesis"],answer:"Membrane (control), cell wall (rigid shape), mitochondria, nucleus, ribosomes. The confusion is MEMBRANE vs WALL: the wall is rigid support and is freely permeable — it controls nothing. Only the membrane is selectively permeable. Saying 'the cell wall controls what enters' loses the mark."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-RIBO":{type:"match",title:"Organelle to Job",instruction:"Write the organelle responsible for each, then name the two cell types that both contain ribosomes.",items:["Joins amino acids into a protein","Releases energy by aerobic respiration","Absorbs light for photosynthesis","Stores cell sap in a plant cell"],answer:"Ribosome, mitochondrion, chloroplast, vacuole. Ribosomes are in BOTH prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells — that is the exam point students miss, because they assume prokaryotes have 'no organelles' and therefore no ribosomes."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-DIFFUSION":{type:"correct",title:"Fix the Diffusion Sentence",instruction:"Find and correct the error in each statement.",items:["'Diffusion moves particles from low to high concentration.'","'Diffusion needs energy from respiration.'","'Diffusion stops completely once concentrations are equal.'"],answer:"1 High to LOW — down the gradient. 2 Diffusion is PASSIVE; active transport is the one needing energy. 3 Particles keep moving randomly, but there is no NET movement — 'net' is the word that earns the mark."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-OSMOSIS":{type:"scenario",title:"Which Way Does the Water Go?",instruction:"For each, state which way water moves and what happens to the cell. Commit before the reveal.",items:["A plant cell in pure water","A plant cell in concentrated sugar solution","A red blood cell in pure water","A red blood cell in concentrated salt solution"],answer:"Into the cell (turgid); out (plasmolysed); into the cell (bursts — lysis); out (crenated/shrivels). Osmosis moves WATER only, from dilute to concentrated, through a partially permeable membrane. The common error is describing sugar moving instead of water."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-ENZYME":{type:"graph",title:"Sketch the Temperature Curve",instruction:"Sketch enzyme activity against temperature. Mark the optimum. Then explain what happens either side of it — the two sides need DIFFERENT explanations.",prompt:"Common error: writing that the enzyme 'dies' above the optimum.",answer:"Below optimum: less kinetic energy, fewer successful collisions with the substrate — the enzyme is unchanged and the effect is reversible. Above optimum: bonds holding the tertiary structure break, the ACTIVE SITE changes shape, the substrate no longer fits — DENATURED, and irreversible. Enzymes are not alive, so they cannot die."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-PHOTO":{type:"correct",title:"Balance the Word Equation",instruction:"Write the word equation for photosynthesis from memory, then check it against these three student attempts and correct each.",items:["'oxygen + water → glucose + carbon dioxide'","'carbon dioxide + water → glucose (light energy is a reactant)'","'carbon dioxide + oxygen → glucose + water'"],answer:"Correct: carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen (light energy, chlorophyll above the arrow). 1 reverses reactants and products. 2 light is a CONDITION, not a reactant — it goes above the arrow. 3 uses oxygen as a reactant; oxygen is a product."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-MITOSIS":{type:"compare",title:"Mitosis or Meiosis?",instruction:"Complete the table, then decide which process made each: a skin cell replacing a scab, and a sperm cell.",headers:["Feature","Mitosis","Meiosis"],rows:[["Number of daughter cells","",""],["Genetically identical?","",""],["Chromosome number","",""],["Where it happens","",""]],answer:"Mitosis: 2 cells, identical, diploid maintained, growth/repair/asexual reproduction — the scab. Meiosis: 4 cells, genetically different, haploid (halved), gamete production — the sperm. The error is saying mitosis 'halves' the chromosome number; that is meiosis."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-GENETICS":{type:"distinguish",title:"Gene or Allele?",instruction:"Label each statement GENE or ALLELE, then write one sentence stating the relationship between them.",items:["A section of DNA coding for a protein","The version for brown eyes rather than blue","There is one for eye colour","A dominant or recessive form"],answer:"Gene: the section of DNA, and 'one for eye colour'. Allele: the brown-eye version, and the dominant/recessive form. Relationship: a gene is the instruction for a characteristic; an allele is one of its alternative VERSIONS. You inherit two alleles of each gene, one from each parent."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-BLOOD":{type:"match",title:"Four Components, Four Jobs",instruction:"Match each blood component to its job and name one adaptation that fits it for that job.",items:["Red blood cell","White blood cell","Platelet","Plasma"],answer:"Red cell: transports oxygen — biconcave for surface area, no nucleus for more haemoglobin. White cell: defence — can change shape to engulf pathogens, has a nucleus. Platelet: clotting. Plasma: transports dissolved substances, including carbon dioxide, urea and nutrients. The error is saying red cells 'carry carbon dioxide' — most is carried by plasma."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-DIGEST":{type:"sequence",title:"Digest, Then Absorb",instruction:"Put the organs in order along the gut, then state for each whether it mainly DIGESTS, ABSORBS, or both.",items:["Small intestine","Mouth","Large intestine","Stomach","Oesophagus"],answer:"Mouth, oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine. Digests: mouth (amylase), stomach (protease, acid), small intestine (enzymes from pancreas). Absorbs: small intestine (nutrients, via villi), large intestine (water). The error is thinking the stomach absorbs food — absorption is overwhelmingly the small intestine, which is why villi matter."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-TRANSPIRATION":{type:"evaluate",title:"Four Conditions, One Rate",instruction:"For each change, state whether transpiration rate RISES or FALLS, and give the reason in terms of evaporation or diffusion.",items:["Temperature rises","Humidity rises","Wind speed rises","Light intensity falls"],answer:"Rises (more kinetic energy, faster evaporation); FALLS (smaller water-vapour gradient outside the leaf); rises (wind removes humid air, keeping the gradient steep); falls (stomata close in darkness). The trap is humidity — students assume 'more water about' means more transpiration, but it flattens the diffusion gradient."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-PLANTS":{type:"compare",title:"Xylem or Phloem?",instruction:"Complete the table. Then say which vessel a sugar-tracing radioactive tracer would appear in.",headers:["Feature","Xylem","Phloem"],rows:[["Transports","",""],["Direction","",""],["Living or dead cells?","",""]],answer:"Xylem: water and mineral ions, upwards only, dead hollow cells strengthened with lignin. Phloem: dissolved sugars, both directions (translocation), living cells with sieve plates. A sugar tracer appears in PHLOEM. Mnemonic that survives the exam: phloem = food."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-DNA":{type:"sequence",title:"Nest the Terms",instruction:"Put these in order from LARGEST to SMALLEST, then write one sentence defining a gene.",items:["Gene","Nucleus","Chromosome","Base","DNA molecule"],answer:"Nucleus > chromosome > DNA molecule > gene > base. A gene is a SECTION of DNA that codes for a protein. The common error is treating gene and chromosome as the same size — a single chromosome carries hundreds of genes."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-REPRO":{type:"chain",title:"Across the Placenta",instruction:"Draw two arrows across a placenta: one for substances going TO the fetus, one for substances leaving. Label each. Then answer the question below.",prompt:"Does maternal blood mix with fetal blood? Justify your answer.",answer:"To the fetus: oxygen, glucose, nutrients, antibodies. From the fetus: carbon dioxide, urea. The blood does NOT mix — exchange happens by diffusion across a barrier with a large surface area. Mixing would risk immune rejection and blood-group incompatibility. 'The blood mixes' is the single most common error here."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-DEFENCE":{type:"classify",title:"Three Defences, One Job",instruction:"Name which white-blood-cell defence each describes, then say which one gives long-term protection.",items:["Engulfing and digesting a pathogen","Producing proteins that lock onto a specific antigen","Neutralising the poisons a pathogen releases"],answer:"Phagocytosis; antibody production (lymphocytes); antitoxin production. Long-term protection comes from ANTIBODIES, because memory cells persist. Phagocytosis is non-specific and leaves no memory. Also worth stating: skin, mucus and stomach acid are barriers, not white-cell defences."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-NUTRITION":{type:"match",title:"Nutrient to Function",instruction:"Match each nutrient group to its main function, then name a deficiency disease for two of them.",items:["Carbohydrate","Protein","Lipid","Vitamin C","Iron","Fibre"],answer:"Energy; growth and repair; energy store and insulation; connective tissue (deficiency: scurvy); haemoglobin (deficiency: anaemia); keeps food moving through the gut. A balanced diet supplies all of these in appropriate AMOUNTS — 'balanced' means proportion, not simply variety."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-DISEASE":{type:"correct",title:"Why Antibiotics Fail on a Cold",instruction:"A patient asks for antibiotics for a cold. Write the explanation you would give in two sentences, using the words 'bacterial' and 'host cell'.",prompt:"Common error: 'antibiotics only kill strong bacteria' or 'viruses are too small'.",answer:"Antibiotics work by attacking structures and processes specific to BACTERIA — cell walls, bacterial ribosomes, bacterial enzymes. Viruses have none of these and reproduce inside the patient's own HOST CELLS, so there is no bacterial target to attack without damaging the patient. Size is irrelevant. Add: overuse drives antibiotic resistance, which is why it matters."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-IMMUNITY":{type:"chain",title:"What a Vaccine Actually Does",instruction:"Complete the chain. Each step must be specific — 'it makes you immune' scores nothing.",prompt:"Dead or inactive pathogen injected → [step] → [step] → [step] → fast response on real infection.",answer:"Antigens on the harmless pathogen are recognised → lymphocytes produce specific antibodies → MEMORY CELLS remain in the body → on real infection antibodies are produced faster and in greater quantity, destroying the pathogen before symptoms develop. The error is thinking the vaccine itself provides the protective antibodies; it triggers the body to make its own and, crucially, to remember."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-HOMEOSTASIS":{type:"sequence",title:"Filter, Then Take Back",instruction:"Put the kidney's stages in order, then state one substance that is ALWAYS reabsorbed and one that never is.",items:["Selective reabsorption","Urine passes to the bladder","Filtration of small molecules from blood","Water balance adjusted by ADH"],answer:"Filtration → selective reabsorption → ADH adjusts water → urine to bladder. Always reabsorbed: glucose (all of it, in a healthy person). Never reabsorbed: urea — it is the waste being removed. The word SELECTIVE is the mark: the kidney filters indiscriminately by size, then takes back what the body needs."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-NERVOUS":{type:"sequence",title:"Build the Reflex Arc",instruction:"Put the reflex arc in order and label the two synapses. Then say why a reflex is faster than a considered action.",items:["Effector (muscle or gland)","Relay neurone","Stimulus","Sensory neurone","Receptor","Motor neurone","Response"],answer:"Stimulus → receptor → sensory neurone → relay neurone (in spinal cord) → motor neurone → effector → response. Synapses sit between sensory/relay and relay/motor. It is faster because the signal does not travel to the conscious brain — the relay neurone in the spinal cord short-cuts it. Reversing SENSORY and MOTOR is the usual error."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-HORMONE":{type:"compare",title:"Nervous or Hormonal?",instruction:"Complete the table, then classify each response: pulling your hand off a hot pan; feeling adrenaline before an exam.",headers:["Feature","Nervous system","Endocrine system"],rows:[["Transmitted by","",""],["Speed","",""],["Duration of effect","",""]],answer:"Nervous: electrical impulses along neurones, very fast, short-lived, precise target. Endocrine: chemical hormones in the BLOODSTREAM, slower, longer-lasting, broader target. Hot pan = nervous (reflex). Adrenaline = hormonal. The error is saying hormones travel along nerves."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-EVOLUTION":{type:"chain",title:"Natural Selection Without the Shortcut",instruction:"Complete the chain for antibiotic-resistant bacteria. Every step must avoid the words 'wanted', 'tried' or 'in order to'.",prompt:"A population of bacteria is exposed to an antibiotic → [step] → [step] → [step] → the population is mostly resistant.",answer:"Random MUTATION means variation already exists — a few bacteria are resistant BEFORE the antibiotic arrives → the antibiotic kills the non-resistant ones → survivors reproduce and pass on the resistance allele → the allele's frequency rises over generations. The error is Lamarckian: bacteria do not become resistant BECAUSE of the antibiotic, and organisms cannot adapt on purpose. Selection acts on variation that is already there."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-ECOLOGY":{type:"calculate",title:"From Quadrat to Population",instruction:"A 1 m² quadrat is placed 10 times in a 500 m² field. Counts of daisies: 4, 6, 3, 5, 7, 2, 5, 4, 6, 8. Estimate the population, showing both steps.",answer:"Mean = 50 ÷ 10 = 5 per m². Estimated population = 5 × 500 = 2,500 daisies. Two exam points: placement must be RANDOM (e.g. coordinates from a random number generator) to avoid bias, and more quadrats give a more reliable mean. Quadrats suit plants and slow-moving organisms — use a transect if you are studying how distribution changes across a gradient."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-CYCLES":{type:"chain",title:"Why Decomposers Matter",instruction:"Complete the chain, then answer: what would happen to plant growth if decomposers were removed?",prompt:"A leaf falls and dies → [step] → [step] → a plant takes up a mineral ion.",answer:"Decomposers (bacteria and fungi) break down the dead material → mineral ions are released into the soil → roots absorb them for making proteins and chlorophyll. Without decomposers, nutrients stay locked in dead matter, the soil is depleted and plant growth fails. Warmth, moisture and oxygen speed decay up — which is also why a fridge slows it."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-FEEDBACK":{type:"diagram",title:"Close the Loop",instruction:"Draw a negative feedback loop for body temperature rising above 37°C. Label: receptor, coordinator, effector, and the response that returns it to normal.",prompt:"Common error: describing the response as making the change bigger.",answer:"Receptor (skin and hypothalamus detect rise) → coordinator (hypothalamus) → effectors (sweat glands secrete sweat; vasodilation of skin arterioles) → temperature FALLS back towards 37°C → the stimulus is removed. NEGATIVE means the response REVERSES the change, returning the variable to its set point. Positive feedback amplifies instead, which is why it is rare in homeostasis."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-THYROID":{type:"chain",title:"The Thyroxine Loop",instruction:"Complete the loop, then state what happens when thyroxine levels rise too high.",prompt:"Thyroxine level falls → [gland] releases [hormone] → [gland] releases thyroxine → metabolic rate rises → ?",answer:"Pituitary releases TSH → thyroid releases thyroxine → metabolic rate rises → rising thyroxine INHIBITS TSH release from the pituitary → thyroxine production slows. That inhibition is the negative feedback. Thyroxine sets basal metabolic rate; too little slows growth and metabolism."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-CYCLE":{type:"match",title:"Four Hormones, One Cycle",instruction:"Match each hormone to its role, then say which two are used in the combined contraceptive pill and why.",items:["FSH","LH","Oestrogen","Progesterone"],answer:"FSH: matures a follicle in the ovary and stimulates oestrogen production. LH: triggers OVULATION (around day 14). Oestrogen: rebuilds the uterus lining, inhibits FSH. Progesterone: maintains the lining, inhibits FSH and LH. The pill uses oestrogen and progesterone because both INHIBIT FSH, so no follicle matures. Confusing FSH with LH is the standard error — FSH grows it, LH releases it."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-TROPHIC":{type:"sequence",title:"Number the Levels",instruction:"Number each organism's trophic level in this chain, then state what level a decomposer occupies.",items:["Fox","Grass","Rabbit"],answer:"Grass = level 1 (producer), rabbit = level 2 (primary consumer), fox = level 3 (secondary consumer). Producers are level ONE — starting the count at the first animal is the usual slip. Decomposers feed on every level, so they are not given a single number; they are usually shown separately."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-FOODCHAIN":{type:"calculate",title:"Why Chains Run Out",instruction:"10,000 kJ enters the producers. Assuming 10% transfer between levels, work out the energy at each level, then explain why chains rarely exceed five links.",answer:"Producers 10,000 kJ → primary consumer 1,000 → secondary 100 → tertiary 10 → quaternary 1 kJ. By the fifth level there is not enough energy to support a viable population. Energy is lost as heat from respiration, in movement, in waste (faeces and urine), and in uneaten parts such as bone. Note the losses are of ENERGY, not of biomass alone."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-BIODIVERSITY":{type:"evaluate",title:"Which Ecosystem Is Healthier?",instruction:"Ecosystem A has 40 species; ecosystem B has 6 but far more individuals. Decide which is more biodiverse and why, then give two human actions that reduce biodiversity and two that protect it.",answer:"A — biodiversity is the VARIETY of species and habitats, not the number of individuals. Reduce: deforestation, pollution, habitat destruction, introducing non-native species. Protect: breeding programmes, protected reserves, replanting hedgerows, reducing deforestation. Higher biodiversity generally means greater stability, because a species lost is more easily compensated for."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-CARBON":{type:"chain",title:"Two Routes From Trees to CO₂",instruction:"Deforestation raises atmospheric carbon dioxide by TWO separate routes. Complete both chains.",prompt:"Route 1: fewer trees → [step] → CO₂ rises.   Route 2: felled timber is burned or decays → [step] → CO₂ rises.",answer:"Route 1: fewer trees means less PHOTOSYNTHESIS, so less CO₂ is removed from the atmosphere. Route 2: combustion or decomposition RELEASES carbon that was locked in the wood. Most students give only route 1 — the mark scheme wants both the reduced uptake and the active release."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-BREEDING":{type:"compare",title:"Who Does the Selecting?",instruction:"Complete the table, then classify each: a farmer breeding the highest-yield wheat; moths darkening in a sooty city.",headers:["Feature","Selective breeding","Natural selection"],rows:[["Who chooses the parents?","",""],["Speed","",""],["Effect on variation","",""]],answer:"Selective breeding: humans choose, fast, REDUCES variation and the gene pool. Natural selection: environmental pressure decides, slow, acts on existing variation. Wheat = selective breeding; moths = natural selection. The risk of selective breeding is inbreeding: a shrunken gene pool makes inherited disease and total crop failure more likely."},
  // ---- GCSE Separate Chemistry
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-ATOM":{type:"calculate",title:"Fill the Shells",instruction:"Write the electronic structure for each, then state which group and period it is in. Use the shell limits 2, 8, 8.",items:["Sodium (11 electrons)","Chlorine (17)","Calcium (20)","Argon (18)"],answer:"Na 2,8,1 — group 1, period 3. Cl 2,8,7 — group 7, period 3. Ca 2,8,8,2 — group 2, period 4. Ar 2,8,8 — group 0, period 3. The two rules that earn marks: the number of OUTER electrons gives the group, the number of SHELLS gives the period. A neutral atom has electrons equal to protons."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-ISOTOPE":{type:"calculate",title:"Protons, Neutrons, Mass Number",instruction:"Complete the table. Then say what makes two atoms isotopes of each other.",headers:["Atom","Protons","Neutrons","Mass number"],rows:[["17 protons, 18 neutrons","17","18",""],["Carbon-14","6","",""],["Oxygen-16","8","",""]],answer:"35; carbon-14 has 8 neutrons; oxygen-16 has 8 neutrons. Mass number = protons + neutrons. Isotopes have the SAME number of protons (so the same element and the same chemistry) but DIFFERENT numbers of neutrons. Because chemistry depends on electrons, isotopes react identically — only mass and stability differ."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-BOND":{type:"sort",title:"Which Structure Explains the Property?",instruction:"Sort each substance by structure, then use the structure to explain the property given.",items:["Diamond — very high melting point","Carbon dioxide — gas at room temperature","Sodium chloride — conducts only when molten or dissolved","Copper — conducts electricity as a solid"],answer:"Giant covalent (diamond): many strong covalent bonds throughout the lattice, so a huge amount of energy is needed. Simple molecular (CO₂): weak INTERMOLECULAR forces between molecules — the covalent bonds inside stay intact. Giant ionic (NaCl): ions fixed in a solid lattice, mobile only when molten or aqueous. Metallic (Cu): delocalised electrons free to move. The classic error is saying you 'break covalent bonds' when boiling CO₂ — you overcome the forces BETWEEN molecules."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-STATES":{type:"correct",title:"Physical, Not Chemical",instruction:"Correct each statement about melting ice.",items:["'The water molecules break apart into hydrogen and oxygen.'","'Melting makes a new substance.'","'The particles get bigger when the solid melts.'"],answer:"1 Molecules stay intact; only the FORCES between them are overcome. 2 Melting is a PHYSICAL change — the substance is the same and it is reversible. 3 Particles do not change size or mass; their arrangement, spacing and energy change. Getting 'the particles themselves change' out of students' answers is the whole point of this starter."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-FORMULA":{type:"calculate",title:"Balance the Charges",instruction:"Work out the formula of each ionic compound by balancing charges. Show the ions first.",items:["Magnesium oxide","Sodium chloride","Calcium chloride","Aluminium oxide"],answer:"Mg²⁺ + O²⁻ → MgO (1:1). Na⁺ + Cl⁻ → NaCl. Ca²⁺ + 2Cl⁻ → CaCl₂. 2Al³⁺ + 3O²⁻ → Al₂O₃. The compound must be electrically NEUTRAL — the total positive charge equals the total negative charge. Writing MgO₂ or AlO shows charges were not balanced."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-REACTION":{type:"classify",title:"Chemical Change or Not?",instruction:"Mark each C (chemical change) or P (physical). For the chemical ones, name the evidence.",items:["Bubbles form when magnesium is added to acid","Ice melts into water","A colour change when two solutions are mixed","Salt dissolves in water","A precipitate forms","The mixture gets noticeably hot"],answer:"C: bubbles (gas produced), colour change, precipitate, temperature change. P: melting, dissolving — both reversible with no new substance. The best single indicator is that a NEW SUBSTANCE has formed and the change is not easily reversed. Dissolving fools students because it looks dramatic, but the salt is recoverable by evaporation."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-ACID":{type:"sort",title:"Place Them on the Scale",instruction:"Place each on a pH scale from 0 to 14, then answer the question below.",items:["Pure water","Stomach acid","Sodium hydroxide solution","Vinegar","Baking soda solution"],prompt:"A solution moves from pH 5 to pH 3. By what factor has hydrogen ion concentration changed?",answer:"Water 7, stomach acid ~1-2, NaOH ~13-14, vinegar ~3, baking soda ~9. pH 5 to pH 3 is TWO steps, and the scale is logarithmic — hydrogen ion concentration is 100× greater, not 2× . Neutral is pH 7 because hydrogen and hydroxide ion concentrations are equal."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-ACIDMETAL":{type:"correct",title:"Three Acid Reactions, Three Products",instruction:"Complete each general equation, then name the test for each gas produced.",items:["acid + metal →","acid + metal carbonate →","acid + metal oxide / alkali →"],answer:"acid + metal → SALT + HYDROGEN (squeaky pop with a lit splint). acid + metal carbonate → SALT + WATER + CARBON DIOXIDE (limewater turns cloudy). acid + base → SALT + WATER only (neutralisation, no gas). The error this catches is giving hydrogen for the carbonate reaction — carbonates give carbon dioxide, and the difference is worth checking every time."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-TITRATION":{type:"sequence",title:"Order the Titration",instruction:"Put the steps in order, then say why a rough titration is done first and why readings must be concordant.",items:["Add acid from the burette, swirling, until the indicator just changes","Record the final burette reading","Pipette 25 cm³ of alkali into a conical flask","Repeat until two readings agree within 0.10 cm³","Add a few drops of indicator"],answer:"Pipette alkali → add indicator → add acid to the endpoint → record reading → repeat for concordant results. The rough run finds the approximate endpoint so later runs can be added dropwise near it. Concordant results (within 0.10 cm³) show the measurement is REPEATABLE, so the mean is reliable. A titration measures the VOLUME needed to neutralise — not the strength of the acid directly."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-ELECTRO":{type:"diagram",title:"Which Electrode, Which Reaction?",instruction:"Draw an electrolysis cell for molten lead bromide. Label the anode and cathode with their charges, show which ion goes where, and write what happens at each.",prompt:"Common error: sending positive ions to the positive electrode.",answer:"CATHODE is NEGATIVE and attracts CATIONS (positive ions, Pb²⁺), which GAIN electrons — REDUCTION. ANODE is POSITIVE and attracts ANIONS (Br⁻), which LOSE electrons — OXIDATION. Opposite charges attract, so positive ions go to the negative electrode. Memory hook: PANIC — Positive is Anode, Negative Is Cathode. Reduction happens at the cathode (both start with a consonant pairing students can hold)."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-YIELD":{type:"calculate",title:"Actual Over Theoretical",instruction:"Calculate the percentage yield, then give three reasons a real yield is below 100%.",prompt:"A reaction could theoretically make 8.0 g of product. A student obtains 6.4 g.",answer:"6.4 ÷ 8.0 × 100 = 80%. Reasons: the reaction is incomplete or reversible; product is lost on transfer or when filtering; side reactions make unwanted products. Note the formula is ACTUAL ÷ THEORETICAL — inverting it gives values above 100%, which should immediately signal an error. Percentage yield says nothing about how FAST or how ECONOMICAL the reaction is; that is rate and atom economy."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-ANALYSIS":{type:"match",title:"Flame Test Colours",instruction:"Match each metal ion to its flame colour, then state one limitation of flame tests.",items:["Lithium","Sodium","Potassium","Calcium","Copper"],answer:"Li crimson/red; Na yellow; K lilac; Ca orange-red; Cu green/blue-green. Limitation: a strong sodium yellow MASKS other colours in a mixture, and the test identifies the metal ion only — not the compound or the negative ion. For mixtures, instrumental methods are more reliable and more sensitive."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-EQUIL2":{type:"evaluate",title:"What Actually Shifts It?",instruction:"For each change, state whether the equilibrium POSITION shifts, and whether the equilibrium CONSTANT changes.",items:["Raising the temperature","Adding a catalyst","Increasing pressure in a gas reaction with unequal moles","Adding more reactant"],answer:"Temperature: position shifts AND the constant changes — the only one that changes the constant. Catalyst: NEITHER — it speeds both directions equally and only shortens the time to reach equilibrium. Pressure: position shifts towards the side with fewer gas moles; constant unchanged. More reactant: position shifts right; constant unchanged. 'A catalyst improves the yield' is the error to kill here."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-MR":{type:"calculate",title:"Count Every Atom",instruction:"Calculate the relative formula mass of each. Use H=1, C=12, N=14, O=16, Ca=40.",items:["H₂O","CO₂","CaCO₃","Ca(OH)₂"],answer:"H₂O = 18. CO₂ = 44. CaCO₃ = 40 + 12 + 48 = 100. Ca(OH)₂ = 40 + 2×(16+1) = 74. The bracket is the trap: everything inside is multiplied by the subscript outside, so there are TWO oxygens and TWO hydrogens, not one of each. Multiply the subscript by the atomic mass, never add it."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-NANO":{type:"calculate",title:"Why Small Means Reactive",instruction:"A cube has sides of 10 cm. Work out its surface area, volume and their ratio. Then repeat for a 1 cm cube, and explain what this tells you about nanoparticles.",answer:"10 cm cube: SA 600 cm², volume 1000 cm³, ratio 0.6. 1 cm cube: SA 6 cm², volume 1 cm³, ratio 6 — TEN times higher. As particles get smaller the surface-area-to-volume ratio rises sharply, so a far greater proportion of atoms sit on the surface where reactions occur. That is why nanoparticles are effective catalysts and why less material is needed. Risks: they may pass into cells or the bloodstream, and long-term effects are not fully known."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-SURFACE":{type:"diagnose",title:"Powder Beats Lumps — But Why?",instruction:"A student says powdered marble reacts faster with acid than marble chips 'because the powder has more energy'. Identify the error and give the correct explanation in terms of collisions.",answer:"The error is confusing surface area with energy. Breaking a solid into smaller pieces does not change particle energy at all. It exposes MORE SURFACE AREA, so more particles are available at the surface to be hit — collisions become more FREQUENT. Energy per collision is unchanged, which is why the temperature effect is different: temperature raises both frequency AND the proportion of collisions exceeding activation energy."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-RATE":{type:"sort",title:"Frequency, Energy, or Both?",instruction:"For each change, state whether it increases collision FREQUENCY, collision ENERGY, or both.",items:["Raising the temperature","Increasing concentration","Using a powder instead of lumps","Adding a catalyst","Increasing gas pressure"],answer:"Temperature: BOTH — particles move faster and more collisions exceed the activation energy. Concentration, surface area and pressure: frequency only. A catalyst: NEITHER — it provides an alternative pathway with LOWER activation energy, so a greater proportion of existing collisions succeed. Temperature is the only one on this list that changes collision energy, and that is why it usually has the largest effect."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-EQUIL":{type:"correct",title:"Equal Rates, Not Equal Amounts",instruction:"Correct each statement about dynamic equilibrium in a closed system.",items:["'At equilibrium both reactions stop.'","'At equilibrium there are equal amounts of reactants and products.'","'Equilibrium can be reached in an open beaker.'"],answer:"1 Both reactions CONTINUE — that is what 'dynamic' means; the forward and reverse RATES are equal. 2 Concentrations are CONSTANT, not equal — the position may lie far to either side. 3 It requires a CLOSED system, or products escape and equilibrium can never establish. All three of these appear in student answers every year."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-EXTRACT":{type:"sort",title:"Carbon or Electrolysis?",instruction:"For each metal, state the extraction method and justify it using the reactivity series.",items:["Aluminium","Iron","Zinc","Gold","Potassium"],answer:"Above carbon (K, Na, Ca, Mg, Al) → ELECTROLYSIS, because their oxides are too stable for carbon to reduce. Below carbon (Zn, Fe, Cu, Pb) → reduction with CARBON in a furnace, which is cheaper. Gold is so unreactive it is found NATIVE, as the uncombined metal. Aluminium is the one students get wrong — it is common and cheap to buy, so they assume it is easy to extract, but electrolysis makes it energy-intensive."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-REDOX":{type:"classify",title:"OIL RIG in Practice",instruction:"For each half-equation, state whether it shows oxidation or reduction and say which species is the reducing agent overall.",items:["Mg → Mg²⁺ + 2e⁻","Cl₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Cl⁻","Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ + e⁻","Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu"],answer:"1 Oxidation (loss). 2 Reduction (gain). 3 Oxidation. 4 Reduction. OIL RIG — Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain (of electrons). In Mg + Cl₂, magnesium is oxidised so it is the REDUCING agent; chlorine is reduced so it is the oxidising agent. That inversion is the part students reliably get backwards: the substance oxidised is the one doing the reducing."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-ORGANIC":{type:"distinguish",title:"Alkane or Alkene?",instruction:"Classify each, give its general formula, and name the test that distinguishes the two families.",items:["Ethene, C₂H₄","Methane, CH₄","Propene, C₃H₆","Ethane, C₂H₆"],answer:"Alkenes (C₂H₄, C₃H₆): general formula CₙH₂ₙ, contain a C=C DOUBLE bond, unsaturated. Alkanes (CH₄, C₂H₆): CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, single bonds only, saturated. Test: bromine water goes from ORANGE TO COLOURLESS with an alkene (it adds across the double bond) and stays orange with an alkane. Note 'colourless', not 'clear' — the mark scheme distinguishes them."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-POLYMER":{type:"compare",title:"Addition or Condensation?",instruction:"Complete the table, then classify: poly(ethene), and a polyester.",headers:["Feature","Addition polymerisation","Condensation polymerisation"],rows:[["Monomer feature","",""],["Small molecule lost?","",""],["Example","",""]],answer:"Addition: monomers have a C=C double bond, NO small molecule lost, all atoms end up in the polymer (100% atom economy) — poly(ethene). Condensation: monomers have TWO functional groups each, a small molecule (usually water) IS lost each time a link forms — polyester. The error is thinking condensation needs a double bond; it needs two reactive ends."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-WATER":{type:"distinguish",title:"Potable Is Not Pure",instruction:"Decide whether each statement describes POTABLE water, PURE water, or both.",items:["Contains dissolved minerals","Boils at exactly 100°C at 1 atmosphere","Safe to drink","Contains only H₂O molecules","Produced by distillation"],answer:"Potable: dissolved minerals, safe to drink. Pure: boils at exactly 100°C, only H₂O molecules, produced by distillation. Only 'safe to drink' can loosely apply to both. In chemistry PURE means a single substance — so tap water is potable but chemically impure, and mineral water advertised as 'pure' is not. Treatment: filtration removes solids, sterilisation (chlorine, ozone or UV) kills microbes."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-ATMOS":{type:"correct",title:"Complete or Incomplete?",instruction:"Write the products of each, then say which produces the toxic gas and why it is dangerous.",items:["Complete combustion of methane","Incomplete combustion of methane"],answer:"Complete (plenty of oxygen): carbon dioxide + water. Incomplete (limited oxygen): carbon monoxide and/or carbon (soot) + water. CARBON MONOXIDE is the danger — it is colourless and odourless, and binds to haemoglobin in place of oxygen, so the blood cannot carry oxygen. Carbon dioxide is the greenhouse gas from complete combustion; students frequently swap the two."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-GAS":{type:"match",title:"Four Gases, Four Tests",instruction:"Match each gas to its test AND the positive result. Getting only half of each scores nothing.",items:["Carbon dioxide","Oxygen","Hydrogen","Chlorine"],answer:"CO₂: bubble through limewater → turns cloudy/milky. O₂: glowing splint → RELIGHTS. H₂: lit splint → squeaky POP. Cl₂: damp litmus paper → bleached white. Two traps: the oxygen test uses a GLOWING splint and the hydrogen test a LIT one — swapping them loses the mark; and the litmus must be DAMP."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-FRACTION":{type:"diagram",title:"Label the Column",instruction:"Draw a fractionating column. Mark where it is hottest and coolest, and where these condense: refinery gases, petrol, kerosene, diesel, bitumen. Then explain what property separates them.",answer:"Hot at the BOTTOM, cool at the top. Bitumen at the very bottom (longest chains), then diesel, kerosene, petrol, refinery gases at the top (shortest). Separation is by BOILING POINT, which rises with chain length because longer molecules have stronger intermolecular forces. Longer chains are also more viscous, less volatile and less flammable — all four properties follow chain length."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-CRACK":{type:"chain",title:"Why Crack at All?",instruction:"Complete the reasoning, then write a balanced example equation.",prompt:"Crude oil gives more long-chain hydrocarbons than the market wants → [step] → [step] → cracking is economically worthwhile.",answer:"Demand for short chains (petrol, and alkenes for polymers) exceeds supply → cracking breaks long chains into shorter, more useful alkanes plus alkenes → supply is matched to demand and higher-value products are made. Example: C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄. Conditions: high temperature with a catalyst, or steam. Note cracking always produces at least one ALKENE — that is what feeds the polymer industry."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-FOOD":{type:"match",title:"Food Test to Result",instruction:"Match each nutrient to its test and the positive colour change. State which test needs heating.",items:["Starch","Reducing sugar","Protein","Lipid"],answer:"Starch: iodine solution, orange-brown → BLUE-BLACK. Reducing sugar: Benedict's solution, blue → green/orange/BRICK-RED, and this is the one that must be HEATED in a water bath. Protein: biuret, blue → PURPLE/lilac. Lipid: ethanol emulsion test → cloudy white emulsion. Always state the colour BEFORE and AFTER — 'it goes blue-black' without the starting colour is a half answer."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-ENERGY":{type:"sort",title:"Renewable Does Not Mean Harmless",instruction:"Sort each into renewable or non-renewable, then name one environmental drawback of a renewable one.",items:["Coal","Wind","Biofuel","Natural gas","Solar","Nuclear"],answer:"Renewable: wind, biofuel, solar. Non-renewable: coal, natural gas, and nuclear (uranium is a finite resource, which is the one students misfile). Renewable means REPLENISHED on a human timescale — not that it is impact-free. Drawbacks: wind farms affect landscape and habitats; biofuels compete with food crops for land and can drive deforestation; solar panel manufacture is energy- and material-intensive."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-BRINE":{type:"diagram",title:"Three Products From Salty Water",instruction:"Draw the electrolysis of concentrated brine. Label what forms at each electrode and what remains in solution, then give a use for each product.",answer:"ANODE: chlorine (bleach, water treatment, PVC). CATHODE: hydrogen (fuel, making ammonia, margarine). SOLUTION: sodium hydroxide (soap, paper, bleach). Sodium is NOT produced despite being in the compound — hydrogen is discharged instead because it is less reactive than sodium. Test the gases: chlorine bleaches damp litmus, hydrogen gives a squeaky pop."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-CHROM":{type:"calculate",title:"Work Out the Rf",instruction:"A spot travels 3.6 cm; the solvent front travels 8.0 cm. Calculate the Rf value, then answer the two questions below.",prompt:"(a) Why must the start line be drawn in pencil? (b) Why must the start line sit ABOVE the solvent level?",answer:"Rf = distance moved by spot ÷ distance moved by solvent = 3.6 ÷ 8.0 = 0.45. Rf is always between 0 and 1 — a value above 1 means the two distances were inverted. (a) Pencil is insoluble; ink would dissolve and run, contaminating the result. (b) If the line is below the solvent, the sample dissolves into the solvent reservoir instead of travelling up the paper. Chromatography separates SOLUBLE substances by how strongly each is attracted to the stationary versus mobile phase."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-CARBON":{type:"evaluate",title:"Is It Really Carbon-Neutral?",instruction:"A company claims its biofuel is carbon-neutral because the crop absorbed as much CO₂ as burning it releases. List the lifecycle stages the claim ignores, then give your verdict.",answer:"Ignored: fertiliser manufacture and application, farm machinery fuel, processing and refining, transport to market, and land-use change — clearing forest or peatland to plant the crop can release a carbon store that takes decades to repay. Verdict: carbon-neutral AT THE POINT OF USE, but rarely across the full lifecycle. The transferable point is that a boundary drawn narrowly enough makes almost anything look neutral, so always ask where the boundary was drawn."},
  // ---- GCSE Separate Physics
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-MOTION":{type:"calculate",title:"Average Speed, Not Halfway Speed",instruction:"Work each out, showing the formula first.",items:["A runner covers 240 m in 30 s. Average speed?","A car travels 30 km at 60 km/h, then 30 km at 30 km/h. Average speed for the whole trip?"],answer:"1 speed = distance ÷ time = 240 ÷ 30 = 8 m/s. 2 NOT 45 km/h. Time = 0.5 h + 1.0 h = 1.5 h for 60 km, so average = 60 ÷ 1.5 = 40 km/h. Average speed is ALWAYS total distance ÷ total time — averaging the two speeds is the classic trap, and it is wrong whenever the times differ."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-FORCE":{type:"calculate",title:"Mass Stays, Weight Changes",instruction:"An astronaut has a mass of 70 kg. Complete the table (g = 10 N/kg on Earth, 1.6 N/kg on the Moon), then state what changes and what does not.",headers:["Location","Mass (kg)","Weight (N)"],rows:[["Earth","70",""],["Moon","",""],["Deep space (g ≈ 0)","",""]],answer:"Earth 70 kg, 700 N. Moon 70 kg, 112 N. Deep space 70 kg, ~0 N. MASS is the amount of matter, measured in kilograms, and never changes. WEIGHT is a force caused by gravity, measured in newtons, W = mg. 'The astronaut is weightless so has no mass' is the error — mass is unchanged everywhere."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-NEWTON":{type:"classify",title:"Is the Resultant Force Zero?",instruction:"For each, state whether the resultant force is zero, and what that means for the motion.",items:["A book resting on a table","A car at a steady 30 mph on a straight road","A skydiver at terminal velocity","A car speeding up","A ball at the very top of its flight"],answer:"Zero: book, steady car, terminal-velocity skydiver — all have NO ACCELERATION. Not zero: the accelerating car; and the ball at the top, where velocity is momentarily zero but weight still acts, so it is accelerating downwards. Newton's First Law: zero resultant force means at rest OR constant velocity. 'No force means not moving' is the misconception — a constant speed in a straight line also needs zero resultant."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-ENERGY":{type:"chain",title:"Name the Stores, Not the Types",instruction:"For each event, write the energy transfer as: store → store, and name the pathway (mechanically, electrically, by heating, by radiation).",items:["A book is lifted onto a shelf","A ball falls from the shelf","A kettle boils water","A car brakes to a stop"],answer:"Lifting: chemical (in the person) → GRAVITATIONAL potential, mechanically. Falling: gravitational → KINETIC, mechanically. Kettle: electrical supply → THERMAL store of the water, electrically then by heating. Braking: kinetic → thermal store of brakes and surroundings, by heating. Energy is not 'used up' — it is transferred between STORES, and dissipated energy is spread out to the surroundings, not destroyed."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-POWER":{type:"calculate",title:"Same Job, Different Power",instruction:"Two cranes each lift a 500 N load 10 m. Crane A takes 5 s, crane B takes 20 s. Calculate the work done and the power of each, then state what differs.",answer:"Work done = force × distance = 500 × 10 = 5000 J for BOTH. Power = work ÷ time: A = 5000 ÷ 5 = 1000 W; B = 5000 ÷ 20 = 250 W. The ENERGY TRANSFERRED is identical; only the RATE differs. Power is energy transferred per second, measured in watts — a watt is a joule per second. Describing the more powerful crane as doing 'more work' is the error."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-CIRCUIT":{type:"diagram",title:"Current Is Not Used Up",instruction:"Draw a series circuit with a cell and two identical lamps. Mark the current before the first lamp, between the lamps, and after the second. Then mark the potential difference across each lamp.",prompt:"Common error: showing the current getting smaller after each lamp.",answer:"The CURRENT IS THE SAME at all three points — there is only one path and charge is conserved, so nothing is 'used up'. What IS shared is POTENTIAL DIFFERENCE: the supply p.d. divides between the components, so each identical lamp gets half. Students confuse the two because both lamps do get dimmer as you add more — that is caused by higher total resistance reducing the current everywhere equally."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-ELECTRIC":{type:"calculate",title:"Three Power Calculations",instruction:"Use P = IV (and V = IR where needed). Show which equation you chose before substituting.",items:["A 230 V kettle draws 10 A. Power?","A 12 V lamp is rated 24 W. Current?","A 2 A current flows through a 20 Ω resistor. Power?"],answer:"1 P = IV = 230 × 10 = 2300 W. 2 I = P ÷ V = 24 ÷ 12 = 2 A. 3 V = IR = 2 × 20 = 40 V, then P = IV = 2 × 40 = 80 W (or P = I²R = 4 × 20 = 80 W). Choosing the equation before substituting is what prevents the usual muddle between P = IV and V = IR."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-TRANSFORM":{type:"correct",title:"Why AC and Not DC?",instruction:"A student writes: 'A transformer works with direct current because the current flows steadily through the coils.' Identify the error and write the correct explanation in three linked steps.",answer:"A transformer needs a CHANGING magnetic field. Steps: alternating current in the primary coil produces a CHANGING magnetic field → the changing field passes through the iron core to the secondary coil → a changing field INDUCES a potential difference in the secondary. Direct current gives a CONSTANT magnetic field, so nothing changes, nothing is induced, and no output p.d. is produced. The core is iron because it is easily magnetised; it does not carry current between the coils."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-CHARGE":{type:"calculate",title:"Charge, Current, Time",instruction:"Use Q = It. Then state the unit of each quantity.",items:["A current of 3 A flows for 20 s. Charge transferred?","240 C passes a point in 60 s. Current?","How long for 5 A to transfer 100 C?"],answer:"1 Q = 3 × 20 = 60 C. 2 I = Q ÷ t = 240 ÷ 60 = 4 A. 3 t = Q ÷ I = 100 ÷ 5 = 20 s. Charge in COULOMBS, current in AMPERES, time in SECONDS. Current is the RATE of flow of charge — one ampere is one coulomb per second. Calling current 'the amount of electricity' hides exactly this distinction."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-PARALLEL":{type:"diagram",title:"Parallel Shares Current, Not Voltage",instruction:"Draw a 12 V supply with two identical lamps in PARALLEL. Mark the p.d. across each lamp and the current in each branch and in the main circuit.",prompt:"Then repeat for the same two lamps in SERIES and compare.",answer:"Parallel: each lamp gets the FULL 12 V, because each branch connects the same two points. The CURRENT splits between branches and the branch currents sum to the main current. Series: the 12 V DIVIDES (6 V each) and the current is the same throughout. The rule to hold: parallel shares current and shares p.d. equally; series shares p.d. and keeps current equal."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-WAVE":{type:"distinguish",title:"Frequency, Wavelength, Period",instruction:"Define each in one line with its unit, then calculate the missing values.",items:["A wave has frequency 50 Hz. What is its period?","A wave travels at 340 m/s with wavelength 2 m. Frequency?","Amplitude is doubled — what happens to frequency?"],answer:"Frequency: complete waves passing a point per second, hertz. Wavelength: distance between two identical points on adjacent waves, metres. Period: time for ONE complete wave, seconds — and T = 1 ÷ f. So 1 ÷ 50 = 0.02 s. Wave speed v = fλ, so f = 340 ÷ 2 = 170 Hz. Doubling amplitude changes NOTHING about frequency — amplitude carries energy/loudness, frequency carries pitch. That independence is the point students miss."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-EM":{type:"sort",title:"Which Waves Cross a Vacuum?",instruction:"Sort each into 'can travel through a vacuum' or 'needs a medium', then state the wave type for each group.",items:["Light","Sound","Radio waves","Water ripples","X-rays","Seismic P-waves"],answer:"Vacuum: light, radio, X-rays — all ELECTROMAGNETIC, all transverse, all travelling at 3×10⁸ m/s in a vacuum. Need a medium: sound, water ripples, seismic waves — all MECHANICAL, requiring particles to vibrate. This is why there is no sound in space but we still see the Sun. Order of the EM spectrum: radio, microwave, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, gamma."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-OHM":{type:"graph",title:"Straight Line or Curve?",instruction:"Sketch current against potential difference for (a) a fixed resistor at constant temperature and (b) a filament lamp. Explain the difference in shape.",answer:"(a) Straight line THROUGH THE ORIGIN — current is directly proportional to p.d., so resistance is constant. This is ohmic behaviour, and it holds only at CONSTANT TEMPERATURE. (b) An S-shaped curve that flattens: as current rises the filament heats, ions vibrate more, electrons collide more often, RESISTANCE INCREASES, so current rises less steeply. A lamp is non-ohmic for exactly that reason — the condition in Ohm's law is not an afterthought."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-HOUSE":{type:"evaluate",title:"Why Houses Are Not Wired in Series",instruction:"List three things that would go wrong if a house were wired in series, then state the two advantages of parallel wiring.",answer:"In series: switching off one appliance would break the circuit and stop EVERYTHING; each appliance would receive only a share of the 230 V, so none would work properly; adding an appliance would dim all the others by raising total resistance. Parallel: every appliance receives the FULL supply p.d., and each can be switched independently without affecting the rest. Each branch also has its own fuse or breaker."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-MAGNET":{type:"diagram",title:"Field Around a Wire",instruction:"Draw the magnetic field around a straight current-carrying wire and around a solenoid. Mark the field direction and state the rule you used. Then say what happens if the current reverses.",answer:"Straight wire: concentric CIRCLES around the wire, direction from the right-hand grip rule (thumb along conventional current, fingers curl the field). Solenoid: field like a bar magnet, uniform and strong inside, with the ends acting as N and S poles. Reversing the current REVERSES the field direction. Field strength rises with current, with more turns, and with an iron core. A wire with no current has no magnetic field at all."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-UNITS":{type:"match",title:"Quantity to Unit",instruction:"Give the correct unit for each, then say which two are most often confused and why.",items:["Energy","Power","Force","Charge","Potential difference","Momentum"],answer:"Joule; watt; newton; coulomb; volt; kg m/s. The pair confused most is ENERGY (joule) and POWER (watt) — a watt is a joule PER SECOND, so power is a rate. That is why an electricity bill in kilowatt-HOURS measures energy, not power: power multiplied by time. Writing 'the kettle uses 2000 W of energy' merges the two."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-MOMENTUM":{type:"calculate",title:"Momentum Has Direction",instruction:"Calculate each, treating right as positive.",items:["A 4 kg object moves right at 3 m/s","A 2 kg ball moves left at 5 m/s","Total momentum of both together"],answer:"1 p = mv = 4 × 3 = +12 kg m/s. 2 p = 2 × 5 = −10 kg m/s (LEFT, so negative). 3 Total = +12 − 10 = +2 kg m/s to the right. Momentum is a VECTOR, so direction and sign matter — adding magnitudes to get 22 is the standard error. In a closed system total momentum is conserved, which only works if signs are handled properly."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-GAS":{type:"explain",title:"Why Squeezing Raises Pressure",instruction:"A sealed syringe of gas is compressed to half its volume at constant temperature. Explain the pressure change in terms of particles, in three steps.",prompt:"Common error: 'the particles get squashed' or 'the particles get hotter'.",answer:"Smaller volume → the same number of particles travel shorter distances between walls, so they hit the walls MORE OFTEN per second → more frequent collisions means greater total force on each unit area → pressure rises. At constant temperature the particles' average SPEED and kinetic energy are unchanged; only collision FREQUENCY changes. Particles themselves are not compressed — the space between them is."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-LIQUID":{type:"evaluate",title:"What Sets the Pressure at Depth?",instruction:"State whether each change increases, decreases, or does not affect the pressure at the bottom of a container of liquid.",items:["Filling it deeper","Using mercury instead of water","Using a much wider container, same depth","Taking it to the Moon"],answer:"Deeper: INCREASES. Denser liquid: INCREASES. Wider container at the same depth: NO CHANGE — this is the one that surprises students, but pressure = height × density × g, and area does not appear. On the Moon: DECREASES, because g is smaller. Pressure at depth depends on depth, density and gravitational field strength only — never on the total volume or the shape of the vessel."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-DENSITY":{type:"calculate",title:"Mass Over Volume",instruction:"Calculate each, watching the units.",items:["200 g object, volume 50 cm³","A 2 kg block measuring 10 cm × 10 cm × 10 cm, in g/cm³","Which floats in water (1 g/cm³): a 5 kg log of volume 8000 cm³?"],answer:"1 ρ = m ÷ V = 200 ÷ 50 = 4 g/cm³. 2 2 kg = 2000 g, V = 1000 cm³, so 2 g/cm³. 3 5000 ÷ 8000 = 0.625 g/cm³, which is less than 1, so it FLOATS. Floating depends on DENSITY, not mass — a heavy log floats and a small coin sinks, which is the intuition to correct here."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-RESIST":{type:"chain",title:"Hotter Wire, Higher Resistance",instruction:"Complete the chain explaining why a metal wire's resistance rises when heated.",prompt:"Temperature rises → [step] → [step] → resistance increases.",answer:"Metal IONS in the lattice vibrate more strongly with greater amplitude → electrons flowing through collide with them MORE OFTEN → electron flow is impeded, so resistance rises. Note it is the fixed ions that vibrate, not the electrons 'slowing down by themselves'. This is why a filament lamp is non-ohmic, and why a thermistor — a semiconductor — does the OPPOSITE, its resistance falling as temperature rises."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-MOTOR":{type:"diagram",title:"Fleming's Left Hand",instruction:"Draw a wire carrying current through a magnetic field between two magnets. Use Fleming's left-hand rule to mark the force direction, then state what happens if you reverse (a) the current and (b) the field.",answer:"Left hand: FIRst finger = FIeld (N to S), seCond finger = Current (conventional, + to −), thuMb = Motion/force. Reversing EITHER the current or the field reverses the force. Reversing BOTH leaves the force unchanged — that is why a motor needs a split-ring commutator, to flip the current every half turn and keep it spinning one way. Force is greatest when the wire is at 90° to the field, and zero when parallel to it."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-RADIATION":{type:"compare",title:"Penetration Against Ionisation",instruction:"Complete the table, then answer: which is most dangerous inside the body, and which outside?",headers:["Type","Stopped by","Penetrating power","Ionising power"],rows:[["Alpha","","",""],["Beta","","",""],["Gamma","","",""]],answer:"Alpha: paper/skin, least penetrating, MOST ionising. Beta: a few mm of aluminium, moderate, moderate. Gamma: thick lead or concrete, most penetrating, LEAST ionising. Penetration and ionisation run in OPPOSITE directions — that is the whole table. Inside the body ALPHA is most dangerous, because all its ionising energy is deposited in nearby tissue. Outside the body gamma is the greater hazard, because alpha cannot get through skin."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-HALFLIFE":{type:"calculate",title:"Halving What Remains",instruction:"A sample has an activity of 800 Bq and a half-life of 6 days. Complete the table, then answer the question.",headers:["Days elapsed","Half-lives","Activity (Bq)"],rows:[["0","0","800"],["6","1",""],["12","2",""],["18","3",""]],prompt:"After how many days does activity fall to 50 Bq?",answer:"400, 200, 100 Bq. 50 Bq is four half-lives, so 24 days. Each half-life halves WHAT REMAINS, so the decay never reaches zero — two half-lives leave a QUARTER, not nothing. 'Two half-lives means it has all gone' is the misconception this catches. Half-life is also random and unpredictable per nucleus; it is a statistical average over very many nuclei."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-BACKGROUND":{type:"sort",title:"Where Background Radiation Comes From",instruction:"Sort each source into NATURAL or HUMAN-MADE, then say which single source contributes most in the UK.",items:["Radon gas from rocks","Cosmic rays","Medical X-rays","Food and drink","Nuclear power waste","Rocks and soil"],answer:"Natural: radon, cosmic rays, food and drink, rocks and soil. Human-made: medical X-rays, nuclear waste. RADON GAS is the largest single UK contributor, and it varies sharply by region depending on local geology (granite areas such as Cornwall are highest). Background radiation is ALWAYS present and must be measured and subtracted before recording a source's true count rate — that subtraction is a standard exam step."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-FISSION":{type:"chain",title:"Splitting and the Chain Reaction",instruction:"Complete the chain for nuclear fission, then say what the control rods and moderator each do.",prompt:"A slow neutron is absorbed by a large unstable nucleus → [step] → [step] → chain reaction.",answer:"The nucleus becomes unstable and SPLITS into two smaller daughter nuclei → energy plus two or three more NEUTRONS are released → those neutrons are absorbed by further nuclei, causing more fission. Control rods (boron) ABSORB surplus neutrons to keep the reaction steady; the moderator SLOWS neutrons so they are more readily absorbed. Fission SPLITS a large nucleus — the opposite of fusion, and the pair students most often swap."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-FUSION":{type:"compare",title:"Fusion Against Fission",instruction:"Complete the table, then say why fusion is not yet used for power generation on Earth.",headers:["Feature","Fission","Fusion"],rows:[["What happens to nuclei","",""],["Fuel","",""],["Conditions needed","",""],["Waste","",""]],answer:"Fission: a large nucleus SPLITS; uranium/plutonium; achievable in a reactor; long-lived radioactive waste. Fusion: light nuclei JOIN; hydrogen isotopes; extremely high temperature and pressure; little long-lived waste. Fusion is not yet viable because containing a plasma at millions of degrees currently costs more energy than the reaction releases. In both, the mass of the products is slightly less than the reactants, and that lost mass is released as energy."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-STARS":{type:"correct",title:"Stars Do Not Burn",instruction:"A student writes: 'The Sun burns hydrogen, and when the fuel runs out the fire will go out.' Identify the two errors and write the correct explanation.",answer:"Error 1: BURNING is combustion, a chemical reaction needing oxygen — there is no oxygen in the Sun and no chemical reaction is involved. The Sun releases energy by nuclear FUSION, joining hydrogen nuclei into helium. Error 2: it will not simply 'go out'. As hydrogen in the core is used up, the Sun will expand into a red giant, then shed its outer layers and leave a white dwarf. Fusion is nuclear, not chemical, and releases far more energy per kilogram."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-DOPPLER":{type:"scenario",title:"Higher or Lower?",instruction:"For each, state whether the observed frequency and wavelength rise or fall.",items:["An ambulance siren approaching you","The same siren after it passes","A galaxy moving away from Earth"],answer:"Approaching: frequency HIGHER, wavelength shorter. Receding: frequency LOWER, wavelength longer. Distant galaxy: light shifted to longer wavelengths — RED-SHIFT. Note the source emits a constant frequency throughout; only the OBSERVED frequency changes, because the waves are bunched up ahead of the source and stretched behind it. The siren does not actually change pitch."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-UNIVERSE":{type:"chain",title:"From Red-Shift to the Big Bang",instruction:"Complete the reasoning chain, then state one further piece of evidence.",prompt:"Light from distant galaxies is red-shifted → [step] → [step] → the universe began from a single point.",answer:"Red-shift means galaxies are moving AWAY from us → more distant galaxies show GREATER red-shift, so they recede faster → the universe is expanding in all directions, so running it backwards converges on a single hot dense point. Further evidence: cosmic microwave background radiation, the leftover heat of the early universe, detected uniformly in all directions. Current estimate of the age: about 13.8 billion years. Note this does NOT mean Earth is the centre — every observer sees the same recession."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-THERMAL":{type:"calculate",title:"Heating Up Against Changing State",instruction:"Use E = mcΔθ. Then explain why the temperature does not rise during melting.",items:["Energy to raise 2 kg of water (c = 4200 J/kg°C) by 30°C?","A 0.5 kg block absorbs 9000 J and rises 20°C. Find c."],answer:"1 E = 2 × 4200 × 30 = 252,000 J. 2 c = E ÷ (mΔθ) = 9000 ÷ (0.5 × 20) = 900 J/kg°C. During MELTING or BOILING the temperature stays constant because the energy supplied breaks the forces BETWEEN particles rather than increasing their kinetic energy — that is LATENT heat, calculated with E = mL, not mcΔθ. Choosing between the two equations is the decision this starter drills."},
  "BIO-GM-01":{type:"sequence",title:"What Actually Carries the Gene?",instruction:"Put the genetic-modification steps in order and commit before the reveal. Name the part that physically carries the gene in, and say what a vector is NOT.",items:["the useful gene is cut out with a restriction enzyme","the gene is inserted into a plasmid or virus","the vector carries the gene into the host cell","the host cell divides and expresses the gene"],answer:"The vector — usually a plasmid or a virus — is the carrier that takes the gene into the host cell. A vector is not the gene itself, and not the enzyme: enzymes cut and join, the vector transports."},
  "CHEM-MIX-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Which Method Gets the Solid Back?",instruction:"A solid is DISSOLVED in water. Commit to the method that recovers the solid before the reveal, then say what filtration would achieve here and why.",items:["filtration","crystallisation / evaporation","chromatography","distillation"],answer:"Crystallisation (evaporating the solvent) recovers a dissolved solid. Filtration only separates an INSOLUBLE solid from a liquid — a dissolved solid passes straight through the paper. Distillation recovers the solvent; chromatography separates dissolved substances from each other."},
  "PHYS-QUANT-01":{type:"sort",title:"Does It Need a Direction?",instruction:"Sort each quantity into scalar or vector and commit before the reveal. Then give one pair from the list that shows why the distinction matters.",items:["speed","velocity","distance","displacement","mass","weight","force"],answer:"Scalars (magnitude only): speed, distance, mass. Vectors (magnitude AND direction): velocity, displacement, weight, force. A runner completing a lap has distance 400 m but displacement 0 — same journey, different quantity, because displacement carries direction."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-TRIAL":{type:"distinguish",title:"What Is the Control For?",instruction:"Commit to what each group tells you before the reveal. A trial gives one group the drug and one group an identical-looking pill with no drug in it. Say what the second group rules out, and why 'the treated group got better' alone proves nothing.",items:["the group given the drug","the group given the identical dummy pill","the difference between the two groups"],answer:"The dummy (placebo) group shows how much improvement comes from time, expectation and being treated at all. Only the DIFFERENCE between the groups is evidence the drug works — improvement in the treated group alone could be entirely placebo effect or natural recovery."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-CIRCULATION":{type:"trace",title:"Count the Trips Through the Heart",instruction:"Trace one red blood cell from the body back to the body and count how many times it passes through the heart. Commit to the number before the reveal, then say what would be lost with only one circuit.",prompt:"body → ? → heart → ? → lungs → ? → heart → ? → body",answer:"Twice — it is a DOUBLE circulation. Blood returns to the heart after the lungs to be pumped again at high pressure. A single circuit would leave the blood slowed by the lungs, delivering oxygen to the body too slowly for a mammal's demand."},
  "MC-SEP-BIO-GASEXCHANGE":{type:"explain",title:"Not Breathing Harder",instruction:"Write down what actually makes an alveolus efficient before the reveal. A student says alveoli work well 'because you breathe hard'. Name the three structural features that do the work, and say which one breathing harder actually changes.",items:["huge total surface area","wall one cell thick","rich blood supply maintaining the gradient"],answer:"Efficiency comes from structure: large surface area, a short (one-cell) diffusion distance, and a blood supply that keeps the concentration gradient steep. Breathing harder only maintains the gradient — it cannot change surface area or diffusion distance."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-MASS":{type:"diagnose",title:"The Flask That Lost Mass",instruction:"Explain the reading before the reveal. A reaction in an OPEN flask on a balance appears to lose 0.8 g. State where the mass went and whether any atoms were destroyed.",prompt:"Open flask, marble chips + acid. Balance reads 0.8 g lower after the reaction.",answer:"Nothing was destroyed — carbon dioxide gas escaped from the open flask, carrying that 0.8 g away. Seal the flask and the reading does not change. Atoms are rearranged, never created or destroyed, which is exactly why equations must balance."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-GROUPS":{type:"classify",title:"The Group Number Is the Clue",instruction:"For each element, commit to its group and predict one property BEFORE the reveal. Then state the single fact about electrons that explains why a whole group behaves alike.",items:["sodium","chlorine","argon","potassium"],answer:"Sodium and potassium are Group 1 (one outer electron — react vigorously, more so down the group); chlorine is Group 7 (seven outer electrons — reactivity DECREASES down the group); argon is Group 0 (full outer shell — unreactive). Group number = outer-shell electrons, which is why the group shares chemistry."},
  "MC-SEP-CHEM-ENERGETICS":{type:"sort",title:"Which Way Does Energy Go?",instruction:"Sort each change as exothermic or endothermic and commit before the reveal. Then say what the SURROUNDINGS feel in each case — this is where the error usually is.",items:["combustion of methane","thermal decomposition of limestone","neutralising acid with alkali","a sports cold pack activating"],answer:"Exothermic (releases energy, surroundings get HOTTER): combustion, neutralisation. Endothermic (takes energy in, surroundings get COLDER): thermal decomposition, the cold pack. 'Exothermic' describes energy leaving the reaction, so the thermometer rises — students often invert this."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-OPTICS":{type:"diagram",title:"Where Does the Ray Bend?",instruction:"Draw a ray entering a glass block at an angle and mark with an X every point where it changes direction. Commit before the reveal, then state what happens to its direction while inside the glass.",prompt:"air → glass block → air. Mark each point where the ray bends.",answer:"It bends at TWO points — the two boundaries — and travels in a straight line in between. Light does not curve inside the glass; refraction happens only where the speed changes, at a surface. Entering, it bends toward the normal; leaving, away from it."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-SPRING":{type:"graph",title:"Where the Straight Line Stops",instruction:"Sketch force against extension for a spring that is stretched too far, and mark the limit of proportionality. Commit before the reveal, then say what is true of the graph after that point.",prompt:"Axes: force (N) on the x-axis, extension (cm) on the y-axis. Mark the limit of proportionality.",answer:"The line is straight through the origin up to the limit of proportionality, then curves — extension grows faster per newton. Beyond it F = ke no longer holds. Proportionality is not the same as the elastic limit, and the spring may also fail to return to its original length."},
  "MC-SEP-PHYS-INSULATION":{type:"match",title:"Which Transfer Does It Block?",instruction:"For each measure, name the energy transfer it mainly reduces: conduction, convection or radiation.",items:["Loft insulation","Double glazing with a vacuum gap","Shiny foil behind a radiator","Draught excluder on a door","Thick carpet on a bare floor"],answer:"Loft insulation: traps air, reducing CONDUCTION and CONVECTION. Vacuum gap: no particles, so it blocks CONDUCTION and CONVECTION entirely. Shiny foil: reflects infrared, reducing RADIATION. Draught excluder: stops convection currents carrying warm air out. Carpet: reduces conduction through the floor. Trapped AIR is the common mechanism in most of these — air is a poor conductor, and trapping it also stops convection currents forming."}
};

// Curated anchor tags: these are the first hand-authored priority set for the
// recently completed subjects. The question-aware recipes below keep the
// content tied to each tag while avoiding a second 150-entry copy of every
// question and scaffold.
// ---- High-use hand-authored anchors ------------------------------------
// These are deliberately concrete interventions for the most frequently
// surfaced tags in subjects that previously relied almost entirely on the
// question-aware fallback. The long tail still uses the builders below.
var MC_NEXT_HAND_AUTHORED = {
  // Criminology: make the boundary between neighbouring concepts visible.
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-1":{type:"distinguish",title:"Crime or Deviance?",instruction:"Commit to a definition before the reveal. Sort two examples into criminal behaviour, deviance, or both, then underline the feature that makes a behaviour prosecutable.",items:["Shoplifting from a supermarket","Wearing pyjamas to a formal ceremony"],answer:"Criminal behaviour breaches a criminal law and can be prosecuted by the state. Deviance violates a social norm without necessarily breaking the law. Shoplifting is both; the clothing example is deviance only unless a separate law is breached."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-2":{type:"classify",title:"Norm Break or Law Break?",instruction:"Vote on each case, then justify the boundary with one precise rule rather than a reaction to how unusual the behaviour feels.",items:["A student ignores an informal dress code","A driver exceeds the legal speed limit","A protest breaks a social expectation but remains lawful"],answer:"Deviance is a norm violation; crime is a legal violation. The first and third may be deviant without being criminal. The second is criminal because it breaches a law, regardless of whether people view it as socially unusual."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-3":{type:"sequence",title:"How Crime Gets Constructed",instruction:"Build a four-step chain from behaviour to criminalisation. Put the mechanism on every arrow and identify where power or public pressure can change the outcome.",prompt:"Behaviour or harm → social reaction → political/legal decision → enforcement and criminal label.",answer:"Crime is socially constructed: societies interpret behaviour, campaigners and institutions frame it as harmful, lawmakers or courts define the legal response, and agencies enforce the new boundary. The same conduct can be treated differently across time or place."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-4":{type:"correct",title:"Legal Change Is Not a New Crime",instruction:"Repair the claim on your board before seeing the answer. Name the exact part that changes when legislation or legal interpretation changes.",prompt:"'If a behaviour becomes criminal, it must always have been criminal but previously ignored.'",answer:"Legal change can alter what counts as criminal. A new Act, repeal, or legal interpretation may create, remove, or redefine an offence; earlier behaviour was not automatically criminal before the boundary changed."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-5":{type:"compare",title:"Culture Changes the Boundary",instruction:"Compare the same behaviour in two societies. Decide what is evidence of cultural difference and what would instead be historical change within one society.",headers:["Question","Cultural difference","Historical change"],rows:[["What varies?","Norms, values and laws between societies","Law, technology, attitudes or enforcement over time"],["Test case","Different societies classify the same conduct differently","One society changes its classification"],["Why it matters?","Crime is not identical everywhere","The boundary is not fixed forever"]],answer:"Cultural differences compare places at the same time; historical change compares the same society across time. Both challenge the idea that crime is a universal, unchanging category."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-6":{type:"sequence",title:"Why Historical Context Matters",instruction:"Put the four influences in a causal chain and commit to which link would change the legal label first.",prompt:"New technology or social attitude → public concern → legal change → altered enforcement and crime data.",answer:"Historical change in technology, attitudes, law and enforcement changes both behaviour and its official classification. A rise in recorded crime may therefore reflect a new offence or reporting practice, not simply more harmful behaviour."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-7":{type:"distinguish",title:"White-Collar or Corporate?",instruction:"Classify each case, then state the feature that separates the individual offender category from the company-benefit category.",items:["An accountant falsifies a client account for personal gain","A company knowingly sells a dangerous product to protect profits"],answer:"White-collar crime is non-violent crime in a professional or organisational context for financial gain, often by an individual. Corporate crime is illegal or harmful conduct carried out by a company or for its benefit. A case can be both."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-8":{type:"correct",title:"Who Benefits From Corporate Crime?",instruction:"Correct the claim and identify whether the benefit is personal, organisational, or both.",prompt:"'Corporate crime is just white-collar crime committed by an employee, so the company cannot be responsible.'",answer:"Corporate crime is harmful or illegal conduct carried out by a company or for its benefit. An employee may act for the organisation, and responsibility can extend beyond the individual when systems, incentives or decisions enable the harm."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-9":{type:"sequence",title:"From Group Structure to Organised Crime",instruction:"Complete the chain without skipping the mechanism. Then circle the feature that distinguishes organised crime from a one-off group offence.",prompt:"Structured group → repeated planning → continuing profit or power → organised criminal activity.",answer:"Organised crime involves planned activity by structured groups for continuing profit or power. The defining feature is sustained organisation and purpose, not simply two or more people committing one offence."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-10":{type:"distinguish",title:"Cybercrime: Tool or Target?",instruction:"Decide whether each case is cybercrime and explain whether the computer system is the target, the tool, or both.",items:["Ransomware encrypts a hospital network","A scammer uses email to deceive a victim","A phone is stolen from a desk"],answer:"Ransomware directly targets a digital system. Email fraud uses a network as the tool. The phone theft is not cybercrime unless the digital system or data is part of the offence. The boundary is the role of computers, networks or digital systems."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-11":{type:"classify",title:"What Makes a Crime Green?",instruction:"Classify each case and justify the decision using harm to the natural environment, not the identity of the offender.",items:["Illegal dumping contaminates a river","A phishing attack steals bank details","A company exceeds a legal emissions limit"],answer:"Illegal dumping and serious environmental pollution are green crime because they cause criminal or harmful damage to the natural environment. Phishing is cybercrime, even though it may have environmental consequences indirectly."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-12":{type:"compare",title:"State Crime Versus Ordinary Crime",instruction:"Complete the comparison before the reveal. Add one sentence explaining why state power can affect visibility and accountability.",headers:["Feature","State crime","Other crime"],rows:[["Who acts?","A state or its agents","Individuals, groups or companies"],["What is harmed?","People, rights or populations","Victims, property or systems"],["Why can it be hidden?","Authority controls information or enforcement","Official agencies are more likely to investigate"]],answer:"State crime is illegal or harmful action carried out by a state or its agents. State power can make evidence, reporting and prosecution harder, so official crime statistics may understate the harm."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-13":{type:"distinguish",title:"Hate Crime Needs a Motive",instruction:"Commit to the classification, then identify the evidence that shows hostility or prejudice rather than simply a dispute or assault.",items:["An assault accompanied by racist abuse","A robbery with no evidence of prejudice","A threatening message targeting a religious group"],answer:"Hate crime is motivated by hostility or prejudice towards a protected or targeted group. The first and third cases show the relevant motive; the robbery is still a crime but is not shown to be hate crime."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-14":{type:"correct",title:"Honour Is Not a Defence",instruction:"Repair the claim and name the controlling behaviour or violence that remains criminal even when a perpetrator invokes family reputation.",prompt:"'An honour crime is acceptable because the offender claims to be protecting the family.'",answer:"Honour crime describes abuse or violence claimed to defend family or community honour. The claim explains a justification used by the offender; it does not make the conduct lawful or remove the victim's rights."},
  "MC-CRIM-AWARE-15":{type:"scenario",title:"Spotting Domestic Abuse",instruction:"Commit to the classification for each behaviour. Then explain why a pattern of control matters even when there is no single visible injury.",items:["Monitoring a partner's messages and finances","Threatening a partner's family to control where they go","A one-off disagreement with no coercion or threat"],answer:"Domestic abuse includes controlling, coercive, threatening or violent behaviour within an intimate or family relationship. The first two examples can form a pattern of coercive control; an ordinary disagreement is not automatically domestic abuse."},

  // Law: force students to apply the legal boundary to facts.
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-1":{type:"distinguish",title:"The Criminal Law Boundary",instruction:"Commit to the definition, then sort the examples into criminal law and civil dispute. State who prosecutes a criminal offence.",items:["The state prosecutes burglary","A claimant seeks compensation for breach of contract"],answer:"Criminal law defines offences against the state and sets penalties; the state prosecutes the offence. Civil law usually resolves disputes between parties, often through compensation or another remedy."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-2":{type:"classify",title:"Actus Reus: The External Element",instruction:"For each case, identify the external act or omission before discussing the defendant's state of mind.",items:["D punches V","D fails to act despite a recognised legal duty","D merely thinks about harming V"],answer:"Actus reus is the external element of the offence: the punch or legally relevant omission. A thought alone is not actus reus. Mens rea must be considered separately."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-3":{type:"distinguish",title:"Mens Rea Is the Mental Element",instruction:"Commit to the mental element in each case, then explain why the harmful result alone is not enough for every offence.",items:["D intentionally breaks a window","D causes harm accidentally with no required fault","D foresees a risk and unreasonably takes it"],answer:"Mens rea is the mental element required for the offence. Intention and recklessness are forms of fault, but the required state of mind depends on the offence; a result by itself does not establish mens rea."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-4":{type:"compare",title:"Intention: Aim or Virtual Certainty",instruction:"Compare direct and oblique intention. Put the decision on the board before revealing the legal test.",headers:["Type","Question to ask","Example"],rows:[["Direct","Was the consequence D's aim?","D shoots V intending to kill"],["Oblique","Did D know the consequence was virtually certain?","D attacks a plane knowing passengers will die"]],answer:"Direct intention focuses on purpose. Oblique intention can be found where the consequence was not the aim but was foreseen as virtually certain and the defendant appreciated that. Mere probability is not enough."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-5":{type:"scenario",title:"Subjective Recklessness",instruction:"Vote on whether D is reckless, then name the fact that proves D actually foresaw the risk.",items:["D sees a warning that a firework may injure someone but lights it anyway","D never considers the possibility and the risk occurs"],answer:"Subjective recklessness requires D to foresee a risk and take it unjustifiably. The warning supports actual foresight in the first case; the second may be negligent but is not subjective recklessness without proof D saw the risk."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-6":{type:"classify",title:"When Does an Omission Become Criminal?",instruction:"Classify each case and identify the legal duty before deciding whether failure to act can create liability.",items:["A stranger walks past a swimmer in danger","A parent fails to feed their child","A driver who caused a crash fails to summon help"],answer:"Omission liability requires a legal duty. A parent has a duty to care for a child, and a person who creates a dangerous situation may have a duty to act. A stranger generally has no general duty to rescue merely because they could help."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-7":{type:"sequence",title:"Causation: From Act to Result",instruction:"Build the causal chain and mark the point at which a new event could break it.",prompt:"D's conduct → factual causation → legal causation → prohibited result.",answer:"Factual causation asks whether the result would have occurred but for D's conduct. Legal causation asks whether D's conduct remained an operating and substantial cause. A sufficiently independent intervening act may break the chain."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-8":{type:"correct",title:"But For What?",instruction:"Repair the student's test. Apply it to the case before the reveal.",prompt:"'Because D's act happened before V's injury, D is factually liable for the result.'",answer:"Factual causation uses the but-for test: would the result have occurred but for D's conduct? Temporal order alone is not enough. If the result would have happened anyway, factual causation is not established."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-9":{type:"scenario",title:"Operating and Substantial Cause",instruction:"Commit to whether legal causation remains intact. Identify the event that is merely background and the event that might break the chain.",items:["D stabs V; hospital treatment is poor but not extraordinary","D stabs V; an independent freak event causes the final injury"],answer:"D's conduct must remain an operating and substantial cause. Ordinary medical treatment or a foreseeable response usually does not break the chain; a truly independent and overwhelming event may do so."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-10":{type:"correct",title:"Take the Victim as Found",instruction:"Correct the claim and explain why the victim's unusual vulnerability does not normally excuse the defendant.",prompt:"'The thin skull rule protects D when V has a hidden vulnerability that makes the injury worse.'",answer:"The thin skull rule means D takes V as found, including unusual physical or psychological vulnerabilities. D may be liable for the full consequence even if a stronger or more typical victim would suffer less harm."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-11":{type:"scenario",title:"Did an Intervening Act Break the Chain?",instruction:"Vote before discussion. For each case, identify whether the later event is foreseeable treatment or an independent cause.",items:["V receives ordinary medical treatment after D's attack","A completely independent third party deliberately causes the final injury"],answer:"An intervening act may break causation only if it is sufficiently independent and significant. Ordinary treatment usually leaves D's conduct operative; a deliberate, overwhelming new cause may break the chain."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-12":{type:"distinguish",title:"Assault Is Apprehension",instruction:"Commit to the offence in each case, then name the victim's expected immediate unlawful violence.",items:["D raises a fist and V fears an immediate punch","D secretly plans violence next month with no present threat","D touches V without consent"],answer:"Assault is an intentional or reckless act causing apprehension of immediate unlawful violence. The raised fist can be assault; a future plan without immediacy is not. Unlawful touching may be battery instead."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-13":{type:"distinguish",title:"Battery Is Unlawful Force",instruction:"Classify the contact and state whether intention or recklessness is present.",items:["D deliberately pushes V","D accidentally brushes past V in a crowded train","D throws a drink knowing it will hit V"],answer:"Battery is the intentional or reckless application of unlawful force to another. The push and knowing throw can qualify; an accidental brush without the required fault normally does not."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-14":{type:"compare",title:"ABH: More Than Trifling",instruction:"Place each injury on the scale, then state the boundary that separates ABH from minor battery.",items:["A momentary red mark","A loss of a tooth","Minor swelling lasting several days"],answer:"ABH is actual bodily harm that is more than transient or trifling. A momentary mark may remain battery only; loss of a tooth and meaningful lasting swelling can cross the ABH boundary."},
  "MC-LAW-CRIM-15":{type:"distinguish",title:"GBH Means Really Serious Harm",instruction:"Commit to the level of harm before the reveal. Explain why the label depends on seriousness, not on whether the injury is fatal.",items:["A fractured jaw requiring surgery","A shallow scratch","A serious internal injury that is not fatal"],answer:"GBH means grievous bodily harm: really serious harm. A fractured jaw or serious internal injury may meet the threshold; a shallow scratch does not. GBH need not be fatal."},

  // Politics: separate institutions, participation and electoral systems.
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-1":{type:"distinguish",title:"Democracy: Who Decides?",instruction:"Commit to the definition, then identify whether each example shows participation, representation, or neither.",items:["Citizens vote for MPs","Citizens vote directly on a referendum","A minister decides without public accountability"],answer:"Democracy means government by the people through participation and representation. Elections show representation; referendums show direct participation. An unaccountable decision alone is not evidence of democracy."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-2":{type:"compare",title:"Direct or Representative?",instruction:"Complete the comparison before the reveal. Add one trade-off for each model.",headers:["Feature","Direct democracy","Representative democracy"],rows:[["Decision","Citizens decide the issue","Elected representatives decide"],["Strength","Direct public input","Practical for complex, frequent decisions"],["Risk","Simplifies complex choices","Representatives may diverge from voters"]],answer:"Representative democracy means citizens elect people to decide on their behalf. It is not the same as direct democracy, where citizens decide the issue themselves."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-3":{type:"classify",title:"A Vote or a Representative?",instruction:"Sort the cases and state which democratic mechanism is operating.",items:["A public votes on a local planning proposal","A voter chooses a candidate for Parliament","An MP votes on a bill after election"],answer:"The public vote on the proposal is direct democracy. Choosing an MP and the MP voting on a bill are representative democracy. The distinction is who makes the final decision."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-4":{type:"distinguish",title:"The Franchise Is the Right to Vote",instruction:"Commit to the term, then name the difference between the franchise and turnout.",items:["The legal right to vote","The percentage of eligible people who actually vote","A campaign persuading people to vote"],answer:"The franchise is the legal right to vote. Turnout is the proportion of eligible voters who cast a ballot. A campaign is participation, not either measurement."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-5":{type:"classify",title:"Participation Beyond Elections",instruction:"Classify each activity as political participation and explain what influence it attempts to exercise.",items:["Signing a petition","Joining a peaceful protest","Reading a political article privately","Contacting an MP about a policy"],answer:"Petitions, protests and contacting an MP are forms of participation because they seek to influence political decisions. Private reading may inform a citizen but is not itself an influence attempt."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-6":{type:"calculate",title:"Turnout Is a Proportion",instruction:"Calculate turnout before the reveal, then explain why a high vote total does not necessarily mean high turnout.",prompt:"800,000 people are eligible to vote and 480,000 cast a ballot. Calculate turnout and state what the remaining 320,000 represent.",answer:"Turnout = 480,000 ÷ 800,000 × 100 = 60%. The remaining 40% were eligible but did not cast a ballot. Turnout is a proportion, not the winning party's vote share."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-7":{type:"scenario",title:"Apathy or an Informed Choice?",instruction:"Commit to the classification, then identify the evidence needed before calling non-voting apathy.",items:["A voter cannot get to the polling station","A voter feels no party represents them and refuses to vote","A voter researches every candidate but abstains"],answer:"Political apathy is a lack of interest or perceived relevance. The second may show apathy or deliberate disengagement, but the first is an access barrier and the third shows interest without participation. Behaviour alone does not prove apathy."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-8":{type:"distinguish",title:"Pressure Group, Not Government",instruction:"Commit to the organisation type, then identify whether it seeks office or policy influence.",items:["A campaign group lobbies MPs on climate policy","A party selects candidates and seeks to form a government","A charity publishes evidence to influence a bill"],answer:"A pressure group seeks to influence policy without forming a government. A political party seeks office; a charity can act as a pressure group when it campaigns to change policy."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-9":{type:"sequence",title:"How a Social Movement Builds Change",instruction:"Build the chain from shared grievance to political change. Name the point at which collective action becomes a social movement.",prompt:"Shared grievance → network and identity → collective action → pressure for social or political change.",answer:"A social movement is a broad network seeking significant change. It is more than one protest: people develop a shared identity, coordinate action and sustain pressure on institutions or public norms."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-10":{type:"distinguish",title:"A Referendum Answers One Question",instruction:"Decide which vote is a referendum, then explain why a general election is not the same instrument.",items:["A public vote on membership of an international organisation","A vote choosing an MP for a constituency","A council election for several local seats"],answer:"A referendum is a public vote on a specific constitutional or policy question. Elections choose representatives; a referendum asks voters to decide the stated issue directly."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-11":{type:"diagram",title:"First Past the Post on the Ballot",instruction:"Draw a four-candidate constituency result. Mark the winner and explain why winning the most votes is not the same as winning over half.",prompt:"A: 38%, B: 31%, C: 21%, D: 10%.",answer:"Candidate A wins because first-past-the-post gives the seat to the candidate with the most votes in the constituency. A has a plurality, not a majority: 62% voted for someone else."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-12":{type:"compare",title:"What Proportional Representation Tries to Match",instruction:"Compare seats with votes before the reveal. State one benefit and one trade-off.",headers:["Question","First-past-the-post","Proportional representation"],rows:[["Main aim","Constituency winner","Seats more closely match vote share"],["Likely effect","Disproportional result possible","Fewer wasted votes, often coalition government"]],answer:"Proportional representation aims to match seats more closely to votes. It can improve representation but may reduce the clarity of a single-party government."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-13":{type:"sequence",title:"How STV Transfers a Vote",instruction:"Complete the count and explain when a voter's next preference can matter.",prompt:"Rank candidates → count first preferences → elect candidates who reach the quota → transfer surplus or eliminate the lowest candidate → repeat.",answer:"Single transferable vote is a preferential proportional system where voters rank candidates. Surplus votes and votes from eliminated candidates transfer according to next preferences, so a ballot can remain useful after a first choice is elected or eliminated."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-14":{type:"distinguish",title:"Supplementary Vote: Two Preferences",instruction:"Commit to the counting rule, then explain what happens if no first-choice candidate has enough support.",items:["Voter marks first and second preference","Only first preferences are counted forever","The top two enter a second round using eligible second preferences"],answer:"Under supplementary vote, voters select first and second preferences. If no candidate wins the required first-choice threshold, the leading candidates remain and eligible second preferences help decide the runoff."},
  "MC-POL-UKPOL-15":{type:"scenario",title:"Why a Safe Seat Matters",instruction:"Commit to the likely political effect, then name one reason a safe seat can reduce electoral competition.",items:["A constituency where one party repeatedly wins by a large margin","A constituency won by one vote after a close campaign"],answer:"A safe seat is likely to remain under one party's control. Parties may focus less campaign attention there, while marginal seats attract more resources because small vote changes could change the result."},

  // GCSE History: force a cause, consequence or policy mechanism into the answer.
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Hire Purchase Opens the Market",instruction:"Commit to the factor before the reveal. Explain the mechanism in one arrow rather than just naming it.",prompt:"Hire purchase → instalments → more households can buy cars and household goods.",answer:"Hire purchase allowed customers to pay in instalments, widening access to expensive goods. It increased consumption but could also leave households in debt."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-02":{type:"sequence",title:"Assembly Line to Cheaper Cars",instruction:"Build the production chain and identify which step lowered the price.",prompt:"Standardised tasks → faster output → lower production cost → cheaper cars → wider ownership.",answer:"Ford divided production into standardised moving-line tasks. Faster, more efficient production lowered costs and prices, making cars available to more consumers."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-03":{type:"compare",title:"Laissez-Faire in Practice",instruction:"Compare the policy claim with the evidence. Circle the option that best represents limited government intervention.",items:["Lower taxation and tariffs","Universal state ownership of factories","High welfare spending and direct control"],answer:"Republican governments generally favoured business through laissez-faire policies, lower taxation and tariffs. This means limited intervention, not that government did nothing at all."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-04":{type:"compare",title:"Prosperity Was Uneven",instruction:"Commit to the group least likely to share the boom, then give the economic mechanism that explains the difference.",headers:["Group","Experience","Mechanism"],rows:[["Car manufacturers","High demand and mass production","Consumer spending"],["Many farmers","Overproduction, falling prices and debt","Too much supply"],["Cinema owners","Growth in leisure consumption","Urban consumer culture"]],answer:"Many farmers did not share fully in prosperity because overproduction pushed prices down and debt increased. The same decade could bring mass consumption to cities and hardship to agriculture."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-05":{type:"correct",title:"Prohibition: Ban, Not Cheap Alcohol",instruction:"Repair the claim and identify the enforcement problem that followed.",prompt:"'Prohibition made alcohol safer and easier to buy because the government regulated every sale.'",answer:"The Eighteenth Amendment prohibited alcohol. Enforcement was difficult, so illegal supply, bootlegging and organised crime grew; alcohol was not simply made safer or easier to obtain."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-06":{type:"sequence",title:"Why the Klan Gained Support",instruction:"Build the causal chain from social anxiety to support. Name at least two prejudices exploited by the Klan.",prompt:"Fear of immigration, modern life and minority groups → prejudice and propaganda → Klan support.",answer:"The Klan exploited racism, anti-Catholicism, anti-Semitism and anxiety about immigration and modern life. Its growth reflected prejudice and fear of change, not support for racial equality."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-07":{type:"correct",title:"Sacco and Vanzetti: Evidence or Prejudice?",instruction:"Commit to the significance, then repair the explanation that treats the case as ordinary criminal justice.",prompt:"'The case was significant because the verdict was accepted as completely fair and free from political pressure.'",answer:"Many people believed Sacco and Vanzetti were treated unfairly because prejudice and Red Scare fears influenced the case, not evidence alone. It became evidence of intolerance and concerns about justice."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-08":{type:"sequence",title:"From Margin Buying to Crash",instruction:"Complete the chain and mark the point at which borrowed money made losses worse.",prompt:"Buying shares on margin → prices fall → investors cannot repay loans → forced selling → further falls.",answer:"Many investors bought shares using borrowed money. When prices fell, they could not repay loans, so forced selling intensified the crash. The misconception is that the crash was caused by a shortage of shares."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-09":{type:"sequence",title:"Crash to Unemployment",instruction:"Build the mechanism from falling demand to job losses. Do not skip the firm's decision.",prompt:"Consumers spend less → firms sell less → production is cut → workers are dismissed → demand falls further.",answer:"As consumers spent less, firms reduced production and dismissed workers. Job losses reduced spending again, deepening the Depression in a downward cycle."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-10":{type:"evaluate",title:"Was Hoover's Response Enough?",instruction:"Commit to a judgement before the reveal. Give one policy feature and one consequence for ordinary Americans.",prompt:"Hoover encouraged cooperation and limited federal intervention while hardship intensified.",answer:"The strongest criticism was over-reliance on voluntary action and limited federal intervention. As unemployment and hardship grew, many Americans saw this as inadequate, even though Hoover did not literally do nothing."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-11":{type:"sequence",title:"Why Declare a Bank Holiday?",instruction:"Put the bank-holiday mechanism in order and identify the confidence problem it addressed.",prompt:"Temporary closure → government inspection → sound banks reopen → panic withdrawals fall → confidence returns.",answer:"The 1933 bank holiday temporarily closed banks while the government inspected them. Reopening sound banks helped stop panic withdrawals and restore confidence in the financial system."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-12":{type:"classify",title:"Relief, Recovery or Reform?",instruction:"Classify the CCC and WPA and explain the immediate problem each programme addressed.",items:["CCC conservation work","WPA public works","A policy changing the banking system"],answer:"The CCC and WPA mainly provided relief through employment on environmental and public projects. They also supported recovery by creating income and infrastructure; they were not primarily a banking reform."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-13":{type:"compare",title:"Why Critics Opposed the New Deal",instruction:"Place each criticism on the correct side of the debate, then state why the criticisms conflict.",items:["Federal spending and regulation were too great","The New Deal did not go far enough","Roosevelt was too passive"],answer:"Conservatives criticised excessive government intervention, spending and regulation. Some left-wing critics argued the New Deal did not go far enough. The criticisms conflict because one demands less intervention and the other more."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-14":{type:"sequence",title:"The War Ends the Depression",instruction:"Build the recovery chain and identify why wartime production mattered more than a single New Deal scheme.",prompt:"War demand → factories expand → jobs increase → incomes and spending rise → unemployment falls.",answer:"The Second World War created enormous demand for US goods and labour. Wartime production sharply reduced unemployment and drove the full recovery, beyond the partial improvements of earlier New Deal measures."},
  "MC-GCSE-HIST-AM-15":{type:"compare",title:"American Dream: Promise and Limit",instruction:"Commit to the meaning, then add the qualification that prevents an overgeneralised answer.",headers:["Claim","Evidence","Qualification"],rows:[["Promise","Hard work could bring a home, car and higher living standards","Post-war consumer prosperity"],["Limit","Access was unequal","Race, income and discrimination shaped opportunity"]],answer:"The American Dream suggested prosperity and social mobility through hard work. It was not equally available to all Americans; access was shaped by income, race and discrimination."},

  // GCSE Psychology: make students discriminate stores, processes and evidence.
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-01":{type:"compare",title:"Sensory Register or STM?",instruction:"Commit to the store, then compare duration and function with short-term memory.",headers:["Store","Duration","What it holds"],rows:[["Sensory register","Less than one second","Raw sensory input before attention"],["Short-term memory","About 18–30 seconds without rehearsal","Attended information"]],answer:"The sensory register has very large capacity but lasts less than one second. Attention selects information from it; it is not the same as short-term memory."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-02":{type:"calculate",title:"Miller's 7 ± 2",instruction:"Commit to the capacity range, then use it to explain why chunking helps rather than claiming STM is unlimited.",prompt:"Give the usual short-term-memory estimate and list the smallest and largest values in the range.",answer:"Miller's estimate is 7 ± 2 chunks: usually 5 to 9 items. Chunking groups information into meaningful units, increasing what can be managed without making capacity unlimited."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-03":{type:"graph",title:"Read the Serial Position Curve",instruction:"Sketch the curve before the reveal. Label primacy, recency and the middle section.",prompt:"Recall is strongest for early and late list items and weaker for middle items.",answer:"The serial position curve shows primacy and recency effects: early items benefit from rehearsal and late items remain accessible in short-term memory. Middle items are recalled less well."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-04":{type:"classify",title:"Which Memory Stores Events?",instruction:"Classify each example and explain why time and place matter for episodic memory.",items:["Remembering your first day at school","Knowing that Paris is the capital of France","Knowing how to ride a bicycle"],answer:"Episodic memory stores personal events linked to time and place. Semantic memory stores facts; procedural memory stores skills."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-05":{type:"correct",title:"Maintenance Is Repetition",instruction:"Repair the claim and state what repetition can and cannot guarantee.",prompt:"'Maintenance rehearsal means linking new information to personal meaning so it is permanently stored.'",answer:"Maintenance rehearsal is repetition that keeps information active and may help transfer it to long-term storage. Adding meaning is elaborative rehearsal, and repetition does not guarantee permanent storage."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-06":{type:"sequence",title:"How Schemas Reconstruct Recall",instruction:"Build the recall chain and identify where existing knowledge can alter the memory.",prompt:"Original event → memory trace → schema influences recall → reconstructed account.",answer:"Bartlett's research showed that recall is reconstructive: people use schemas and expectations when remembering. The recalled account can therefore change from the original event."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-07":{type:"compare",title:"Interference Is Competition",instruction:"Compare proactive and retroactive interference and write one example of each.",headers:["Type","Direction","Example"],rows:[["Proactive","Old learning disrupts new","Old password intrudes on the new one"],["Retroactive","New learning disrupts old","New number makes the old one harder to recall"]],answer:"Interference occurs when similar memories compete and make retrieval harder. The direction matters: old affects new in proactive interference; new affects old in retroactive interference."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-08":{type:"correct",title:"Confidence Is Not Accuracy",instruction:"Commit to whether the recollection is true, then explain why confidence alone cannot prove accuracy.",prompt:"'A false memory is simply an event that has been forgotten.'",answer:"A false memory is a confident but inaccurate recollection. It may contain details that were not experienced or have changed; forgetting is absence of recall, not confident invention."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-09":{type:"distinguish",title:"Acoustic Coding",instruction:"Commit to the coding and give one pair of words that could be confused because they sound alike.",prompt:"Short-term memory is mainly associated with acoustic coding.",answer:"Acoustic coding represents sound or verbal information. Similar-sounding words can be confused; this is distinct from semantic coding, which concerns meaning."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-10":{type:"distinguish",title:"Semantic Long-Term Coding",instruction:"Commit to the coding, then explain how meaning differs from sound or visual appearance.",prompt:"Long-term memory is mainly associated with semantic coding.",answer:"Semantic coding stores meaning. A learner may remember the idea of a sentence even if the exact sound or visual layout is forgotten."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-11":{type:"calculate",title:"Less Than One Second",instruction:"Commit to the duration before the reveal, then explain why attention must act quickly.",prompt:"A sensory trace is presented for 0.8 seconds. Does it fit the usual duration of sensory memory? What happens if attention does not select it?",answer:"Yes: less than one second fits the usual duration. Without attention, the raw sensory input rapidly fades rather than entering short-term memory."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-12":{type:"compare",title:"Working Memory Is Active",instruction:"Compare a passive store with the working-memory model. State what active processing means.",items:["Holding a phone number while repeating it","Manipulating numbers to calculate a total","A permanent fact stored for years"],answer:"The working-memory model emphasises active processing and separate components. Repeating and manipulating information are working-memory activities; a permanent fact describes long-term storage."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-13":{type:"scenario",title:"Central Executive in Action",instruction:"Commit to the component, then identify the attentional decision in the scenario.",prompt:"A student switches between reading a source, ignoring a notification and deciding which detail to record.",answer:"The central executive provides attentional control: it directs focus, allocates resources and coordinates tasks. It is not simply a passive store of words or images."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-14":{type:"distinguish",title:"Phonological Loop",instruction:"Classify each task as mainly verbal/auditory or visual/spatial, then explain which working-memory component is involved.",items:["Repeating a spoken instruction","Rehearsing a list of words","Rotating a shape mentally"],answer:"The phonological loop handles verbal and auditory information, so the first two tasks use it. Mental rotation is mainly visuospatial sketchpad activity."},
  "MC-GCSE-PSY-MEM-15":{type:"diagram",title:"Visuospatial Sketchpad",instruction:"Draw a simple route and annotate the visual or spatial information being held. Then name the component.",prompt:"A learner mentally rotates a map to find the route from school to the library.",answer:"The visuospatial sketchpad holds and manipulates visual and spatial information. The route and mental rotation are not primarily verbal rehearsal."},

  // A-Level History: turn factual recall into causation, significance and
  // interpretation work without overwriting the separate GCSE starters.
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-1":{type:"sequence",title:"From Tariff Division to 1906",instruction:"Build the causal chain and identify the event that weakened Conservative support before the election.",prompt:"Tariff controversy → Conservative division → voter reaction → Liberal landslide of 1906.",answer:"The Liberals won a large Commons majority after the Conservatives were weakened by tariff controversy and division. The point is causation: the election result was not just a fact but an outcome shaped by political disagreement."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-2":{type:"compare",title:"Free Trade or Tariff Reform?",instruction:"Complete the comparison and state which side Chamberlain represented.",headers:["Policy","Core idea","Political association"],rows:[["Free trade","Unrestricted trade and no protective tariffs","Liberal tradition"],["Tariff reform","Imperial preference and protection","Joseph Chamberlain's campaign"]],answer:"Joseph Chamberlain's tariff reform supported imperial preference and protection rather than unrestricted free trade. The key distinction is policy, not simply whether a politician wanted economic change."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-3":{type:"distinguish",title:"New Liberalism Means State Action",instruction:"Commit to the definition, then identify the problem New Liberal reform aimed to address.",items:["State action to address poverty and social risks","Complete laissez-faire and no welfare intervention","Imperial preference and protective tariffs"],answer:"New Liberalism accepted greater state action to address poverty and social risks. It did not mean total state control, but it moved beyond the older assumption that government should intervene as little as possible."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-4":{type:"correct",title:"What the 1902 Education Act Changed",instruction:"Repair the claim and name both institutional changes in the answer.",prompt:"'The 1902 Education Act abolished local education authorities and made all schools non-denominational.'",answer:"The Act replaced school boards with local education authorities and supported denominational schools. It reorganised administration; it did not abolish local education or remove religious schooling."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-5":{type:"distinguish",title:"School Meals: A Welfare Response",instruction:"Commit to the purpose before the reveal, then connect provision to the problem of poverty and health.",prompt:"A local authority provides meals for children whose families cannot afford adequate food.",answer:"School meals provided food for children whose families could not afford it. They were a welfare response to poverty and poor health, not a measure about school administration or military training."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-6":{type:"compare",title:"Meals or Medical Inspections?",instruction:"Separate the two reforms and explain the different problem each addressed.",items:["Providing food to children","Checking children's health through school","Replacing school boards"],answer:"School meals addressed nutrition and poverty. Medical inspections checked children's health through the school system. Replacing school boards was an administrative change under the 1902 Education Act."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-7":{type:"distinguish",title:"Why the 1908 Pension Was Significant",instruction:"Commit to the policy mechanism, then state who contributed and who received the benefit.",prompt:"Explain why the Old Age Pensions Act was a significant change in welfare provision.",answer:"It created a non-contributory pension for many people aged over seventy. Recipients did not first pay contributions into an insurance fund, so the measure represented a wider role for the state in supporting old age."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-8":{type:"sequence",title:"From Job Seeker to Labour Exchange",instruction:"Complete the mechanism and identify the intervention made by the state.",prompt:"Unemployed worker → state-supported office → vacancy information → better job matching.",answer:"The Labour Exchanges Act 1909 created state-supported offices to match job seekers with vacancies. It aimed to reduce frictional unemployment by improving information and matching, not by directly creating every job."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-9":{type:"evaluate",title:"Was the People's Budget Radical?",instruction:"Commit to a judgement before the reveal. Give one revenue measure and one political consequence.",prompt:"Lloyd George's 1909 budget raised revenue through progressive taxation and land-related measures.",answer:"The Budget can be judged radical because it sought progressive taxation and land-related revenue to fund social reform. Its rejection by the House of Lords also created a constitutional conflict, so its significance was political as well as financial."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-10":{type:"compare",title:"National Insurance: Health and Unemployment",instruction:"Complete the comparison and state what made the scheme contributory.",headers:["Feature","Health insurance","Unemployment insurance"],rows:[["Purpose","Support eligible workers during sickness","Support eligible workers without work"],["Funding","Workers, employers and state contributed","Workers, employers and state contributed"],["Key idea","Insurance, not universal free payment","Insurance, not universal free payment"]],answer:"The National Insurance Act 1911 provided contributory health and unemployment insurance for eligible workers. Contributions from workers, employers and the state distinguished it from a wholly non-contributory benefit."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-11":{type:"correct",title:"Who Paid the Contributions?",instruction:"Repair the sentence and identify the three contributors before the reveal.",prompt:"'The 1911 insurance schemes were paid for entirely by the Liberal government, so workers had no contribution.'",answer:"Workers, employers and the state all contributed to the 1911 insurance schemes. The shared contribution model was central to the insurance principle, although it also created criticism and practical burdens."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-12":{type:"compare",title:"Why Unions Opposed Insurance",instruction:"Compare the intended benefit with the union criticism and decide whether the opposition was about welfare or control.",headers:["View","Argument"],rows:[["Government/supporters","Insurance protected eligible workers against sickness and unemployment"],["Trade union critics","Compulsory contributions and approved-society administration could reduce union independence"]],answer:"Union opposition did not necessarily reject all welfare. Critics argued that compulsory contributions and state-approved administration reduced union independence and control over members' support."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-13":{type:"sequence",title:"Budget Rejection to Constitutional Crisis",instruction:"Build the chain and name the institution whose power was challenged.",prompt:"People's Budget → House of Lords rejection → political crisis → pressure for Parliament Act 1911.",answer:"The House of Lords rejected Lloyd George's 1909 Budget, creating a constitutional crisis over the Lords' power to block elected government. The conflict helped lead to legislation limiting that veto power."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-14":{type:"correct",title:"What the Parliament Act Limited",instruction:"Repair the claim and distinguish money bills from other public bills.",prompt:"'The Parliament Act 1911 abolished the House of Lords and gave the Commons unlimited power over every issue.'",answer:"The Act limited the Lords' power to veto money bills and allowed them only to delay other public bills. It did not abolish the Lords or give the Commons unlimited power over every issue."},
  "MC-HIST-BRIT1-15":{type:"compare",title:"Home Rule: Proposal and Crisis",instruction:"Separate the policy proposal from the political conflict it created. Add one reason the issue became so divisive.",headers:["Term","Meaning"],rows:[["Home Rule","An Irish parliament within the United Kingdom"],["Home Rule crisis","Political conflict over that proposal"],["Ulster opposition","Fear of separation and resistance to an Irish parliament"]],answer:"Home Rule was the proposal for an Irish parliament while retaining Westminster responsibilities. The crisis was the wider conflict over the proposal, intensified by unionist opposition and fears about the future of the United Kingdom."},

  // PE: make physiology and energy-system misconceptions observable.
  "MC-PE-01":{type:"calculate",title:"Cardiac Output Is a Product",instruction:"Calculate cardiac output before the reveal, then identify which variable changed in the scenario.",prompt:"At rest HR = 70 bpm and SV = 70 ml. During exercise HR = 180 bpm and SV = 120 ml. Use Q = HR × SV and give the answer in litres per minute.",answer:"Rest: 70 × 70 = 4,900 ml/min = 4.9 L/min. Exercise: 180 × 120 = 21,600 ml/min = 21.6 L/min. Cardiac output rises because both heart rate and stroke volume increase; do not add them or leave the answer in unexplained millilitres."},
  "MC-PE-02":{type:"sequence",title:"Why the Curve Shifts Right",instruction:"Build the mechanism from intense exercise to oxygen unloading. Circle the first link a wrong answer would reverse.",prompt:"Exercise intensity rises → CO₂ and temperature rise → pH falls → haemoglobin affinity falls → more O₂ unloads to working muscle.",answer:"The Bohr effect links increased CO₂ to more H⁺ and lower pH; higher muscle temperature also contributes. The oxygen dissociation curve shifts right, meaning haemoglobin releases oxygen more readily. A right shift is not improved loading in the lungs; it is improved unloading at the muscle."},
  "MC-PE-03":{type:"sequence",title:"Sliding, Not Shrinking",instruction:"Put the contraction steps in order and correct the idea that actin and myosin filaments themselves shorten.",prompt:"Action potential → Ca²⁺ release → Ca²⁺ binds troponin → tropomyosin moves → myosin binds actin → power stroke.",answer:"Calcium exposes binding sites on actin by moving tropomyosin. Myosin heads form cross-bridges and pull actin towards the centre; the actin and myosin filaments slide past one another rather than shortening themselves."},
  "MC-PE-04":{type:"compare",title:"Marathon or Sprint Energy?",instruction:"Compare the event demands before the reveal. Name the dominant system and the evidence that supports your choice.",headers:["Event","Dominant system","Reason"],rows:[["Marathon at 80% VO₂ max","Aerobic","Long duration and oxygen supply"],["Maximal 100 m sprint","ATP-PC","Immediate, high-power effort"],["Hard 800 m effort","Anaerobic glycolysis","High intensity over roughly 10 seconds–2 minutes"]],answer:"A marathon runner at 80% VO₂ max primarily uses the aerobic system because sustained work depends on oxygen-supported ATP production. All systems contribute, but duration and intensity determine which dominates."},
  "MC-PE-05":{type:"distinguish",title:"VO₂ Max Is Not Just Speed",instruction:"Commit to the definition, then separate the measure from the performance outcome it can support.",items:["Maximum oxygen uptake per minute relative to body mass","The fastest speed a runner can reach","A direct guarantee of race victory"],answer:"VO₂ max is the maximal rate of oxygen uptake, expressed in ml/kg/min, and indicates cardiorespiratory fitness. It can support endurance performance but does not guarantee victory: economy, threshold, tactics and training also matter."},
  "MC-PE-06":{type:"sequence",title:"What EPOC Restores",instruction:"Complete the recovery chain and distinguish the fast and slow components.",prompt:"Exercise stops → oxygen consumption stays elevated → phosphocreatine and oxygen stores are restored → lactate and temperature are dealt with.",answer:"EPOC is excess post-exercise oxygen consumption. The fast component helps restore phosphocreatine and oxygen stores; the slower component covers processes such as lactate metabolism, elevated temperature and continued circulation. It is more precise than the older idea of a single oxygen debt."},
  "MC-PE-07":{type:"compare",title:"Slow-Twitch or Fast-Twitch?",instruction:"Complete the comparison and choose the fibre type best suited to a marathon. Explain the feature that makes the choice defensible.",headers:["Feature","Type I","Type IIx"],rows:[["Contraction","Slower","Fast"],["Fatigue","Resistant","Rapidly fatigued"],["Main metabolism","Aerobic/oxidative","High-power, mainly anaerobic"],["Best match","Endurance","Explosive effort"]],answer:"Type I fibres are slow oxidative, fatigue-resistant and suited to endurance. Type IIx fibres produce high force and power but fatigue quickly. A marathon primarily demands Type I characteristics."},
  "MC-PE-08":{type:"sequence",title:"Breathing During Maximal Exercise",instruction:"Put inspiration and expiration in order, then identify which additional muscles are recruited as demand rises.",prompt:"Diaphragm contracts → thoracic volume rises → pressure falls → air enters; during hard exercise, accessory inspiratory muscles assist.",answer:"At rest, inspiration is active through the diaphragm and external intercostals, while expiration is mainly passive elastic recoil. During maximal exercise, accessory inspiratory muscles such as the scalenes and sternocleidomastoid assist; expiration also becomes actively driven."},

  // English Language & Literature: move from technique labels to analysis.
  "MC-ENG-01":{type:"correct",title:"Semantic Field, Not Just Imagery",instruction:"Repair the student's label, then make the semantic link earn its place in the analysis.",prompt:"Words from a war poem: 'blood', 'wounds', 'battlefield', 'carnage', 'fallen'. Student: 'The poet uses imagery of death and violence.'",answer:"A semantic field is the more precise term: the words belong to a related conceptual domain. The analysis must then explain the cumulative effect—how the field makes violence feel pervasive or shapes the reader's view of war—not stop at naming a technique."},
  "MC-ENG-02":{type:"distinguish",title:"Free Indirect Discourse",instruction:"Commit to the narrative method, then underline the clues showing a third-person narrator merging with a character's thoughts.",prompt:"'She would go to the party, she supposed. What was the point, really? Nobody ever talked to her anyway.'",answer:"This is free indirect discourse: third-person narration merges with the character's thought patterns without reporting verbs or speech marks. The rhetorical question and subjective phrasing reveal her perspective while the narrator remains in third person."},
  "MC-ENG-03":{type:"correct",title:"Polysyndeton Has a Rhythm",instruction:"Correct the technique label and explain the effect created by repeated conjunctions.",prompt:"'And the thunder, and the lightning, and the rain all fell at once.' Student: 'This is repetition for emphasis.'",answer:"The precise term is polysyndeton: deliberate multiplication of coordinating conjunctions beyond grammatical necessity. The repeated 'and' creates accumulation, weight or breathlessness; the answer must connect the form to the experience of the storm."},
  "MC-ENG-04":{type:"distinguish",title:"Deixis Depends on Context",instruction:"Sort the examples by deixis type and explain what information the reader needs to interpret them.",items:["I / you","here / there","now / yesterday"],answer:"Deixis points to the speech situation: personal deixis identifies participants, spatial deixis identifies location, and temporal deixis identifies time. The reference shifts with who speaks, where they are and when the utterance occurs."},
  "MC-ENG-05":{type:"correct",title:"Bathos Drops the Tone",instruction:"Commit to whether the example is bathos, then identify the movement from elevated to trivial material.",prompt:"A poem describes heroic deeds and ends with the hero eating toast. Student: 'Bathos means any powerful ending.'",answer:"Bathos is an abrupt descent from elevated or serious material to the trivial or mundane, often creating comic, deflating or ironic effect. The contrast between heroic deeds and eating toast creates the bathos."},
  "MC-ENG-06":{type:"correct",title:"Dramatic Irony Needs Unequal Knowledge",instruction:"Repair the claim and state who knows the crucial information in a dramatic-irony example.",prompt:"'The play uses dramatic irony whenever a character makes a mistake.'",answer:"Dramatic irony occurs when the audience knows something a character does not. A mistake alone is insufficient; the gap in knowledge creates tension, anticipation or pathos."},
  "MC-ENG-07":{type:"compare",title:"Register Is Situation, Not Formality Alone",instruction:"Compare the register of a legal notice and a text message. Name field, relationship and mode as evidence.",headers:["Dimension","Legal notice","Text message"],rows:[["Field","Specialist/legal vocabulary","Everyday topic or slang"],["Relationship","Institution to recipient","Peers or familiar contacts"],["Mode","Formal written conventions","Informal abbreviated style"]],answer:"Register is the variety of language appropriate to a particular social situation. Formality is one feature; field, relationship and mode also shape the choice of vocabulary and grammar."},
  "MC-ENG-08":{type:"correct",title:"From Repetition to Meaning",instruction:"Upgrade the student's comment by adding pattern, context and effect. Do not accept 'for emphasis' as the final explanation.",prompt:"A student sees repeated 'darkness' in a Gothic novel and writes: 'The word darkness is repeated for emphasis.'",answer:"A stronger analysis explains what the pattern makes meaningful: recurring 'darkness' may associate the setting with concealment, fear or moral uncertainty. Technique, pattern and interpretation must be connected."},

  // Mandarin: keep reading, vocabulary and text-type starters tied to the
  // actual IB task rather than a generic language drill.
  "MC-MAND-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Read for Two Reasons",instruction:"Commit to the two reasons before the reveal. Underline the words in the text that support each one.",prompt:"李明认为手机有帮助。Find two reasons from the blog post and separate them from the drawback he concedes.",answer:"He can keep in contact with friends around the world and find information whenever he needs it. The drawback is sometimes spending too much time on social media; although he admits it, he still thinks the phone is useful."},
  "MC-MAND-02":{type:"correct",title:"虽然……但是……",instruction:"Complete the concessive sentence, then explain what the connector does to the argument.",prompt:"虽然手机有时候让我花太多时间，____。",answer:"A strong completion is 但是总体来说手机让我的生活更方便。虽然……但是…… means 'although…however…': it acknowledges a drawback before asserting the main, contrasting point."},
  "MC-MAND-03":{type:"calculate",title:"Nearly Three Tenths",instruction:"Commit to the quantitative change before the reveal. Translate the amount and reject the absolute claim.",prompt:"上海实施垃圾分类后，回收率提高了将近三成。What does 将近三成 mean, and what attitude change is also described?",answer:"将近三成 means nearly 30% (three tenths). The text also says many residents gradually accepted the system after initially finding it troublesome. 不少 means quite a few, not all."},
  "MC-MAND-04":{type:"distinguish",title:"强制推行 in Context",instruction:"Give both the vocabulary gloss and the contextual implication before seeing the reveal.",prompt:"Explain 强制推行 in the Shanghai recycling article and state whether residents could simply choose not to follow it.",answer:"强制 means compulsory/forced and 推行 means implement or roll out: together, mandatory implementation. The system was required by law, so it was not merely a voluntary campaign."},
  "MC-MAND-05":{type:"classify",title:"Two Effects of Sleep Loss",instruction:"Extract both effects from the diary entry and classify them as immediate cognitive or longer-term health effects.",items:["影响记忆力和注意力","长期下去影响身心健康","让所有学生马上停止使用手机"],answer:"Sleep loss affects memory and concentration, and over time can affect physical and mental health. The text does not claim every student immediately stops using a phone; that is an invented absolute."},
  "MC-MAND-06":{type:"correct",title:"Diary Register Check",instruction:"Choose the opening that fits a diary, then name the convention that rules out a formal letter opening.",prompt:"A student begins a diary entry with 你好！ and writes a formal report about sleep. Repair the opening.",answer:"A diary should include a date/day, first-person reflection and personal reaction, for example: 2024年10月20日 星期日\n今天真是让我印象深刻的一天。你好 is a letter-style greeting, and a formal report register does not fit the diary task."},
  "MC-MAND-07":{type:"compare",title:"高考与IB: Read the Contrast",instruction:"Complete the comparison from the passage, then identify which criticism belongs to 高考 rather than IB.",headers:["Feature","高考","IB课程"],rows:[["Strength claimed","公平、客观","批判性思维、研究能力"],["Criticism","压力大、只考查记忆","费用较高"],["Access/context","One exam can decide university entry","Mainly in international schools"]],answer:"The critics say 高考 puts students under great pressure and tests mainly memory, so it cannot assess ability comprehensively. The cost criticism applies to IB, not 高考; the contrast marker 相比之下 signals the shift."},
  "MC-MAND-08":{type:"rewrite",title:"Formal Recommendation",instruction:"Rewrite the weak sentence using a concessive connector, formal register and a clear recommendation before the reveal.",prompt:"Weak: 我觉得IB很好，学校可以试试看。",answer:"A stronger version is: IB课程虽然费用较高，但其注重批判性思维和综合素质培养，因此我强烈建议贵校认真考虑引进这一课程体系。 It uses 虽然……但……, formal 贵校, precise vocabulary and a clear recommendation."},

  // GCSE Maths anchors.
  "MC-MATH-NUMBER":{type:"calculate",title:"Standard Form Is Place Value",instruction:"Write the answer before the reveal, then explain how many places the decimal moves and why the exponent is negative.",prompt:"Write 0.00072 in standard form.",answer:"0.00072 = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴. The decimal moves four places right to make 7.2, so the power is −4; a positive exponent would describe a number larger than one."},
  "MC-MATH-FACTORS":{type:"calculate",title:"HCF Means Common and Highest",instruction:"List the factors of both numbers before choosing the answer. Circle the largest factor that appears in both lists.",prompt:"Find the highest common factor of 36 and 48.",answer:"Common factors include 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12; the highest is 12. Prime factors: 36 = 2²×3² and 48 = 2⁴×3, so the common product is 2²×3 = 12. Do not choose the largest factor of either number; it must divide both."},
  "MC-MATH-ALGEBRA":{type:"correct",title:"Collect Like Terms",instruction:"Rewrite the expression in two labelled moves: collect the x-terms, then collect the constants. Commit before the reveal and identify the term an incorrect answer has combined illegally.",prompt:"Simplify 3x + 5 − x + 2.",answer:"3x − x = 2x and 5 + 2 = 7, so the answer is 2x + 7. x-terms can combine with x-terms; constants combine with constants; unlike terms cannot be added together."},
  "MC-MATH-EQUATIONS":{type:"calculate",title:"Balance Both Sides",instruction:"Solve the equation on your board before the reveal. Record the inverse operation used at each step and substitute your answer back to check it.",prompt:"Solve 5x − 7 = 18.",answer:"Add 7: 5x = 25. Divide by 5: x = 5. Check: 5(5) − 7 = 18; changing only one side breaks equality."},
  "MC-MATH-RATIO":{type:"calculate",title:"Ratio Parts Before Shares",instruction:"Commit to the larger share before the reveal. Find the total number of parts, the value of one part, and then multiply for the larger share.",prompt:"A ratio is 3:5 and the total is 40. What is the larger share?",answer:"There are 8 parts; 40 ÷ 8 = 5 per part, so the larger share is 5 × 5 = 25; the shares 15 and 25 total 40."},
  "MC-MATH-PERCENT":{type:"calculate",title:"Increase, Not Just Find",instruction:"Calculate the percentage change first, then add it to the original. Circle the step that prevents a decrease answer.",prompt:"Increase 80 by 15%.",answer:"15% of 80 is 12; 80 + 12 = 92. Equivalently, 80 × 1.15 = 92. Finding 12 alone gives the increase, not the new value."},
  "MC-MATH-ANGLES":{type:"calculate",title:"Triangle Angles Total 180°",instruction:"Write the angle equation before solving. Check the three resulting angles add to 180°.",prompt:"The angles in a triangle are x, 2x and 3x. Find x.",answer:"x + 2x + 3x = 180, so x = 30°. The angles are 30°, 60° and 90°; 360° is for a full turn."},
  "MC-MATH-MEASURES":{type:"calculate",title:"Area Uses Square Units",instruction:"Write the rectangle formula and substitute both dimensions. Label the answer in square centimetres.",prompt:"Find the area of a rectangle measuring 7 cm by 4 cm.",answer:"Area = 7 × 4 = 28 cm². Perimeter would be 22 cm; area measures the surface inside the boundary."},
  "MC-MATH-PROBABILITY":{type:"classify",title:"Count the Successful Outcomes",instruction:"List the even outcomes before forming the fraction. Keep the denominator as all equally likely outcomes.",prompt:"A fair six-sided die is rolled. What is the probability of an even number?",answer:"Even outcomes are 2, 4 and 6: 3 out of 6, so P(even) = 1/2. The denominator is six because every face is possible."},
  "MC-MATH-STATISTICS":{type:"calculate",title:"Mean Is Total Over Count",instruction:"Add all values before dividing by how many values there are. Estimate whether the answer lies between the smallest and largest values.",prompt:"Find the mean of 4, 6, 9 and 11.",answer:"Total = 30 and there are 4 values, so mean = 7.5. Dividing by the largest value or choosing the median gives a different statistic."},
  "MC-MATH-SEQUENCES":{type:"sequence",title:"Find the Common Difference",instruction:"Calculate the difference between adjacent terms, then use it once more. State whether the sequence is arithmetic.",prompt:"The sequence 5, 9, 13, 17 continues with which term?",answer:"The common difference is +4, so the next term is 21. Adding the same difference each time makes it arithmetic."},
  "MC-MATH-FACTORISE":{type:"correct",title:"Factorise to Two Brackets",instruction:"Find two numbers whose product is 12 and whose sum is 7. Expand your brackets to check.",prompt:"Factorise x² + 7x + 12.",answer:"x² + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4). Expanding checks both the product 12 and the middle term 7x."},
  "MC-MATH-FRACTIONS":{type:"calculate",title:"Common Denominator First",instruction:"Make the denominators the same before adding. Keep the numerator change visible.",prompt:"Simplify 3/4 + 5/8.",answer:"3/4 = 6/8, so 6/8 + 5/8 = 11/8 = 1⅜. Adding numerators and denominators directly is not valid."},
  "MC-MATH-SURDS":{type:"correct",title:"Take Out Square Factors",instruction:"Rewrite 50 as a square number times a remainder. Check no square factor remains inside the root.",prompt:"Simplify √50.",answer:"√50 = √(25 × 2) = 5√2. The square factor becomes 5 outside the root."},
  "MC-MATH-SUBSTITUTION":{type:"calculate",title:"Substitute With Signs",instruction:"Replace every variable with its signed value, use brackets for the negative number, then follow powers before addition.",prompt:"If a = 3 and b = −2, find 2a² + b.",answer:"2(3²) + (−2) = 18 − 2 = 16. The negative value of b must remain negative."},
  // Added with the gcse-maths retag: two errors that had no tag of their own.
  "MC-MATH-PROBRULES":{type:"sort",title:"AND or OR?",instruction:"Commit to an operation for each before the reveal: write × or + beside each question, then state the condition each rule needs.",items:["P(A and B) for independent events, P(A)=0.4 and P(B)=0.25","P(A or B) for mutually exclusive events, P(A)=0.3 and P(B)=0.4"],answer:"AND multiplies: 0.4 × 0.25 = 0.1 — valid because the events are INDEPENDENT. OR adds: 0.3 + 0.4 = 0.7 — valid because they are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE and cannot both happen. Adding an 'and' or multiplying an 'or' is the usual slip; each rule also depends on its condition holding."},
  "MC-MATH-ALGEBRAPRAC":{type:"diagnose",title:"Does the Solution Change?",instruction:"Commit to yes or no before the reveal. Multiply both sides of 2x + 1 = 7 by 3, write the new equation, solve it, and compare with the original solution.",prompt:"2x + 1 = 7. Multiply BOTH sides by 3. What is the new equation, and what is x?",answer:"6x + 3 = 21, and x = 3 — the same solution as before. Doing the same thing to both sides changes how the equation looks, never which value satisfies it. Multiplying only one side is what breaks it."},
  "MC-MATH-ANGLE2":{type:"calculate",title:"Quadrilateral, Not Triangle",instruction:"Write the total for a quadrilateral before you add anything up, then form an equation and solve. State how you would check the total is right.",prompt:"A quadrilateral has angles 82°, 96°, 104° and x°. Find x.",answer:"Angles in a quadrilateral total 360°, so x = 360 − 282 = 78°. Check by splitting the shape into two triangles: 2 × 180 = 360. Using 180 is the usual slip."},
  "MC-MATH-AREA2":{type:"correct",title:"Half, or You've Made a Rectangle",instruction:"A student answers 84 cm² for a triangle with base 12 cm and height 7 cm. Commit to what they did wrong before the reveal, then give the correct area.",prompt:"Triangle: base 12 cm, height 7 cm. Student's answer: 84 cm².",answer:"They found the area of the surrounding RECTANGLE. A triangle is half of it: ½ × 12 × 7 = 42 cm². The height must also be perpendicular to the base, not a slanted side."},
  "MC-MATH-BEARINGS":{type:"diagram",title:"From North, Clockwise, Three Figures",instruction:"Sketch a compass and mark 135° and 325° as bearings. Commit before the reveal, then state the three rules a bearing must obey.",prompt:"Mark bearings of 135° and 325°. Which compass directions do they lie between?",answer:"135° lies between east and south; 325° between west and north. Bearings are measured FROM NORTH, CLOCKWISE, and written with three figures (so 45° is written 045°). Measuring anticlockwise or from another direction is the usual error."},
  "MC-MATH-BOUNDS":{type:"calculate",title:"Half a Unit Above",instruction:"Commit to the upper bound before the reveal. State the rounding unit first, then halve it, and say why the upper bound is not 8.49.",prompt:"A length is 8.0 cm correct to the nearest centimetre. Find its upper bound.",answer:"The unit is 1 cm, so the bound is half a unit above: 8.5 cm. Anything below 8.5 rounds down to 8. The upper bound is 8.5 exactly, not 8.49 — it is the limit the value can approach, written with ≤ or <."},
  "MC-MATH-BOUNDS2":{type:"distinguish",title:"The Unit Decides, Not the Digits",instruction:"Two measurements look similar but round to different units. Commit to both upper bounds before the reveal, then state what actually sets the bound.",items:["8 cm to the nearest centimetre","7.4 cm to the nearest millimetre"],answer:"8 cm to the nearest cm: upper bound 8.5. 7.4 cm to the nearest mm (0.1 cm): upper bound 7.45. The ROUNDING UNIT sets the bound — half of it either side — not how many digits the number happens to have."},
  "MC-MATH-CALC-STANDARD":{type:"correct",title:"You Cannot Add the Powers",instruction:"A student writes 3.7 × 10⁴ + 2.6 × 10³ = 6.3 × 10⁷. Commit to what went wrong before the reveal, then do it properly.",prompt:"Calculate 3.7 × 10⁴ + 2.6 × 10³.",answer:"They added coefficients and powers, which is the rule for MULTIPLYING, not adding. To add, match the powers first: 37 000 + 2 600 = 39 600 = 3.96 × 10⁴."},
  "MC-MATH-CIRCLE":{type:"correct",title:"The Equation Stores r Squared",instruction:"Write the equation for a circle with centre (2, −1) and radius 5, then read the radius from (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 16. Commit to both before the reveal.",prompt:"(a) centre (2, −1), radius 5 → equation?   (b) (x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 16 → radius?",answer:"(a) (x − 2)² + (y + 1)² = 25 — note +1, because the centre's y is −1 and the form is (y − b)². (b) radius 4, not 16: the right-hand side is r², so take the square root."},
  "MC-MATH-CIRCLE2":{type:"calculate",title:"Halve It, Don't Double It",instruction:"Commit to the radius before the reveal, then say which way round the doubling goes and what would happen to an area calculation if you got it backwards.",prompt:"A circle has diameter 10 cm. Find its radius.",answer:"Radius 5 cm — the radius is HALF the diameter. Using 10 as the radius would give an area four times too big, since area depends on r²."},
  "MC-MATH-CIRCLETHEOREM":{type:"match",title:"Name the Circle Theorem",instruction:"Commit to an angle for each before the reveal, then name the theorem you used. One of these is not a theorem at all but a definition.",items:["the angle between a tangent and the radius at the point of contact","the angle between a tangent and a chord, when the alternate segment angle is 35°"],answer:"Tangent and radius meet at 90° — always. Tangent and chord equals the angle in the alternate segment, so 35°. Students often assume the tangent-chord angle is also 90°; it only is when the chord is a diameter."},
  "MC-MATH-COMPLEMENT":{type:"calculate",title:"Everything Sums to One",instruction:"Commit to the answer before the reveal, then write the general rule and say what it would mean if your two probabilities summed to more than 1.",prompt:"The probability an item is faulty is 0.04. Find the probability it is good.",answer:"1 − 0.04 = 0.96. All outcomes together must total 1, so P(not A) = 1 − P(A). A total above 1 means an outcome has been double-counted; a total below 1 means one is missing."},
  "MC-MATH-COMPOUND":{type:"correct",title:"Compound Multiplies, Simple Adds",instruction:"A student says a 12% annual fall over 3 years is a 36% fall. Commit to whether that is right before the reveal, then find the true multiplier.",prompt:"£18,000 depreciating 12% each year for 3 years. What is it worth?",answer:"Not 36%. Each year multiplies by 0.88, so after 3 years: 18000 × 0.88³ = £12,266.50 — a fall of about 31.9%, not 36%. Compound change multiplies repeatedly; only simple change adds the same amount each time."},
  "MC-MATH-COORDINATES":{type:"diagram",title:"Which Coordinate Flips?",instruction:"Plot (3, −2) and reflect it in the y-axis. Commit to the image before the reveal, then state which coordinate changes sign for each axis.",prompt:"Reflect (3, −2) in the y-axis. Then reflect the original in the x-axis.",answer:"In the y-axis: (−3, −2) — the x-coordinate flips. In the x-axis: (3, 2) — the y-coordinate flips. The rule is the opposite of what most students guess: reflecting in the y-axis changes x, because the y-axis is the mirror line."},
  "MC-MATH-CORRELATION":{type:"evaluate",title:"Strong Does Not Mean Caused",instruction:"A correlation coefficient is close to −1. Commit to what that describes before the reveal, then judge the claim underneath it.",prompt:"r ≈ −0.95 between hours of TV watched and exam score. A student concludes: 'watching TV causes lower grades.'",answer:"r ≈ −0.95 means a STRONG NEGATIVE linear relationship: as one rises the other falls. It does not establish cause — a third factor (time spent revising, for instance) could drive both. Strength of correlation says nothing about direction of cause."},
  "MC-MATH-CUMFREQ":{type:"calculate",title:"Read Up From the Frequency Axis",instruction:"Commit to the frequency you would read across from before the reveal. State where the median, Q1 and Q3 sit on a cumulative frequency graph of 80 values.",prompt:"Total frequency 80. At what cumulative frequency do you read the median? Q1? Q3?",answer:"Median at 40 (n/2), Q1 at 20 (n/4), Q3 at 60 (3n/4). Read ACROSS from the frequency axis, then DOWN to the value axis. Reading up from the value axis instead answers a different question entirely."},
  "MC-MATH-DECIMALPERCENT":{type:"calculate",title:"One Step, Not Two",instruction:"Find 15% off £240 in a single multiplication. Commit to the multiplier before the reveal, then say what 0.15 on its own would have given you.",prompt:"A price of £240 is reduced by 15%. Find the sale price.",answer:"A 15% reduction leaves 85%, so × 0.85 = £204. Multiplying by 0.15 gives £36 — that is the DISCOUNT, not the price. Subtracting it afterwards works too, but the single multiplier is fewer steps and fewer errors."},
  "MC-MATH-ERROR":{type:"calculate",title:"Half a Unit Either Side",instruction:"Write the error interval using inequality signs. Commit before the reveal, then explain why one end uses ≤ and the other <.",prompt:"A measurement is 4.6 cm correct to the nearest millimetre. State its error interval.",answer:"4.55 ≤ x < 4.65. The unit is 1 mm = 0.1 cm, so the interval reaches half a unit each side. The lower end is included because 4.55 rounds up to 4.6; the upper end is excluded because 4.65 rounds up to 4.7."},
  "MC-MATH-ESTIMATION":{type:"calculate",title:"Round First, Then Multiply",instruction:"Estimate before calculating anything exactly. Commit to your rounded values and your estimate, then say why an estimate that needs a calculator has missed the point.",prompt:"Estimate 49.8 × 2.1 by rounding each to 1 significant figure.",answer:"50 × 2 = 100. The exact answer is 104.58, so the estimate is close and was obtained mentally. Rounding to 1 significant figure means one non-zero digit — 49.8 → 50, not 49.8 → 49."},
  "MC-MATH-EXPONENTIAL":{type:"distinguish",title:"The Multiplier Is Not the Rate",instruction:"For each model, commit to the annual percentage change before the reveal. Then state the rule connecting the multiplier to the rate.",items:["P = 500(1.08)ⁿ","P = 12000(1.024)ⁿ","P = 18000(0.88)ⁿ"],answer:"Growth of 8%, growth of 2.4%, and a FALL of 12%. The multiplier is 1 + rate: 1.08 means +8%, and 0.88 means −12%. Reading 1.08 as '108% growth' or 0.88 as '88% decrease' is the usual error — subtract 1 first."},
  "MC-MATH-FRACTIONS2":{type:"calculate",title:"Place Value Names the Denominator",instruction:"Convert 0.375 to a fraction without a calculator. Commit to your starting denominator before the reveal, then simplify fully and say how you knew when to stop.",prompt:"Write 0.375 as a fraction in its simplest form.",answer:"Three decimal places means thousandths: 375/1000. Divide by 125 to get 3/8. Stop when the only common factor left is 1 — check by confirming 3 and 8 share no factor."},
  "MC-MATH-FREQUENCY":{type:"calculate",title:"Divide by the Frequency Total",instruction:"Commit to the mean before the reveal. Show the two totals you need separately, and name the number most students divide by instead.",prompt:"Values 2, 4 and 7 with frequencies 3, 5 and 2. Find the mean.",answer:"Σfx = 6 + 20 + 14 = 40; Σf = 10; mean = 40 ÷ 10 = 4. Divide by the TOTAL FREQUENCY (10), not by the number of different values (3). For grouped data, use the class midpoint as x — and plot at the midpoint too."},
  "MC-MATH-GEOMETRIC":{type:"sequence",title:"Multiply, and Mind the Term Number",instruction:"Commit to the fifth term before the reveal. Write the terms out and count carefully — the exponent trap is worth spotting.",prompt:"First term 3, common ratio 2. Find the 5th term.",answer:"3, 6, 12, 24, 48 — the 5th term is 48. Using arⁿ⁻¹ = 3 × 2⁴ = 48. The common slip is 3 × 2⁵ = 96, because the FIRST term already uses r⁰; the exponent is one less than the term number."},
  "MC-MATH-GRAPHS":{type:"distinguish",title:"Which Number Is the Gradient?",instruction:"For each equation, commit to the gradient and the y-intercept before the reveal. One is not in y = mx + c form — rearrange it first.",items:["y = 3x − 4","y = 7 − 2x","2y = 6x + 10"],answer:"Gradients 3, −2 and 3; intercepts −4, 7 and 5. In y = mx + c, m is the coefficient OF x. In y = 7 − 2x the gradient is −2, not 7 — rewrite as y = −2x + 7 to see it. Always rearrange to this form before reading anything off."},
  "MC-MATH-GRAPHTRANS":{type:"diagram",title:"Which Way Did It Move?",instruction:"Sketch y = 2x + 1 and y = 2x − 5 on the same axes. Commit to the transformation before the reveal, and state what stayed the same.",prompt:"y = 2x + 1 is transformed to y = 2x − 5. Describe the transformation.",answer:"A translation of 6 units DOWN — the gradient is unchanged at 2, so the lines are parallel. Changing c slides the line vertically; only a change in m would tilt it."},
  "MC-MATH-HISTOGRAM":{type:"calculate",title:"Frequency Is the Area",instruction:"Commit to the frequency before the reveal. Say what the height of a histogram bar represents, and why it is not the frequency.",prompt:"A histogram bar has class width 5 and frequency density 6. Find the frequency.",answer:"Frequency = density × width = 6 × 5 = 30. The HEIGHT is frequency density, so the frequency is the AREA of the bar. Reading the height as the frequency is the classic histogram error, and it only coincidentally works when the width is 1."},
  "MC-MATH-INDICES":{type:"sort",title:"Add, Subtract, or Multiply the Powers?",instruction:"Commit to an answer for each before the reveal, then write the rule you used beside it.",items:["2³ × 2⁴","2⁸ ÷ 2³","(2³)⁴","2ˣ = 32, find x"],answer:"2⁷ (multiply → ADD indices), 2⁵ (divide → SUBTRACT), 2¹² (power of a power → MULTIPLY), and x = 5 since 32 = 2⁵. The usual error is multiplying the indices when multiplying the terms: 2³ × 2⁴ is 2⁷, not 2¹²."},
  "MC-MATH-INEQUALITY":{type:"correct",title:"At Least, and the Flip",instruction:"Commit to both answers before the reveal. Translate the words into a symbol, then solve — and watch the second one carefully.",prompt:"(a) Which inequality means 'x is at least 4'?   (b) Solve −2x > 8.",answer:"(a) x ≥ 4 — 'at least' includes 4, so the sign carries the equals. (b) x < −4: dividing by a NEGATIVE reverses the inequality sign. Leaving it as x > −4 is the standard error; test x = 0 to see it fail."},
  "MC-MATH-IQR":{type:"calculate",title:"Subtract, Don't Average",instruction:"Commit to the interquartile range before the reveal, then say in one sentence what a small IQR tells a teacher about the data.",prompt:"Q1 = 18 and Q3 = 41. Find the interquartile range.",answer:"41 − 18 = 23. The IQR is Q3 − Q1, a measure of SPREAD — not the average of the quartiles and not the median. A small IQR means the middle half of the data is tightly clustered, and it ignores outliers at both ends."},
  "MC-MATH-ITERATION":{type:"calculate",title:"Sign Change, Then Iterate",instruction:"Do both parts and commit before the reveal. Part (a) needs two substitutions; part (b) needs one careful evaluation.",prompt:"(a) f(x) = x³ − 4x − 1. Show a root lies between x = 2 and x = 3.   (b) xₙ₊₁ = (10 + xₙ²)/6 with x₀ = 2. Find x₁.",answer:"(a) f(2) = −1 and f(3) = 14. The sign CHANGES between them, so a root lies in between. (b) x₁ = (10 + 4)/6 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333. A sign change locates a root; the iteration then refines it. Substituting into the wrong side, or stopping after one step and calling it the root, are the usual errors."},
  "MC-MATH-LINEAREQ2":{type:"sequence",title:"Expand Before You Solve",instruction:"Solve two ways — expand first, and divide first — and commit to whether they agree before the reveal.",prompt:"Solve 2(x + 3) = 18.",answer:"Expanding: 2x + 6 = 18, so 2x = 12 and x = 6. Dividing first: x + 3 = 9, so x = 6. Both give x = 6. The error to avoid is 2x + 3 = 18 — the 2 multiplies EVERYTHING in the bracket, not just the first term."},
  "MC-MATH-LINEARGRAPH":{type:"calculate",title:"Build It From m and c",instruction:"Commit to the equation before the reveal. Identify which given number is m and which is c, and say how you would find c if the point were not on the y-axis.",prompt:"A line has gradient 2 and passes through (0, 5). Find its equation.",answer:"y = 2x + 5. The point (0, 5) is ON the y-axis, so c = 5 directly. If the point were (3, 11) instead, substitute: 11 = 2(3) + c, giving c = 5 again. c is only read off directly when x = 0."},
  "MC-MATH-MEAN2":{type:"calculate",title:"Run the Mean Backwards",instruction:"Commit to the total before the reveal. Write the mean formula, then rearrange it rather than guessing which way the division goes.",prompt:"Twelve employees have a mean salary of £28,000. Find the total salary bill.",answer:"mean = total ÷ count, so total = mean × count = 28 000 × 12 = £336,000. Dividing instead gives £2,333, which is far too small to be a total of twelve salaries — a quick sense-check catches it."},
  "MC-MATH-MEDIAN":{type:"correct",title:"Order First",instruction:"A student reads the median of 3, 8, 4, 10, 6 straight off as 4. Commit to what they forgot before the reveal, then give the median and say what it means.",prompt:"Find the median of 3, 8, 4, 10 and 6.",answer:"They took the middle of the UNORDERED list. Sort first: 3, 4, 6, 8, 10 — the median is 6. It means half the values lie below it and half above. With an even count, take the mean of the middle two."},
  "MC-MATH-NORMAL":{type:"calculate",title:"How Many Standard Deviations Out?",instruction:"Commit to the z-score before the reveal, then say what the mean represents in a normal distribution and what z = 0 would mean.",prompt:"Mean 100, standard deviation 15. Find the z-score of a value of 130.",answer:"z = (130 − 100)/15 = 2 — the value is TWO standard deviations above the mean. z = 0 is exactly at the mean, which is also the median and the mode for a normal distribution. z counts standard deviations, not raw units."},
  "MC-MATH-NTH":{type:"calculate",title:"n Is the Position",instruction:"Commit to both answers before the reveal. Substitute the position number, and be careful which number goes in.",prompt:"(a) nth term 5n − 2: find the 10th term.   (b) nth term n² + 2: find the 3rd term.",answer:"(a) 5(10) − 2 = 48.   (b) 3² + 2 = 11. Substitute the POSITION for n. A frequent error is solving 5n − 2 = 10 instead, which answers 'which term equals 10?' — a different question."},
  "MC-MATH-PARALLEL":{type:"distinguish",title:"Same, or Negative Reciprocal?",instruction:"Commit to both gradients before the reveal, then state the test that tells you two lines are perpendicular.",prompt:"A line has gradient 1/2. What is the gradient of (a) a parallel line, (b) a perpendicular line?",answer:"(a) 1/2 — parallel lines have EQUAL gradients. (b) −2 — negate and invert. The test: perpendicular gradients multiply to −1, and ½ × −2 = −1. Answering −1/2 (negated only) or 2 (inverted only) is the usual half-done error."},
  "MC-MATH-PERCENT2":{type:"correct",title:"Same Units Before You Divide",instruction:"A student answers 15% for 45 minutes of stops in a 3-hour journey. Commit to their mistake before the reveal, then work it out correctly.",prompt:"A journey has 45 minutes of stops in 3 hours. What percentage is spent stopped?",answer:"They divided 45 by 3 without converting. Use the same units: 3 hours = 180 minutes, so 45/180 = 0.25 = 25%. Mixing minutes with hours is the error — convert before dividing, always."},
  "MC-MATH-PERIMETER":{type:"distinguish",title:"Around It, Not Inside It",instruction:"Commit to the perimeter before the reveal, then say which measurement would have an answer in cm² and why this one does not.",prompt:"A regular hexagon has side length 6 cm. Find its perimeter.",answer:"6 × 6 = 36 cm. Perimeter is the distance AROUND, so it adds all six sides and is measured in cm. Area is the space inside, measured in cm². 'Regular' is what allows the single multiplication — all sides are equal."},
  "MC-MATH-POLYGON":{type:"calculate",title:"Exterior Angles Always Total 360",instruction:"Commit to the number of sides before the reveal, then state the total that never changes no matter how many sides a polygon has.",prompt:"A regular polygon has an exterior angle of 24°. How many sides does it have?",answer:"360 ÷ 24 = 15 sides. The EXTERIOR angles of any polygon total 360°, regardless of the number of sides. Using 180 here — the interior angle sum of a triangle — is the usual confusion."},
  "MC-MATH-POLYGON2":{type:"calculate",title:"Exterior First, Interior After",instruction:"Commit to both angles before the reveal. Find the exterior angle first, then use it to get the interior — and say why that order is easier.",prompt:"A regular polygon has 12 sides. Find each exterior angle, then each interior angle.",answer:"Exterior = 360 ÷ 12 = 30°. Interior = 180 − 30 = 150°, because they sit on a straight line. Going exterior-first avoids the (n−2) × 180 formula entirely; going the other way, students often forget to divide the interior SUM by n."},
  "MC-MATH-PROBABILITY2":{type:"calculate",title:"The Second Denominator Shrinks",instruction:"Commit to both fractions before the reveal. Write the second probability carefully — the bag has changed.",prompt:"5 red and 3 blue counters. Two taken WITHOUT replacement. Find P(both red).",answer:"5/8 × 4/7 = 20/56 = 5/14. After one red is removed there are 4 reds left out of 7 counters — BOTH numbers drop. Using 5/8 × 5/8 treats it as with replacement, which is the standard error."},
  "MC-MATH-PROBABILITY4":{type:"calculate",title:"Count the Successes",instruction:"Commit to the probability before the reveal. List the outcomes that satisfy the condition rather than guessing, and watch the wording.",prompt:"A fair spinner has five equal sections numbered 1 to 5. Find P(number > 3).",answer:"Successes are 4 and 5, so 2/5. 'Greater than 3' EXCLUDES 3 — including it gives 3/5 and is the usual slip. Read whether the wording is 'more than' or 'at least' before counting."},
  "MC-MATH-PROOF":{type:"explain",title:"Say It in Algebra First",instruction:"Commit to an algebraic expression for each phrase before the reveal, then use one to explain why n² − n is always even.",items:["any integer","an even number","an odd number","two consecutive integers"],answer:"n; 2n; 2n + 1; n and n + 1. Now n² − n = n(n − 1), a product of two CONSECUTIVE integers — one of any two consecutive integers must be even, so the product is even. Testing a few numbers is not a proof; the algebra has to cover every case."},
  "MC-MATH-PROPORTION":{type:"calculate",title:"Constant Ratio, Not Constant Difference",instruction:"Commit to y before the reveal. Find the constant of proportionality first, and say what a student who added 4 has assumed instead.",prompt:"y is directly proportional to x. When x = 6, y = 18. Find y when x = 10.",answer:"k = 18 ÷ 6 = 3, so y = 3x and y = 30. A student adding 4 (because x rose by 4) has assumed a constant DIFFERENCE — that is linear with an intercept, not proportion. Direct proportion always passes through the origin."},
  "MC-MATH-PYTHAGORAS":{type:"diagram",title:"Which Side Is the Hypotenuse?",instruction:"Sketch each situation and mark the hypotenuse before calculating. Commit to whether you add or subtract, then find the length.",prompt:"(a) A 5 m ladder rests with its foot 3 m from a wall. How far up does it reach?   (b) A chord is 8 cm from the centre of a circle of radius 10 cm. Find half the chord.",answer:"(a) The LADDER is the hypotenuse: √(25 − 9) = 4 m — subtract, because you are finding a shorter side. (b) The RADIUS is the hypotenuse: √(100 − 64) = 6, so the full chord is 12 cm. Add only when finding the hypotenuse itself."},
  "MC-MATH-QUADRATIC":{type:"calculate",title:"Substitute Into the Formula Carefully",instruction:"Write a, b and c down separately with their signs BEFORE substituting. Commit to your two roots, then check them against the sum of the roots.",prompt:"Solve x² − 5x − 4 = 0 to 2 decimal places.",answer:"a = 1, b = −5, c = −4. x = (5 ± √(25 + 16))/2 = (5 ± √41)/2, giving 5.70 and −0.70. Check: the roots should sum to −b/a = 5, and 5.70 + (−0.70) = 5. Losing the sign of b or c is the usual error."},
  "MC-MATH-QUADRATIC2":{type:"correct",title:"A Square Root Has Two Answers",instruction:"A student solves x² = 49 and writes x = 7. Commit to what is missing before the reveal, then say how many solutions a quadratic can have.",prompt:"Solve x² = 49.",answer:"x = ±7 — both 7 and −7 square to 49, so the negative root is missing. A quadratic has at most two real solutions; writing only the positive one loses half the answer. Only √49 on its own means the positive root."},
  "MC-MATH-QUADRATIC3":{type:"sequence",title:"Divide Before You Root",instruction:"Commit to the order of operations before the reveal. Solve 3x² = 75 and say what happens if you take the square root first.",prompt:"Solve 3x² = 75.",answer:"Divide by 3 first: x² = 25, so x = ±5. Rooting first gives √3 x = √75, which is true but leaves more work. The error to avoid is treating 3x² as (3x)² — the square applies only to x."},
  "MC-MATH-QUADRATICGRAPH":{type:"graph",title:"Roots, Then the Line of Symmetry",instruction:"Sketch a parabola crossing at x = 2 and x = 5. Commit to the equation and to the turning point's x-coordinate before the reveal.",prompt:"(a) A quadratic crosses the x-axis at 2 and 5. Give an equation.   (b) Where is the turning point of y = x² − 4x + 1?",answer:"(a) y = (x − 2)(x − 5) = x² − 7x + 10 — note the SIGNS flip in the brackets. (b) The turning point sits midway between the roots, at x = −b/2a = 2. Reading the roots straight out of the brackets without flipping the sign is the standard slip."},
  "MC-MATH-RANGE":{type:"calculate",title:"Range Is One Number",instruction:"Commit to the range before the reveal, then say what it measures and why it is not written as an interval.",prompt:"Find the range of 7, 12, 4, 19 and 9.",answer:"19 − 4 = 15. The range is a single number measuring SPREAD, not the interval '4 to 19' and not an average. Because it uses only the two extremes, one outlier can distort it completely — which is why the IQR is often preferred."},
  "MC-MATH-RATIO2":{type:"calculate",title:"Scale Up, Don't Add On",instruction:"Commit to the answer before the reveal. Find the amount for one cake first, and say what a student who added 200 g has assumed.",prompt:"A recipe uses 250 g of flour for 10 cakes. How much for 18 cakes?",answer:"250 ÷ 10 = 25 g per cake, so 18 × 25 = 450 g. The error to avoid is adding the difference in CAKES to the GRAMS — 8 more cakes does not mean 8 more grams, giving 258 g. Find the amount for one cake first, then scale."},
  "MC-MATH-RECIPROCAL":{type:"calculate",title:"Turn It Upside Down",instruction:"Commit to the reciprocal before the reveal, then state what any number multiplied by its reciprocal must equal.",prompt:"What is the reciprocal of 5/8? And of 4?",answer:"8/5, and 1/4. A number times its reciprocal is always 1: (5/8) × (8/5) = 1. The reciprocal is not the negative — the reciprocal of 5/8 is 8/5, not −5/8. Whole numbers work too, since 4 = 4/1."},
  "MC-MATH-RECURRING":{type:"sequence",title:"One Step at a Time",instruction:"Commit to u₅ before the reveal. Write out every step rather than jumping — and count how many steps you actually need.",prompt:"u₁ = 4 and uₙ₊₁ = uₙ + 3. Find u₅.",answer:"u₂ = 7, u₃ = 10, u₄ = 13, u₅ = 16. Getting from u₁ to u₅ takes FOUR steps, not five. A recurrence defines each term from the previous one, so you cannot substitute 5 directly the way you would with an nth-term rule."},
  "MC-MATH-SAMPLING":{type:"calculate",title:"Scale the Proportion Up",instruction:"Commit to the estimate before the reveal. Write the sample proportion first, then say what assumption the whole method rests on.",prompt:"A sample of 80 contains 34 adults. Estimate the number of adults in a population of 600.",answer:"34/80 = 0.425, so 0.425 × 600 = 255. The method assumes the sample is REPRESENTATIVE of the population — a biased sample makes the estimate worthless however carefully the arithmetic is done. Adding rather than scaling is the usual arithmetic error."},
  "MC-MATH-SCALE":{type:"calculate",title:"Convert, Then Divide",instruction:"Commit to the map distance before the reveal. Convert both to the same unit first, and state which way the division goes.",prompt:"A map has scale 1:50 000. What map distance represents 2 km?",answer:"2 km = 200 000 cm. Divide by 50 000 to get 4 cm on the map. Real distance is BIGGER, so the map distance must be smaller — multiplying by 50 000 instead gives an absurd 100 km of paper, which is the sense-check."},
  "MC-MATH-SCATTER":{type:"evaluate",title:"A Trend, Not a Rule",instruction:"A scatter diagram shows strong positive correlation. Commit to what that means before the reveal, then judge the two claims underneath.",prompt:"Claims: (a) 'every point with a higher x has a higher y'  (b) 'as x increases, y tends to increase'",answer:"(b) only. Positive correlation describes an overall TREND — individual points can and do buck it. Claim (a) treats correlation as a rule with no exceptions, which even a strong correlation never guarantees. A line of best fit predicts, it does not dictate."},
  "MC-MATH-SECTOR":{type:"calculate",title:"What Fraction of the Circle?",instruction:"Write the fraction of the full circle before calculating anything. Commit to both answers, then say what changes if the question asks for perimeter instead.",prompt:"(a) Sector area: radius 6 cm, angle 60°.   (b) Sector area: radius 9 cm, angle 80°, to 3 s.f.",answer:"(a) 60/360 = 1/6 of π × 36 = 6π cm².  (b) 80/360 = 2/9 of π × 81 = 18π ≈ 56.5 cm². Always start with θ/360. For a PERIMETER you need the arc (same fraction of the circumference) PLUS the two radii — forgetting the radii is the usual omission."},
  "MC-MATH-SEQUENCES2":{type:"calculate",title:"The First Term Is n = 1",instruction:"Commit to the first term before the reveal, then say what a student who answered 4 has done.",prompt:"The nth term of a sequence is 4n + 1. Find the first term.",answer:"Substitute n = 1: 4(1) + 1 = 5. A student answering 4 has read off the coefficient of n — that is the COMMON DIFFERENCE, the step between terms, not the first term. Sequences start at n = 1, not n = 0."},
  "MC-MATH-SIMPLEPROB":{type:"calculate",title:"Successes Over Total",instruction:"Commit to the probability before the reveal, then write the general formula and say what the denominator must always be.",prompt:"A spinner has 4 equal sections, 3 of them red. Find P(red).",answer:"3/4. Probability = successful outcomes ÷ TOTAL outcomes, so the denominator is every section (4), never just the unsuccessful ones. Writing 3/1 or 3 as the answer ignores that a probability can never exceed 1."},
  "MC-MATH-SIMULTANEOUS":{type:"sequence",title:"Add to Eliminate",instruction:"Commit to whether you add or subtract before the reveal. Line the equations up, eliminate one variable, then check your pair in BOTH equations.",prompt:"Solve x + y = 11 and x − y = 3.",answer:"ADD them: the y terms cancel, giving 2x = 14, so x = 7 and y = 4. Add when the matching coefficients have OPPOSITE signs, subtract when they are the same. Check in both: 7 + 4 = 11 and 7 − 4 = 3 — checking in only one equation catches nothing."},
  "MC-MATH-SPEED":{type:"calculate",title:"Mind the Half Hour",instruction:"Commit to the speed before the reveal. Say what 1.5 hours is in minutes, and what would go wrong if you used 1 hour 50.",prompt:"A car travels 84 miles in 1.5 hours. Find its average speed.",answer:"84 ÷ 1.5 = 56 mph. 1.5 hours is 1 hour 30 minutes, not 1 hour 50 — time is decimal only when written as a decimal of an hour. Speed = distance ÷ time; dividing time by distance gives hours per mile, which is not what was asked."},
  "MC-MATH-SPHERE":{type:"correct",title:"Cube It, Then Undo the Cube",instruction:"Commit to the rearrangement before the reveal. Write the volume formula, then make r the subject rather than guessing the root.",prompt:"A sphere has volume 500 cm³. Which equation finds r?",answer:"V = (4/3)πr³, so r = ∛(3V ÷ 4π) = ∛(1500 ÷ 4π) ≈ 4.92 cm. Volume uses r CUBED, so undoing it needs a cube root, not a square root. Using r² here is the standard confusion with the surface-area formula."},
  "MC-MATH-STANDARD2":{type:"calculate",title:"Multiply the Fronts, Add the Powers",instruction:"Commit to the answer before the reveal — and check the final form is valid standard form before you stop.",prompt:"Calculate (3.2 × 10⁵)(4 × 10²).",answer:"3.2 × 4 = 12.8 and 10⁵ × 10² = 10⁷, giving 12.8 × 10⁷. That is NOT standard form — the coefficient must be between 1 and 10, so adjust to 1.28 × 10⁸. Stopping at 12.8 × 10⁷ is the usual half-finished answer."},
  "MC-MATH-TANGENT":{type:"diagram",title:"Two Tangents, One Length",instruction:"Sketch a circle with two tangents from the same external point. Commit to the second length before the reveal, then name the shape the two tangents and two radii form.",prompt:"A tangent from an external point is 12 cm long. How long is the other tangent from that point?",answer:"12 cm — tangents drawn from the same external point are EQUAL. With the two radii they form a kite, symmetrical about the line to the centre, with right angles where each tangent meets its radius. Assuming the lengths differ is the error."},
  "MC-MATH-TIME":{type:"correct",title:"Time Runs in Sixties",instruction:"A student converts 3.5 hours to 350 minutes. Commit to their error before the reveal, then convert correctly.",prompt:"Convert 3.5 hours into minutes.",answer:"They treated the decimal as if time were base 100. An hour is 60 minutes: 3.5 × 60 = 210 minutes. The 0.5 is HALF an hour, so 30 minutes — hence 3 hours 30 minutes. Watch for 2.75 hours meaning 2 h 45 min, not 2 h 75 min."},
  "MC-MATH-TRANSFORM":{type:"diagram",title:"Enlargement Scales the Distance",instruction:"Plot a point and enlarge it by scale factor 3 about the origin. Commit to what happens to its distance from the centre before the reveal.",prompt:"A point is enlarged by scale factor 3 about the origin. What happens to its distance from the origin? What happens to an area?",answer:"The distance TRIPLES — multiply both coordinates by 3. But area is multiplied by 3² = 9, not by 3. Enlargement does not mean 'add 3', and a scale factor between 0 and 1 makes the shape smaller while still being called an enlargement."},
  "MC-MATH-TRANSFORM2":{type:"distinguish",title:"Which Coordinate Got Negated?",instruction:"Commit to the transformation for each mapping before the reveal, then state the rule linking the negated coordinate to the mirror line.",items:["(x, y) → (x, −y)","(x, y) → (−x, y)","(x, y) → (−x, −y)"],answer:"Reflection in the x-axis; reflection in the y-axis; rotation of 180° about the origin. Negating y reflects in the X-axis — the mirror line is the axis whose coordinate stays the SAME. Swapping these two is the most common error."},
  "MC-MATH-TRANSLATION":{type:"correct",title:"Down and Left Are Negative",instruction:"Commit to the column vector before the reveal, then say which entry is which and what a negative in each position means.",prompt:"A translation moves 4 right and 3 down. Write it as a column vector.",answer:"Top entry is horizontal, bottom is vertical: 4 on top, −3 underneath. Right and up are positive; LEFT and DOWN are negative. Writing 3 rather than −3, or putting the vertical component on top, are the two usual errors."},
  "MC-MATH-TRIG":{type:"sort",title:"Label the Sides First",instruction:"For each triangle, label opposite, adjacent and hypotenuse relative to the angle BEFORE choosing a ratio. Commit to the ratio and the answer.",prompt:"(a) Opposite 7 cm, hypotenuse 12 cm — find the angle.   (b) A tree 12 m tall casts a 7 m shadow — find the angle of elevation.",answer:"(a) Opposite and hypotenuse → SIN: sin θ = 7/12, θ = 35.7°. (b) Opposite and adjacent → TAN: tan θ = 12/7, θ = 59.7°. Choosing the ratio before labelling the sides is what goes wrong; and finding an ANGLE needs the inverse function."},
  "MC-MATH-VECTORS":{type:"calculate",title:"Component by Component",instruction:"Commit to both answers before the reveal. Keep the x and y components in separate columns throughout — never mix them.",prompt:"(a) a = (2, 1), b = (−1, 3). Find 2a + b.   (b) a = (3, −1). Find |2a|.",answer:"(a) 2a = (4, 2), so 2a + b = (3, 5) — scale each component, then add matching components. (b) 2a = (6, −2), so |2a| = √(36 + 4) = √40 = 2√10 ≈ 6.32. Magnitude uses Pythagoras; adding the components (6 + −2 = 4) is the usual error."},
  "MC-MATH-VOLUME":{type:"calculate",title:"Area of the Face, Times the Length",instruction:"Commit to the volume before the reveal. Write the cross-sectional area first, and say which measurement gets squared.",prompt:"A cylinder has radius 4 cm and height 10 cm. Find its volume to 3 s.f.",answer:"Circle area = π × 4² = 16π, then × 10 = 160π ≈ 503 cm³. The RADIUS is squared, not the height — a prism's volume is always cross-sectional area × length. Using the diameter in place of the radius gives four times too much."},
  "MC-MATH-VOLUME2":{type:"calculate",title:"Three Dimensions, Cubic Units",instruction:"Commit to the volume before the reveal, then state the units and say what you would have found by multiplying only two of the measurements.",prompt:"A cuboid measures 3 cm by 4 cm by 5 cm. Find its volume.",answer:"3 × 4 × 5 = 60 cm³. Volume multiplies all THREE dimensions and is measured in cubic units. Multiplying only two gives the area of one face (12, 15 or 20 cm²) — the units are the quickest check that you have used the right number of dimensions."},
  "MC-MATH-TRIANGLERULES":{type:"distinguish",title:"No Right Angle, No SOHCAHTOA",instruction:"For each triangle, commit to the rule you would use before the reveal. Say what all three have in common and why SOHCAHTOA is not available.",items:["a = 8 cm, A = 30°, find b when B = 45°","two sides 8 cm and 11 cm with an included angle of 40°, find the area","three sides known, find an angle"],answer:"Sine rule (a/sinA = b/sinB); area = ½ab sinC; cosine rule. None has a right angle, so SOHCAHTOA does not apply — it is only defined for right-angled triangles. Match the rule to what you are GIVEN: two sides and the included angle points to ½ab sinC."},

  // Combined GCSE Science anchors.
  "BIO-CELL-01":{type:"compare",title:"Shared Structures, Different Cells",instruction:"Write the feature you think both cells share before the reveal. Complete a two-column comparison of prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells, then circle the structure that proves your answer.",prompt:"Which feature is present in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells?\n\nComparison headings: shared structure | prokaryote only | eukaryote only",answer:"Both have a cell membrane, cytoplasm, ribosomes and genetic material. Prokaryotes lack a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles; eukaryotes have them."},
  "BIO-MICRO-01":{type:"calculate",title:"Magnification: Multiply, Don’t Add",instruction:"Commit to a numerical answer and show the operation before the reveal. Calculate the total magnification, then write one sentence explaining why the two magnifications are multiplied.",prompt:"Eyepiece = 10×; objective lens = 40×. Total magnification = ?",answer:"10 × 40 = 400×. The light passes through both stages of magnification, so their factors multiply."},
  "BIO-CELL-02":{type:"distinguish",title:"Ribosome or Mitochondrion?",instruction:"Choose the organelle before the reveal. Match each function to ribosome, mitochondrion or nucleus, then state the rule that separates protein synthesis from energy release.",items:["site of protein synthesis","site of aerobic respiration","contains most genetic material","makes proteins from amino acids"],answer:"Ribosome: protein synthesis / makes proteins from amino acids. Mitochondrion: aerobic respiration. Nucleus: contains most genetic material."},
  "BIO-MITOSIS-01":{type:"sequence",title:"Why Mitosis Makes Identical Cells",instruction:"Put the mechanism in order before the reveal. Number the steps and explain how each daughter cell receives the same genetic information.",items:["DNA is replicated","chromosomes are separated","one identical set reaches each end of the cell","the cell divides into two daughter cells"],answer:"DNA is replicated → chromosomes are separated → one identical set reaches each end → the cell divides. Identical DNA copies, not simply cell division, explain the result."},
  "BIO-GEN-01":{type:"classify",title:"Gene, Allele, or Characteristic?",instruction:"Classify each phrase before the reveal, then write a one-line rule distinguishing an allele from a gene and from the characteristic observed.",items:["a version of a gene","a section of DNA coding for a feature","brown eyes","the B form of an eye-colour gene"],answer:"Allele: a version/form of a gene; gene: a section of DNA coding for a feature; characteristic: brown eyes; the B form is an allele."},
  "BIO-GEN-02":{type:"calculate",title:"Punnett Square, Not Guesswork",instruction:"Draw the four outcomes before the reveal. Circle the one outcome with two recessive alleles.",prompt:"Two heterozygous parents have a child. What is the probability of two recessive alleles?",answer:"Aa × Aa gives AA, Aa, Aa and aa. One of four outcomes is aa, so the probability is 25%."},
  "BIO-EVOL-01":{type:"sequence",title:"Mutation to Characteristic",instruction:"Build the mechanism and mark the condition needed for a mutation to affect the phenotype.",prompt:"Mutation in DNA → altered protein or regulation → changed characteristic → possible selection.",answer:"A mutation can alter a protein or its regulation, producing a new characteristic. Selection increases a useful allele only if it improves survival or reproduction in that environment."},
  "BIO-EVOL-02":{type:"sequence",title:"Natural Selection Changes Frequency",instruction:"Complete the chain and identify why the population, not one individual, changes over generations.",prompt:"Variation → selection pressure → advantageous individuals reproduce more → allele becomes more common.",answer:"Individuals with useful inherited variation reproduce more, so their allele is passed on more often and increases in the population over generations. Individuals do not evolve because they need to."},
  "BIO-DISEASE-01":{type:"correct",title:"Antibiotics Do Not Kill Viruses",instruction:"Repair the claim and name the biological structure or process the drug targets.",prompt:"'Antibiotics are ineffective against viruses because viruses are too small for the medicine to reach.'",answer:"Antibiotics target bacterial structures or processes, such as cell walls or bacterial protein synthesis. Viruses reproduce inside host cells using host machinery, so antibacterial antibiotics do not treat viral infections."},
  "BIO-MED-01":{type:"compare",title:"Control Group: Fair Comparison",instruction:"Identify the role of each group and state what the control allows the researcher to attribute to the drug.",items:["Treatment group receives the new drug","Control group receives placebo or standard care","Both groups receive the new drug"],answer:"The control group provides a comparison without the new treatment. Differences in outcomes can then be attributed more confidently to the drug rather than natural recovery or expectation."},
  "BIO-IMMUNE-01":{type:"sequence",title:"What Vaccination Produces",instruction:"Build the immune response chain and identify the role of memory cells when the pathogen returns.",prompt:"Antigen exposure → lymphocytes respond → antibodies and memory cells → faster secondary response.",answer:"Vaccination exposes the immune system to antigens without causing the full disease. Memory cells remain, so a later infection triggers a faster, larger antibody response."},
  "BIO-MED-02":{type:"apply",title:"Monoclonal Antibodies in Context",instruction:"Commit to one medical use, cite the specific binding rule, and predict what happens if the target antigen changes.",prompt:"A monoclonal antibody is designed to bind a tumour marker present on cancer cells but not healthy cells.",answer:"The antibody binds specifically to the target antigen and can carry a drug or signal to the cancer cell. If the antigen changed or was absent, targeting would be reduced."},
  "BIO-DIFF-01":{type:"sequence",title:"Why Cells Specialise",instruction:"Complete the chain from gene expression to tissue function. Correct the idea that specialised cells contain different genes.",prompt:"Same genome → different genes switched on → different proteins → specialised structure and function.",answer:"Cells usually contain the same genetic information but express different genes, producing different proteins, structures and functions."},
  "BIO-TRANSPORT-01":{type:"compare",title:"Active or Passive Transport?",instruction:"Compare direction and energy requirement before classifying each example.",headers:["Feature","Active transport","Diffusion"],rows:[["Direction","Against concentration gradient","Down concentration gradient"],["Energy","Requires energy","No direct energy input"],["Example","Mineral ions into root hair","Oxygen into a cell"]],answer:"Active transport moves against a concentration gradient using energy. Diffusion is net movement down the gradient without direct energy input."},
  "BIO-ENZYME-01":{type:"correct",title:"Denaturation Changes the Active Site",instruction:"Repair the claim and link temperature or pH to the enzyme-substrate interaction.",prompt:"'When an enzyme is denatured, it stops working because the substrate has disappeared.'",answer:"Denaturation changes the enzyme's three-dimensional shape, including the active site, so the substrate no longer fits effectively. The substrate has not disappeared."},
};
Object.keys(MC_NEXT_HAND_AUTHORED).forEach(function (tag) {
  if (!MC_STARTERS[tag]) MC_STARTERS[tag] = MC_NEXT_HAND_AUTHORED[tag];
});

// Second wave for the remaining high-use A-Level anchors. These are added
// only where the earlier bank has no hand-authored entry for the tag.
var MC_FOLLOWUP_HAND_AUTHORED = {
  "MC-BIO-E1-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Partially Permeable Means Selective",instruction:"Commit to what crosses the membrane, then explain why 'nothing can pass' is wrong.",prompt:"A partially permeable membrane allows some substances through but restricts others according to size, charge or transport mechanism.",answer:"Partially permeable means selective, not completely open or completely sealed. Small suitable molecules may cross; other substances require transport proteins or cannot cross."},
  "MC-BIO-E1-02":{type:"sequence",title:"Mitochondrion to ATP",instruction:"Build the energy chain and replace the phrase 'burning glucose' with the precise process.",prompt:"Glucose and oxygen → aerobic respiration → energy transfer → ATP for cell processes.",answer:"Mitochondria are the main site of aerobic respiration, which transfers energy from glucose to ATP. They do not literally burn glucose; ATP is the usable energy currency."},
  "MC-BIO-E1-03":{type:"distinguish",title:"Peptide Bonds Join Amino Acids",instruction:"Commit to the monomers and identify the bond before the reveal.",prompt:"A peptide bond joins which two biological molecules?",answer:"A peptide bond joins amino acids. Many amino acids joined by peptide bonds form a polypeptide; a glycosidic bond joins sugars and a phosphodiester bond joins nucleotides."},
  "MC-BIO-E1-04":{type:"sequence",title:"Semi-Conservative Replication",instruction:"Complete the replication chain and identify what each daughter DNA molecule conserves.",prompt:"Strands separate → complementary nucleotides pair → two DNA molecules form, each with one original strand.",answer:"Replication is semi-conservative because each daughter DNA molecule contains one original strand and one newly synthesised complementary strand."},
  "MC-BIO-E1-05":{type:"correct",title:"Ribosomes Are Not Mitochondria",instruction:"Repair the organelle-function swap and state where translation occurs.",prompt:"'Ribosomes release energy by aerobic respiration.'",answer:"Ribosomes are the site of translation and protein synthesis. Mitochondria are associated with aerobic respiration and ATP production; confusing the organelles swaps their functions."},
  "MC-BIO-GAP1-01":{type:"correct",title:"Reliability Needs Repeats",instruction:"Choose the change that improves reliability and explain why one result is not enough.",prompt:"Repeat an investigation several times, identify anomalous results and calculate a mean where appropriate.",answer:"Repeats reveal random variation and allow a mean to be calculated. Reliability concerns consistency; it is not the same as validity or simply using a larger measuring instrument."},
  "MC-BIO-GAP1-02":{type:"compare",title:"Control Variables Stay Constant",instruction:"Name the independent variable, dependent variable and one control variable before the reveal.",headers:["Role","Example in photosynthesis test"],rows:[["Independent variable","Light intensity"],["Dependent variable","Rate of photosynthesis"],["Control variable","Temperature or carbon dioxide concentration"]],answer:"A control variable is kept constant so changes in the dependent variable can be attributed to light intensity rather than temperature or carbon dioxide concentration."},
  "MC-BIO-GAP1-03":{type:"calculate",title:"Make Percentage Uncertainty Smaller",instruction:"Commit to the design change and explain why the absolute uncertainty becomes a smaller fraction of the reading.",prompt:"A low percentage uncertainty is generally obtained by measuring a larger quantity or repeating and averaging readings.",answer:"Percentage uncertainty = absolute uncertainty ÷ measured value × 100. Increasing the measured quantity while keeping the instrument uncertainty similar reduces the percentage uncertainty."},

  "MC-CHEM-E1-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"First Ionisation Removes One Electron",instruction:"Commit to the particle removed and state the charge and state of the atom before and after.",prompt:"First ionisation energy is the energy needed to remove one electron from each atom in one mole of gaseous atoms.",answer:"It removes one electron from a gaseous atom, forming a gaseous 1+ ion. It is not the energy to remove a proton or all electrons at once."},
  "MC-CHEM-E1-02":{type:"compare",title:"Isotope: Same Protons, Different Neutrons",instruction:"Complete the particle comparison and explain why isotopes remain the same element.",headers:["Particle","Isotopes of one element"],rows:[["Protons","Same number"],["Neutrons","Different number"],["Electrons in neutral atoms","Usually same number"]],answer:"Isotopes have the same proton number, so they are the same element, but different neutron numbers. Their chemistry is usually similar because chemical behaviour depends mainly on electrons."},
  "MC-CHEM-E1-03":{type:"calculate",title:"Relative Atomic Mass Is Weighted",instruction:"Use abundance as a weight rather than taking a simple average. Show the numerator and denominator.",prompt:"An element has isotopes of masses 10 and 11 with abundances 20% and 80%. Find its relative atomic mass.",answer:"Ar = (10×20 + 11×80) ÷ 100 = 10.8. A simple average would wrongly treat the two isotopes as equally common."},
  "MC-CHEM-E1-04":{type:"calculate",title:"One Mole Is Avogadro's Number",instruction:"Commit to the number and unit before the reveal, then distinguish amount of substance from mass.",prompt:"How many particles are in one mole?",answer:"One mole contains 6.02 × 10²³ particles. A mole measures amount of substance; its mass depends on the substance's molar mass."},
  "MC-CHEM-E1-05":{type:"distinguish",title:"Ratio or Actual Formula?",instruction:"Compare the information each formula preserves and give a paired example.",headers:["Formula","Meaning"],rows:[["Empirical","Simplest whole-number ratio of atoms"],["Molecular","Actual number of each atom in a molecule"]],answer:"The empirical formula gives the simplest ratio, while the molecular formula gives the actual composition. For hydrogen peroxide, HO is empirical and H₂O₂ is molecular."},
  "MC-CHEM-E1-06":{type:"diagram",title:"Coordinate Bond: One Pair Donated",instruction:"Draw an arrow from the electron-pair donor to the acceptor and label the empty orbital or electron deficiency.",prompt:"A coordinate bond forms when one atom supplies both electrons in a shared pair.",answer:"A coordinate bond is a covalent bond in which both bonding electrons come from one atom, such as a lone pair donated to an electron-deficient species."},
  "MC-CHEM-E1-07":{type:"diagram",title:"Polar Bonds Can Cancel",instruction:"Draw the bond dipoles and decide whether the molecular shape makes them cancel.",prompt:"A molecule with polar bonds may be non-polar overall if its bond dipoles cancel because of a symmetrical shape.",answer:"Individual polar bonds have uneven charge distribution, but a symmetrical molecular shape can make the dipoles cancel. Shape matters as well as electronegativity difference."},
  "MC-CHEM-E1-08":{type:"compare",title:"Giant Covalent Means Many Strong Bonds",instruction:"Compare a giant covalent structure with a simple molecule and identify the feature responsible for a high melting point.",headers:["Structure","Particles/bonds","Effect"],rows:[["Giant covalent","Many atoms joined by strong covalent bonds","Many bonds must be broken"],["Simple molecular","Molecules with weak intermolecular forces","Forces between molecules are overcome"]],answer:"Giant covalent structures generally have very high melting points because many strong covalent bonds must be broken, not because they contain especially strong intermolecular forces."},

  "MC-PHYS-E1-01":{type:"graph",title:"Gradient Gives Velocity",instruction:"Draw a displacement-time graph and label the gradient. State what a horizontal section means.",prompt:"The gradient of a displacement-time graph represents velocity.",answer:"Velocity is change in displacement divided by time, so it is the gradient. A horizontal section has zero gradient and therefore zero velocity; a steeper gradient means greater velocity."},
  "MC-PHYS-E1-02":{type:"scenario",title:"Zero Resultant Force",instruction:"Commit to the motion, then state the condition on acceleration rather than assuming the object must be stationary.",prompt:"A resultant force of zero acts on a moving object.",answer:"Zero resultant force means zero acceleration. The object may remain at rest or continue at constant velocity; motion does not require a resultant force once velocity is constant."},
  "MC-PHYS-E1-03":{type:"calculate",title:"Work Needs Displacement Along the Force",instruction:"Decide whether work is done in each case and explain the angle or displacement condition.",items:["A force acts but the object does not move","A force is perpendicular to the displacement","A force acts along the displacement"],answer:"Work done is force × displacement in the force direction. No displacement means no work; a perpendicular force does no work in that direction; a force along the displacement transfers energy."},
  "MC-PHYS-E1-04":{type:"distinguish",title:"Current Is Measured in Amps",instruction:"Match each quantity to its unit and explain why current is not measured in volts.",items:["Current — ampere (A)","Potential difference — volt (V)","Resistance — ohm (Ω)"],answer:"The SI unit of electric current is the ampere. Volts measure energy transferred per unit charge, while ohms measure resistance; the quantities are related but not interchangeable."},
  "MC-PHYS-E1-05":{type:"diagram",title:"Hooke's Law Has a Limit",instruction:"Sketch force against extension and mark the limit of proportionality. Explain what changes beyond it.",prompt:"A material obeys Hooke's law while extension is proportional to force.",answer:"The straight-line section through the origin is the proportional region. Beyond the limit of proportionality, extension is no longer directly proportional to force; permanent deformation may follow the elastic limit."},
  "MC-PHYS-E1-06":{type:"calculate",title:"Half-Life Halves What Remains",instruction:"Complete three half-lives and state why the sample does not reach zero after a fixed number of half-lives.",prompt:"A 160 Bq sample has a half-life of 2 hours. Find its activity after 6 hours.",answer:"Six hours is three half-lives: 160 → 80 → 40 → 20 Bq. Each half-life halves what remains; individual decay is random and the activity approaches zero rather than switching off at one moment."},
  "MC-PHYS-10":{type:"compare",title:"Mass and Weight Are Different",instruction:"Complete the comparison and calculate the weight of a 70 kg object where g = 10 N/kg.",headers:["Quantity","Mass","Weight"],rows:[["Meaning","Amount of matter","Gravitational force"],["Unit","kg","N"],["Equation","—","W = mg"]],answer:"Mass stays 70 kg; weight = 70 × 10 = 700 N. Weight changes with gravitational field strength, while mass does not."},
  "MC-PHYS-13":{type:"sequence",title:"Energy Cannot Vanish",instruction:"Track the energy stores and transfers in the scenario before the reveal.",prompt:"A falling object loses gravitational potential energy and gains kinetic energy; some energy may heat the surroundings through air resistance.",answer:"Energy is transferred between stores, not destroyed. With air resistance, some gravitational potential energy is dissipated as thermal energy, so the kinetic-energy gain is less than the original potential-energy loss."},

  "MC-CS-C1A-01":{type:"sequence",title:"Decomposition Makes a Problem Manageable",instruction:"Break the problem into named sub-problems before the reveal and explain why each can be solved separately.",prompt:"Large problem → smaller tasks → solve and test each task → combine into a solution.",answer:"Decomposition means breaking a complex problem into smaller, manageable sub-problems. It reduces cognitive load and makes coding, testing and debugging more focused."},
  "MC-CS-C1A-02":{type:"correct",title:"Abstraction Keeps the Signal",instruction:"Repair the claim and name one detail that can be hidden without changing the useful model.",prompt:"'Abstraction means deleting important information until the model is incomplete.'",answer:"Abstraction removes or hides unnecessary detail while keeping the features needed for the problem. A map omits every physical object but retains roads and locations relevant to navigation."},
  "MC-CS-C1A-03":{type:"distinguish",title:"Syntax or Meaning?",instruction:"Classify the error and state when a compiler or interpreter is likely to detect it.",items:["Missing bracket","Variable stores the wrong value but the code is valid","Misspelled keyword"],answer:"Syntax errors break the language rules and are usually detected before or during translation. A semantic or logic error can be syntactically valid but produce the wrong meaning or result."},
  "MC-CS-C1A-04":{type:"correct",title:"Semantic Error: Legal but Wrong",instruction:"Commit to whether the code can run, then identify why the output does not match the intended meaning.",prompt:"A program uses + where it should calculate an average using ÷. The code runs but gives the wrong answer.",answer:"This is a semantic or logic error: the syntax is valid, but the operation does not express the intended algorithm. A runtime or syntax error is not required for a program to be wrong."},
  "MC-CS-C1A-05":{type:"classify",title:"Good Test Cases Find Boundaries",instruction:"Create normal, boundary and erroneous test cases for an input that accepts integers from 1 to 100.",items:["Normal case","Boundary cases","Erroneous cases"],answer:"Normal: a typical value such as 50. Boundary: 1 and 100, plus just outside 0 and 101 when testing rejection. Erroneous: text, blank input or a decimal if the specification forbids them."},
  "MC-CS-C1A-06":{type:"calculate",title:"Test the Edge, Not the Middle",instruction:"List the boundary values and the values just outside the permitted range before the reveal.",prompt:"A mark must be an integer from 0 to 100 inclusive. Which values are essential for boundary testing?",answer:"Test 0 and 100 as valid boundaries, and −1 and 101 as just-outside invalid values. Boundary testing targets the edges where errors in comparison operators often appear."},
  "MC-CS-C1A-07":{type:"distinguish",title:"Pre-Condition Before the Algorithm",instruction:"Commit to what must already be true before the subroutine runs, then separate it from the result it promises.",prompt:"A pre-condition states that the input list is sorted before a binary search begins.",answer:"A pre-condition is a condition assumed true before an algorithm or subroutine runs. It is not the output or post-condition; binary search relies on sorted input to be valid."},
  "MC-CS-C1A-08":{type:"distinguish",title:"Post-Condition After the Algorithm",instruction:"State what must be true after execution and explain why it is different from an input assumption.",prompt:"After a successful search, the post-condition says the returned index identifies the target value or reports that it is absent.",answer:"A post-condition describes the state or result guaranteed after the algorithm finishes. It is checked after execution, whereas a pre-condition must hold before execution."},

  "MC-MEDIA-E1-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Sign: Form Plus Meaning",instruction:"Commit to the signifier and signified in each example before the reveal.",prompt:"In a road sign, what counts as the signifier and what is the signified meaning?",answer:"The signifier is the physical form—image, word, sound or colour. The signified is the concept or meaning associated with it. Semiotic analysis connects the two rather than treating an image as meaning itself."},
  "MC-MEDIA-E1-02":{type:"correct",title:"Anchorage Narrows Meaning",instruction:"Repair the claim and explain how a caption or headline guides interpretation of an image.",prompt:"'Anchorage gives an image one fixed meaning, so audiences cannot interpret it differently.'",answer:"Anchorage directs or narrows the preferred meaning of an image, often through a headline or caption, but audiences may still negotiate or oppose that reading."},
  "MC-MEDIA-E1-03":{type:"compare",title:"Denotation Before Connotation",instruction:"Separate what is literally present from the cultural associations it may create.",headers:["Level","Question","Example: red rose"],rows:[["Denotation","What is literally shown?","A red flower"],["Connotation","What ideas or values are associated?","Love, romance or passion"]],answer:"Denotation is the literal description. Connotation is the cultural or emotional meaning attached to it; confusing the two turns interpretation into unsupported assertion."},
  "MC-MEDIA-E1-04":{type:"correct",title:"Stereotype Is a Repeated Representation",instruction:"Repair the claim and identify the feature that makes a representation stereotypical.",prompt:"'A stereotype is any representation of a person in a media text.'",answer:"A stereotype is a simplified, repeated and often exaggerated representation of a social group. A single representation is not automatically a stereotype; repetition and reduction are central."},
  "MC-MEDIA-E1-05":{type:"sequence",title:"Representation Is Constructed",instruction:"Build the chain from selection to audience meaning and identify where ideology can enter.",prompt:"Selection and omission → media codes → representation → audience interpretation.",answer:"Representations are constructed through selection, omission and media codes. They do not simply mirror reality; choices about whose viewpoint is shown and how can reproduce or challenge ideology."},
  "MC-MEDIA-E1-06":{type:"distinguish",title:"Preferred Reading Is Not Guaranteed",instruction:"Classify each audience response and explain which one matches the producer's intended reading.",items:["Accepts the dominant message","Negotiates part of the message","Rejects the dominant message"],answer:"A preferred reading accepts the dominant or intended meaning. A negotiated reading accepts some elements but modifies others; an oppositional reading rejects the preferred interpretation."},
  "MC-MEDIA-E1-07":{type:"apply",title:"Genre Gives the Audience a Contract",instruction:"Apply genre conventions to a new text and predict what happens when the text breaks one convention.",prompt:"A trailer uses a detective, clues and a final reveal. Identify the genre expectations and one effect of subverting them.",answer:"Genre provides recognisable conventions that help audiences anticipate content and tone. Subverting a convention can create surprise, ambiguity or challenge expectations, but the audience needs enough shared convention to recognise the genre."},
  "MC-MEDIA-E1-08":{type:"compare",title:"Uses and Gratifications Are Active",instruction:"Compare passive-effects thinking with audience-needs thinking and apply both to one media choice.",headers:["Model","Audience role","Question asked"],rows:[["Hypodermic needle","Passive recipient","What does media do to people?"],["Uses and gratifications","Active chooser","What do people use media to do?"]],answer:"Uses and gratifications argues that audiences actively choose media to meet needs such as information, identity, social interaction or entertainment. It contrasts with the passive audience assumed by the hypodermic model."},

  "MC-RS-E1-01":{type:"sequence",title:"The Four Noble Truths",instruction:"Put the truths in order and identify the practical response that follows diagnosis of suffering.",prompt:"Dukkha → samudaya → nirodha → magga.",answer:"The truths are suffering, its cause in craving, the possibility of ending it, and the path leading to its end. The sequence moves from diagnosis to cure; it is not simply a list of Buddhist beliefs."},
  "MC-RS-E1-02":{type:"correct",title:"Anatta Is No Permanent Self",instruction:"Repair the claim and distinguish no permanent self from the claim that people do not exist at all.",prompt:"'Anatta teaches that a person has no experiences, thoughts or identity of any kind.'",answer:"Anatta denies a permanent, unchanging self or soul. It does not deny that people experience thoughts and identity; it explains them as changing processes rather than a fixed essence."},
  "MC-RS-E1-03":{type:"distinguish",title:"Ontology Starts With Concept",instruction:"Commit to the type of argument and explain why it is described as a priori.",prompt:"The ontological argument attempts to establish God's existence from the concept or definition of God.",answer:"The ontological argument is a priori: it reasons from the concept of a greatest conceivable being rather than observation of the world. It differs from cosmological and teleological arguments, which begin with empirical features."},
  "MC-RS-E1-04":{type:"compare",title:"The Problem of Evil Challenges Omnipotence",instruction:"Complete the comparison and state the tension between the attributes.",headers:["Attribute","Meaning","Tension with evil"],rows:[["Omnipotent","All-powerful","Could prevent evil"],["Omnibenevolent","All-loving","Would want to prevent evil"],["Omniscient","All-knowing","Knows evil and suffering occur"]],answer:"The problem of evil challenges belief in a God who is simultaneously omnipotent, omnibenevolent and omniscient. A response must explain why evil exists without simply dropping one attribute unnoticed."},
  "MC-RS-E1-05":{type:"compare",title:"Paley's Watch Analogy",instruction:"State the mapping in the analogy, then give one reason Hume thinks the comparison is weak.",headers:["Watch analogy","Universe"],rows:[["Complex order","Complex order"],["Watch has a designer","Paley infers a designer"],["Hume's challenge","Universe may not be sufficiently similar to an artefact"]],answer:"Paley argues that a watch's design implies a designer, so the universe's apparent design implies God. Hume objects that the universe is not known to be like a human-made machine and that the analogy may not justify the conclusion."},
  "MC-RS-E1-06":{type:"distinguish",title:"What Counts as Religious Experience?",instruction:"Classify each report and identify the feature that makes it an experience claim rather than an argument from design.",items:["A feeling of unity with the divine","A vision or revelation","An inference from complexity in nature"],answer:"A religious experience is a personal encounter or perceived awareness of the divine, such as a vision, conversion or mystical unity. An inference from nature is an argument, not an experience itself."},
  "MC-RS-E1-07":{type:"correct",title:"Verification Has a Cost",instruction:"Commit to the strength and limitation before the reveal. Do not confuse meaningful with empirically proven.",prompt:"'The verification principle proves that every religious statement is false.'",answer:"Verificationism claims a statement is meaningful only if analytically true or empirically verifiable. This challenges many religious claims, but it does not straightforwardly prove them false; critics also question whether the principle verifies itself."},
  "MC-RS-E1-08":{type:"compare",title:"Religious Diversity and Truth",instruction:"Compare two possible responses to diversity and state what problem each creates.",headers:["Response","Strength","Problem"],rows:[["Exclusivism","Preserves one tradition's truth claim","Must explain sincere rival faiths"],["Pluralism","Takes multiple traditions seriously","May dilute exclusive truth claims"]],answer:"Religious diversity challenges the assumption that one tradition's interpretation is obviously the only possible truth. A response must address both the evidence of different traditions and the consequences for truth claims."},

  "MC-HSC-E1-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Equality Is Fair Access",instruction:"Commit to the principle and separate equality from treating every person identically regardless of need.",prompt:"Equality in a care setting means people are protected from discrimination and have fair access to support.",answer:"Equality concerns fair treatment and protection from discrimination. Fair access may require reasonable adjustments; identical treatment is not always equitable when people's needs differ."},
  "MC-HSC-E1-02":{type:"scenario",title:"Choice Needs Capacity and Information",instruction:"Apply the principle to the case and identify what the professional must provide before a decision.",prompt:"A service user is given accessible information, time and support before choosing between two care options.",answer:"The person's right to make an informed choice is linked to autonomy and consent. Professionals should provide understandable information and check capacity without making the decision for the individual."},
  "MC-HSC-E1-03":{type:"distinguish",title:"Hazard or Risk?",instruction:"Sort the terms and state the consequence that makes a risk assessment useful.",items:["Wet floor","Likelihood someone slips and severity of harm","A completed accident report"],answer:"A hazard is something with the potential to cause harm, such as a wet floor. Risk considers the likelihood and severity of harm. An accident report records an event after it happens."},
  "MC-HSC-E1-04":{type:"correct",title:"Confidentiality Has Boundaries",instruction:"Repair the absolute claim and identify the safeguarding exception.",prompt:"'Confidential information must never be shared with anyone outside the care team.'",answer:"Information should normally be shared only with appropriate people who need it and with consent where possible. It may need to be shared without consent when safeguarding or serious risk requires it, following policy and law."},
  "MC-HSC-E1-05":{type:"scenario",title:"Person-Centred Means the Person Leads",instruction:"Choose the care response and explain which preference, value or choice it respects.",items:["Follow the individual's preferred routine where safe","Use the organisation's routine for everyone","Ignore a preference because it takes longer"],answer:"Person-centred care reflects the individual's preferences, values and choices rather than organisational convenience. It remains safe and professional but does not treat the person as a task to complete."},
  "MC-HSC-E1-06":{type:"sequence",title:"Responding to Safeguarding",instruction:"Put the response in order and identify why promising secrecy is unsafe.",prompt:"Notice concern → listen and reassure without promising secrecy → record accurately → report through safeguarding procedure.",answer:"A safeguarding concern should be taken seriously, recorded factually and reported to the appropriate person or procedure. A worker should not investigate alone or promise secrecy if another person may be at risk."},
  "MC-HSC-E1-07":{type:"classify",title:"Protected Characteristics",instruction:"Commit to which examples relate to protected characteristics and distinguish them from ordinary preferences.",items:["Age discrimination","Discrimination because of disability","A dislike of a food flavour"],answer:"The Equality Act protects people from discrimination based on protected characteristics such as age and disability. A personal food preference is not automatically a protected characteristic."},
  "MC-HSC-E1-08":{type:"compare",title:"Risk Assessment Finds the Control",instruction:"Complete the chain from hazard to control and explain why the control should be proportionate.",prompt:"Hazard: a wet floor in a care home corridor. Identify risk, who may be harmed and a sensible control.",answer:"Risk is a person slipping and being injured; residents, visitors and staff may be harmed. A proportionate control is to isolate and dry the area, use signage and review why the spill occurred."},

  "MC-BUS-T1-01":{type:"distinguish",title:"Start With the Customer",instruction:"Commit to the starting point and trace the information flow before the reveal.",headers:["Approach","Starts with","Risk"],rows:[["Product-oriented","What the firm can make","Product may not match demand"],["Market-oriented","Customer needs and research","Must keep adapting to customers"]],answer:"A market-oriented firm begins with customer needs and market research, then develops its offer. A product-oriented firm begins with what it can produce and tries to sell it."},
  "MC-BUS-T1-02":{type:"apply",title:"Segmentation Makes the Offer Specific",instruction:"Apply segmentation to a new product and state the benefit for targeting and promotion.",prompt:"A sportswear firm targets school athletes aged 14–18 rather than everyone. What benefit does segmentation create?",answer:"Segmentation divides a broad market into groups with shared characteristics. Targeting a defined group helps the firm tailor product, price and promotion, making marketing more relevant and reducing wasted spending."},
  "MC-BUS-T1-03":{type:"distinguish",title:"Brand Is More Than a Logo",instruction:"Sort the elements and explain how they combine to create a brand identity.",items:["Name and visual identity","Customer associations and reputation","One temporary discount"],answer:"A brand is the identity and set of associations that distinguish an offering, including name, design, reputation and customer expectations. A discount is a promotion, not the whole brand."},
  "MC-BUS-T1-04":{type:"compare",title:"Primary or Secondary Research?",instruction:"Classify each source and identify the trade-off between relevance and cost.",items:["A survey designed and run by the firm","A government industry report","An online questionnaire written for this launch"],answer:"The firm's survey and its launch questionnaire are primary research: new data collected for the purpose. A government report is secondary research. Primary data can be more relevant but usually takes more time and money."},
  "MC-BUS-T1-05":{type:"scenario",title:"What a Focus Group Reveals",instruction:"Commit to the kind of evidence a focus group can provide and explain why it cannot estimate the whole market reliably.",prompt:"A firm shows three package designs to eight potential customers and asks them to discuss their reactions.",answer:"A focus group explores opinions, language, attitudes and reactions in depth. It gives qualitative insight but a small group is not statistically representative of the whole market."},
  "MC-BUS-T1-06":{type:"calculate",title:"Elastic Demand and Revenue",instruction:"Predict the revenue effect before the reveal and state the condition that makes a price cut worthwhile.",prompt:"Demand is price elastic. A business cuts price by 10%. What is likely to happen to quantity demanded and total revenue?",answer:"Quantity demanded rises by a larger percentage than the price falls, so total revenue is likely to increase. This relies on demand being elastic; with inelastic demand, revenue would likely fall."},
  "MC-BUS-T1-07":{type:"distinguish",title:"Penetration Pricing Buys Adoption",instruction:"Commit to the objective and identify the market condition that makes a low launch price useful.",prompt:"A new streaming service launches at a low price to attract customers quickly, then plans to build market share.",answer:"Penetration pricing sets a low initial price to gain market share and encourage adoption, often where competition is strong or network effects matter. It is not the same as skimming, which begins high."},
  "MC-BUS-T1-08":{type:"sequence",title:"How Distribution Reaches the Customer",instruction:"Build the channel from producer to consumer and identify where an intermediary changes the route.",prompt:"Producer → wholesaler or retailer → consumer. Compare with a direct online sale.",answer:"A distribution channel is the route a product takes from producer to customer. Intermediaries may provide storage, reach and convenience; direct online sales remove them but leave the firm with more fulfilment responsibility."},
  "MC-BUS-T1-09":{type:"distinguish",title:"USP Gives a Reason to Choose",instruction:"Commit to the feature that makes the product distinctive and separate it from a generic quality claim.",prompt:"A product's unique selling point is a feature or benefit that differentiates it from competitors.",answer:"A USP gives customers a specific reason to choose the product over alternatives. 'Good quality' is too general unless the business can show what makes that quality unique and valued."},
  "MC-BUS-T1-10":{type:"evaluate",title:"E-Commerce: Reach Against Capability",instruction:"Commit to a judgement and weigh one benefit against one operational condition for a small firm.",prompt:"Evaluate e-commerce as an advantage for a small firm.",answer:"E-commerce can widen market reach and operate beyond shop hours with lower premises costs. The benefit depends on digital marketing, fulfilment, delivery reliability and the firm's ability to compete for online attention."},
  "MC-BIO-09":{type:"correct",title:"Osmosis Is Water Movement",instruction:"Repair the definition and identify the membrane and concentration conditions.",prompt:"'Osmosis is the movement of water from low to high concentration.'",answer:"Osmosis is the net movement of water through a partially permeable membrane from higher water potential to lower water potential. Saying simply 'low to high concentration' is incomplete and can reverse the water gradient."},
  "MC-BIO-10":{type:"compare",title:"Active or Facilitated?",instruction:"Compare direction, energy and membrane proteins before classifying the transport process.",headers:["Feature","Active transport","Facilitated diffusion"],rows:[["Gradient","Against","Down"],["Energy","Required","No direct ATP"],["Proteins","Carrier or pump","Channel or carrier"]],answer:"Active transport uses energy to move substances against a concentration gradient. Facilitated diffusion uses membrane proteins but moves down the gradient without direct energy input."},
  "MC-CHEM-09":{type:"correct",title:"Boiling Does Not Break Covalent Bonds",instruction:"Repair the explanation and identify which forces are overcome when water boils.",prompt:"'Water has a high boiling point because the O–H covalent bonds are very strong.'",answer:"Boiling water overcomes intermolecular hydrogen bonds between molecules, not the covalent O–H bonds inside each molecule. Stronger intermolecular forces require more energy to separate molecules."},
  "MC-PHYS-09":{type:"compare",title:"At the Top: Velocity or Acceleration?",instruction:"Commit to both quantities before the reveal and explain why zero velocity does not mean zero acceleration.",headers:["Quantity at highest point","Value","Reason"],rows:[["Velocity","0 m/s","The object changes direction"],["Acceleration","g downward","Gravity still acts"]],answer:"At the highest point velocity is momentarily zero, but acceleration remains g downward. Acceleration describes the change in velocity, so the object immediately begins moving downward."},
  "MC-MEDIA-E1-09":{type:"apply",title:"Close-Up: What Becomes Important?",instruction:"Apply the shot size to a new media example and identify the meaning it can construct.",prompt:"A close-up isolates a character's face immediately before they reveal a secret.",answer:"A close-up emphasises facial expression, emotion and reaction by making the face the dominant visual information. Its meaning depends on context; it can create intimacy, tension or scrutiny rather than having one fixed effect."},
  "MC-MEDIA-E1-10":{type:"sequence",title:"Non-Linear Narrative Reorders Time",instruction:"Put the audience's experience in order and explain how an anachrony changes information.",prompt:"A film shows the consequence first, then returns to the earlier event that caused it.",answer:"A non-linear narrative presents events out of chronological order. The reordering can create mystery, delay explanation, reveal a later consequence early or make the audience reconstruct causation."},
};
Object.keys(MC_FOLLOWUP_HAND_AUTHORED).forEach(function (tag) {
  if (!MC_STARTERS[tag]) MC_STARTERS[tag] = MC_FOLLOWUP_HAND_AUTHORED[tag];
});

var MC_FINAL_HAND_AUTHORED = {
  // A-Level Geography anchors.
  "MC-TEC-09":{type:"apply",title:"Hazard Impact Is Unequal",instruction:"Commit to a judgement, then name one physical factor and one vulnerability factor.",prompt:"Why are some tectonic hazards more destructive in low-income countries?",answer:"Impact depends on hazard characteristics and vulnerability: building quality, preparedness, emergency response, population density and governance can turn a similar physical event into a much larger disaster."},
  "MC-TEC-10":{type:"distinguish",title:"Vulnerability Is Exposure Plus Capacity",instruction:"Separate the hazard from the vulnerability evidence before the reveal.",prompt:"Identify what vulnerability focuses on in hazard risk.",answer:"Vulnerability concerns how likely people or infrastructure are to be harmed and how able they are to cope and recover. It is not simply the magnitude of the physical hazard."},
  "MC-TEC-11":{type:"sequence",title:"Destructive Margin Mechanism",instruction:"Complete the plate-motion chain and label the crust that is destroyed.",prompt:"Oceanic plate converges → denser plate subducts → melting and magma rise → earthquakes and volcanoes.",answer:"At a destructive boundary, an oceanic plate is forced beneath another plate because it is denser. Subduction generates earthquakes and magma, and oceanic crust is recycled into the mantle."},
  "MC-TEC-12":{type:"sequence",title:"Why Tsunami Waves Grow",instruction:"Build the offshore-to-coast chain and identify why the wave becomes dangerous near shore.",prompt:"Seafloor displacement → long wavelength wave → shallow water → slower speed and greater wave height.",answer:"A tsunami displaces a large volume of water. As it reaches shallow coastal water, it slows and its energy is compressed into a higher, more damaging wave; it is not simply a normal wind wave."},
  "MC-TEC-13":{type:"calculate",title:"Return Period Is Not a Date",instruction:"Translate the return period into probability and correct the idea that the event is due exactly on schedule.",prompt:"A flood has a 100-year return period. What annual probability does that imply, and what does it not guarantee?",answer:"A 100-year return period implies about a 1% annual probability, not one flood exactly every 100 years. Events can cluster or fail to occur for much longer because probability resets each year."},
  "MC-TEC-14":{type:"apply",title:"Ash Disrupts More Than Breathing",instruction:"Apply the hazard to two systems and identify one indirect impact on infrastructure.",prompt:"How can volcanic ash affect people and infrastructure?",answer:"Ash can damage lungs and contaminate water, while its weight can collapse roofs and its particles can damage aircraft engines. The disruption can spread far beyond the eruption site through transport and supply networks."},
  "MC-TEC-15":{type:"evaluate",title:"Hazard Management Is Governance",instruction:"Commit to a judgement and weigh physical protection against the quality of decisions, funding and enforcement.",prompt:"Why is hazard management often described as a governance issue?",answer:"Governance determines whether risk is mapped, warnings reach people, building codes are enforced and resources are distributed. A seawall or monitoring system cannot reduce risk reliably if institutions are weak or exclusionary."},
  "MC-TEC-16":{type:"apply",title:"Volcanic Soil: Risk and Resource",instruction:"State the economic benefit and one condition that limits it.",prompt:"What is a possible economic benefit of volcanic soils?",answer:"Weathered volcanic ash can create fertile soils that support productive agriculture and livelihoods. The benefit depends on climate, farming practice and hazard frequency; eruptions can also destroy crops and infrastructure."},
  "MC-TEC-17":{type:"compare",title:"Magnitude Against Impact",instruction:"Compare the physical size of an event with its human impact and give one reason they may diverge.",prompt:"A smaller earthquake causes more deaths than a larger one. Is that possible?",answer:"Yes. Magnitude measures physical energy released, while impact depends on exposure and vulnerability such as population density, building quality, time of day and emergency response."},
  "MC-TEC-18":{type:"diagram",title:"Risk Is Not the Same as Hazard",instruction:"Draw the relationship between hazard, exposure, vulnerability and capacity before the reveal.",prompt:"Hazard + exposed population + vulnerability − capacity → disaster risk.",answer:"Risk rises when a hazardous event affects exposed people who are vulnerable and lack capacity to cope. Reducing exposure, vulnerability or improving capacity can reduce risk even when the hazard itself cannot be removed."},
  "MC-TEC-19":{type:"sequence",title:"Forecast to Recovery",instruction:"Put the management stages in order and explain what changes after the event.",prompt:"Monitoring and prediction → preparation → response → recovery and adaptation.",answer:"Hazard management includes monitoring or prediction, preparation before the event, emergency response during and immediately after it, and recovery that should reduce future vulnerability rather than simply restore the previous risk."},
  "MC-TEC-20":{type:"evaluate",title:"Protection Has Trade-Offs",instruction:"Commit to a judgement and include one beneficiary, one displaced risk and one condition for success.",prompt:"Evaluate the claim that building a seawall solves coastal hazard risk.",answer:"A seawall can protect a valuable frontage, but it may transfer erosion downdrift, be expensive to maintain and encourage development behind the defence. It manages risk for a place under particular conditions; it does not remove the coastal system's energy."},
  "MC-TEC-21":{type:"compare",title:"Prediction Versus Preparation",instruction:"Classify each action and explain why a prediction is not a guarantee.",items:["Forecasting a likely eruption","Practising an evacuation route","Building an emergency shelter"],answer:"Forecasting is prediction; evacuation practice and shelters are preparation. A forecast estimates likelihood and timing but cannot guarantee the event, so preparation remains necessary."},
  "MC-TEC-22":{type:"apply",title:"Risk Changes With Development",instruction:"Apply the concept to two countries and identify the development mechanism, not just the income label.",prompt:"Explain why two countries facing the same storm can experience different losses.",answer:"Differences in planning, housing quality, warning systems, healthcare, insurance and recovery finance affect vulnerability and capacity. Development matters through these mechanisms, not as a label that automatically predicts the outcome."},
  "MC-TEC-23":{type:"correct",title:"Climate Does Not Cause Every Hazard",instruction:"Repair the claim and separate the physical trigger from the conditions that shape its impact.",prompt:"'A hazard becomes a disaster because nature is powerful, so human decisions are irrelevant.'",answer:"Human decisions shape exposure and vulnerability: where people build, how structures are designed, whether warnings work and how resources are shared. Physical power matters, but disaster risk is co-produced by environmental and social conditions."},

  // A-Level Maths anchors.
  "MC-MATH-03":{type:"calculate",title:"Differentiate Term by Term",instruction:"Differentiate each term and keep the constant term visible until the end.",prompt:"Differentiate f(x) = 5x³ − 4x² + 7x − 2.",answer:"f'(x) = 15x² − 8x + 7. The constant −2 differentiates to zero; the power rule multiplies by the power and reduces it by one."},
  "MC-MATH-02":{type:"calculate",title:"Integrate and Add the Constant",instruction:"Increase each power, divide by the new power, and add +C. Differentiate your result to check.",prompt:"Find ∫(6x² + 4x − 3) dx.",answer:"The integral is 2x³ + 2x² − 3x + C. Differentiating returns 6x² + 4x − 3; omitting +C loses the family of possible antiderivatives."},
  "MC-MATH-04":{type:"sequence",title:"Complete the Square",instruction:"Take half the x coefficient, square it, add and subtract it, then write the bracketed form.",prompt:"Complete the square for x² + 6x + 11.",answer:"x² + 6x + 11 = (x + 3)² + 2. The turning point is (−3, 2); the common error is using +6 inside the bracket instead of half the coefficient."},
  "MC-MATH-05":{type:"correct",title:"Stationary Point Is Not Automatically a Root",instruction:"Repair the student's method and name the derivative equation needed for a turning point.",prompt:"A student sets f(x) = 0 to find turning points of f(x) = x³ − 3x² − 9x + 5.",answer:"Turning points occur where f'(x) = 0, not necessarily where f(x) = 0. Differentiate first: f'(x)=3x²−6x−9, then solve for stationary x-values and use f(x) to find their coordinates."},
  "MC-MATH-06":{type:"correct",title:"Logarithms Do Not Split Sums",instruction:"Commit to whether the identity is valid, then state the product rule that is actually allowed.",prompt:"A student writes log(a + b) = log(a) + log(b).",answer:"The identity is false. log(ab)=log(a)+log(b), but a sum inside a logarithm cannot be split this way. The error changes addition into multiplication."},
  "MC-MATH-07":{type:"calculate",title:"Binomial Coefficients Choose Terms",instruction:"Identify the term number and coefficient before substituting. State which combination produces x³.",prompt:"Find the coefficient of x³ in (1 + x)⁵.",answer:"The x³ term is ⁵C₃x³, so the coefficient is 10. The power of x determines the lower index; it is not simply 5³."},
  "MC-MATH-08":{type:"correct",title:"Contradiction Assumes the Opposite",instruction:"Repair the opening and explain why assuming the desired conclusion makes the proof circular.",prompt:"A proof by contradiction starts: 'Assume √2 is irrational.'",answer:"To prove √2 is irrational by contradiction, assume √2 is rational, write it as a fraction in lowest terms, and derive an impossibility. Assuming irrationality is assuming the conclusion, not its negation."},
  "MC-MATH-10":{type:"correct",title:"The Integral Needs the Power Rule",instruction:"Find the missing factor and add the constant. Differentiate the proposed answer to expose the error.",prompt:"A student writes ∫(3x² + 2) dx = x³ + 2x.",answer:"The correct integral is x³ + 2x + C. The student's expression is missing +C; differentiating x³+2x gives 3x²+2, so the main mathematical form is right but the family of antiderivatives is incomplete."},
  "MC-MATH-13":{type:"diagram",title:"Turning Point From Vertex Form",instruction:"Complete the square, plot the vertex and decide whether it is a maximum or minimum.",prompt:"Complete the square for y = x² − 6x + 11 and state the turning point.",answer:"y=(x−3)²+2, so the turning point is (3,2). The coefficient of the squared bracket is positive, so it is a minimum."},
  "MC-MATH-16":{type:"correct",title:"Quotient Rule Has Two Terms",instruction:"Write the quotient rule before differentiating and identify what the student's numerator omitted.",prompt:"Differentiate y = (3x² + 1)/x. A student differentiates the numerator and divides by the derivative of x.",answer:"The quotient rule is (v du/dx − u dv/dx)/v². Alternatively rewrite y=3x+x⁻¹, giving dy/dx=3−x⁻². Differentiating numerator and denominator separately is not valid."},
  "MC-STAT-03":{type:"calculate",title:"Binomial Probability Is Cumulative",instruction:"Choose the correct cumulative probability expression before calculating and distinguish ≤ from =.",prompt:"X ~ B(20,0.3). How would you find P(X ≤ 5)?",answer:"Use the binomial cumulative probability P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=5), or the calculator's cumulative function. P(X=5) alone excludes all smaller outcomes."},
  "MC-STAT-06":{type:"graph",title:"Gradient Predicts Change",instruction:"Interpret the gradient in context and state what a one-unit increase in x does to the predicted y.",prompt:"A regression line is ŷ = 3 + 2.5x. What does gradient 2.5 represent?",answer:"For each one-unit increase in x, predicted y increases by 2.5 on average. It is an association-based prediction, not proof that changing x causes y to rise."},
  "MC-MATH-09":{type:"correct",title:"Stationary Does Not Mean Minimum",instruction:"Use the sign of the gradient on both sides of x=2 before classifying the point.",prompt:"A student finds dy/dx=0 at x=2 and concludes it is a minimum because the function was decreasing before it.",answer:"A stationary point can be a maximum, minimum or stationary inflection. A minimum requires the gradient to change from negative to positive; decreasing before x=2 alone is not enough."},

  // Language subjects and PE: the remaining anchor gaps.
  "MC-PE-09":{type:"distinguish",title:"ATP-PC Is Immediate Power",instruction:"Match the event to the dominant system and state the approximate duration before fatigue limits it.",prompt:"A sprinter produces ATP primarily through the phosphocreatine system in the first 10 seconds.",answer:"The ATP-PC system supplies immediate, high-power ATP for roughly the first 0–10 seconds. It does not dominate a marathon or provide the main energy source for prolonged work."},
  "MC-PE-11":{type:"apply",title:"Progressive Overload Must Progress",instruction:"Commit to the training change and explain why repeating the same load eventually stops producing adaptation.",prompt:"A performer gradually increases training load while preserving safe technique and recovery.",answer:"Progressive overload increases the training demand beyond the current level so the body adapts. It can change frequency, intensity, duration or volume; an unsafe sudden jump is not effective progression."},
  "MC-PE-13":{type:"diagram",title:"Levers Change Force and Speed",instruction:"Draw the biceps lever and label fulcrum, effort and load before deciding its mechanical advantage.",prompt:"Use the elbow and forearm to explain the lever in a biceps curl.",answer:"The elbow is the fulcrum, the biceps supplies effort and the forearm or hand carries the load. A third-class lever favours movement speed and range rather than force multiplication."},
  "MC-PE-15":{type:"distinguish",title:"Angular Momentum Is Rotational",instruction:"Separate linear and angular motion and identify which body action changes rotational momentum.",prompt:"Define angular momentum in sport and give one example.",answer:"Angular momentum is rotational momentum, related to moment of inertia and angular velocity. A diver changes it by altering body shape and distribution of mass, not simply by running faster in a straight line."},
  "MC-PE-10":{type:"sequence",title:"Lactic System and Lactate",instruction:"Build the energy pathway and correct the idea that lactate is simply a poisonous waste product.",prompt:"Glucose → anaerobic glycolysis → ATP and lactate-related fatigue response.",answer:"Anaerobic glycolysis breaks glucose down rapidly to produce ATP when oxygen delivery cannot meet demand. Lactate can be transported and metabolised; fatigue is not explained by calling it a simple poison."},
  "MC-PE-12":{type:"apply",title:"Newton's Third Law in Sport",instruction:"Name the action-reaction pair and place the two forces on different objects.",prompt:"A sprinter pushes backwards on the track. What is the reaction force?",answer:"The sprinter pushes backwards on the track; the track pushes forwards on the sprinter with an equal and opposite force. The forces act on different objects, so they do not cancel on the sprinter."},
  "MC-PE-14":{type:"apply",title:"Drag Depends on More Than Speed",instruction:"Commit to the factor with the greatest effect in the scenario and state one way an athlete can reduce drag.",prompt:"A cyclist increases speed and changes body position. Explain which change can greatly alter drag.",answer:"Drag rises strongly with speed and depends on frontal area, shape and air density. A streamlined, tucked position reduces frontal area and turbulence; the effect is not determined by body mass alone."},

  "MC-ENG-09":{type:"apply",title:"AO3 Must Change the Reading",instruction:"Commit to whether the context is relevant, then connect it to the writer's choices rather than adding background facts.",prompt:"Macbeth was written after the Gunpowder Plot when James I feared witchcraft. Apply this context to a reading of the play.",answer:"Useful AO3 explains how Jacobean anxieties about treason, kingship or witchcraft shape the significance of the text. A fact dump earns little; context must alter or deepen the interpretation of the writer's choices."},
  "MC-ENG-11":{type:"correct",title:"Quote, Analyse, Do Not Paraphrase",instruction:"Upgrade the sentence after the quotation by naming the precise image and its effect on the reader.",prompt:"'The bell jar' means Esther feels trapped. Improve this analysis.",answer:"The metaphor's glass enclosure suggests an apparently transparent but sealed space: Esther can see the world yet cannot breathe or participate freely. Analysis moves beyond repeating 'trapped' by examining image, connotations and effect."},
  "MC-ENG-13":{type:"compare",title:"AO4 Makes the Connection Specific",instruction:"Rewrite the comparison so both texts are linked through a shared idea and a meaningful difference.",prompt:"Weak link: 'Similarly, Text B also shows this.'",answer:"A stronger comparison names the shared method or idea and then differentiates it, for example: both writers present isolation through restricted dialogue, but Text A makes silence oppressive while Text B makes it protective. AO4 is a relationship, not alternating summaries."},
  "MC-ENG-15":{type:"correct",title:"Unseen Analysis Starts With Meaning",instruction:"Repair the technique-catalogue opening and make the first sentence answer the question about the writer's effect.",prompt:"'This extract uses simile, metaphor, adjectives and repetition.'",answer:"Begin with an interpretive claim about the writer's construction of the moment, then select a precise quotation and analyse how the method creates that meaning. Listing techniques without an argument is not analysis."},
  "MC-ENG-10":{type:"correct",title:"AO1 Needs an Argument",instruction:"Turn the plot summary into a claim about significance, then support it with a short quotation and analysis.",prompt:"'Gatsby throws lots of parties. He is trying to impress Daisy. This shows he loves her.'",answer:"The response needs a conceptual argument, such as Gatsby's extravagant parties construct love as performance and display, while the chosen detail reveals his attempt to manufacture access to Daisy. Plot summary alone does not explain significance."},
  "MC-ENG-12":{type:"evaluate",title:"An Opening Needs a Line of Argument",instruction:"Commit to one interpretation of an opening and connect structure, language and reader effect before the reveal.",prompt:"A student writes detailed technique analysis but never explains why the opening matters to the whole text.",answer:"A strong response explains how the opening establishes viewpoint, conflict, setting or expectations that later developments confirm or challenge. Accurate analysis without a whole-text argument plateaus because significance is missing."},
  "MC-ENG-14":{type:"correct",title:"AO1, AO2 and AO3 Must Work Together",instruction:"Repair the essay plan by assigning each note to an assessment objective and then linking them in one sentence.",items:["Accurate quotation and idea","Analysis of a word or structure","Relevant contextual interpretation"],answer:"AO1 communicates a clear argument and references the text; AO2 analyses language, form or structure; AO3 uses relevant context to illuminate interpretation. High-level writing connects them instead of writing three isolated paragraphs."},

  "MC-DE-09":{type:"correct",title:"Dative After Mit",instruction:"Commit to the case and identify the preposition that controls it.",prompt:"Ich fahre mit meinem Bruder in die Stadt. Why is it meinem Bruder?",answer:"Mit takes the dative. Masculine der Bruder becomes dem Bruder; the possessive mein becomes meinem. The accusative meinen would be wrong after mit."},
  "MC-DE-11":{type:"calculate",title:"Relative Pronoun Case",instruction:"Find the role of the relative pronoun inside the relative clause, not the gender of the noun alone.",prompt:"Der Mann, ___ ich gestern geholfen habe, ist mein Nachbar.",answer:"The correct form is dem: helfen takes the dative, and Mann is masculine singular. The relative pronoun's case comes from its function in the clause."},
  "MC-DE-13":{type:"compare",title:"Passive State or Passive Process",instruction:"Compare the two sentences and identify whether the emphasis is on an action or a resulting state.",headers:["Form","Meaning"],rows:[["Die Brücke wird gebaut","The bridge is being built; process/action"],["Die Brücke ist gebaut","The bridge is built; resulting state"]],answer:"werden + past participle describes the passive process. sein + past participle describes the resulting state; confusing them changes the time and focus."},
  "MC-DE-15":{type:"rewrite",title:"In Order To: Um … Zu",instruction:"Rewrite the sentence with the correct infinitive construction and preserve the subject relationship.",prompt:"She works hard in order to pass the exam.",answer:"Sie arbeitet hart, um die Prüfung zu bestehen. Um … zu expresses purpose when the subject is the same; a subordinate clause with damit is used when the subjects differ."},
  "MC-DE-COV-01":{type:"classify",title:"Natur und Umwelt",instruction:"Sort the issue into the correct course theme and add one precise German noun or phrase.",items:["Klimawandel","Arbeitslosigkeit","Jugendkultur"],answer:"Klimawandel belongs to Natur und Umwelt. Arbeitslosigkeit belongs to Die Welt der Arbeit; Jugendkultur belongs to a social or cultural theme. The theme label must match the evidence."},
  "MC-DE-COV-02":{type:"distinguish",title:"Die Welt der Arbeit",instruction:"Commit to the theme's focus and distinguish employment vocabulary from environmental vocabulary.",prompt:"What does Die Welt der Arbeit focus on?",answer:"It covers work, employment, unemployment, training, careers and workplace conditions. It is not simply a general theme about the world; the evidence should relate to labour and working life."},
  "MC-DE-COV-03":{type:"apply",title:"Music Shows Cultural Change",instruction:"Apply one musical example to a claim about cultural change and name the evidence that supports it.",prompt:"Explain how music can provide evidence of cultural change in German-speaking societies.",answer:"Lyrics, genres, performers and audience practices can reveal changing identities, political concerns, technology and youth culture. The answer needs a specific example and mechanism, not the claim that music 'reflects society' alone."},
  "MC-DE-COV-04":{type:"evaluate",title:"Integration Has Two Sides",instruction:"Commit to a judgement and weigh a policy or social challenge against evidence of participation and inclusion.",prompt:"Evaluate one challenge connected with immigration and integration in Germany.",answer:"Challenges may include language access, discrimination, employment and social cohesion, while successful integration can be shown through education, work and civic participation. A balanced judgement must distinguish policy barriers from stereotypes about migrants."},

  "MC-FR-08":{type:"evaluate",title:"Identify Attitude, Then Prove It",instruction:"Commit to the writer's attitude and underline two linguistic clues before the reveal.",prompt:"A reading question asks for the writer's attitude. What makes a top-band response?",answer:"A top-band response names a precise attitude, supports it with two relevant quotations or linguistic features and explains how those choices convey the attitude. 'Positive' or 'negative' alone is too vague."},
  "MC-FR-09":{type:"correct",title:"Y Goes Before a Place",instruction:"Complete the pronoun and explain what it replaces.",prompt:"Tu vas souvent en France? — Oui, j'___ vais chaque été.",answer:"The answer is y: Oui, j'y vais chaque été. Y replaces a place introduced by à or a related location phrase; it is not the direct-object pronoun le or la."},
  "MC-FR-11":{type:"sequence",title:"Two Object Pronouns",instruction:"Put the pronouns in the French order before the verb and identify the direct and indirect objects.",prompt:"He gives it (the book) to me.",answer:"Il me le donne. Me is the indirect object 'to me' and le is the direct object 'it'; both pronouns come before the conjugated verb in this structure."},
  "MC-FR-13":{type:"correct",title:"Still Living: Depuis + Present",instruction:"Repair the tense and explain why French uses the present for an action that began in the past and continues.",prompt:"I have been living in Lyon for three years: J'ai habité à Lyon depuis trois ans.",answer:"J'habite à Lyon depuis trois ans is correct for an action that began three years ago and continues now. French commonly uses the present with depuis; the perfect implies a completed past stay."},
  "MC-FR-15":{type:"rewrite",title:"After Finishing: Après Avoir",instruction:"Rewrite the sentence with the past infinitive and check that the subject remains the same.",prompt:"After finishing his homework, he went out.",answer:"Après avoir fini ses devoirs, il est sorti. Après avoir + past participle expresses a completed action before the main verb when the subject is the same."},
  "MC-FR-COV-01":{type:"classify",title:"Le Monde du Travail",instruction:"Sort the vocabulary into work theme and non-work theme, then use one item in a sentence.",items:["le chômage","un entretien d'embauche","la biodiversité"],answer:"Le chômage and un entretien d'embauche belong to Le monde du travail. La biodiversité belongs to the environment theme; accurate theme classification helps keep examples relevant."},
  "MC-FR-COV-02":{type:"apply",title:"Festivals Carry Values",instruction:"Apply one festival or tradition to a claim about community identity and name what evidence would support it.",prompt:"What can festivals and traditions reveal about a French-speaking community?",answer:"They can reveal history, religion, regional identity, language, food and changing attitudes. A strong answer uses a specific festival and explains what its practices or public debate show about the community."},
  "MC-FR-COV-03":{type:"evaluate",title:"Immigration: Weigh the Evidence",instruction:"Commit to one positive impact and one condition or challenge before the reveal.",prompt:"Evaluate one possible positive impact of immigration on French society.",answer:"Immigration can add skills, entrepreneurship, cultural exchange and demographic support. The impact depends on access to work, education and social inclusion; a balanced answer avoids treating immigration as automatically positive or negative."},
  "MC-FR-COV-04":{type:"sequence",title:"Occupied France Needs Context",instruction:"Build the historical chain from occupation to lived experience and identify which evidence would support the claim.",prompt:"Occupation → collaboration/resistance → daily restrictions and choices → liberation and memory.",answer:"Study of occupied France requires political control, collaboration, resistance, daily life, persecution and the later memory of the period. A response should connect specific evidence to the historical question rather than list events."},

  "MC-SP-06":{type:"rewrite",title:"Spanish Passive Voice",instruction:"Rewrite the active sentence using the formal passive structure and identify the agent if needed.",prompt:"La empresa construyó el puente. Express: The bridge was built by the company.",answer:"El puente fue construido por la empresa. Spanish also commonly uses se constructions; choose the form that fits the focus and register rather than translating English word-for-word."},
  "MC-SP-07":{type:"correct",title:"Pedí Que + Subjunctive",instruction:"Repair the verb and explain why the subordinate clause requires the subjunctive.",prompt:"I asked her to come: Le pedí que venía.",answer:"Le pedí que viniera is correct: pedir que expresses a request or influence, so the subordinate verb uses the imperfect subjunctive to match the past main verb. Venía is indicative and changes the meaning."},
  "MC-SP-08":{type:"sequence",title:"The More…, the Better…",instruction:"Complete both parts of the proportional construction before the reveal.",prompt:"The more you study, the better you will do.",answer:"Cuanto más estudies, mejor te irá. Cuanto más + subjunctive introduces the increasing condition, followed by the comparative result; translating each English word separately often breaks the structure."},
  "MC-SP-09":{type:"classify",title:"Four A-Level Spanish Themes",instruction:"Name the four themes and sort one example into each before the reveal.",items:["Evolución de la sociedad española","Cultura política y artística","Inmigración y sociedad","La dictadura franquista"],answer:"The four themes are Evolución de la sociedad española, Cultura política y artística, Inmigración y sociedad, and La dictadura franquista. Theme labels should guide the example, not replace it."},
  "MC-SP-11":{type:"apply",title:"Volver: Film Evidence",instruction:"Commit to one interpretation and support it with a film technique and a moment, not just plot summary.",prompt:"Use Volver to discuss how family or memory is represented.",answer:"A strong response links a specific scene, performance, colour, camera choice or motif to an interpretation of family or memory. Plot summary says what happens; film analysis explains how Almodóvar constructs meaning."},
  "MC-SP-13":{type:"sequence",title:"Third Conditional in Spanish",instruction:"Complete the two-part chain and keep the condition separate from the result.",prompt:"If the government had invested more in renewable energy, we would have reduced emissions.",answer:"Si el gobierno hubiera invertido más en energías renovables, habríamos reducido las emisiones. Si + pluperfect subjunctive takes the conditional perfect in the result; mixing the tenses changes the conditional type."},
  "MC-SP-15":{type:"compare",title:"Spanish Political System",instruction:"Complete the comparison and state who forms the national government.",headers:["Institution","Role"],rows:[["Monarch","Head of state with mainly ceremonial functions"],["Cortes Generales","Parliament: Congreso and Senado"],["Government","Executive led by the Prime Minister"]],answer:"Spain is a parliamentary constitutional monarchy. The monarch is head of state, while the elected parliament and government exercise political authority; treating the monarch as the sole political decision-maker is inaccurate."},
  "MC-SP-COV-01":{type:"classify",title:"Family Structure Is Changing",instruction:"Classify each example as evidence of change in family structure and explain the social trend it represents.",items:["More single-person households","Greater recognition of same-sex families","A fixed family model unchanged since the past"],answer:"Single-person households and recognition of same-sex families show changing family structure. The third statement denies change and cannot serve as evidence; a strong answer explains demographic or social causes."},
  "MC-SP-COV-02":{type:"apply",title:"Festivals and the Spanish-Speaking World",instruction:"Apply a named festival to identity, history or regional difference and give one concrete cultural detail.",prompt:"What can festivals and traditions show about the Spanish-speaking world?",answer:"They can show regional identity, religion, history, language and changing social values. Use a named event and a specific practice; saying only that festivals are 'important' is not evidence."},
  "MC-SP-COV-03":{type:"evaluate",title:"Immigration and Integration",instruction:"Commit to one challenge and one policy response before the reveal.",prompt:"Evaluate an issue connected with immigration and integration in Spain.",answer:"Challenges may include language access, employment, discrimination and social cohesion. A balanced evaluation weighs evidence and policy responses such as education, legal protection or pathways to participation rather than relying on stereotypes."},
  "MC-SP-COV-04":{type:"sequence",title:"Franco to Democratic Memory",instruction:"Build the historical chain and identify where memory and interpretation enter the account.",prompt:"Franco dictatorship → repression and control → transition to democracy → debates over historical memory.",answer:"The dictatorship involved political repression, censorship and centralisation; the transition changed institutions but did not erase all consequences. Contemporary debates over memory show how the past remains politically and socially contested."},

  // ---- A-Level History (AQA 7042). One per misconception tag introduced by
  // the retag in data/forge-data.js; see docs/history-misconception-mapping.md.
  // MC-HIST-NEWLIB, -WARCAUSE, -NEWDEAL-END and -DISSOLUTION currently sit on a
  // single question each, so they are browsable but will not reach the heatmap
  // until each gains a second question in the rewrite pass.
  // The legacy MC-HIST-BRIT1-* entries above still serve the 67 definitional
  // questions that deliberately keep their per-question tag.
  "MC-HIST-LIB-WELFARE":{type:"classify",title:"Who Was Actually Covered?",instruction:"For each person, decide which Liberal reform (if any) helped them by 1914. Two are covered by nothing at all — find them before you look up.",items:["A 72-year-old widow with no income","A shipyard riveter earning £120 a year","That riveter's wife, at home with three children","A 9-year-old in a board school","A 30-year-old shop assistant","A farm labourer in a trade with no insurance scheme"],answer:"Widow: Old Age Pensions Act 1908 — 5s a week at 70, means-tested. Riveter: National Insurance 1911, BOTH parts — shipbuilding was one of the cyclical trades in the unemployment scheme. Child: school meals (1906) and medical inspections (1907). COVERED BY NOTHING: the riveter's wife and the farm labourer. Part I health insurance covered the WORKER, not dependants, and Part II unemployment covered only about 2.25 million men in building, shipbuilding and engineering. The shop assistant gets health insurance only. That is the point students miss: the reforms were SELECTIVE, aimed at the young, the old and the insured male worker in particular trades. Universal provision is 1945–48, thirty years later, which is why calling 1906–14 'the birth of the welfare state' needs heavy qualification."},
  "MC-HIST-CONTRIB":{type:"classify",title:"Who Paid For It?",instruction:"Mark each reform C (contributory — the recipient paid in first) or N (non-contributory — funded from taxation). Then state which of the two the 1911 Act was, and why that mattered politically.",items:["Old Age Pensions Act 1908","National Insurance Act 1911, Part I (health)","National Insurance Act 1911, Part II (unemployment)","School meals, 1906"],answer:"N: the 1908 pension (funded from taxation, means-tested, nothing paid in) and school meals (funded from the rates). C: both parts of the 1911 Act. Health insurance was tripartite — 4d from the worker, 3d from the employer, 2d from the state, which Lloyd George sold as 'ninepence for fourpence'. Unemployment insurance was likewise contributory. Why it mattered: contribution made the benefit a RIGHT rather than charity, which avoided the stigma of the Poor Law and, crucially, avoided a means test — but it also excluded anyone who had not paid in, which is why the poorest were often outside the scheme entirely. The test to apply: did you pay in first (contributory, claimed as of right) or is it paid from general taxation (non-contributory, usually means-tested)?"},
  "MC-HIST-NEWLIB":{type:"correct",title:"Is New Liberalism Just Socialism?",instruction:"A student writes: 'New Liberalism was really socialism, because it abandoned individual freedom in favour of state control.' Find the error, then give two pieces of evidence from the 1906–14 reforms that disprove it.",prompt:"Consider what Hobhouse and Hobson actually argued freedom requires, and look at how the 1911 Act was funded and what the Liberals never touched.",answer:"The error is treating state action as automatically anti-liberal. New Liberals (Hobhouse, Hobson) redefined freedom as POSITIVE liberty: a man who is starving, sick or illiterate is not free in any meaningful sense, so the state must supply the conditions in which individual freedom can actually be exercised. That is an argument FOR liberalism's own goal, by a different route. Evidence: (1) the 1911 Act was CONTRIBUTORY, deliberately preserving the principle of self-help — the recipient paid in, rather than being provided for; (2) the Liberals nationalised nothing, left private property and the market untouched, and had no theory of class conflict. Socialism proposes common ownership and analyses society by class; New Liberalism proposes state action to free the individual within a capitalist economy. The distinction earns marks precisely because the surface — more state spending — looks the same."},
  "MC-HIST-LORDS":{type:"sequence",title:"Which Came First?",instruction:"Put these six events in order, then answer in one sentence: did the Parliament Act cause the Budget crisis, or the other way round? Finally, state exactly what the Act did to the Lords' power.",items:["The Parliament Act becomes law","The Lords reject the People's Budget","Lloyd George introduces the People's Budget","The Liberals win a second election in December 1910","The Budget finally passes","The January 1910 general election"],answer:"Order: Budget introduced (April 1909) → Lords reject it (November 1909) → January 1910 election → Budget passes (April 1910) → December 1910 election → Parliament Act (August 1911). The causal direction is the whole point: the BUDGET caused the crisis, and the Parliament Act was the CONSEQUENCE. Rejecting a money bill broke a 250-year convention, which turned a row about land taxes into a constitutional question about whether an unelected chamber could veto the elected one. What the Act did: removed the Lords' veto on money bills entirely; replaced the veto on other public bills with a delay of two years; and cut the maximum life of a Parliament from seven years to five. It did NOT abolish the Lords or its powers — a suspensory veto remains, and was used against Irish Home Rule immediately afterwards."},
  "MC-HIST-SUFFRAGE":{type:"compare",title:"Fawcett or Pankhurst?",instruction:"Complete the table from memory, then answer: which organisation was larger, and by roughly how much? Most students guess wrong.",headers:["Feature","NUWSS","WSPU"],rows:[["Founded",""," 1903"],["Leader","",""],["Methods","",""],["Rough membership","",""],["Response to war in 1914","",""]],answer:"NUWSS: founded 1897, led by Millicent Fawcett, constitutional methods — petitions, lobbying, public meetings, backing sympathetic candidates; around 50,000 members by 1914; supported the war effort while continuing to press the case. WSPU: founded 1903, led by Emmeline Pankhurst, militant methods — window-breaking, arson, hunger strikes and forcible feeding under the Cat and Mouse Act; only about 2,000 members; suspended campaigning entirely and turned to patriotic recruitment. The size answer surprises students: the CONSTITUTIONAL society was roughly twenty-five times larger than the militant one. 'Suffragette' was coined as mockery by the Daily Mail in 1906 and adopted by the WSPU as a badge; suffragist is the older and broader word covering both. Using them interchangeably loses the distinction the whole historiographical debate turns on — whether militancy advanced the cause or set it back."},
  "MC-HIST-FRANCHISE":{type:"correct",title:"'Women Got the Vote in 1918'",instruction:"Find everything wrong with that sentence. State precisely who was enfranchised in 1918, roughly what proportion of adult women that was, and when the remaining women got the vote.",prompt:"The Act also changed the male franchise. Work out why the two changes had to happen in the same bill.",answer:"The Representation of the People Act 1918 gave the vote to women over 30 who were householders, wives of householders, occupiers of property worth £5 a year, or university graduates — about 8.4 million women, roughly 40% of adult women. Younger working women, including most of the munitions workers, were excluded. All men over 21 got the vote (19 for servicemen), so the electorate remained majority male. Equal franchise came with the Equal Franchise Act 1928, ten years later. The age bar was deliberate arithmetic: after the war's casualties, enfranchising all adult women would have made women a MAJORITY of the electorate, which Parliament would not accept. Why the two changes travelled together: war service had destroyed the residence qualification for millions of soldiers, so a bill to re-enfranchise them was unavoidable — and once the franchise was being rewritten, women's claim could not be kept off the table."},
  "MC-HIST-WARCAUSE":{type:"evaluate",title:"Did War Work Win the Vote?",instruction:"Write one sentence for the claim that women's war work won the franchise, and one against. Then give a judgement — and make sure your judgement deals with the age limit.",answer:"FOR: war work made the anti-suffrage position publicly untenable and gave opponents such as Asquith a way to change position without admitting the pre-war campaign had been right. AGAINST: the women actually enfranchised — over 30, householders or married to householders — were largely NOT the young, single munitions workers whose war service is being credited; if war work were the cause, the Act enfranchised close to the wrong women. JUDGEMENT: the war was the OCCASION rather than the cause. The mechanism was procedural — millions of soldiers had lost their residence qualification, so a franchise bill had to be passed anyway, and the Speaker's Conference of 1916–17 settled the terms. The pre-war NUWSS campaign had already built the parliamentary majority; the war supplied the moment and the political cover. Note the historiographical point: the 'reward for war work' story was congenial to everyone afterwards, which is part of why it survived."},
  "MC-HIST-HOMERULE":{type:"chain",title:"An Irish Crisis or a British One?",instruction:"Complete the chain, naming a specific organisation, document or event at each step. Then answer the question in the title with evidence.",prompt:"Third Home Rule Bill introduced, 1912 → [step: Ulster's response] → [step: armed force] → [step: nationalist response] → [step: the army] → outcome in September 1914.",answer:"Third Home Rule Bill (1912) → the Ulster Covenant, September 1912, signed by roughly 471,000 people pledging to resist → the Ulster Volunteer Force, formed January 1913, around 100,000 men, armed by the Larne gun-running of April 1914 → the Irish Volunteers formed in response, November 1913 → the Curragh incident, March 1914, when around 60 British officers indicated they would resign rather than coerce Ulster → the Home Rule Act reaches the statute book in September 1914 and is immediately suspended for the duration of the war. Why it is a BRITISH crisis: the Conservative leader Bonar Law told the 1912 Blenheim rally he could 'imagine no length of resistance' to which Ulster might go that he would not support — a party of government endorsing extra-legal resistance — and the Curragh raised the question of whether the army would obey a Liberal cabinet. The constitutional order itself was in doubt, which is why the crisis belongs in a British politics answer, not only an Irish one."},
  "MC-HIST-GENSTRIKE":{type:"correct",title:"Revolution, and a Union Victory?",instruction:"A student writes: 'The 1926 General Strike was an attempt at revolution, and the unions won concessions for the miners.' Both halves are wrong. Correct each, then state what the miners' position was by the end of the year.",prompt:"The strike lasted nine days. The miners' dispute lasted seven months.",answer:"Not revolution: the TUC called the strike in support of miners locked out after the government subsidy ended and the Samuel Commission recommended wage cuts. TUC leaders — Thomas above all — repeatedly insisted it was an INDUSTRIAL dispute, and their evident anxiety to end it is the strongest evidence they had no political aim; a general council seeking to overthrow the state does not call the action off unilaterally after nine days. Not a victory: the TUC called it off on 12 May 1926 having obtained NO concessions, leaving the miners out alone. They stayed out until November and returned on longer hours and lower pay — precisely what the lockout had been about. The consequence ran the other way: the Trade Disputes Act 1927 made sympathetic strikes illegal and changed union political funding from contracting-out to contracting-in, cutting Labour's income. The General Strike is a defeat, and the standard essay error is to narrate its drama and then forget to say who lost."},
  "MC-HIST-THIRTIES":{type:"compare",title:"Two Towns, One Decade",instruction:"Complete the table, then write a sentence that is true of BOTH columns at once. That sentence is the answer to any 'hungry thirties' question.",headers:["","Jarrow / Merthyr","Coventry / Slough"],rows:[["Unemployment, mid-1930s","",""],["Main industries","",""],["Direction of living standards","",""],["Housing",""," "]],answer:"Jarrow and Merthyr: unemployment around 67% and 62% in 1934, in coal, shipbuilding and steel — the old export staples — with falling living standards, malnutrition recorded in medical reports, and the 1936 Jarrow Crusade. Coventry and Slough: unemployment around 5%, in cars, electrical goods, chemicals and light engineering, with rising living standards and a private housing boom — nearly 293,000 houses built in 1936 alone, affordable because cheap money had cut mortgage rates. The sentence true of both: for those IN WORK, real wages rose roughly 15% across the decade because prices fell faster than wages, while for the long-term unemployed in the staple regions the same decade was the worst in living memory. The 1930s were simultaneously a decade of rising average living standards and of catastrophic regional collapse, which is why 'were the 1930s the hungry thirties?' is answered by DISAGGREGATING — by region, by industry, by whether the household had work — not by picking a side."},
  "MC-HIST-NATGOV":{type:"correct",title:"Whose Government Was It?",instruction:"A student writes: 'The National Government of 1931 was a Conservative government.' Explain what is right and what is wrong about that, name the Prime Minister, and state what his own party did to him.",answer:"Wrong in form, largely right in substance — and the gap between the two is the answer. The Prime Minister was Ramsay MACDONALD, leader of the Labour Party, who formed the National Government in August 1931 after his cabinet split over a proposed 10% cut in unemployment benefit. Only a handful of Labour MPs followed him; the party expelled MacDonald, Snowden and Thomas, so the Labour Prime Minister of a 'National' government was no longer a member of the Labour Party. In substance the Conservatives dominated: after the October 1931 election the government held 554 seats, of which 473 were Conservative, and Labour was reduced from 288 seats to 52. So it was a Conservative-dominated administration fronted by a Labour Prime Minister his own party regarded as a traitor. Both halves must be stated — 'Conservative government' loses the crisis, and 'Labour government' loses the arithmetic."},
  "MC-HIST-GOLD":{type:"chain",title:"The Best Thing That Happened?",instruction:"Complete the chain from leaving gold to recovery, naming a specific mechanism at each step. Then explain why 1925 is the necessary other half of this answer.",prompt:"Britain leaves the gold standard, September 1931 → [step: the exchange rate] → [step: monetary policy] → [step: what that made possible] → recovery by 1937.",answer:"Leaving gold → sterling falls roughly 25%, from $4.86 to about $3.40, making exports competitive and imports dearer → monetary policy is freed from defending the parity, so Bank Rate is cut to 2% in 1932 and held there: the 'cheap money' policy → cheap mortgages drive a private housing boom and domestic demand, while protection (1932) shelters the new industries → by 1937 Britain has recovered faster than most of Europe. Sidney Webb's reaction on hearing it — that nobody had told them they could do that — captures how the constraint had been treated as a law of nature. Why 1925 matters: Churchill's return to gold at the pre-war parity of $4.86 OVERVALUED sterling by perhaps 10%, forcing exporters to cut costs, which in coal meant wage cuts, which produced the lockout and the General Strike. Keynes had predicted exactly this in The Economic Consequences of Mr Churchill. So 1931 is not simply a humiliation: it undid a decision that had already cost the country dearly."},
  "MC-HIST-APPEASE":{type:"evaluate",title:"September 1938 — What Did Chamberlain Know?",instruction:"You may use ONLY what was known before 30 September 1938. On that basis, write two sentences justifying Munich and one criticising it. Then say what changed by March 1939.",prompt:"Banned from your answer: the Second World War, the Holocaust, and anything Hitler did after 1938.",answer:"JUSTIFICATION: the Chiefs of Staff advised that Britain could not fight Germany, Italy and Japan simultaneously, and rearmament — especially radar and Hurricane and Spitfire production — needed time that only delay could buy; the Dominions made clear they would not follow Britain into war over Czechoslovakia, and public memory of 1914–18 was overwhelming, with the Soviet Union distrusted after the purges and no land route to Prague in any case. CRITICISM: Munich handed Germany the Czech border fortifications, the Skoda works and the equivalent of some 35 divisions, strengthening the enemy more than the year's delay strengthened Britain — and Hitler's record since the Anschluss in March 1938 gave ample reason to doubt his word. WHAT CHANGED: the occupation of Prague in March 1939 destroyed the claim that Hitler sought only German-speaking populations, at which point appeasement's own premise collapsed and Chamberlain guaranteed Poland. The marks come from judging on 1938 information and then dating the turn, not from condemning a decision with knowledge nobody had."},
  "MC-HIST-BEVERIDGE":{type:"match",title:"Report or Statute?",instruction:"Match each of Beveridge's five giants to the measure that actually addressed it, with a date. Then answer: how many of these did the Beveridge Report itself enact?",items:["Want","Disease","Ignorance","Squalor","Idleness"],answer:"Want → National Insurance Act 1946 and National Assistance Act 1948. Disease → NHS Act 1946, operational 5 July 1948. Ignorance → the Butler Education Act 1944. Squalor → New Towns Act 1946 and the post-war housing programme. Idleness → the 1944 Employment White Paper committing government to a high and stable level of employment. The Beveridge Report enacted NONE of them: it was a report, published December 1942, which sold over 600,000 copies and shaped the political argument, but it legislated nothing whatever. Two details are worth holding on to. The Butler Act came in 1944, under Churchill's wartime COALITION and from a Conservative minister — so the earliest of the five was not a Labour measure at all. And the gap between report and delivery is six years, which is why 'Beveridge created the welfare state' is a sentence that cannot survive a date check."},
  "MC-HIST-CONSENSUS":{type:"sort",title:"Agreed, or Fought Over?",instruction:"Sort each item into 'both parties genuinely agreed' or 'contested within a shared framework'. Two look like agreement and are not.",items:["That the NHS should exist","Ownership of iron and steel","The commitment to full employment","How many council houses to build","The independent nuclear deterrent","Road haulage nationalisation"],answer:"Genuinely agreed: the existence of the NHS, the full employment commitment and the nuclear deterrent — the Conservatives kept all three after 1951. Contested: iron and steel, denationalised in 1953 and renationalised in 1967; road haulage, largely denationalised; and council housing, where Macmillan deliberately outbuilt Labour by promising and delivering 300,000 houses a year. That last is the one students misread as agreement: both parties built, so it looks consensual, but they were competing hard on the number. The definition to hold: consensus means a shared FRAMEWORK — mixed economy, welfare state, full employment, NATO — inside which the parties fought about ownership, priorities and scale. It does not mean they stopped disagreeing. Worth adding that 'post-war consensus' is a retrospective label, popularised by Addison in 1975, and that historians such as Pimlott have disputed whether it existed at all — so a good answer uses the term with the quotation marks visible."},
  "MC-HIST-SUEZ":{type:"correct",title:"Was Suez a Military Defeat?",instruction:"A student writes: 'Britain was defeated at Suez and had to withdraw.' The second half is true and the first is not. Explain what actually forced the withdrawal, and name the three pressures in order of importance.",answer:"Militarily the operation was succeeding: the landings took Port Said and the canal zone was being secured when the ceasefire came. What forced withdrawal was FINANCIAL and DIPLOMATIC. First and decisive: a run on sterling, with reserves falling sharply through November 1956, and an American refusal to support an IMF loan or release oil supplies unless Britain accepted a ceasefire — Eisenhower, furious at being deceived and facing a presidential election, used the currency as a lever. Second: condemnation in the UN General Assembly, alongside Soviet threats, leaving Britain isolated. Third: a domestic and Commonwealth split, with the Cabinet itself divided. Eden resigned in January 1957. The lesson contemporaries drew, and the reason Suez appears in every 'decline' question, is that Britain could no longer undertake a major military action against American opposition — a limit on power, not a failure of arms."},
  "MC-HIST-RECON":{type:"compare",title:"Two Reconstructions",instruction:"Complete the table. Then answer in one sentence: what does merging these two into a single 'Reconstruction' lose?",headers:["","Presidential","Radical / Congressional"],rows:[["Dates","",""],["Driven by","",""],["Terms for the South","",""],["Position of freedpeople","",""],["How it ended","",""]],answer:"Presidential (1865–67): driven by Andrew Johnson; lenient terms — ratify the 13th Amendment, repudiate Confederate debt, take a loyalty oath, with generous pardons; Southern states responded with the Black Codes, restoring near-servitude, and elected ex-Confederates to Congress; it ended when the 1866 midterms returned a Republican supermajority. Radical (1867–77): driven by Congress, Stevens and Sumner; the Military Reconstruction Acts divided the South into five military districts and required ratification of the 14th Amendment and black male suffrage; freedpeople voted, held office and were supported by the Freedmen's Bureau; it ended with the Compromise of 1877 and the withdrawal of federal troops. What merging them loses: the CONFLICT is the period. Johnson was impeached in 1868 and survived by a single vote, and the fight between President and Congress over who would define freedom is the reason Reconstruction went as far as it did and the reason it could be reversed."},
  "MC-HIST-AMENDMENTS":{type:"match",title:"Thirteen, Fourteen, Fifteen",instruction:"Match each amendment to its content and year without notes. Then answer two questions: which one contains a loophole still in use today, and why did women's suffrage need a separate amendment?",items:["Abolished slavery","Citizenship, due process and equal protection binding on the states","Voting rights not to be denied on grounds of race"],answer:"13th (1865) abolished slavery — 'except as a punishment for crime whereof the party shall have been duly convicted', the loophole that underwrote convict leasing in the South and remains in the text. 14th (1868) established national citizenship, due process and equal protection, and crucially bound the STATES, which is what later made federal civil rights litigation possible. 15th (1870) barred denial of the vote on grounds of race, colour or previous condition of servitude. Why the 19th Amendment was needed: the 15th says nothing about SEX, and the deliberate omission split the suffrage movement in 1869 between those who would accept black male suffrage first and those who would not. The other half of the 15th's weakness is what it does not prohibit — literacy tests, poll taxes and residency rules were race-neutral on their face and so survived, which is exactly how disfranchisement was accomplished without repealing anything."},
  "MC-HIST-JIMCROW":{type:"correct",title:"Who Imposed Segregation?",instruction:"A student writes: 'Jim Crow was federal law imposed on the South by Washington.' Correct it, then name three federal decisions that made segregation possible without imposing it.",answer:"Jim Crow was STATE and LOCAL law — statutes and municipal ordinances passed across the South from the 1870s onwards. The federal role was permissive rather than authoring. Three decisions: the Civil Rights Cases (1883), striking down the Civil Rights Act of 1875 and holding that the 14th Amendment reached state action but not private discrimination; Plessy v Ferguson (1896), upholding 'separate but equal'; and Williams v Mississippi (1898), upholding literacy tests and poll taxes as race-neutral on their face. Federal government also segregated its OWN civil service under Wilson from 1913, so Washington was not merely passive. Why the distinction earns marks: if segregation had been federal law, repealing it would have been the remedy. Because it was state law permitted by federal courts, the remedy had to be federal power turned the other way — which is what Brown, the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 actually are."},
  "MC-HIST-LAISSEZ":{type:"sort",title:"Laissez-Faire, Really?",instruction:"Sort each into 'government staying out of the economy' or 'government intervening'. Then rewrite the definition of Gilded Age laissez-faire so that it survives your own sorting.",items:["Land grants of some 130 million acres to the railroads","The McKinley Tariff of 1890","Federal troops sent against the Pullman strike, 1894","The Sherman Antitrust Act used against a union","The Interstate Commerce Commission, 1887","No federal income tax before 1913"],answer:"Only the last is genuine abstention. Everything else is intervention: enormous land subsidies built the railroads, tariffs approaching 50% protected domestic manufacturers from competition, federal troops and injunctions broke strikes, the Sherman Act was turned against labour combinations far more readily than against corporate ones, and the ICC created federal regulation of railroads — feeble at first, but a precedent. Rewritten definition: Gilded Age laissez-faire meant that government declined to regulate WAGES, HOURS AND CONDITIONS or to protect workers' organisation, while intervening vigorously to subsidise, protect and defend capital. It was selective, not minimal. Students who take 'laissez-faire' at face value cannot then explain how the railroads were financed or why troops appear in a strike narrative — and the essay that says government 'did nothing' contradicts its own evidence a paragraph later."},
  "MC-HIST-POPPROG":{type:"compare",title:"Populist or Progressive?",instruction:"Complete the table, then decide which movement each of these belongs to: Free Silver, the direct primary, the Pure Food and Drug Act, the subtreasury plan.",headers:["","Populism","Progressivism"],rows:[["Decade","",""],["Base","",""],["Typical supporter","",""],["Core demands","",""],["Political vehicle","",""]],answer:"Populism: 1890s; rural South and West; the indebted farmer; Free Silver to inflate the money supply, railroad and grain-elevator regulation, the subtreasury plan, a graduated income tax; the People's Party, absorbed by the Democrats when Bryan ran in 1896 on the Cross of Gold. Progressivism: 1900s–1910s; urban; the middle-class professional, journalist or reformer; regulation of corporations, efficiency and expertise, direct democracy, consumer protection, prohibition; both major parties, under Roosevelt and Wilson. Placements: Free Silver and the subtreasury are Populist; the direct primary and the Pure Food and Drug Act are Progressive. The connection worth noting is that many Omaha Platform demands were eventually enacted by Progressives — the income tax, the direct election of senators — so Populism looks like a defeated movement whose programme largely passed. But the BASE and the TARGET differ: debtor farmers against creditors and railroads, versus urban professionals against trusts and machine politics. Treating them as one movement makes the 1896 realignment unintelligible."},
  "MC-HIST-RIGHTSDECLINE":{type:"chain",title:"A Right on Paper",instruction:"The 15th Amendment was never repealed. Complete the chain explaining how black voting in the South nearly vanished anyway, naming a specific device or case at each step.",prompt:"15th Amendment ratified, 1870 → [step: 1877] → [step: state constitutions from 1890] → [step: the courts] → Louisiana in 1904.",answer:"15th Amendment (1870) → the Compromise of 1877 withdraws federal troops and with them any will to enforce → Redeemer governments write new state constitutions, beginning with the Mississippi Plan of 1890: poll taxes, literacy tests and 'understanding clauses' administered by white registrars, with grandfather clauses exempting whites whose grandfathers had voted → Williams v Mississippi (1898) upholds these as race-neutral on their face → in Louisiana, registered black voters fall from 130,344 in 1896 to 1,342 in 1904. That is a 99% collapse achieved without amending the Constitution once. The lesson is the one the whole period teaches: a right that is not ENFORCED is not a right, and the devices were designed to be formally colour-blind precisely so that they would survive judicial review. It is also why the 1965 Voting Rights Act attacks the machinery — suspending literacy tests, sending federal registrars — rather than restating the principle, which had been on the books for 95 years."},
  "MC-HIST-CRASH":{type:"distinguish",title:"Crash or Depression?",instruction:"Label each item C (belongs to the Wall Street Crash) or D (belongs to the Depression). Then answer: what proportion of Americans owned shares in 1929, and what does that imply about the Crash as a cause?",items:["Black Thursday and Black Tuesday, October 1929","Around 9,000 bank failures by 1933","Unemployment reaching about 25%","Margin calls wiping out leveraged investors","The money supply contracting by about a third","The Smoot-Hawley Tariff, 1930"],answer:"C: Black Thursday and Tuesday, and the margin calls. D: bank failures, 25% unemployment, monetary contraction, Smoot-Hawley. Only about 2.5% of Americans owned shares in 1929 — which is why the Crash cannot by itself explain a collapse that put a quarter of the workforce out of work. The Crash was an EVENT of days; the Depression was a contraction lasting from 1929 to 1933, driven by bank failures destroying deposits and credit, a money supply allowed to fall by roughly a third, debt deflation raising the real burden of what was owed, and a collapse in world trade that Smoot-Hawley worsened by inviting retaliation. The relationship is occasion and cause: the Crash destroyed confidence and exposed the leverage in the system, but the Depression came from what the banking system and the Federal Reserve did next. An answer that stops at October 1929 has explained the trigger and left out the mechanism."},
  "MC-HIST-NEWDEAL-PLAN":{type:"sort",title:"One Plan, or Improvisation?",instruction:"Sort these into the First New Deal (1933–34) and the Second (1935–36). Then identify the two measures in the list that flatly contradict each other, and explain what forced the change of direction in 1935.",items:["The Agricultural Adjustment Administration","The National Recovery Administration","The Civilian Conservation Corps","The Wagner Act","Social Security","The Works Progress Administration"],answer:"First New Deal: AAA, NRA, CCC — relief and recovery through cooperation with business, industrial codes and direct employment. Second New Deal: Wagner Act, Social Security, WPA — reform, with rights for labour and permanent structures rather than emergency measures. The contradiction: the AAA paid farmers to destroy crops and slaughter livestock to raise prices while Americans went hungry, and the NRA suspended antitrust law to let industries fix prices and output — the opposite of the trust-busting tradition the Democrats had inherited, and of what later policy resumed. What forced the shift: Schechter v United States (1935) struck down the NRA as an unconstitutional delegation of legislative power, and pressure from Long, Coughlin and Townsend showed the political cost of doing too little. Roosevelt himself called for 'bold, persistent experimentation' and said openly that if a method failed you should admit it and try another. The New Deal was improvised, and answers that describe it as a single coherent programme cannot explain why its two halves point in different directions."},
  "MC-HIST-NEWDEAL-END":{type:"graph",title:"Plot the Unemployment Line",instruction:"Sketch US unemployment from 1933 to 1944 using these figures, then mark the year the Depression ended. Explain the bump in 1938 before you look at the answer.",prompt:"1933: 24.9%. 1937: 14.3%. 1938: 19.0%. 1941: 9.9%. 1944: 1.2%.",answer:"The line falls steeply to 1937, JUMPS BACK UP in 1938, then falls away to almost nothing by 1944. The 1938 bump is the recession of 1937–38, caused when Roosevelt, believing recovery was secured, cut federal spending and the new Social Security payroll taxes began withdrawing purchasing power — unemployment rose by nearly five points in a year. That bump is the decisive evidence: recovery was not self-sustaining, and the moment stimulus was withdrawn the economy fell back. Unemployment does not reach pre-1929 levels until war production, and the date to mark is 1941–42, not 1933–39. The verdict to hold: the New Deal cut unemployment enormously, provided relief to millions and rebuilt the institutional order — but it did not END the Depression, and Roosevelt's own 1937 retrenchment demonstrates why. Relief, recovery and reform are three different claims, and the New Deal's record is much stronger on the first and third than the second."},
  "MC-HIST-NDOPP":{type:"classify",title:"Attacked From Which Side?",instruction:"For each critic, decide whether they attacked the New Deal for doing TOO MUCH or TOO LITTLE. Then explain what the Second New Deal owed to the second group.",items:["The American Liberty League","Huey Long","Father Coughlin","Dr Francis Townsend","The Supreme Court in Schechter and Butler","The American Medical Association"],answer:"Too much: the Liberty League, the Supreme Court (on constitutional rather than political grounds — Schechter killed the NRA, Butler the AAA) and the AMA, which fought any move towards health insurance. Too LITTLE: Long, whose Share Our Wealth promised every family $5,000 and who was a credible threat to Roosevelt in 1936 until his assassination in 1935; Coughlin, whose radio audience ran into the tens of millions; and Townsend, whose plan for $200 a month to every citizen over 60 had organised clubs across the country. What the Second New Deal owed them: Social Security and the Wealth Tax Act of 1935 were in part an answer to exactly this pressure — Roosevelt moved left because the electoral threat was coming from his left. The standard essay error is to present opposition as uniformly conservative, which makes the 1935 shift look unmotivated and misses that the New Deal's most radical measures were a response to critics who thought it far too timid."},
  "MC-HIST-CIVILRIGHTS-LAW":{type:"evaluate",title:"What Did Brown Actually Change?",instruction:"Brown v Board was decided in 1954. Estimate what percentage of black students in the South were in desegregated schools ten years later. Commit to a number before you read on, then explain what finally moved it.",prompt:"Brown II (1955) ordered desegregation to proceed with 'all deliberate speed'. Note what that phrase does not contain.",answer:"About 2%. Ten years after the most celebrated ruling in American constitutional law, roughly 98% of black students in the South were still in segregated schools. 'All deliberate speed' contained no DEADLINE, which handed the pace to the states that had no intention of moving; 101 members of Congress signed the Southern Manifesto in 1956 pledging resistance; and Little Rock in 1957 showed that enforcing a single school's integration took the 101st Airborne. What finally moved the figure was money and machinery: Title VI of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 allowed federal funding to be withdrawn from segregated districts, and Green v County School Board (1968) required plans that actually worked. The principle to carry into any civil rights answer: a court ruling is a permission to litigate, not a change in anyone's life. Law changes what can be demanded; enforcement, funding and political will change what happens — and the gap between them is measured here in years and in that 2%."},
  "MC-HIST-CIVILRIGHTS-START":{type:"sequence",title:"Before Montgomery",instruction:"Put these in chronological order. Every one happened BEFORE the Montgomery Bus Boycott of 1955. Then say what the sequence tells you about how the movement's legal victories were won.",items:["Truman desegregates the armed forces","The NAACP is founded","The Double V campaign begins","Sweatt v Painter","Executive Order 8802 on defence industry employment","The Great Migration begins in earnest"],answer:"NAACP founded 1909 → Great Migration from about 1916 → Executive Order 8802 in 1941, conceded after A. Philip Randolph threatened a march on Washington → Double V from 1942 → Truman's Executive Order 9981 desegregating the armed forces in 1948 → Sweatt v Painter in 1950. Montgomery is 1955 — the movement was already more than four decades old. What the sequence shows about the legal strategy: the NAACP under Charles Hamilton Houston and Thurgood Marshall deliberately attacked GRADUATE and professional schools first, in cases such as Sweatt, because no state could plausibly claim a separate black law school was equal — building precedent case by case until Brown could be attempted. Nothing about that is spontaneous. Treating 1955 as the beginning turns a planned campaign, a mass migration that created northern black electorates, and a wartime bargaining position into a story about one woman's tiredness on one bus."},
  "MC-HIST-COLDWARPOLICY":{type:"match",title:"Three Different Instruments",instruction:"For each of the Truman Doctrine, the Marshall Plan and NATO, state the year, the KIND of commitment it is, and its cost or membership. Then put them in order and explain why the order matters.",items:["Truman Doctrine","Marshall Plan","NATO"],answer:"Truman Doctrine, March 1947: a declared PRINCIPLE — that the United States would support free peoples resisting subjugation — attached to $400 million for Greece and Turkey. Marshall Plan, 1948–52: an ECONOMIC programme, roughly $13 billion to sixteen countries, formally offered to the Soviet Union and its satellites, whose refusal hardened the division of Europe. NATO, April 1949: a binding MILITARY alliance of twelve founding members, whose Article 5 treats an attack on one as an attack on all. Why the order matters: each escalates the nature of the commitment — from a statement of intent, to money, to a treaty obligation to go to war. That escalation is the argument of the period, and it is why Kennan, whose Long Telegram supplied the analysis, spent years objecting that his case for selective resistance at points of industrial importance had been turned into an open-ended global guarantee. Treating the three as one policy loses both the escalation and the argument about it."},
  "MC-HIST-TUDORCLAIM":{type:"evaluate",title:"How Strong Was Henry VII's Claim?",instruction:"Rank these four foundations of Henry VII's title from strongest to weakest, then explain the two things he did in 1485 and 1486 that tell you he knew the hereditary claim was thin.",items:["Descent through the Beauforts","Victory at Bosworth","Marriage to Elizabeth of York","Parliamentary recognition"],answer:"Weakest by some distance is the DESCENT. Henry's claim ran through his mother Margaret Beaufort, from John of Gaunt's third marriage; the Beauforts were legitimated by Richard II in 1397 but expressly barred from the succession by Henry IV's patent of 1407. His father's line brought no royal blood at all — Owen Tudor's standing came from marrying Henry V's widow. Strongest in practice was VICTORY, reinforced by marriage and confirmed by Parliament, which recognised the title as resting on possession rather than on a genealogical argument nobody wanted examined. The two revealing acts: he dated the start of his reign to the day BEFORE Bosworth, which made everyone who fought for Richard III a traitor by definition; and he was crowned in October 1485 but did not marry Elizabeth of York until January 1486, deliberately establishing that he held the crown in his own right and not through his wife. Both are the behaviour of a man whose claim needed manufacturing, which is also why the pretenders were so dangerous."},
  "MC-HIST-PRETENDERS":{type:"compare",title:"Simnel and Warbeck",instruction:"Complete the table without notes. Students routinely swap these two — check yours against the endings in particular, which are nothing alike.",headers:["","Lambert Simnel","Perkin Warbeck"],rows:[["Claimed to be","",""],["Years active","",""],["Foreign backers","",""],["Decisive event","",""],["What happened to him","",""]],answer:"Simnel: claimed to be Edward, Earl of Warwick; active 1486–87; backed by the Earl of Lincoln and Margaret of Burgundy, who funded around 2,000 German mercenaries, with Irish support and a coronation in Dublin; destroyed at the Battle of Stoke Field in June 1487; PARDONED and put to work in the royal kitchens, later becoming a falconer. Warbeck: claimed to be Richard of Shrewsbury, the younger Prince in the Tower; active 1491–99, far longer; backed at various points by Margaret of Burgundy, Charles VIII of France, Maximilian and James IV of Scotland, who married him to his own kinswoman Catherine Gordon; captured in 1497, confessed, and EXECUTED in 1499 alongside the real Earl of Warwick. The endings are the memory hook: Simnel to the kitchens, Warbeck to the scaffold. Henry's treatment of Simnel was calculated humiliation — a crowned king turned scullion is an argument that the whole business was absurd — while Warbeck's foreign backing made him a diplomatic weapon and so a far graver threat."},
  "MC-HIST-BREAK":{type:"sort",title:"Jurisdiction or Doctrine?",instruction:"Sort each measure into 'changes who is in CHARGE of the Church' and 'changes what the Church BELIEVES'. Then answer: by Henry VIII's death in 1547, was England Protestant in doctrine?",items:["Act in Restraint of Appeals, 1533","Act of Supremacy, 1534","Dissolution of the monasteries","Six Articles, 1539","The Great Bible in English, 1539","Ten Articles, 1536"],answer:"Jurisdiction: the Act in Restraint of Appeals (no appeals to Rome — 'this realm of England is an empire'), the Act of Supremacy, and the dissolution, which transferred property and power rather than belief. Doctrine: the Ten Articles, which leaned mildly Protestant; the English Bible, a genuinely Protestant principle; and the Six Articles, which went hard the other way. Was England Protestant in 1547? NO. The Six Articles reaffirmed transubstantiation, clerical celibacy, private masses and auricular confession, and made denial of them punishable by death — contemporaries called it the whip with six strings. Henry heard mass, and died in a church whose doctrine was substantially Catholic under a king who was its Supreme Head. That is the distinction the whole topic rests on: Henry's break was about AUTHORITY, not belief. Doctrinal Protestantism arrives under Edward VI with the Prayer Books of 1549 and 1552 — which is why 'Henry VIII made England Protestant' fails on a date check."},
  "MC-HIST-DISSOLUTION":{type:"calculate",title:"Follow the Money",instruction:"Using these figures, work out why the monasteries were dissolved — and then explain why the Crown was no better off by 1547.",prompt:"Valor Ecclesiasticus (1535) valued monastic income at roughly £160,000 a year. The Crown's ordinary annual revenue was roughly £100,000. Around 800 houses were dissolved between 1536 and 1540.",answer:"Monastic income was worth more than one and a half times the Crown's entire ordinary revenue — the single largest transfer of property in English history before the twentieth century, and a motive that needs no supplementary explanation. The religious case, built on Cromwell's visitations and their reports of corruption, supplied the PRETEXT rather than the reason; the smaller houses went first on the argument that they were lax, and the great houses followed regardless. Why the Crown was no better off by 1547: the land was SOLD, mostly to gentry and nobility, to pay for Henry's French and Scottish wars in the 1540s. A permanent annual income was converted into a one-off windfall and spent. The lasting consequence is political rather than financial: several thousand families now held former monastic land and had a direct material stake in the Reformation never being reversed — which is a substantial part of the answer to why Mary could not undo it."},
  "MC-HIST-REBELLION":{type:"classify",title:"What Did They Actually Want?",instruction:"For each rebellion, state the PRIMARY grievance and whether the rebels sought to remove the monarch. Only one of the five did.",items:["Pilgrimage of Grace, 1536","Western (Prayer Book) Rebellion, 1549","Kett's Rebellion, 1549","Wyatt's Rebellion, 1554","Northern Rebellion, 1569"],answer:"Pilgrimage of Grace: religion above all — the dissolution — mixed with taxation and hostility to Cromwell; the Pontefract Articles profess loyalty to Henry and demand the removal of evil counsellors. Western Rebellion: religion, demanding the restoration of the Latin mass and the Six Articles against the 1549 Prayer Book. Kett: ENCLOSURE and corrupt local government — its 29 articles are overwhelmingly agrarian and barely religious at all, which is why it is the standard trap in a list like this. Wyatt: opposition to the Spanish marriage rather than religion as such, though it aimed to replace Mary with Elizabeth. Northern Rebellion: restoring Catholicism and putting Mary Stuart on the throne — the ONE that genuinely sought to depose the monarch. The Tudor pattern to take away: rebels almost always proclaimed loyalty to the monarch and demanded the dismissal of advisers, because attacking the sovereign directly was treason and lost the waverers. Reading that convention as evidence they were merely reformist is as wrong as reading every rebellion as an attempted coup."},
  "MC-HIST-ELIZSETTLE":{type:"evaluate",title:"Did 1559 Settle Anything?",instruction:"Name the two Acts of 1559 and one deliberate ambiguity in the Prayer Book. Then list what the settlement had provoked within twenty years, and judge whether 'settlement' is the right word.",answer:"The Act of Supremacy made Elizabeth Supreme GOVERNOR rather than Supreme Head — a concession both to Catholic consciences and to the objection that a woman could not head the Church — and the Act of Uniformity imposed the 1559 Prayer Book with a 12d fine for non-attendance. The calculated ambiguity: the words of administration at communion splice the 1549 and 1552 wordings together, so that a worshipper could understand the sacrament as the real presence or as a memorial and neither would be contradicted. What followed within twenty years: the Vestiarian controversy from 1566, the Admonition to Parliament in 1572 and the Presbyterian campaign of Cartwright and Field, the Northern Rebellion of 1569, the bull Regnans in Excelsis excommunicating Elizabeth in 1570, and the plots from Ridolfi onwards — with penalties escalating to £20 a month for recusancy in 1581 and the priesthood itself made treason in 1585. JUDGEMENT: 'settlement' describes an intention, not an outcome. It was a framework built to be liveable rather than believable, and its studied vagueness was both the reason it held and the reason it was fought over for forty years."},
  "MC-HIST-MINISTERS":{type:"compare",title:"How Powerful Were Wolsey and Cromwell?",instruction:"Complete the table, then answer the question that decides the topic: what did each man's power actually rest on, and how do we know?",headers:["","Wolsey","Cromwell"],rows:[["Peak offices","",""],["Signature achievement","",""],["Cause of fall","",""],["Fate","",""]],answer:"Wolsey: Lord Chancellor from 1515 and papal legate from 1518, controlling both church and state patronage; his achievements ran from the Field of Cloth of Gold to reform of the Court of Chancery; he fell in 1529 for failing to obtain the annulment, was arrested in 1530 and died at Leicester on his way to trial. Cromwell: Vicegerent in Spirituals and chief minister, architect of the break with Rome, the Act of Supremacy and the dissolution; he fell over the Anne of Cleves marriage, was attainted WITHOUT TRIAL and executed on 28 July 1540. What their power rested on: the king's favour, and nothing else. Neither had a great landed affinity or a noble following — a butcher's son and a cloth-worker's son — and that was precisely why Henry raised them, since a man with no independent base cannot become a rival. How we know: both fell within months of a single personal failure, and neither could mobilise anything to save himself. This is also the crux of the Elton debate: if Cromwell built a genuinely bureaucratic Tudor revolution in government, it is odd that his own position depended entirely on one man's goodwill."},
  "MC-HIST-JUDGEMENT":{type:"rewrite",title:"Narrative Into Judgement",instruction:"Rewrite this paragraph so that it argues rather than narrates. You must add a criterion for judgement, keep every fact, and reach a verdict in the final sentence.",prompt:"Student: 'In 1931 MacDonald formed the National Government. Britain left the gold standard in September. Interest rates were cut to 2% and stayed there. House building increased and new industries grew in the Midlands and the South. Unemployment remained very high in the North East and South Wales.'",answer:"Everything in the original is accurate, and it scores in the middle band because it is a LIST in chronological order with no argument. Model rewrite: 'The recovery of the 1930s was real but regionally selective, and its cause lay in monetary policy rather than in any deliberate programme. Leaving the gold standard in September 1931 freed the Bank to cut rates to 2%, and cheap money — not the National Government's fiscal orthodoxy — drove the housing boom and the growth of the new Midlands and Southern industries. Judged by aggregate output, the decade was a success; judged by the 67% unemployment of Jarrow, it was a catastrophe that policy never addressed. The recovery should therefore be understood as a by-product of a forced devaluation rather than an achievement of government, which is why it reached the regions that needed it least.' What changed: a criterion is stated (aggregate output versus regional distribution), the facts are subordinated to a claim, and the verdict answers a question rather than reporting a sequence. Every fact from the original survives — judgement is not the removal of detail, it is the addition of an argument the detail supports."},
};
